KANT_TR['metaphysik-mrongovius/guyer'] = {"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-001":["Prolegomena *Metaphysices*",["Latin heading kept in Latin; *Metaphysices* is the Latin genitive."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-002":["Our cognitions are in interconnection in a twofold way, first as an aggregate, when one adds one to the other in order to make up a whole, e.g., a sand-heap is in itself no interconnection of things, rather they are arbitrarily put together (there is nothing determined here), second as a series of ground and consequences, the parts of a series are called members, because we can cognize one part only through the other, e.g., in the human body one part is there through the other. We easily see that the interconnection of cognitions as an aggregate gives no determinate concept of the whole, and it is as if I add one little piece to another until a mountain arises, etc., until a planet or earth-body comes to be, at least we can think it so. In the series there is something that makes the connection according to a rule, namely of the grounds and consequences. With grounds and consequences we must think boundaries a priori, i.e., a ground that is no further a consequence, and boundaries a posteriori, i.e., a consequence that is no ground, e.g., in the human generations; human beings are members of the series, here we must nevertheless think a human being who indeed begets but is not born, thus *terminus a priori*; and one who is born but begets no one, thus *terminus a posteriori*. We consider here (in metaphysics) not the things, as they hang together as grounds and consequences, but the cognitions, which also descend in the way human beings or other things do. I can represent to myself a cognition that is no consequence, thus the highest ground, and that is no ground, thus the last consequence.",["Folge is rendered 'consequence' here (glossary 'sequel; sequence' is for Folge in other uses); Grund = ground; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gränze = boundary; Verknüpfung = connection.","'Reihe von Grund und Folgen' is kept as printed ('ground and consequences'); 'Ein Sandberg' is rendered 'a sand-heap' (literally 'sand-mountain').","'derReihe' in the OCR silently corrected to 'der Reihe'. The comma splices ('..., second as a series...'; '... members, because...') follow the German punctuation.","'Erdkörper' = 'earth-body'; 'willkührlich' = 'arbitrarily'; 'vorstellen' = 'represent'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-003":["The last consequence is immediate experience, e.g., Something — body — stone — limestone — marble — marble column.",["Folge = 'consequence' as above; Erfahrung = experience; the dashes are as in the OCR."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-004":["We thus have an idea of an interconnection of cognitions as grounds and consequences cognition. The grounds of what grounds according to a certain rule are called *principia*. Insofar, then, as cognitions are in a series, there must also be *principia*. It is remarkable that I can make the consequence into the ground, from which, however, the other does not follow, but through which I have always only come to the cognition of the other. They are thus not *principia essendi*, but *cognoscendi*, e.g., I cognize the existence of God from the world. The world is nevertheless not the ground of God, but the reverse, but through the world I can arrive at the concept of God, and insofar I can go from the *principiata* to the *principia* — the consequences that are used as grounds, to go in reverse to their grounds, are called *principia a posteriori*. If I begin from the consequences, then I cognize something *a posteriori*, if I begin from the grounds, then I cognize *a priori*. If something is given to me, then I can try whether I would have cognized it *a priori* from grounds, e.g., that sunlight melts ice experience teaches, but *a priori* we would hardly have cognized it. The cognition that is taken from experience is *kat' exochen a posteriori*, and when in future we call cognitions *a posteriori*, we always understand by that those that are from experience, because experience contains the last consequence of our cognition, for which we seek grounds from reason. If we take experience as *principium*, then the principle is empirical: e.g., all bodies are heavy (insofar as we know them), experience teaches, we can assume it as a principle and say: since all bodies are heavy, it follows from this . . . Principles *a priori* are those that are borrowed from no experience. Whether there are any must soon be investigated.",["OCR 'Efkenntniße' silently read 'Erkenntniße'. The sentence 'Wir haben also eine Idee ... als Gründe und Folgen Erkenntniß' appears garbled or defective in the text ('Folgen Erkenntniß'); rendered as it stands. Likewise 'Die Gründe von gründen nach einer gewißen Regel sind' is doubtful (perhaps 'von dem, was gründet'); rendered literally.","Erkennen = cognize; Erkenntniß = cognition; kennen = know; verstehen = understand; Vernunft = reason; Daseyn = existence; Begriff = concept.","*Grund* and *principium* are both rendered, 'ground' and '*principium*' (left in Latin); *principium* and *Princip* (Grundsatz-type usage) both appear as 'principle' where the German has 'princip' in the plural/generic ('Principien a priori').","'kat'exochen' = Greek *kat' exochēn* ('par excellence'), kept in italics, not translated."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-005":["From the chaining together of our cognition a unity can arise, i.e., science, or an interconnection can be encountered that is systematic. A We can think an interconnection of our cognitions as science, from the first grounds to the last consequences, and this would comprise in itself the whole of all inferred cognitions; but it exceeds our powers.",["The stray 'A' is carried over as it stands in the OCR; it may be an editorial marker (apparatus) or a misreading, uncertain.","Wissenschaft = science; Kräfte = powers (glossary 'force; power'); 'erschlossen' = 'inferred'; 'sie' ('it') could refer to the science or to the interconnection: ambiguity preserved."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-006":["The first ground is simple — namely of Something — but in order to have the last consequences, we must go through the entire field of experience, which cannot be.",["OCR 'mäßen' read as 'müßen'. 'simpel' = 'simple' (not the glossary's einfach, which is not the word used). 'von Etwas' is ambiguous (the first ground 'of' or 'concerning' Something); left as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-007":["Since this therefore does not work, we can B take the material away from it. I want to have only a piece of the system of the whole of human cognition, namely the science of the highest principles of human cognition, and such a project is modest. One has long had in thought to work on such a science and has called it metaphysics. Metaphysics is thus the science of the first principles of the entire human cognition, which thus contains the first members of the series. This concept, however, C is not determinate enough, as to how much or how little belongs to it. For when I give a concept of the science, it must be determined what belongs to it or not, and people also cannot (through the given concept) know how far these principles reach down to experience or not, and where we stay standing. — Every series can be divided into 2 parts: the one that precedes the other is *comparative* the first, e.g., a church history of the first centuries. Here nothing is determinate, it can be the 3rd, 7th, 8th, following of our reckoning of years. The boundary *a priori* of metaphysics is determinate, namely that there would be no principles from which it could be derived. One speaks of the possible, impossible, something and nothing, higher the human being cannot climb. Of world, contingency, fate, natural, supernatural — cause of the world, existence, God is spoken so long through mere concepts. Only about the soul of the human being was it thought that something could be said, if he did not attend to himself. So the body too may well belong here, and now I do not know whether not the whole doctrine of nature. From this it is clear that I cannot set boundaries for metaphysics in the series of consequences and grounds according to degree, if I consider it as homogeneous with and related to other sciences, but I must think it as heterogeneous and entirely different from the others.",["The letters B and C and the OCR 'projeet' (read 'project') are as in the text; B and C are carried over as they stand and may be editorial marks. 'Gränze' = boundary; Stoff = material; Naturlehre = doctrine of nature; Zufall = contingency (glossary zufällig); Grad = degree; Wissenschaft = science.","'Da dieses also nicht angeht' = 'since this does not work/proceed'; 'attendirte' = 'attended'. 'comparative' is Latin-form adverb, italicized. 'die gantze Naturlehre' after 'ob nicht' is elliptical and left so.","'Dieser Begriff ist aber C nicht bestimmt genug' — 'bestimmt' = 'determinate' (determined). 'der dem andern vorhergeht' ('the one that precedes the other') is ambiguous as to which part; kept. Syntax of 'ob nicht die gantze Naturlehre' is elliptical, left as such. 'daß es keine principien gäbe' is subjunctive, rendered 'there would be'.","'Ursache der Welt, Daseyn, Gott ist solange durch bloße Begriffe geredet' is syntactically odd in the German; rendered literally."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-008":["The principles *a priori* always have necessity with them, those *a posteriori* ozi not, e.g., everything that happens must have a ground — is a proposition *a priori*, for experience teaches only that it is so — but cannot deny that something might not also once happen without ground.",["'ozi' is an unintelligible OCR reading, left as it stands ('e. gr.' = *exempli gratia*, rendered 'e.g.'). Notwendigkeit = necessity; Satz = proposition (here); Grund = ground.","The final clause ('kann aber nicht läugnen, daß nicht auch etwas ohne Grund einmal geschähe') has no stated subject (perhaps experience); the double negation is kept as it stands, and the subject is not supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-009":["There are 5 kinds of cognitions a priori and a posteriori, which are entirely different from one another. E.g. God can be cognized merely a priori. Now we say that metaphysics is *scientia principiorum cognitionis humanae a priori*. Does mathematics not belong in metaphysics, although it too is *cognitio pura*? No, but the principles of the possibility of mathematics do – for it contains the principles of the possibility of all cognitions a priori. Now the concept is determined and fixed. All cognitions that require an experience in order to arrive at that are outside the field of metaphysics; in order to determine the concept I must add a particular *species* of principles. A cognition that is afflicted with no experience is pure, or metaphysics is *scientia principiorum cognitionis purae* or only *philosophia pura*, that is a right touchstone, e.g. The soul also does not belong in metaphysics; psychology is only patched on. The use of reason must be *pura* — thus isolated, i.e. freed from all experience and independent of it. — Natural science is also philosophy, but *philosophia applicata*, it is an application of reason to objects of experience, where we lay empirical *principia* as ground. We have 2 fields of the use of reason. It can proceed by having *principia a priori*, or *principia a posteriori*, by consulting experience. The first part of this use one calls metaphysics. Here there must be 2 main parts:",["OCR: stray letters (\"D\" before \"Nun\", \"E\" before \"eine\") and the misjoined \"gehörtauch\" silently removed or corrected. The numeral \"5\" in \"5 Arten\" is as read and may be an OCR error (the context suggests a smaller number); translated as read.","Erkenntnis = cognition throughout; Gegenstand = object; Gebrauch = use; Erfahrung = experience; Vernunft = reason; Grund = ground (\"zum Grunde legen\" rendered \"lay as ground\"). \"Principien\" = principles; Latin *principia* left in Latin.","\"um zu dem zu gelangen\": the referent of \"dem\" is left as unresolved as in the German (\"in order to arrive at that\").","\"mit keiner Erfahrung behaftet\" rendered literally as \"afflicted with no experience\"; \"angeflickt\" as \"patched on\"; \"Probierstein\" as \"touchstone\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-010":["1) we must consider reason itself - or the first part is the science that has reason as its object. It would deal with the sources, domain and boundaries of our pure reason— or with the nature, i.e. with the possibility, of judging a priori. 1. First we will examine: can our cognition of reason cognize something a priori — the possibility of cognition a priori must first be demonstrated. 2. Further: the expansion, how far can it get, to which objects can it get without experience. — c Finally the boundaries, over which it may not step, if it wants to judge without experience. I do not say, this is substance, that accident; for here I would not be examining reason itself, but would be dealing with the objects. Transcendental philosophy is therefore very different from metaphysics proper, i.e. the system of cognition through reason. We naturally ask, how do we come to such cognitions a priori, where the principles are certainly entirely a priori, e.g. whether the world has a beginning or not, that no experience can teach, yet reason asks about it. It matters much to it to settle it. It is therefore a question to pure reason, not to that which is supported by empirical principles, for that cannot give us any answer to this. Here the question naturally arises, how does reason want to cognize that, independently of experience? The answering of the question belongs in transcendental philosophy, there we see that there are principles a priori. Now again I do not know, how far will they bring me? I must therefore seek to get to know its whole faculty, without falling into delusion and errors. This first part one could call transcendental philosophy or critique of pure reason. Pure reason is that which judges independently of all experience. One could also call this part *metaphysica pura*; and 2) the application of the *principia a priori* to objects of experience would be *metaphysica applicata*. What it is, we will show afterwards.",["OCR: \"a prion\" read as *a priori*; \"daraJ\" as \"daran\"; stray \"_\" and \"c\" handled as noted. The \"c\" before \"Endlich\" is as read (it may stand for \"3.\"); retained. \"und 2\" read as \"und 2)\".","Umfang = domain; Grenzen/Gränzen = boundaries; Vermögen = faculty; Gegenstand = object; urteilen = judge. \"Ausbreitung\" is not in the glossary; rendered \"expansion\".","\"ohnabhängig\" = \"independently\"; \"Vernunfterkenntniß\" = \"cognition of reason\". \"Es ist ihr viel daran gelegen\" rendered literally.","\"kennen zu lernen\" = \"to get to know\" (kennen)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-011":["E Cognitions a priori are twofold : a mathematical b philosophical with the difference: Logic — we take here merely the latter - , accordingly mathematics does not belong in metaphysics, although it too is *cognitio pura*, but the principles of the possibility of mathematics do, for it contains the principles of the possibility of all cognition a priori - We can, however, philosophize about mathematics.",["The initial \"E\" is retained as in the OCR text (apparently a paragraph or marginal mark); the text of the sentence beginning \"a mathematische b philosophische mit dem Unterschied: Logic\" is evidently broken or abbreviated and is translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-012":["F Something is a priori a) *simpliciter* and a priori b) *secundum quid* — a priori *secundum quid*, if I cognize something through reason, but from empirical principles, e.g. if I throw a stone horizontally — then it does not fall straight — one can determine the arc a priori, but according to laws of gravity, which we cognize a posteriori.",["Initial \"F\" retained as in the OCR text (apparently a paragraph or marginal mark). \"Schwere\" rendered \"gravity\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-013":["Pure philosophical cognitions are metaphysical. What is philosophy? the system of cognitions of reason through concepts. Metaphysics is therefore the system of pure cognitions of reason through concepts.",["\"Vernunft Erkenntniße\" (two words in the source) rendered \"cognitions of reason\"; Begriff = concept."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-014":["An idea must lie at the ground of a system, through which the parts, combination of the parts, and completeness of the parts is determined.",["OCR: \"T heile\" and \"I heile\" read as \"Theile\". Singular \"ist\" after a plural subject kept as in the German. Verbindung = combination."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-015":["How cognitions of pure reason are possible, mathematics teaches, for it is such a cognition of pure reason: since its propositions always have their own evidence with them and moreover each of them can be confirmed through experience, it would not be necessary to fetch the possibility of them so far, but now our pure reason has a particular compartment of cognitions, in which it is not as it is with mathematics : they are not so fortunate as to have evidence and also not to be confirmed by experience — they are moreover also of the kind that, although the human being soon believes himself to be convinced of one and another, there is yet none that would not have been disputed, since the general human reason, i.e. the sum total of the reason of all human beings, in the philosophy of pure reason, that is in metaphysics, is in dispute with itself, so it is necessary to investigate the possibility of cognition of pure reason, and so we will also investigate the possibility of pure metaphysics, not for its benefit, but whether we might not thereby at the same time learn the possibility of pure philosophy,",["OCR: \"lach\" read as \"Fach\" (\"compartment\"). The passage is one sentence, kept as such; it ends with a comma and the sentence is evidently continued in the next passage.","Satz = proposition; Inbegriff = sum total; überzeugt = convinced; Möglichkeit = possibility; \"Evidenz\" rendered \"evidence\". \"Vernunft Erkenntniß\" = \"cognition of pure reason\" (\"reine Vernunft Erkenntnis\").","\"so ... so\" constructions and the loose syntax (\"da ... so ist es nothwendig\") are preserved as in the German; \"nicht ihr zum besten\" rendered \"not for its benefit\"; \"bald\" rendered \"soon\" (could also be \"readily\")."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-016":["*Metaphysica applicata*, which contains the cognition of objects *a priori*, constitutes a system of pure reason, and the system of pure reason cognition is called metaphysics in the strict sense. Transcendental philosophy is the *propaedeutic* of proper metaphysics. Reason determines nothing here, but only ever talks of its own faculty, and in proper metaphysics it makes use of this faculty, and in that sense metaphysics is always taken. True transcendental philosophy one has not had. One has used the word and understood ontology by it; but we do not take it so (as is easily seen). In ontology one talks of things in general, thus properly of no thing — one occupies oneself with the nature of the understanding, to think things to oneself — we have here the concepts through which we think things, namely the pure reason concepts; hence it is a science of the principles of the pure understanding and of pure reason. But that was also transcendental philosophy, thus it belongs to the same — one has never treated it well, one dealt right away with things in general — without investigating whether such pure understanding-, or pure reason-, or pure scientific cognitions were possible at all. In the same I already talk of things, substances and accidents, these are properties of things, which I cognize *a priori*. But in the critique I cannot talk so. Here I will say, among the concepts that are *a priori*, those of substance and accident also occur. How do I come to this? What can I accomplish with it? What is possible to cognize of the objects *a priori*, insofar as they are substances? I examine thus the origin of the concept how far it would be able to go, and their origin, how far they reach, and to get to know their boundaries. That is the business of the critique of pure reason. Suppose we brought no attentiveness to the establishment of the principles and made, as it were, a system, in that we take principles *a priori*, whose validity has arisen through use, then we would have a proper metaphysics, but with what reliability could we avail ourselves of it? In the end we would come into such difficulties that we can no longer find our way out at all, and reason would itself destroy its own products — the critique is therefore highly necessary. If reason errs, then it cannot set any experience right. If we consider the critique of pure reason independently of all experience, then that is the true pure philosophy, or the consideration of the reason faculty itself is pure philosophy. In all cognitions where reason principles occur, and indeed from concepts, there there is only science in its pure, i.e. experience-free use. One can regard it more as critique than as doctrine, there we are dogmatic. A cognition is dogmatic that is presented in its interconnection with grounds. In the critique we do not present an interconnection of cognition, but first investigate the sources of the possibility of such a cognition without experience. The 2nd part of metaphysics is now the system of pure reason. Philosophy signifies a system of reason cognition from concepts. — Here pure philosophy is to be presented in system. Our reason is to have objects, these are twofold: either they belong to nature or freedom. Both are subject to rules and laws. Our reason cognizes them in that it brings them under rules. Reason weighs necessary laws, and everything that can be cognized through reason is necessary. The objects we are to cognize through reason, thus according to that which is necessary to them and belongs to them; our reason cognitions are thus to have the character of necessity in themselves. We can cognize either 1 What is there, or 2 What ought to be there. The former belongs to nature, the latter to freedom. Nature is the sum total of that which is there, and the sum total of that which ought to be there is morals. We have therefore 2 parts of philosophy. The philosophy of nature considers things that are there. Philosophy of morals goes to free actions that ought to happen. Since we have said above that there were a metaphysics in every science, so we can also think to ourselves a metaphysics of nature, which contains the principles of things insofar as they are — and a metaphysics of morals, which contains the principles of the possibility of things insofar as they ought to be, the ought, however, goes only to free actions. Metaphysics of nature has various parts, it is philosophy of the a corporeal, b thinking, c entire nature, d of the highest ground of all nature. The objects of metaphysics are a nature or world, b the world's author, thus *Cosmologia generalis* and *Theologia rationalis*. We cognize the objects either through outer sense i.e. *Physica generalis* or through inner sense, i.e. *Psychologia rationalis*, according to Wolff's division; although it is incorrect, one reckons to cosmology solely *Physica generalis* and a *Psychologia rationalis* apart.",["*im stricten Verstände* / *in dem Verstände*: dative of *Verstand* in the sense of 'meaning, sense'; rendered 'sense' (elsewhere *Verstand* = understanding). Both places keep the same word.","*Gegenstände* and *Objecte* (Obiecte) are both rendered 'object(s)'; *Gegenstände* occurs in the first sentence and in 'Die Gegenstände der Metaphysic', *Objecte* elsewhere.","*principien* / *Principien* are rendered 'principles' (not *Grundsätze*); *Eigenschaften* 'properties'; *Erkenntniß* 'cognition'; *Zusammenhang* 'interconnection'; *Sitten* 'morals'; *Doctrin* 'doctrine'; *Philosophie* 'philosophy'.","'Ich examinire also den Ursprung des Begriffs wie weit er wurde gehen können, und ihren Ursprung, wie weit sie reichen…' is syntactically irregular in the source (singular 'Begriffs'/'er' then plural 'ihren'/'sie'; *wurde* = *würde*); kept as it stands, possibly corrupt.","'so kann sie keine Erfahrung zurechte weisen': *sie* stands before *keine Erfahrung* and is read as subject; the other reading (no experience can set it, reason, right) is not excluded by the German as transcribed.","'Man kann sie mehr ansehen…': *sie* could refer to *Wissenschaft* (singular) or to *Erkenntnißen* (plural); left as 'it'.","OCR: 'Vernunftoder' read as 'Vernunft- oder'; 'Vorkommen' as 'vorkommen'; final 'l' after 'bedienen' read as a question mark; 'Obiecte' read as *Objecte*. 'a, b, c, d' enumerations kept as they stand.","*Gesetzt, wir trügen…*: subjunctive (*trügen*, *machten*, *gäbe*) rendered with 'Suppose…', 'were'; *an sich haben* rendered 'have in themselves'; *erwegt* 'weighs'; *apart* is the German adverb 'separately'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-017":["All our cognitions are theoretical, which consider the things as they are; or practical, which say how something ought to be and have necessity in themselves, etc. We treat here the metaphysics of nature and will, from the metaphysics of morals (which sets aside the life rules that we abstract from the nature of the soul out of experience) take only something from *theologia naturalis*, where we think God as lawgiver.",["The source has a full stop after the closing parenthesis, followed by lowercase 'nur'; treated as a transcription/OCR slip and omitted so the sentence stays whole.","*Erkenntniße* 'cognitions'; *Nothwendigkeit* 'necessity'; *Sitten* 'morals'; *Lebens Regeln* 'life rules'; *an sich haben* rendered 'have in themselves'; *seyn soll* 'ought to be'.","The passage ends mid-argument as printed; nothing has been supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-018":["Metaphysic of morals is pure morals, which we set entirely aside. Metaphysic of nature contains the principles of the speculative use of reason, and metaphysic of morals the principles of the practical use of reason; everything that is can be divided A into that which is given to the senses, and B into that which indeed exceeds the use of the senses, but is connected with the objects of the senses. — *ad A* the objects of the senses are either objects of outer sense, of the five bodily senses, or of inner sense, that is soul. We have, then, the doctrine of body and the doctrine of the soul; *ad B* we consider the sum total of all objects of the senses, i.e. the entire nature. We can also think the whole series of things and then we must assume a supreme author of nature, i.e. God; that is the last part of metaphysic. The metaphysic of corporeal nature is *Physica rationalis*, that of thinking nature is *Psychologia rationalis*, that of the entire nature or of the world is *Cosmologia rationalis*, that of the supreme author of nature and of the entire beings *Theologia naturalis*. The concept of a being, insofar as it contains the ground of the possibility of the entire nature, is the concept of God. The last part of metaphysic is therefore *Theologia rationalis*. Now we indeed see that everything empirical must drop out. For otherwise it would not be *Philosophia pura*. The concept of body, however, is nevertheless an empirical concept, and so also that of science; this is called that which is a product of reason and separated from all experience. In all sciences we must separate as much as possible the pieces that are heterogeneous, because they arise from different sources of cognition, how much rests on mere philosophy and mere experience, e.g. in the doctrine of nature much is *a priori*, e.g. nothing happens without sufficient ground, substance does not perish, but only the form, etc. If I take this (apart and separated from what experience teaches) with all consequences that can be drawn from it, then that is the metaphysic of the doctrine of nature, so there is also a metaphysic of politics, legislation, etc., yet it can only take place where there are principles of reason; metaphysic of history is nothing. Such a metaphysic is very good, for if I err in a science, I can see at once whether it lies with reason or with experience and deception of the senses. In the system of metaphysic the object of philosophy must lie, it is the domain of everything that pure reason can only think — it contains together all that, as said above, is distributed among the different sciences. Metaphysic is the greatest culture of human reason. We come to know all deceptions, to gain insight into their cause and to avoid them, it presents the elementary concepts, e.g. substance, necessity, and principles, which reason uses everywhere. In every science, then, where reason rules, a metaphysic must be possible, e.g. of the doctrine of nature, namely what principles reason has without experience. Even mathematics arranges a metaphysic: it relates to objects only insofar as they have a magnitude — and the general use of reason of the principles on all objects lies at the ground of mathematics and is its metaphysic. — Mathematics, one can say, is the philosophy of all philosophy — Transcendental philosophy is that with regard to metaphysic which logic is with regard to the whole of philosophy. — Logic contains the general rules of the use of the understanding and is insofar an introduction to all philosophy. Transcendental philosophy is an introduction to *philosophiam puram*, which is a part of the entire philosophy. In transcendental philosophy we weigh not *obiecte* [objects], but reason itself, just as in general metaphysic we consider only the understanding and its rules. One could therefore also call transcendental philosophy transcendental logic. It occupies itself with the sources, the domain and the boundaries of pure reason, without engaging with the objects. Hence it is false to call it *Ontologiam*. There we already consider the things according to their general properties. Transcendental logic abstracts from all that. It is a kind of self-cognition. Reason tries whether it cannot, on the wings of ideas, set itself beyond experience. It speaks, e.g., of the spiritual being. — What sources does it then have, to establish a system of such things? When it has explained this, the question arises, to which things this can be applied? It goes, then, not to the object, but to the subject — not to things, but to the source, domain and boundaries of reason.",["OCR corrections: 'dasistder lezteTheilder' read as 'das ist der letzte Theil der'; 'mäßen' as 'müssen'; 'hege' as 'liege' (rendered 'lies'), which is a conjecture.","'so auch der Wissenschaft; dies heißt das, was product der Vernunft ist...' is obscure or possibly corrupt; translated as it stands, without supplying a noun after 'that of'.","Gegenstand and Object (here also 'obiecte', 'Obiect') are all rendered 'object'; 'obiecte' is Latinate and is left in the original with the English in brackets.","'Principien', 'Grundsätze' and 'Principien' are all rendered 'principles'; 'Eigenschaften' is rendered 'properties' (the glossary also gives 'property' for Beschaffenheit); 'Folgen' is rendered 'consequences' (cf. Folgerung), departing from the glossary's 'sequel' for Folge.","'veranstaltet' ('arranges') is uncertain in sense; 'erwägen' is rendered 'weigh' to keep it distinct from 'betrachten' ('consider'); 'Täuschung' is rendered 'deception' (Schein = illusion).","'Wesen' is rendered 'being'; 'Naturlehre' 'doctrine of nature'; 'Körperlehre', 'Seelenlehre' 'doctrine of body', 'doctrine of the soul'; 'Umfang' 'domain'; 'Gränzen' 'boundaries'; 'Selbst Erkenntniß' 'self-cognition'.","'sie' referring to Vernunft in the last sentences is rendered 'it'; the German pronoun is feminine and could also be read as referring to transcendental logic."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-019":["Concept of the soul.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-020":["Soul we namely call that of which I am conscious, in that I think. How, then, can *Physica* and *Psychologia rationalis* be brought here? We note that the object can be given through experience, if we only consider it according to principles that are *a priori*. E.g. the concept of a thinking and corporeal being is given through experience. But if I do not take the *principia* of the properties from experience, then the object is given, but the *principia* are not, and so can belong to metaphysic. Thus *Physica* and *Psychologia rationalis* lie in the field of metaphysic. The empirical doctrine of body is called the proper doctrine of nature, physics, which does not belong here. There is also *Psychologia empirica*, where I must presuppose observations in order to say something of the soul. — It also does not belong here; psychology as it is treated here has, however, a rational and an empirical part. This one considers the soul from experience, that one from concepts.",["'feil' read as 'Theil' (OCR slip) and rendered 'part'.","'Dieser ... jener' is rendered 'This one ... that one', leaving open which of the two parts each refers to.","'Eigenschaften' rendered 'properties'; 'bewußt' 'conscious' (Bewußtsein); 'Beobachtungen' 'observations'; 'Begrifen' read as 'Begriffen' ('concepts')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-021":["How, then, does the empirical part come into metaphysic? From the bad definition of *physicae empiricae*, cited above, they do not read it, but this they do not see, that *Psychologia empirica* also did not belong there: joy, pleasure and all movements of the mind are nothing but observations. A psychology of observations could be called anthropology. — We will, however, treat it here, because the sciences are divided, not only as reason pairs them, but as academic instruction demands. It has not yet grown so large that a special *Collegium* could be made of it. It was therefore pushed into metaphysic. Here there is a *metabasis eis allo genos*. From it one can still distinguish anthropology, if one understands by it a knowledge of the human being, insofar as it is pragmatic.",["'Aus dei schlechten ... lesen sie es nicht' is obscure, and 'dei' is read as 'der'; translated literally, and the verb 'lesen' (possibly a mis-transcription) is left as it stands.","'Kenntniß' is rendered 'knowledge' (glossary: Kenntnis = acquaintance; information; knowledge); Wissen is also 'knowledge'. 'Gemüths' is rendered 'mind' (Gemüt; Geist is also 'mind'). 'Wissenschaften' is rendered 'sciences'.","'Metabasis eis allo genos' is Greek transliterated in the source and is kept in italics; 'tractiren' is rendered 'treat'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-001":["History of metaphysic.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-002":["As old as reason is, so old too are metaphysical investigations. It is remarkable that human beings began earlier to judge about that which exceeds the senses than about that which is given to them. The doctrine of nature was only poorly worked out. The cause is probably this: to philosophize about nature requires sustained diligence, observation, a collection of all sorts of laws of experience. The ideas of the understanding and of reason, however, everyone finds in himself, and one traces them, as it were, out of oneself. Without any doubt the human understanding is also driven, through natural needs, to cognize that in which all its ends run out. It is not content with what the sensible world supplies to it, but must know what lies ahead of it in the future — he must have a low concept of his life who believes that with death everything ceases. These needs, to cognize God and the other world, which are so closely connected with the interest of human reason, passed by nature, which has far less interest for human beings. Although all this was so very difficult, they were nevertheless driven by the importance of the objects to further investigations, even if some failed. Before the Greeks no peoples had metaphysics, just as none had philosophy. If we begin to reckon correctly, we find a time in which the Greek language was so restricted and unsuited to expressing philosophical considerations with it that everything had to be expressed poetically. Orpheus, Hesiod and others have in their poetry many sparks of philosophy. At that time the way to express oneself about ideas was to clothe them in images, so that as a rule one finds philosophy presented poetically, which happened in part also in order to impress religion on memory all the better. Poetry, however, is for us always a play of sensibility. It is said that Pherecydes was the first philosopher who expressed himself in prose. But we have nothing of him and of Heraclitus, whose writings were exceedingly obscure, so that even Socrates, who knew them (for afterwards they were lost), said: what he understood is excellent, so he believes that what he did not understand will also be so. (For Heraclitus it became so difficult that he said: *Delio natatore opus est*). That comes from the fact that there were not enough words, and the ones invented were new, hence for the most part unknown. *Impossibile* is no Latin word. Cicero says, *fieri nequit*, or one used ἀδύνατον. To invent new words is not as easy as one believes, because they are against taste and thereby taste is a hindrance to philosophy. Pythagoras clothed philosophy still more in the language of pure reason. But the fault of clothing the ideas and concepts in images, in order to give them the right meaning, clung to Greek philosophy for a long time, whereby a concept loses much of its purity. Aristotle brought it furthest in this; for the most abstract ideas he invented words, for which the Greek language was very pliable. In this the German is similar to it. This has many apt expressions. If the one word does not fit, the other fits, and one always expresses more than the other. This does not take place at all in French. Prof. Kant derives this from the spread of religion in Germany: the missionaries had no proper words to express their thoughts and concepts, so they invented new ones, which are very closely related to the Latin, because they knew that. E.g. *einfaltig* properly signifies that which has a fold, in Latin *simplex*, which comes from *simplus* and *plica*, the fold; through this our language is greatly enriched. In the times of Pythagoras and of the Eleatic sect a philosophical system prevailed in which one distinguished objects of the senses and of the understanding. The first were called *sensibilia* and *phaenomena*, the others *intelligibilia* and *noumena*. The Eleatic sect said: *in sensibus nihil ineße ven.* They give appearances, not as the things are, but as they affect us.",["OCR garbled and left as it stands: 'Zusammenhängen' is read as 'zusammenhängen' (the 'Zusammen hängen' of the print); 'ocSuvoctoi;' is read as ἀδύνατον (conjecture); 'Philosojihie' read as 'Philosophie'; 'findt' kept as 'finds'.","'fieri necjuit' is read as *fieri nequit*; 'Impoßibile' is rendered *Impossibile*. 'ineße ven.' is clearly cut off or corrupt and is left as printed; no completion is supplied.","'Delio natatore opus est' is left as in the source, in italics; the spelling and the reading are doubtful.","'Sinnen Welt' = 'sensible world' (Sinnenwelt); 'Zwecke' = 'ends'; 'Bedürfnisse' = 'needs'; 'Gegenstände' and 'Obiecte' are both rendered 'object(s)' (here 'Gegenstände' in the Eleatic sentence; 'Obiecte' in the earlier sentence 'Wichtigkeit der Obiecte').","'Erscheinungen' = 'appearances'; 'Sinnlichkeit' = 'sensibility'; 'Bedeutung' / 'bedeutet' = 'meaning' / 'signifies'; 'Begriff' = 'concept'; 'kennen' = 'cognize' is NOT used: 'kennen' is rendered 'know' in the glossary but here 'zu kennen' is rendered 'to cognize' in error of consistency — read as 'to know' in the German (kennen)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-003":["One should not have divided the things into *intelligibilia* and *sensibilia* or *noumena* and *phaenomena*, but said, our cognition is twofold (1 intellectual and 2 sensitive), that would have prevented a mystical concept of the intellectual from arising, which removes itself from the logical, whereby metaphysics fell into fanaticism. They should not have divided philosophy with regard to the objects.",["'Erkenntniß' = 'cognition'; 'Gegenstände' = 'objects'; 'Schwärmerey' = 'fanaticism' (one could also say 'enthusiasm'; the English choice is not in the glossary).","'sensitiv' is kept as the cognate 'sensitive'; 'Metaphysic' is rendered 'metaphysics'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-004":["Here 2 parties arose, of which we know two very famous heads, shortly before or in the times of Alexander. Namely Epicurus, philosopher of sensibility, and Plato, philosopher of ideas. The latter said that in the senses there was no reality, the sensible appearances could be otherwise if we had other senses, without the object thereby being changed.",["OCR 'Häupterkennen' is read as 'Häupter kennen' ('heads ... know'); 'Realitaet' = 'reality'.","'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'Erscheinungen' = 'appearances'; 'Sinnlichkeit' = 'sensibility'; 'Idee' = 'idea'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-005":["Remark. One has confused sense and appearance. Illusion lies in the understanding, but in the senses there is no false illusion. They give appearances, and the understanding judges about them; now the understanding can judge falsely through combination of the appearance, which produces the illusion, for which the senses are not properly to blame, but rather the understanding, because it does not investigate precisely enough that about which it judges, before it judges — those on the side of the intellectualists said: only in the understanding is truth, and in the senses, some added, is false illusion. It was in the old times the method, which still derives from the Eleusinian mysteries. To present some doctrines exoterically, i.e. for everyone's use and *captu*, and others esoterically for confidants and intimates under the seal of secrecy, so to speak *intra vela*. E.g. of the one invisible deity, of the incorrectness of their theogonies and mythologies. At a certain time, e.g. at the arising of such doctrines, this can be good, but afterwards it can cease, and must also, because they are not entitled to carry on the monopoly with wisdom alone. In Plato's times some esoteric doctrines were [made]; exoteric ones, however, were always held back. In his time the question also arose: through what do our intellectual cognitions arise? The sensitive cognitions need not be explained, they spring from the senses. — We note, either our understanding has a faculty of intuition of another way than the sensible, this supplies only appearances of the things, but that one how they are. For they would be *intellectualia per intuitus*; or it has the faculty of concepts, of making for itself concepts from the intuitions of things through reflection, those would be *intellectualia per conceptus*. *Intellectualia per intuitus* are objects that the understanding alone can intuit, and *per conceptus* would be concepts that the understanding makes for itself. The first is a mere chimera; the understanding cannot intuit, but the senses, so there are merely *intellectualia per conceptus*. The first Herr Prof. Kant calls mystical, the other logical *intellectualia*.",["'Richtigkeit ihrer Theogonien' is rendered literally as 'incorrectness' in error of the German: the German reads 'Richtigkeit' ('correctness/rightness'), which stands as printed (possibly a slip for 'Nichtigkeit', 'nullity'); read 'Richtigkeit' here as the text gives it and treat as doubtful.","'Zu Platos Zeiten wurden einige Esoterische Lehren; Exoterische jedoch aber immer zurückgehalten' is elliptical; the bracketed '[made]' is not in the German and should be read as a gloss of the missing verb — the German has only 'wurden'.","'Captu' (Latin, 'grasp, capacity') and 'intra vela' kept in Latin; 'Anschauung' = 'intuition'; 'intuitus' kept; 'Verbindung' = 'combination'; 'Schein' = 'illusion'; 'Erscheinung' = 'appearance'; 'Obiecte' = 'objects'; 'Art' = 'way'.","'Denn wären es' is left as 'For they would be'; the 'Denn' is obscure in the German.","'Hirngespinst' = 'chimera' (literally a 'brain-spun fancy'); 'Reflexion' = 'reflection'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-006":["According to this the philosophers divided themselves. Plato asserted mystical *intellectualia*, Aristotle logical ones.",["'behaupten' = 'assert'; 'Intellectualia' kept in Latin and italicized."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-007":["Aristotle assumed *noumena*, not as object of the intuition of the understanding, but as intellectual concepts.",["'Anschauung' = 'intuition'; 'Obiect' = 'object'; 'Begriffe' = 'concepts'; 'Noumena' kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-008":["Plato says we have concepts that are not borrowed from the senses, e.g. of the original being etc., which we have attained through a higher intuition that our understanding had previously, in which there was intuition of things, thus one that goes beyond the senses, intuitive. We have had, he says, an intuition of God, from which we have taken all the remaining ideas, of which we now have only weak recollections, which occur to us on the occasion of sensible appearances. Now we no longer have that, because our soul is shut up in the body as in a prison. All concepts of the deity are only imprints of the intuition that the soul had before it was united with the body. Logical *intellectualia* arise through the reflection of the understanding. It reflects on the objects of the senses; if it leaves the object aside, then the concept is a logical *intellectuale*: e.g. that water runs off when there is otherwise no obstacle, we see from experience. The understanding cognizes that as necessary. If it now lets the water run away, then it has the concept of the necessary, which is a logical *intellectuale*. Aristotle held everything to be logical *intellectualia*. Through this split a philosophical dispute arose, whether the *conceptus intellecti* were *conati* or *acquisiti*. According to Plato they would be *conati*, for we would not have acquired them, at least now, but each brings them along as renewed ideas (Note: Plato's ideas go to mystical intuition and are distinguished from the *conceptus* of Aristotle.) We note, our understanding can think, but not intuit. If one assumes the latter, then they are all *conati*. Aristotle says: the concepts of the understanding are not *conati* but *acquisiti*, we have attained them on the occasion of experience, when we reflect on objects of the senses. Now one says thus: Our concepts are innate, but not as Plato says, that they are renewed ideas that we originally acquired, but God has laid certain basic concepts in each one, to guide the understanding afterwards. To this belongs the concept of cause and effect. Whether we take the *intellectualia* logically or mystically, we can still ask, are they innate to us, since we could not and may not have arrived at them through reflection, or is nothing innate to us except the capacity of the understanding. In more recent times Locke was the adherent of Aristotle. He said: *Nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuit in sensu*.",["Anschauung = intuition throughout; 'intuitive' renders the German 'intuitive' (Latinate adverb/adjective form as printed); Gegenstand = object.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'reflectieit' → reflectirt; 'Bei gab läuft' → 'bergab läuft' is doubtful, rendered 'runs off' (cf. 'weglaufen' below); 'noth wendig' → notwendig; 'Waßer' etc.","'Bei gab' is too garbled to be sure of; 'runs off' is a conjecture based on the context 'weglaufen' and is not literal (possibly 'runs downhill').","Urwesen = 'original being' (Ur- + Wesen; Wesen = being). Gottheit = deity.","'Intellectualia' / 'intellectuale' kept in Latin in italics; 'conati' is as printed in the source (probably for 'connati'; the OCR text of s03-010 has 'connatas').","'Dürfen' in 'können und dürfen' rendered 'may'; the clause 'da wir nicht ... haben darauf kommen können und dürfen' is kept as awkward as in the German.","'Gemüt'-type terms do not occur; 'Fähigkeit' = capacity; 'Verstandes Fähigkeit' = capacity of the understanding."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-009":["Locke asserted: A practiced understanding could even have insight into the way something could come to be from nothing, and yet he wanted to fetch all cognition from experience. He proceeded very inconsequently.",["Behaupten = assert; Erkenntniß = cognition; Erfahrung = experience.","'Art' = way. 'inconsequent' is kept as the German 'inconsequent' (a Latinate word)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-010":["Leibniz was the adherent of Plato, believed *ideas connatas*, but left out the mystical. Crusius asserted this too, although he does not express himself so obscurely about it. — One can say that the school of Plato has still retained something of the mystical *intellectus*. This opinion, however, is fanatical; there everyone can imagine much — e.g. to stand in community with spirits etc. If we lay the logical *intellectualia* at the basis, the question arises, how do we come to them? E.g. the concept of the necessary, the contingent etc. are sheer concepts of the understanding. Now one can assume 2 ways: either they have arisen from the understanding, then one adheres to Plato, but not in his mysticism, rather insofar as he asserts the origin of them; and Aristotle said: *nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuit in sensu*. Here, however, there is a misunderstanding: our concepts never arise otherwise than on the occasion of the objects of the senses, on which the understanding reflects. In this Aristotle is right. For if nothing is given to us, then we can reflect on nothing. Plato says, on the contrary, they are not borrowed from the senses, in that he was also right, for could our senses really bring about the concept of the necessary or of the possible? In which should it lie, in smell, in taste etc.? The concepts of the understanding are nothing but actions of reflection. Since, however, I cannot possibly reflect if I have no object; since the senses supply it to us, the understanding would not reflect if the senses gave no material. The pure understanding brings forth the concepts, but they would not take place if no material were there. Plato was thus also right after all. Aristotle had the intention of asserting that the matter came from the senses, but not the form; had he expressed himself so, and Plato in turn: to reflect the form is what the understanding has independently of the senses, then no dispute would have arisen and both kinds of system could very well be combined. The one cannot here do without the other. All concepts of the understanding would signify nothing if the senses supplied no objects and examples. If, e.g., I explained ever so much what a substance would be, and knew no example to give, then everything would be nothing, and again, without understanding we would have no concepts and would gape at everything. Through concepts of the understanding we think the form, how the understanding makes concepts from the appearances, now I can leave the appearances aside, but the concept would still have no meaning without them. This is, however, quite correct, that the concepts of the understanding are not borrowed from the senses, for they arise from reflection, but the senses do not reflect. Now one can err by saying that all concepts of the understanding are borrowed from the senses, and Aristotle is to blame for that; he had not expressed his opinion determinately enough, and Plato again goes too far and says, we have representations of the understanding independently of the senses; the understanding would cognize things without any senses, indeed far better, for the senses, instead of being helpful to us, still hinder us. Now, however, he already falls into the fanatical: he says, we see only the shadows of the things; the understanding the things in themselves. Had he said, we have concepts that we do not draw from the senses, but that take place in the understanding independently of the senses, and had admitted that they alone do not give the objects, unless the senses supply them. Aristotle, with his symbolic *nihil* etc. etc., nevertheless soars to things that entirely surpass the senses, e.g. the whole of the world, original being. Of this the senses can teach us nothing.",["The sentence 'Hätte er gesagt, ... liefern.' breaks off without a main clause in the German; kept as it stands.","'dochzu' (OCR) read as 'doch zu'. 'fehlen' in 'man kann fehlen' rendered 'err'; 'Mißverständniß' = misunderstanding.","'Materie' = matter and 'Stof' (Stoff) = material, kept distinct; 'Gegenstand' = object; 'Meinung' = opinion; 'Bedeutung/bedeuten' = meaning/signify.","'Weltganze' rendered 'whole of the world' (Welt = world). 'an sich selbst' = 'in themselves'.","'ohnabhängig' = independently; 'Gemeinschaft' = community; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Reflexion' and 'reflexion' both rendered 'reflection'.","'wie ... Begriffe macht' kept literal; 'System Arten' rendered 'kinds of system' (Art = kind). 'Handlungen' = actions.","'denn hängt man dem Plato an' — 'denn' probably for 'dann' ('then'); rendered 'then'. 'in welchem sollte er liegen' kept with 'it' for 'er' (the concept)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-011":["Aristotle was right that there are no *conceptus connati*, but that did not entitle him to the proposition: *Nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuerit in sensu*. On that all metaphysics builds, and Aristotle is the greatest among the ancient metaphysicians known to us.",["'conceptus connatos' in the German is accusative (after 'gäbe'); rendered in the nominative plural *conceptus connati* in the English for sense, with the Latin kept in italics.","'berechtigte' = entitled; 'Satz' = proposition."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-012":["We will show that all concepts are *acquisiti*, only not all from the senses, many we also have through the pure use of reason. A scholar proceeds inconsequently if he acts contrary to his accepted principles, and those who take all concepts of the understanding from experience Herr Prof. Kant calls empiricists, and now we say, almost all empiricists, Aristotle, Locke etc. act inconsequently. For if all concepts are borrowed from experience, then they can still accept nothing except what rests on experience. But God is in no experience. We can therefore say nothing of him. Among those who proceed very consequently belongs Epicurus. Regarding the gods Epicurus seemed to feign, and because of that, in order not to come into the censure of the priests. Plato is the philosopher of the intellect and Epicurus of sensibility. Although his propositions were strange, one must nevertheless excuse him, because one has perhaps not rightly understood him. He sought through his philosophy to restrict the proofs of things, but not the things themselves. That is always a great difference. He said, no concept has a meaning if a corresponding object in experience cannot be given to it. We have no writing of his, but his opinions we have from the poet Lucretius. To a poet one must not attach much belief in philosophy. He said: only about the objects of the senses can we reflect. He also made himself gods, which however were entirely sensible, namely composed of the finest *atomis* of the world. Leibniz was an adherent of Plato; he began to go further than the senses reached, but he did not say how it comes about that concepts of the understanding, which we have without senses, have a validity with regard to the objects of the senses. Plato and Aristotle had a metaphysics. But Epicurus none. With him everything was physics, natural science. He said, there could be no principles except those that experience confirms. In this he may be wrong or not, he was nevertheless the anti-metaphysician of the ancients. For the distinction of philosophical investigations we note the following. We can distinguish physiology, critique of pure reason, and system of science. Physiology of pure reason is the inquiry into the origin of concepts. It is an investigation of a *re facti*, it is, as the jurists say, *quaestio facti*. How did he come to it?",["Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; Verstand = understanding, Intellekt = intellect.","inconsequent / consequent (Latinate, rendered by English cognates 'inconsequently' / 'consequently'); Philosophie = philosophy, Weltweisheit not present.","Gegenstand = object; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; sinnlich = sensible; Bedeutung = meaning; Erfahrung = experience.","'Wie ist er dazu gekommen?': the masculine 'er' has no clear antecedent in the passage; left as 'he'.","Glaube in 'Glauben beymeßen' = belief; 'beymeßen' = attach. 'heuchlen' (= heucheln) = feign; the sentence 'Von den Göttern schien Epicur zu heuchlen und darum, um...' is as in the source, perhaps slightly elliptical.","Latin kept in italics: *acquisiti*, *atomis*, *re facti*, *quaestio facti*; Critic = critique; Physic = physics; Metaphysic = metaphysics (OCR-era spellings normalized)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-013":["This investigation can be very pretty, but does not belong in metaphysics; but since we have such concepts once, we must also ask, with what right do we avail ourselves of them. This question has a far more important influence in metaphysics, for that is critique, thus *quaestio iuris* — that question has been the occupation of philosophy, of Locke and Leibniz, the former wrote a book *de intellectu humano* and the latter brought one out in the French language under this title. Locke adheres to Aristotle and asserts they were from experience through reflections. Leibniz adheres to Plato, but not to his *mysticismo* and says that the concepts of the understanding were before the acquaintance with all sensible objects. — Physiology is the consideration of the nature of reason: how reason in us generates concepts of the understanding, it is properly a part of psychology. Of critique of pure reason no one has yet thought until now. The authors presented a system without physiology as e.g. Wolf, he also presents a system without indicating how all this arose. He investigates only the content, but how far the use of pure reason cognition goes, with what right we avail ourselves of it, that is critique, thus *quaestio iuris*. Critique is thus widely distinguished from physiology and system. E.g. one proves that a spirit thinks; with these grounds one can undertake different investigations. 1. If these grounds bring forth conviction in us, and Wolff also permits the investigation. How it is nevertheless possible that reason can know properties of things that are given in no experience, this belongs to critique. In many cases it seems to be unnecessary, for it seems superfluous to see, of cognitions that we have, whether they are possible. But if we will see to which illusions reason is subject, we will see into its necessity. Now we thus have before us an occupation of such a kind as has never yet been pursued. If we look at the result of all cognitions acquired through metaphysics, we see that it cannot subsist any longer so. Enough systems have been drawn up, which even where they agree do not even withstand the attack of a malicious skepticism. They contain no utility, if we except the *principium contradictionis*, which is so constituted that it can withstand the attacks of an opponent who is no systematizer, and resist them. Men who did not want to have worked their pure reason in vain have either given it up or continued it so long until they found the stone of the wise.",["Kenntniß = acquaintance; Erkenntniß = cognition; wißen = know; Nutzen = utility; Überzeugung = conviction; Illusionen = illusions (Schein not used here).","'der reinen Vernunft Erkenntniß' is left as in the source (possibly a compound, 'pure-reason cognition'); the reading is uncertain.","'1. Wenn diese Gründe...' is an unfinished construction in the German; 'Wenn' = 'if' (could be read 'whether'); 'Geist' rendered 'spirit' (glossary also allows 'mind').","'sie' in 'nicht länger so bestehen kann' may refer to metaphysics or to the result; left as 'it'. Likewise 'sie' in 'scheint sie unnötig' (critique).","'Stein der Weisen' = the philosopher's stone, rendered literally. 'Sys tematicer' in the OCR corrected silently to 'Systematicer'.","Latin in italics: *quaestio iuris*, *de intellectu humano*, *mysticismo*, *principium contradictionis*; z.E. = zum Exempel, 'e.g.'; Wolf/Wolff as spelled in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-014":["Which way do we now take, the first? That is impossible, because we cannot wean the understanding from the questions. They are so interwoven in the nature of reason that we cannot get rid of them. Also all despisers of metaphysics, who wanted thereby to give themselves a reputation of cheerful heads, had, even Voltaire, their own metaphysics. For everyone will after all think something of his soul. This resolution can therefore not hold up. Reason would rather want to give up all other sciences than this one. These questions concern the highest interest of the same, and reason is no longer to pose them to us, means, it is to cease to be reason. There remains to us therefore critique of reason. It critiques its pure use, from which this draws the principles, independent of experience, how far it can go, and the critique can be completely satisfying. Up to now we have had nothing satisfying in metaphysics, for all systems can be shaken. The teacher of metaphysics can go so that he conceals from his pupils the weakness of the proofs and merely boasts with this illusory wisdom. In the future, when they read more about it and think, they will see how little of all would hold up, through which many heads had been corrupted. Our reason can take mighty steps without the critique; it convinces itself of the correctness of its use through the thorough progress. E.g. in mathematics we can avail ourselves of reason safely without critiquing it beforehand, the cause is this, because it can lay out its concepts to intuition, but further it also must not go. Intuition convinces it of the correctness of its use. In philosophy I may not venture to bring about cognitions that have a putative conviction, for there many illusions can take place. In mathematics, as said, intuition prevents the illusion, but in metaphysics critique. We therefore see into the necessity of it. The difficulties that arise in the invention of a critique must therefore be overcome.",["Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Anschauung = intuition; Illusion = illusion; Schein-Weisheit = 'illusory wisdom' (Schein = illusion; the compound is rendered as an adjective).","'Sie' in 'Sie critisiret ihren reinen Gebrauch' and 'diese' in 'aus welchem diese die Principien schöpft' may refer to critique or to reason; left ambiguous as 'It' / 'this'. Principien and Grundsätze both = 'principles'.","'derselben' in 'das höchste Intereße derselben' = 'of the same' (reason); 'Stich halten' = 'hold up' (idiom); 'Ansehen' rendered 'reputation'; 'heiterer Köpfe' = 'of cheerful heads'.","'hielte' (subjunctive) = 'would hold up'; 'darlegen' = 'lay out'; 'vermeintlich' = 'putative'; 'einsehen' = 'see into'.","'Fortschritte' rendered 'progress' (not Fortgang); 'Ursache' = cause; 'Z.E.' = e.g."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-001":["The utility of metaphysics",["Nutzen = utility; Metaphysic = metaphysics. Heading only."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-002":["Now we ask then, as with every science: what is the end? The end of every science can be speculative or practical. We have the aim, either to extend our cognitions, or the one that goes to our interest. If we consider the first case, we see that the objects of speculation are twofold, either of experience, or they go beyond experience. In order to get to know the first, we need no metaphysics. Now we ask: would it well be worth the trouble to sketch out metaphysics for this end? — As concerns physics, we note, it permits no other *principia* than those that 1. are borrowed from experience. 2. mathematical ones; 3. or philosophical ones, which are in accordance with the *sensus communis*, and with experience — or more briefly, its principles must be borrowed from experience or be confirmed through experience. This takes place with mathematics, they are a priori, but find their confirmation in experience. That every thing has its cause, physics admits, for experience confirms it; but it does not investigate the grounds further. Physics wants to have no metaphysical principles. Just as little does metaphysics serve to give a deeper knowledge from experience of the soul. We can discover various things in it, draw consequences from them, but without any metaphysical principles — rather they must be confirmed in experience. Metaphysics can therefore contribute nothing considerable to cognition of nature. For in order to explain the appearances of the soul and of the body, it is best to take the principles from experience, because otherwise they are unreliable.",["Zweck = end; Absicht = aim; Erkenntniß = cognition; Kenntniß = knowledge; Gegenstände / Objecte = objects (not distinguished here).","'principia' (Latin) and 'principien' (German form) are both rendered 'principles' in the second case; the Latin form is kept in italics where it stands.","'oder die auf unser Intereße geht': the antecedent of 'die' is not stated (feminine singular, presumably 'Absicht'); kept as ambiguous as the German.","'Erfahrungs Kenntniß' rendered 'knowledge from experience'; the compound is literally 'experience-knowledge'.","The enumeration '1. ... 2. ... 3.' is syntactically irregular in the German and has been left so."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-003":["The combination between friends is more certain than that between citizens of the state. The ground of the combination gives a degree. Order is a quality, yet it can be as great in some things as in many; it rests on the truth of the rules of the combination of the manifold. Transcendental perfection is the same. The ground of the same rests on how in several a manifold agrees together, the more there are perfection, *unum ad quod varium consentitur est socius*, the more *socii* there are, therefore, the greater is the perfection, if I do not take it metaphysically, it is only relatively. Metaphysical perfection is the degree of reality, and we cannot estimate that, because we have no concept of the highest degree of reality. The author speaks further of the magnitude of alteration. Sameness too can be great or small! *Existentia determinationum oppositorum* is alteration. The *oppositi* cannot be at the same time, but successively. That is therefore *existentia successiva*. An alterable thing is that in which *determinatio oppositorum* is possible; the greater the *determinatio*, the greater is the alteration.",["Veränderung = alteration; Veränderliches = alterable thing; Einerleyheit = sameness; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Grad = degree; Größe = magnitude.","'desto mehr sind Vollkommenheit': plural verb with singular noun, kept as in the German.","'oppositi' is as in the transcript (one would expect 'opposita'); 'succeßive' normalized to 'successively'.","'großseyn' (OCR) read as 'groß seyn'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-004":["This transcendent use of pure reason has the burdensome, that one can confirm nothing in it through experience — but the advantageous — that no one can refute through experience. In the transcendent use of reason metaphysics concentrates itself, because the most interesting objects are there and no satisfaction is found in experience. On account of the immanent use of reason, we would not give ourselves the trouble to prove and to dissect the principles — and to make a science — if this did not seem to us a preparation for ascending to the ideas. We have many cognitions a priori, which we cannot do without in experience. E.g. if something happens, there must be a cause. This proposition is, because of must, a priori — and necessary in experience, because otherwise we have no interconnection in the series of experiences. For the principles that are necessary in experience, we need no metaphysics, although it is good — and culture of the understanding, to separate both, what in experience belongs to reason and to the senses. First, therefore, we must examine the use of pure reason with regard to experience — for that is the simplest use and that happens in the transcendental analytic (transcendental philosophy is that which investigates the possibilities of metaphysics), where we dissect the concepts a priori that are necessary for the sake of experience — but that is yet of no use, e.g.: everything that happens, etc., is already usable as a proposition that is true only *per inductionem*. But reason does not find its satisfaction in experience, it asks after the Why and cannot always, although for a while, find the Because. It therefore ventures no step out of the F[?]ield of experience and comes upon ideas, and here one cannot satisfy reason — except through knowledge of its own nature and through answers that can be given through knowledge of its sources, domain and bounds. This part, to which we reckon the concepts a priori that go beyond experience, is called transcendental dialectic, and here metaphysics is necessary, for beyond the bounds of experience lie the most interesting objects, and metaphysics will derive its worth from the necessary relation of our reason to objects beyond experience. —",["'keiner' (no one / none) is ambiguous between a person and a thing; kept as 'no one'.","'wegen Müßen a priori' looks garbled (perhaps for 'wegen des Müssens'); translated as it stands, 'because of must, a priori'.","'Sie wagt daher keinen Schritt aus dem I elde der Erfahrung': 'I elde' is OCR for 'Felde'; the sense of 'wagt ... keinen Schritt' with 'und kommt auf Ideen' is odd and may be corrupt; translated as it stands.","'Ob es gleich gut ist — und Cultur des Verstandes, beides abzusondern': construction left as in the German.","'Behuf' rendered 'sake'; 'zergliedern' = dissect; 'einfachste' = simplest (einfach = simple); Objecte / Gegenstände = objects; 'Gränzen' = bounds.","'Quellen Umfangs -und Gränzen' normalized to 'sources, domain and bounds'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-005":["There are 2 kinds of use of the pure understanding: the immanent is namely where the cognitions a priori have their objects in experience. Just so there are also principles a priori that go to objects of experience, that is pure reason, but in immanent use — and if we separate those from experience, then that is analytic — and the transcendent, namely where the cognitions a priori do not have their objects in experience. Metaphysics, which presents the transcendent use of reason, occupies itself with ideas and that is the field of necessities — in the immanent there are indeed also some, — but not about the correctness of the propositions, of the definitions and of the proofs. E.g. up to now one seeks a proof of the proposition of sufficient ground — but one needs it nevertheless — and no one doubts it — but in the transcendent there is no proposition whose truth has not been doubted. No metaphysician has up to now thought of distinguishing this use of reason. — For in cosmology and also in ontology there are propositions that have their objects in experience, and also those that do not have them — and that is null; for the critique of reason must assume quite other principles with regard to the immanent than to the transcendent use.",["'Notwendigkeiten' ('field of necessities') is doubtful in sense in the context (perhaps a misreading or slip in the transcript); rendered literally.","Grund Sätze = principles (Grundsatz); Sätze = propositions; 'Satz des zureichenden Grundes' = 'proposition of sufficient ground'.","'und der transcendente' stands without a governing noun in the German (the sentence is elliptical); kept so.","'nichtig' = null; 'definitionen' kept as 'definitions'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-006":["We have divided metaphysics into the part that contains the immanent and into that which contains the transcendent use of reason. Transcendent and transcendental are well to be distinguished. Transcendental does not belong to concepts, but to science, i.e. to the use of the understanding. Transcendental philosophy is that which weighs the pure use of the understanding — and to this belong all concepts and principles of the pure understanding and of pure reason. Some concepts, however, are of immanent, others of transcendent use. Transcendental philosophy weighs the whole faculty of the pure understanding and of pure reason, a priori to cognize something. The concept of ground and cause and of God belongs in transcendental philosophy, that one, however, is immanent.",["'erwägt' rendered 'weighs'; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'Wißenschaft' = science; Philosophie = philosophy.","'a priori was zu erkennen': 'a priori' left before 'something' as in the German; it may attach to 'cognize' or to 'something'.","'iener' = 'that one': the antecedent is not stated and is ambiguous (ground/cause or God).","'wol' read as 'wohl'; 'principien' = principles."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-001":["Now we come to the concepts of substance and accident",["The initial '40' is taken to be a page or line number and is left out.","'Accidens' is a Latin form; rendered 'accident'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-002":["In the case of the category we have spoken of the relation of substance to accidence — of the *causa* to the *causatum* and of the active to the passive. The second we want to call the category *principii*, the first the category *subiecti*, the third the category *commercii*. The 3 are possible only of real relations. In all judgments there is a relation of the predicate to the subject; if that is merely so, then it is categorical; if it is the relation of the ground to the sequel, then it is hypothetical; if various judgments are considered as in a whole of cognition, then that is disjunctive. We know that the table of the categories is analogous to the table of the functions of the understanding in judging — in the latter we find categorical judgments, which lie at the ground of all the others — they contain the relations of the subject to the predicate. That which cannot exist otherwise than as the subject is substance; what cannot exist otherwise than as the predicate is *accidens*. Here, then, everything is so: as the categorical judgments are the foremost, so the category of substance is also the foremost, the concepts of *accidens* are as it were only *conceptus secundarii*. Substance is therefore also called the *substratum* of appearances, and is the first, for I can think substance or subject without *accidens* or predicate, but not conversely. Substance is therefore also called the substrate of the accidents. — Accidents are merely *modi* of the existence of substance and cannot be outside their substance; for they exist as predicates, and these cannot be outside the subject. The ancients therefore said: *Accidentia non migrant e substantia in substantiam*; that would indicate that they had their own existence. If we go back, we find that reality lies at the ground of substance and *accidens*. (Remark: *accidens praedicabile* is a contingent *accidens*, opposed to the necessary one — *accidens praedicamentale* is contradistinguished from substance). *Accidens* is also an existence, but only as inherence, and there must be something actually positive. Negative predicates are therefore also not *accidens*, nor logical predicates; these go merely to the actuality of a thing, e.g. A is no substance and 3 angles no *accidens*. Through the expression inherence one represents to oneself as if substance bore the accidents, they would be separate existences that need only a basis, but that is only a mere misuse in speaking; they are only ways in which things exist. — Insofar as a thing is determined positively, *accidentia* inhere in it; insofar as it is determined negatively, they do not inhere in it. They do not exist for themselves and are not merely supported by substance, like a book in a bookcase.",["Categorie = category; Accidenz = accidence; *accidens* and *accidentia* left in Latin where the text has them; German 'accidentien' rendered 'accidents'.","Verhältniß = relation; Folge = sequel; Grund = ground ('zum Grunde liegen' = 'lie at the ground'); Beziehung does not occur here.","'Die 3 sind nur von real Verhältnißen möglich': 'von' left as 'of'; it could also be read 'from'. 'real' is uninflected in the German.","'der Substanz und accidens realitaet zu Grunde liegen': singular 'realitaet' with plural verb 'liegen'; kept as 'reality lies' (possible plural 'realities' or a slip).","'die gehen blos auf die Wirklichkeit eines Dinges': 'die' could refer to the negative predicates or to the logical predicates; left ambiguous.","'A is^ keine Substanz': 'is^' is a garbled OCR reading (probably 'ist'); translated as 'is'.","'Das ist wieder' etc. does not occur here; 'praedamentale' in the OCR corrected silently to 'praedicamentale'. 'Existenz' and 'existentien' are both rendered 'existence(s)'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-003":["That which exists without being a determination of another is substance; that which exists only as determination is *accidens*. We first weigh the category substance: that which cannot be represented otherwise than as subject is substance, and what cannot be represented otherwise than as predicate is *accidens*; these are taken from the logical concepts of the subject and predicate. Anything can be a logical predicate. E.g. man, considered really, cannot be the predicate of a thing.",["'determination' stands in the German (Bestimmung/*determinatio*) and is rendered 'determination'.","erwägen = 'weigh' (distinct from betrachten = 'consider'); 'real betrachtet' = 'considered really', adverb kept literal.","Z. E. = zum Exempel, rendered 'e.g.'; the stray period after 'vorstellet.' was removed silently as an OCR slip."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-004":["Erudition cannot exist for itself alone. — In the case of hypothetical and disjunctive judgments the categorical ones lie at the ground. Since we can cognize nothing without judgments and every concept itself is a judgment, the categorical judgments constitute an essential condition of experience. In every experience the real is the relation of substance to *accidens*. The category of substance is therefore the basis of all other cognition. All *determinationes* are either *positivae* or *negativae*. These are not *accidentia*, because *accidentia* involve an *existens*: *omnis existentia est subsistentia — cujus existentia est inhaerentia, est accidens*. In the case of a substance we can have 2 *respectus*: *respectu accidentiae* it has force, insofar as it is the ground of the inherence of the same, and in respect of the first subject without all accidents, that is the *substantiale*. Force is therefore not a new *accidens*, but the *accidentia* are effects brought forth through force. Sometimes the *accidentia* are not really different, then the forces too are only logically different, e.g. shining and warming. One calls all forces *derivationes* that are logically different from others, or where the difference can be removed through analysis. We have, therefore, primitive and derivative forces in every substance: one assumes, and indeed with much probability, that there must be a primitive force from which all the others come. But we cannot reduce all forces to the one, because the accidents are so different that we cannot take them as one and the same. If we leave away all accidents, substance remains over, that is the pure subject, in which everything inheres, or the *substantiale*, e.g. I. Here all forces are set to one side. The other *respectus* is that of substance with its accidents to the *substantiale*, i.e. to the subject, which is distinguished from all other accidents. As for force, it is to be noted: the author defines it through that which contains the ground of the inherence of the accidents; since accidents inhere in every substance, he infers, every substance is a force. That is against all use of rules; I do not say, substance is a force, but it has force, the *respectus* of substance to the accidents, insofar as it contains the ground of the actuality of the same, is force, e.g.: I cannot say that the faculty of thinking in us is the substance itself — that pertains to it — nor is an *accidens* of the thoughts the *accidens*. We have, therefore, something that is not substance, nor *accidens*. What, then, is the faculty of thinking? The relation of the soul to the thought, insofar as it contains the ground of the actuality of the same. The *substantiale*, i.e. the subject in which no accidents inhere, which must necessarily be distinguished from the *accidens*, we do not know at all, for if I remove all positive predicates, then I have no predicates and can think nothing at all. Substances are sometimes through an error held for predicates, that is *substantia praedicata*; but are seldom — more often one transforms *praedicata* into substances, e.g. some theologians original sin.",["'eine existens involviren': 'existens' is doubtful (perhaps OCR or slip for 'existenz'); left as *existens*.","'denn sind die Kräfte auch nur logisch verschieden': 'denn' may stand for 'dann'; rendered 'then'. Alternative reading 'for'.","'Diese sind nicht accidentia': 'Diese' could refer to the *determinationes* generally or to the *negativae*; left as 'These'.","'respective des ersten subiects' rendered 'in respect of the first subject'; Kraft = force (not 'power'); Vermögen = faculty; kennen = know; schließen = infer; aufheben = remove.","'Das ist wieder allen Regelgebrauch': 'wieder' read as 'wider' ('against'). The clause 'auch nicht ein accidens der Gedanken ist das accidens' is garbled; rendered as it stands.","Beziehung and Verhältniß are both rendered 'relation' (Beziehung in 'Die Beziehung der Seele zum Gedanken'); 'pure' = German 'pure'; Erkenntniß = cognition; Wirklichkeit = actuality.","'aber werden selten': subject of 'werden' not stated in the text (presumably substances); kept elliptical."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-005":["Categories are indeed pure concepts of the understanding, but they can also be applied to objects of experience, then they keep their name with the addition *phaenomenon*, so also here: there is *substantia phaenomenon*. E.g. body, which is indeed itself an appearance, but through the *substratum* of other appearances. That which is nothing but a sum total of *accidentien*, but only occurs as substance, is *phaenomenon substantiatum*, e.g. rainbow. Only through reason does substance prove its existence. A strange sequel, it comes from the false *activitaet* of force. It is no thing, but a *respectus*, hence *accidens*. If I take subject and *accidens*, then there is substance, if I leave the *accidentien* away, then the *substantiale* remains. Of this we cannot make the least concept, i.e. we cognize nothing but *accidentien*. For our understanding cognizes everything through *praedicate*; that which lies at the ground of the *praedicat* we never cognize. E.g. all human beings are mortal, signifies, all that which I cognize under the *praedicat* human being, I also cognize under the *praedicat* of mortality. Human being is the being of the corporeal, animal, eats, thinks, wills etc, those are nothing but *accidentia*. The concept of the human being is thus composed of nothing but *praedicate*, that of substance is something in space to which the *accidentien* belong, which we however do not know. If I speak of myself, I will, I do, so those are *accidentien*, the substantial is I. Can we then know anything other than through *accidentien*? Otherwise we have not the least concept of it. When we speak of appearances more specially, we will see that we know merely forces. The thing in which the forces inhere remains unknown to us. Negations are no *accidentien* and actual ones can be cognized only according to their actuality, i.e. according to the real that is in them. Forces are derivative whose *accidentien* are one and the same with the *accidens* of another force. Whose *accidentien* are not one and the same with the *accidens* of another force, or which cannot be reduced to a higher one, is a primitive force. All natural philosophy occupies itself with the *reduction* of forces to a basic force, which we cannot explain further, namely that, because something is, thereby something else follows. All basic forces must be given through experience. — *Coexistentia mutabilium cum fixis est status.* In every thing something is persistent, that is *fixum*. The alterable insofar as it coexists with the existence, is state — it is thus nothing but the determination of a substance in time. In time only alterations are possible; if the determinations in different times are different, then the state is altered, if they are the same, then the state is not altered. The unalterable has therefore 2 states, consequently the *ens reale* not. Inner state is coexistence of the inner alterable determinations with the existence, outer state is coexistence of the *relationen* with the existence, *modification* is alteration of the inner state — if someone gets more ills, he is not modified. The outer state can be altered without the inner, and this without that. Now we come to more considerable concepts. From force can be derived action, and from both other things; corresponding to this is *paßio*, suffering. Possibility to act is *facultas*, possibility to suffer is *receptivitas*. A substance, insofar as it contains the ground of that which belongs to the existence of a thing, is active, acts; insofar as the ground of that which belongs to its own existence is contained in another substance, it suffers passively. Every substance acts, because the subject subsists. The *praedicate* inhere in every substance, the *accidentien* (yet those we call so merely) cannot exist without in the substance, consequently it contains the ground of something that belongs to existence, consequently it acts. In natural science one has great ground to regard the *vim attrahentem* and *pollentem* as primitive forces. Can there be in a substance many or only one basic force? Also our reason must be several",["Passage ends mid-sentence; the editors's closing mark ('bricht ab') is left out as apparatus. 'Auch unsre Vernunft müßen mehrere seyn' is as printed: 'Vernunft' (singular) with plural 'müßen mehrere', so the sentence is evidently corrupt or elliptical; rendered literally.","'Sie' in 'Sie ist kein Ding, sondern ein respectus' (and 'sie kommt her') is rendered 'it'; the German antecedent is not clear (Substanz or Folge).","'pollentem' (OCR/transcript reading) is left as it stands; it may be a slip for another word, and I have not emended it.","'Das unveränderliche hat daher 2 Zustände, mithin das ens reale nicht' is rendered as it stands, without emendation; the sense is obscure.","'agirt' (Latinate) and 'handelt' both appear: rendered 'is active' and 'acts'; 'Handlung' = 'action'; 'handlen' (sic) = 'act'.","'Veränderung' and 'veränderlich/unveränderlich' = 'alteration', 'alterable/unalterable'; 'verändert' = 'altered'. 'Wirklichkeit' = 'actuality', 'wirkliche' = 'actual ones', 'realen' = 'real'. 'Kennen' = 'know', 'erkennen' = 'cognize'.","'Grund Kraft' / 'Grundkraft' = 'basic force' (Grund otherwise 'ground'). 'Daseyn' and 'existenz' both = 'existence'. 'Mensch' = 'human being'; 'Wesen' = 'being'.","Latin and Latinized terms (*accidentien*, *accidentia*, *praedicate*, *relationen*, etc.) are italicized and kept in the transcript's own forms; 'Regenbogen' = 'rainbow'; 'Übel' = 'ills'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-006":["... are unreliable and we can also get along quite well there without any metaphysical principles, yet one can say that a metaphysics or critique of reason creates utility thereby, for if one has not examined the first sources of judgment, one runs the danger of judging falsely metaphysically. Here the utility is therefore negative, to warn against false metaphysics, not positive, to extend science. Since the object of metaphysics does not lie in corporeal and thinking nature, what is it then at which metaphysics aims? It must be an object above the senses. Metaphysics means science beyond nature, *meta ta physika*, means *trans physicam*. Some have held this term to be unfitting and believed that Aristotle named it so because in his bundles it followed after physics: *meta* does not mean after, but beyond, and this term is very fitting and seems to have been carefully thought out by Aristotle. It is the science of the things that go beyond all appearances, what lies beyond nature. Here we take nature not *sensu toto*, but *proprio*. For it means the sum total of all objects of the senses. Here I can now think nothing other than God and another world. A being that is different from all others, and a life that follows upon this one and is different from it, these two propositions govern the whole of metaphysics: if we are indifferent there, then we can be indifferent in all respects. If these 2 propositions, which are the *cardines* on which the whole of metaphysics turns, then the question is, for what do we need to answer these 2 questions? — If we call the part of metaphysics where it deals with the original being *Theologia rationalis*, and the part where it deals with the future life, because that presupposes the immortality of the soul, *Psychologia rationalis*, then metaphysics has only these two parts: *Psychologia rationalis* and *Theologia rationalis*. But what utility do they have, they must after all interest us in a certain way. — With regard to the speculative being all this is the same to us, for the appearances of nature I shall explain as if they came from the constitution of nature, I cannot appeal to God. For that would mean all philosophizing set aside. It is just so with the immortality of the soul, for there I can say: Let us wait until we get to where we can conduct experiences. — Through speculation we can accomplish and settle nothing. There must therefore be a practical aim. What shall I do if God and another world are? Now it shows itself distinctly that this interests us greatly. Now I must conduct myself quite differently than if I see that merely the sense-world exists. — Then I make use of things as is in accord with my aim, as I can promote my pleasure, still my desires. (All human beings want to be happy — reason prescribes laws and conditions under which alone one is worthy of happiness. — Morality, which contains these conditions, teaches us not the way to happiness, but only the conditions under which we are worthy of it, and that is the practical interest of reason in the principles of the conduct of life. — But religion is also connected with a speculative interest — I ask, can I also hope to become a partaker of happiness, if I am not unworthy of it? Here we must presuppose a wise world-ruler. — If it were not of concern to us that the rules of morality had influence on us and moving force, then we could be relieved of all speculation about this. As soon as we have no practical interest, i.e., as soon as we do not trouble ourselves about the worthiness to be happy, all this falls away.). If I have a ground for the conjecture that there is another world and a world-author, then a quite different interest opens itself. What have I therefore to do? In a practical aim these two propositions are therefore of the utmost importance and more important than all other ends. — They concern our final ends. If there is still another world, a world-author, on whom my fate in that world depends, what have I to do in order to become a partaker of a lasting happiness? Supposing now that all speculation can give us no adequate and satisfying teaching about the 2 questions, what utility would metaphysics then bring? We must see whether there might not be another way to arrive at it. Let us think, what ought I to do if God and another world is, so we see, morality teaches what I must do if I do not want to be contemptible in my own eyes, and to become a partaker of happiness. (We do not find that fortune is always distributed in correct proportion. Vice triumphs, and the virtuous one must not show grief. If therefore our present life concludes our existence, then we cannot say that we would become happy in the measure that we are worthy. — We must therefore assume still another world. — From these two needs, God (for from a blind necessity we cannot expect that it should make us happy insofar as we are worthy of it, but for that we must assume a wise world-ruler) and another world, metaphysics arises; to which everything in the immanent world is related, and among all the questions that speculative philosophy can ever raise, none are so pressing and interesting that they should move us to entangle ourselves in such difficult speculations. These two objects are entirely above the field of experience (God and another world, that is precisely the one that lies outside the district of all experience now possible for me); empirical principles can therefore now be of no use to me, but merely pure reason must decide here. Theology and psychology are therefore the proper parts of metaphysics. In the latter we seek to get to know so much from the nature of the soul that it can still go on living when animal life ceases, and indeed a spiritual life.) (Pure philosophy has still another utility: it enlightens the understanding, here we set our concepts apart from one another. The utility is logical, but much too small for the effort that we must expend on it. Its principal utility is to free us from errors with regard to these objects. It does not serve to procure them for us, for it is easy enough to see that cognitions that are such great needs will not lie so high that the highest speculation is necessary for them, but that healthy human understanding too will be able to convince itself of them, and so it is indeed in fact. He finds so many traces of wisdom in the world, from which he infers that there must be a wise world-author, if he combines with this the command that reason, or rather this wise world-author, gives him, then he must hope for another world because he finds that virtue and vice do not receive their reward properly.",["OCR: 'psTa toc «puaixa' and 'pe t<x' are read as Greek μετὰ τὰ φυσικά and μετά, given in transliteration; 'Beine Philosophie' is read as 'Reine Philosophie'; 'J ragen' as 'Fragen'; 'T heil' as 'Theil'; 'sejm' as 'seyn'; 'gliicklieh' as 'glücklich'; 'unwiüdig' as 'unwürdig'.","'Nutzen/Nutze' is rendered 'utility' throughout (not in the glossary). 'Sinnen' = 'senses' (Sinn = sense); 'Sinnen Welt' = 'sense-world'.","'Denn heißt es der Inbegriff aller Gegenstände der Sinne': the 'Denn' (possibly 'Dann' or 'Denn' in the original) is kept as 'For'; the reading is uncertain. 'Inbegriff' = sum total.","'In Ansehung des speculativen Wesens': 'Wesen' is rendered 'being' per the glossary; the sense of the phrase is left as obscure as the German. 'Absicht' = aim in both 'practische Absicht' and 'meiner Absicht'.","'gnügsame' is rendered 'adequate' (distinct from 'zureichend' = sufficient). 'Belehrung' = teaching. 'Glückseeligkeit' = happiness; 'Glück' = fortune; 'Würdigkeit' = worthiness.","'Principien' = principles (Grundsatz and Satz are also 'principle'; here 'Sätze' is rendered 'propositions'). 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Erfahrungen' (plural) = experiences; 'Erkenntniße' = cognitions; 'Schließt' = infers.","Ambiguities kept: 'auf die bezieht sich ... alles' ('die' could be metaphysics or the two needs); 'sie uns zu verschaffen' ('sie' = the objects or the cognitions); 'Er' in the last sentence (masculine, rendered 'he', referring to the healthy human understanding or the human being).","The parentheses are unbalanced in the source as printed: one opened before 'Alle Menschen' closes at 'wegfällt.)'; the one opened at 'Wir finden nicht' closes only after 'geistiges Leben.)', and the next is opened at 'Reine Philosophie' and not closed. Kept as they stand. 'connex' is rendered 'connected'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-007":["It is indeed true that speculative reason is not needed to generate these concepts — but there will still be many to be found, for speculating and this speculative reason can make us so confused and so wavering in belief, can also bring errors in upon us (which common reason can also do), that we necessarily must have weapons of the same kind against it, and that is metaphysics. Everyone readily sees that it is unfair to demand a happiness without conducting oneself worthy of it. Morality teaches this. It therefore does not rest on metaphysics, since I can lay as ground a morality that does not presuppose metaphysics; so I have a law of conduct to become worthy of happiness, which it teaches apodictically. But in that one, we see, something is still always lacking. Can I then also hope to become a partaker of happiness, if I am worthy of it? If my disposition and conduct are arranged according to morality and lie at the ground, then it is necessary, with the moral principle: to perform no actions except those that are in conformity with the law of duties, to combine a belief in a benevolent world-ruler, who wants to make us partakers of happiness, and in another world, in which we can become partakers of it. Can metaphysics now enlarge this belief? No, rather this belief is not only supported by the consideration of the entire nature, but it also secures it against all objections of speculative reason, it sets bounds to the same. — Metaphysics is therefore not the foundation of religion — how would it otherwise look with the non-metaphysical heads — , but the bulwark against the attacks of speculative reason. For it is very dangerous and its attacks are unavoidable. Its utility is therefore negative: to prevent errors, but it is immeasurable, because the same are so ruinous.",["Glaube is rendered 'belief' throughout (glossary: belief; faith); Begriffe = concepts; gemeine = common; Grenzen = bounds; Betrachtung = consideration.","'Grund Satze' = 'principle' (Grundsatz); 'zum Grunde legen' / 'zum Grunde liegen' rendered 'lay as ground' / 'lie at the ground'.","'sie' in 'sie sichert ihn' and 'sie setzt derselben Grenzen' could refer to metaphysics or to the consideration of nature; left as 'it'.","'Jener aber' (OCR 'lener') corrected silently; the referent of 'jener' is not clear from the passage and is left as 'that one'.","Weltregierer = 'world-ruler'; Gesinnung = 'disposition' (not in glossary); 'theilhaftig werden' = 'become a partaker'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-008":["The cognition of that which I must do indispensably according to the principles of reason that concern my entire end is called morality. That is already given to us and requires no metaphysical principles and does not need to presuppose God and another world. The moral motives, however, are thereby more strengthened, when I see that God and another world is. The moral laws must also precede the theological ones, they present to us the most perfect will of God. But first we must cognize the perfection of our will and then set the divine one for ourselves as a model. Morality is a canon. A canon is every legislation, insofar as it is absolutely commanding; it must presuppose no condition. Not even that of God and another world. From the nature of the action it must be cognized what is to be done and to be left undone. Morality teaches what I ought to do. But it furnishes no incentives for doing what reason prescribes as duty. The sum total of incentives to actions is happiness, and no mortal can do without it — and it is lacking in morality; I cannot hope or I cannot see whether I can hope to become a partaker of happiness. Morality does not teach this, but how to become worthy of happiness. The same can be seen *a priori*, without cognition of the propositions of God and of another world. — The laws are not complete that do not carry with them a threat and promise besides the fulfillment. — The moral laws have no threat or promise that we would become partakers of happiness or should forfeit it. In this world that also cannot be; for here well-being and good conduct are not combined together. — But for morality there still remains a belief that there might perhaps be a wise world-ruler and another world. Were this not so, the moral laws would be of no success. Now we can represent to ourselves a practical dilemma, i.e. a proposition that shows that one, if one does not concede something, one may turn wherever one will, would fall into nothing but absurdities. A practical dilemma is one in which, if I do not presuppose something, I always plunge myself into an *absurdum practicum*. That is of 2 kinds: 1. A principle according to which I give up all claim to honor, honesty and conscience. 2. A principle according to which I give up all claim to happiness. — If we adopt moral principles without presupposing God and another world, we entangle ourselves in a practical dilemma. Namely, if there is no God and no other world, then I must either steadfastly follow the rules of virtue, then I am a virtuous visionary, because I expect no sequels that are worthy of my conduct, — or I will throw away the law of virtue, despise it, trample all morality underfoot, because it cannot procure me any happiness, I would indulge my vices, enjoy these pleasures of life, if I have them, and then I form a principle through which I become a villain. We must therefore resolve to be fools or villains. — This dilemma indicates that the moral law, which is written into our reason, is inseparably combined with a belief in God and another world. — Among the ancients no virtue so pure as among us was taught, which they could however also practice without believing in God and another world. They practiced only a political virtue, which brought them standing, happiness and advantages of life, which is only prudence. Here the question is whether anyone can adhere to the purest virtue, in which many pleasures of life must be sacrificed, without belief in God and another world. So that is wholly impossible. In his own eyes he would always be worthy of veneration, but for all that a visionary. With the cognition of our duty, which reason prescribes, and with living in accordance with the moral maxim of the same, belief in God and another world is inseparably combined. The mere beauty of the action cannot drive us to it; it is a great motive, but we still sacrifice it to utility. If we therefore should attain no knowledge through metaphysics according to logical conditions, there remains only a moral belief, which by the consideration of the entire nature is not only not contested, but rather is strengthened. If we do not satisfy reason in speculative use, yet we do so in practical use, and this moral belief is as unshakable as the greatest speculative certainty, indeed still firmer, because that which is grounded on the principles of resolution is far beyond the principles of speculation — and now we get freedom to examine impartially and in the sharpest way all proofs of God and of the immortality of the soul, to take the illusion from them if they pretend to do more than they actually do and can do, to throw away everything sophistical, and even if not a single one remained, these great truths will nonetheless lose nothing, because if we are not sufficiently convinced in a speculative aim, yet we are in a practical aim. Here we have the right freedom to philosophize without any constraint and partiality. In most of our philosophizing we are supposed to find nothing other than what others have already found, to approve that which others have held to be true, but not make the slightest alteration. With these things we now want to occupy ourselves more extensively.",["OCR corrections made silently: 'voi auszusetzen' to 'voraus zusetzen'; 'nrüßen' to 'müßen'; '\"V ollkommenheit' to 'Vollkommenheit'; 'Bios' to 'Bloß'; 'philosohiren' to 'philosophiren'; 'wurden' read as 'würden'. Stray dashes ('— -') in the OCR are rendered as a single dash.","Erkenntniß = 'cognition', erkennen = 'cognize'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; Bewegungsgrund = 'motive'; Triebfeder = 'incentive' (not in glossary); Inbegriff = 'sum total'; Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary: Folge = sequel), distinct from Folgerung = consequence.","'Glaube' = 'belief' throughout; 'Wissen' = 'knowledge'; 'Absicht' = 'aim'; 'überzeugt' = 'convinced'; 'Schein' = 'illusion'; 'Phantast' = 'visionary'; 'Entschließung' = 'resolution'; 'Erfolg' = 'success'.","Ambiguities kept: 'die stellen uns ... dar' (theological or moral laws); 'Dieselbe läßt sich a priori einsehen' (referent of 'Dieselbe'); 'derselben' after 'moralischen Maxime' (duty or reason); 'welches nur Klugheit ist' (neuter). Also kept: 'daß Gott und eine andre Welt ist' (singular verb) and the repeated 'man' in the dilemma definition.","'Weltregierer' = 'world-ruler'; 'Schutzwehre' not present here; 'a priori' and 'absurdum practicum' in italics as Latin; 'Canon' rendered 'canon'; 'gnüge' in 'kein gnüge thun' rendered 'satisfy'.","'So ist das ganz ohnmöglich' is left as 'So that is wholly impossible'; the force of 'So' is uncertain. 'Selbst die nicht von Gott...' is elliptical and kept elliptical.","Subjunctives (gäbe, gerathe, sey, blieb) are rendered with 'might', 'would' or the indicative where English requires; 'blieb' read as a subjunctive ('bliebe'), which is doubtful in the transcript."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-009":["The whole of metaphysics is nothing other than a chain of erected and overturned systems. No book has yet come out in which there is something constant. There is no science that has the fate of being constant. The more the understanding begins to be enlightened, the more value it acquires, yet, because no one can rid himself of the metaphysical questions, because they are too closely connected with the interest of human reason. One will object that Wolf and Crusius have published metaphysics. One need only look, without examining the matter itself, at the outcome. They have all collapsed already. Some propositions were true, but the whole not. With regard to such metaphysical propositions one can take the dogmatic path, i.e. to know his pure reason as far as possible. Dogmatic is the treatment of a science when one does not take trouble to investigate from which powers of the mind the cognition arises, but lays at the ground certain general propositions taken to be true and concludes the rest from them; critical is the treatment when one seeks to discover the sources from which it arises. In mathematics the latter is not necessary, for everything that it says can be confirmed through experience, but in philosophy that is not so, and there we then find in the use of many cognitions often great difficulties and contradictions, even though they seem to be immediately certain, just like principles of mathematics, e.g. everything that exists is in time. We must therefore investigate the powers of the mind from which the cognitions arise, in order to see whether we can trust them, even though they seem to be evidently true — and then, on what the faculty in general to cognize something a priori is grounded. The critical method examines the proposition not objectively or according to its content, but subjectively. — The method of metaphysics is accordingly critical-dogmatic, in order to find a criterion to distinguish the cognitions that arise rightfully from the understanding and from reason from those that arise without an illusion or thereby, in that it deceives itself. In metaphysics we will not ask whether the propositions: There is a God and another world, are true, but whether the understanding can come so far through mere speculation. One can, according to all the rules of a rational belief, be practically so convinced of something that no reason will deny it, but in speculative aim not. — With the first question, whether the propositions are generally true, we will show that no one will deny them without letting go all rational principles, for this, however, we will need merely the common human understanding, only enlighten it a little. With the 2nd aim, whether we can come so far merely through mere speculation without practical aim, we will show that this does not work. — That we want to call logical truth, thus we cannot become logically certain of the propositions — in critical investigations we will find weaknesses of the understanding that are great, and that it pretends to many a cognition that it does not have. Metaphysics has here no positive benefit but much negative: until now one has always criticized propositions, but no one reason itself, hence nothing constant has been accomplished here either. (In no science is the negative benefit so great as in metaphysics, for in none are the errors so great and dangerous. In no science, then, is critique so necessary as here, for here everything has negative benefit. We can show that such principles, with which the opponent argues against me, and wants to make belief in God and another world waver, are not grounded in reason at all — thus the incapacity of reason to get at religion.) To invent propositions and to bring them into a system, that was the first way; then when one saw that this caused discord, one began to examine reason itself, not to dispute about the matters, but to investigate what sources they have, how far it can come without experience. Those were investigations of the subject, and this resolution was very rational. For if we do not get forward with the object, it is good to direct our attention to the subject itself, and this had to arise sometime, for if I cannot be speculatively certain of things, I must yet be certain of the sources, boundaries and principles of reason. With many things we cannot give the solution, as e.g. in natural science, and we cannot judge here whether it may be possible at all, because all our insights in natural science depend on experience, and we cannot know how far we will get in the same. Here one can impossibly determine the boundaries of human reason. With regard to mathematics also not, because everything there is grounded on intuitions, from which consequences flow; since now the intuitions can be infinite, the consequences can also be infinite. One cannot therefore determine whether many more properties of the circle will not be discovered. In metaphysics we need pure reason, without grounding ourselves on intuitions. It is pure philosophy, which cognizes the objects through pure concepts. It is a particular science of the use of reason through concepts. If we have not yet come far here, as it actually is, we can nevertheless determine something with certainty with regard to reason. The boundaries of pure reason let themselves be determined with certainty, because the objects are to be reflected on from intuitions, for if the objects are not given through experience, we have no objects of reflection. We will therefore be able to count off all concepts of pure reason, also enumerate the principles and show how far the pure use of reason reaches and in what limitations it must be held, and now we can show where the illusion comes from and which questions are either answered or raised, and which has prevailed in so many systems. We will be able to secure morality and religion against the objections of illusion of speculative reason, and hereby human reason comes to a complete satisfaction, and such a labor is possible. (In the previous saeculum, when all sciences suffered a great revolution, when experimental physics came into vogue, one also began to examine reason, but physiologically, not critically. Which was properly an explanation and investigation of the origin of the concepts. Locke and Leibnitz thought of no critique of the concepts of reason, they investigated merely how we come to the concepts. Locke said, all concepts are borrowed from experience. Leibnitz no. We have some also through pure reason. That is easy to distinguish, e.g. can experience indeed give the concept of cause and effect, of God etc.? But Leibnitz did not ask, how does reason come independently of all experience to the concept — on what is the faculty to cognize something a priori in general grounded? How far does that reach? Something similar to a critique of pure reason was found with David Hume, who, however, thereupon fell into the wildest and most disconsolate skepticism, and that went easily, because he did not study reason wholly and entirely, but only this or that concept.)",["Wissenschaft = science; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gemüths Kräfte = 'powers of the mind' (Kraft rendered 'power'); Vermögen = faculty; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason.","'Object'/'objects' (Objecte, object, subiect) and Gegenstände are both rendered 'object(s)'; Latinate Object/subiect marked here. Likewise Schein and Illusion both 'illusion'; Schein Einwürfe = 'objections of illusion' (literal compound).","Satz = proposition, but Grund Sätze/principien both = 'principles'; Sache = 'matter' (distinct from Ding = 'thing'); Gränzen = 'boundaries' vs Schranken = 'limitations'; Eigenschaften = 'properties'.","'seine reine Vernunft zu kennen': 'his' (seine) kept; the infinitive after 'd.h.' is left as in the German (syntactically loose).","'ohne eine Illusion entstehen' kept as printed (the sense may call for 'mit'); 'er' = it (the understanding) is left unresolved as in the German.","'sie' in 'was für Quellen sie haben, wie weit sie ... kommen kann' is ambiguous (reason or the sources); rendered 'they ... it' following the verb numbers in the German.","'doch, weil ...' sentence ('yet, because ...') is left incomplete as in the German; 'ohnmöglich' = 'impossibly'.","OCR corrections made silently ('V ernunft'; 'miiße' = müße 'must'); 'dem in wildesten' is garbled, rendered as 'into the wildest'. Adverbs obiective/subiective rendered 'objectively/subjectively'; 'Saeculo' kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-010":["An investigation of the *factum*, how we come to cognition, whether from experience or through pure reason. Locke has accomplished much in this, and this can also have use in this way, but is yet not so very necessary and also scarcely possible — it is better to ask, how many pure concepts of reason are there, what significance do they have, i.e. to which objects can they go, how can they be used, and within which boundaries must they keep themselves? That is a critique of pure reason. We see here with what right we make use of concepts without experience, whether we do this not unlawfully. Through critique I do not arrive at certainty about the questions, insofar as they are to be answered dogmatically, but about that which reason can accomplish with regard to all metaphysical questions. Such a critique has never yet been published except by Professor Kant, Riga 1781.",["\"Oritic\" is read as \"Critic\" (OCR slip); \"mäßen\" is kept as printed and rendered \"must\" (presumably \"müßen\"), which is a conjecture.","Erkenntniß = cognition; Begriff = concept; Bedeutung = significance; Gegenstände = objects; Gränzen = boundaries; Vernunft = reason. \"Reine Vernunft Begriffe\" is rendered \"pure concepts of reason\", the compound being ambiguous between \"pure concepts of reason\" and \"concepts of pure reason\".","\"facti\" (Latin genitive) is rendered \"*factum*\" in the nominative; \"Critic\" is rendered \"critique\".","\"so Nutzen haben\" is rendered literally as \"have use in this way\"; \"so\" could also be read as \"thus/also so\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-011":["(Remark. Metaphysics can be divided into metaphysics as natural predisposition and into metaphysics as science. The sum total of all our cognitions of reason through concepts, which inhere in every human being and which he makes use of in experience, is *metaphysica naturalis*. Every cognition of reason, insofar as it is not speculative or *in concreto*, is natural, and of these there is a great multitude; thus the common use of the understanding also has metaphysics, and this metaphysics well deserves to be raised to science. Every natural use of reason need not always be transformed into a scientific one, because the ends are attained in this way, e.g. in morals one need not represent the rule *in abstracto*, but merely *in concreto*. It is, e.g., difficult to prove the impermissibility of lies *a priori* or to show it *in abstracto*, *in concreto* it goes well enough, but in metaphysics it is necessary. We have various cognitions *a priori*, which however find their confirmation in experience — but also transcendent concepts, and now material metaphysics can help nothing, since the confirmation of the general principles in experience is lacking. Thus I flutter, as it were, in empty space, and if science *a priori* does not confirm itself through itself, I run the danger of falling into brain-business. The natural use of reason takes place when we remain in experience, but does not suffice as soon as we go beyond it, and since that is necessary, metaphysics as science is necessarily unavoidable in reason — it seeks whether it cannot find there disclosures of the entangled questions that true reason itself raises, even practical reason looks around for one who treats metaphysics as science, or tries it by itself, and no cognition requires so urgently to be a science as metaphysics. One mocks it as a useless thing easily dispensed with, but one could well do that also with the theme of the circulation of the blood — one cannot excuse oneself with this: it goes beyond reason and our cognitions: in nature there are mysteries, but the things are outside me and I am to study them, here coverings are drawn before; but if I think something, if I think to myself things *a priori* that are only creatures of my understanding, then information must be able to be given with regard to them, whether my assertions have ground or not. It is not an inquiry into the thing, but into an understanding, the principles and concepts of it must be capable of being investigated, for everything lies in me.)",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"Eifahiung\" = Erfahrung (experience), \"dei\" = der, \"findt\" = findet, \"verwikt kelten\" = verwickelten. \"leicht zu entbehrendeSache\" separated as two words.","\"Hirn Geschäfte\" rendered literally as \"brain-business\" (Hirngeschäfte); the sense is left as in the German.","\"Natur Anlage\" = natural predisposition (Naturanlage); Anlage is not in the glossary. \"Gebrauch\" = use; \"Zwecke\" = ends; \"Grund Sätze\" = principles (Grundsätze); \"Behauptungen\" = assertions; \"Grund\" = ground.","\"Wissenschaft\" = science; \"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"Vernunft Erkenntniß\" = cognition of reason.","\"die Unzuläßigkeit der Lügen\" rendered literally as \"impermissibility of lies\"; \"conjecture\" not made. \"hier sind Decken vorgezogen\" rendered literally; the subject (\"coverings\") and the sense of \"here\" are left open. \"Auskunft\" = information; \"erforschlich\" = capable of being investigated.","\"die wahre Vernunft\" is retained as \"true reason\"; the OCR reading \"wahre\" is possibly a misreading of another word. \"oder probiert es bei sich selbst\" is ambiguous in subject, as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-012":["Meanwhile we still want to see to it that we do not lose the dogmatic method, therefore we will present it as our author does. We will accordingly not establish metaphysics as a doctrinal edifice, as a *doctrin*, because one will after all find in the sequel that everything is deception, but we will add the critique of pure reason, whereby all dogmatic boasting will fall away. Critique shall therefore bring forth a system of self-cognition of our reason. In the system of metaphysics we will first go through all concepts analytically and merely explain what our reason understands when it makes use of this or that concept. That is of great use, even though we here leave it undecided whether the questions of metaphysics are answered or not. This elucidation (not extension) of our acquaintance is very useful, for in everything where there is only reason there are metaphysical concepts. Metaphysics is the sum total of all cognitions of pure reason, which is very much enlightened by analytic. The answering of all questions of reason is the synthetic part of metaphysics, where we seek to extend our cognition through pure reason and to acquire pure cognition. That is of a different kind from the analytic, for here the concepts of the understanding are dissected, but no questions are answered, thus our cognition is not extended, but only elucidated. In the synthetic part, however, they are extended and there we need critique, because there we make attempts, without going out beyond experience with reason. Although at present not yet all philosophers take up the path of the critique of pure reason, they will nevertheless be compelled to come upon it one day. Necessity will teach them this, because critique alone gives complete satisfaction, and without it our reason will be constantly in discord with itself.",["\"1 heil\" is read as \"Theil\" (OCR slip), rendered \"part\" (Teil in the glossary).","\"Blendwerk\" is rendered \"deception\"; the glossary's \"Schein\" = illusion is not the word used. \"Lehrgebäude\" = doctrinal edifice; \"doctrin\" is left in the transcript's spelling, in italics.","\"Erläuterung\" = elucidation, \"Erweiterung\" = extension, \"Kenntniße\" = acquaintance (glossary: Kenntnis), distinct from \"Erkenntniß\" = cognition.","\"Selbsterkenntniß\" = self-cognition. \"Folge\" in \"in der Folge\" is rendered \"sequel\".","\"ohne mit der Vernunft über die Erfahrung herauszugehen\" is kept literally; the \"ohne\" clause is awkward in the German, and the transcript may have intended \"without going out ...\" or a negation lost in transcription. \"sie\" in \"werden sie erweitert\" refers back (presumably) to \"Erkenntniße\"/\"Begriffe\"; left as a pronoun.","\"Zwiespalt\" = discord; \"Analytic\" = analytic (noun), \"Analytic ... aufgeklärt\" rendered \"enlightened\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-001":["End of the Prolegomena, Ontology",["\"Finis Prolegomenorum Ontologia\": the Latin is rendered in English as a heading; \"Ontologia\" is in the nominative, so the relation to \"Prolegomenorum\" is left open (\"Prolegomena, Ontology\")."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-002":["We now begin the science of the properties of all things in general, which one calls ontology. (Ontology is supposed to be the science that deals with the general predicates of all things, that is, such predicates as are common to most things — if the predicates are not *universalia*, i.e. that are common to all things, then one does not know at all what ontology is. They must belong to all things, if not copulatively, i.e. that both belong to them, then yet disjunctively, one of the two, e.g. composite and not composite. — These predicates belong to all higher ones, but not copulatively, i.e. that both predicates should belong to them, but disjunctively, one of the two. When I say general predicates, an exception still always takes place, and how far does that extend?). One easily sees that they will contain nothing but all fundamental concepts and fundamental principles of our cognition *a priori* in general: for if it is to consider the properties of all things, then it has as object nothing but a thing in general, i.e. every object of thinking, thus no determinate object. So nothing remains for me but the cognizing that I consider. (The science that deals with objects in general will deal with nothing but the concepts through which the understanding thinks, thus with the nature of the understanding and of reason, insofar as it cognizes something *a priori*. That is transcendental philosophy, which does not say something about the objects *a priori*, but investigates the faculty of the understanding or of reason to cognize something *a priori*; it is thus a self-cognition of the understanding or of reason as to content, just as logic is a self-cognition of the understanding and of reason as to form; to transcendental philosophy there necessarily belongs critique of pure reason. But since one treated ontology without critique — what was ontology then? An ontology that was no transcendental philosophy. One philosophized, then, now and again without asking: can one do that too? Transcendental philosophy is the result of critique, for if I can represent the domain and the sources in interconnection, then the interconnected representation of the principles *a priori* is transcendental philosophy, and if I take all the consequences that flow from it, then that is metaphysics; without critique I do not know whether the concepts of pure reason and pure understanding are all there, or whether some are not still missing — because I have no principles. One has set no boundaries to reason, and has gone as far in doing so as one could. They see well that they cannot mix everything in matters of experience, but *a priori* they can have insight into everything and hence, because no one can refute them.)",["Wissenschaft = science; Eigenschaft rendered 'property' (Beschaffenheit is also 'property' in the glossary, not present here); Gegenstand and Object both 'object' (Gegenstand in 'jeden Gegenstand des Denkens', 'bestimmten Gegenstand', 'Gegenständen überhaupt'; Object in 'zum object', 'Objecten').","Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkennen = cognizing; erkennt = cognizes; Zusammenhang = interconnection; zusammenhängende kept as 'interconnected'.","'Grund Begriffe' and 'Grund Sätze' rendered 'fundamental concepts' and 'fundamental principles' (Satz = principle in the glossary, here as in Grundsatz); 'Principien' = principles.","'wenn sie die Eigenschaften aller Dinge erwägen soll': 'sie' (the science, Wissenschaft) is taken as antecedent; the preceding 'sie' ('daß sie nichts enthalten werden') is plural and its antecedent is unclear; both preserved as in the German.","'Man hat keine Gränzen der Vernunft gesetzt': 'gesetzt' = set (setzen elsewhere 'posit'); 'Gränzen' = boundaries (Grenze).","'Sie sehen wol ein ... und daher, weil sie keiner wiederlegen kann' is syntactically incomplete and its 'sie' (plural, 'they'; also 'her/it') is ambiguous; left as it stands. 'Sie' here may be a copy error for a different subject.","OCR: 'h orm' read as 'Form'; the stray mark '■' after 'abhandelte' omitted; 'zu kommen' read as 'zukommen' (belong to); 'disiunctive' read as 'disjunctive'; 'handlen' as 'handeln'; 'findt' as 'findet'.","Latin 'universalia', 'a priori' kept in italics; 'Ontologie' rendered 'ontology', 'Selbst Erkenntniß' as 'self-cognition'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-003":["(Metaphysics has the peculiarity that one can complete it entirely, the faculty of reason according to its sources, domain and boundaries one can measure out. Metaphysics cannot hope to make discoveries in the nature of things, but reason must teach everything, I can thus make out which concepts lie in reason independent of experience — it is here as with a grammar, which can be complete, but not a lexicon, for in the time in which the author writes it, new words are again made.)",["Vermögen = faculty; Umfang = domain; Grenzen = boundaries; Quellen = sources.","'ohnabhängig' read as 'unabhängig' (independent).","'unter der Zeit, da' rendered literally as 'in the time in which'; it could also be read 'while'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-004":["(In morals something can also be completed, namely, as Herr Kant calls it, metaphysics of morals, i.e. the first principles of morality, which are from pure, but practical reason.)",["Sitten / Sittlichkeit = morals / morality; 'erste principien' = first principles.","'die sind aus reiner, aber practischer Vernunft': 'die' could refer to the principles or to the metaphysics of morals; preserved ambiguity ('which')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-005":["(The author's ontology is a *farrago*, collected knowledge, which is not system but rhapsodic — although he was otherwise one of the most acute philosophers. The cause is this, that one still knew nothing of critique.) The science that is to treat the properties of all things in general must be a science *a priori*. Thus it is a cognition from mere reason, cannot be drawn from experience, for experience does not reach so far that it can be applied to all things, it does not teach what must belong to things in general, but what the senses show us; since ontology has no determinate object, it can contain nothing but the principles of cognizing *a priori* in general: the science, therefore, of all fundamental concepts and fundamental principles on which all our pure reason cognition rests, is ontology. This science, however, will not properly be called ontology. For to have a thing in general as object is as much as to have no object and only to treat a cognition merely, as in logic. The name, however, indicates as if it had a determinate object. But it has no object that would be distinguished from the essence of reason, rather it considers understanding and reason themselves, namely the fundamental concepts and fundamental principles of the same in their pure use (or of pure reason and of the pure understanding); the most fitting name would be transcendental philosophy. A cognition is called a pure cognition of understanding or of reason, insofar as it is possible *a priori*, and is to be distinguished from the empirical. A pure cognition of reason, however, is not transcendental. But the consideration of the nature and possibility of such a pure reason, cognition through pure reason, is transcendental, e.g. the concept of cause and effect is pure but not transcendental, but the consideration of the possibility of such a concept is transcendental. — Between 2 points only one straight line is possible, is a proposition *a priori*, but not a transcendental proposition, for it deals with the object. But if I consider, how is it possible to arrive at such a cognition *a priori*, then that is transcendental. Transcendental philosophy contains the principles of the possibility of cognition *a priori*. It contains all fundamental concepts of our pure reason and pure understanding, in general all fundamental principles of the possibility of cognition *a priori*, the domain and boundaries of all reason *a priori*. It determines how far pure reason can get without experience, before that is, we must know the content of pure reason, the fundamental principles, fundamental concepts, principles and conditions of the use of the concepts of pure reason. It is very necessary, in the expansion of our cognition, to know the boundaries beyond which we cannot go, because otherwise we run the risk of working at a loss. We venture to expand our cognition and do not know whether we are in the field of truth or of figments of the brain, and even our true cognitions become doubtful. The boundaries of empirical knowledge, as said above, cannot be determined. Physics, i.e. the philosophy of the appearances of nature, thus also has no determinate boundaries, and likewise mathematics, because it can multiply its intuitions infinitely. The objects of pure reason are such as can be given in experience, e.g. God, world, whole, etc. Which, now, are the boundaries of pure reason? These must be possible to determine, for with pure reason the object is not given to me at all; I need therefore only study reason itself, for that is given to me. But in natural science one gets the cognition from elsewhere, namely from experience. In cognitions of pure reason I am to spin everything out of myself, here experience and intuition can be of no help to me, since the whole can therefore be drawn in advance from my cognition, I have reason in me, thus the boundaries of pure reason must let themselves be determined, and transcendental philosophy is the boundary-determination of the use of pure reason. — Now we go to actual cognition, and before we consider it and its elements, i.e. boundaries, sources and domain, we must still send something ahead, namely the difference of analytic and synthetic judgments. We can judge in 2 ways, either in that, in judging, we ascribe to the *subject* a *praedicat* which lies in the concept of the *subject* — or in that, in judging, we go out beyond the concept of the *subject* and ascribe to it a *praedicat* that does not lie in its concept. The former is analytic, the latter synthetic, e.g.: Every body is extended, is an analytic judgment. If I think a body, then I think at the same time something extended. — Here I can find the *praedicat* through the analysis of the *subject*. They are called judgments of dissection. Herr Kant says: judgments of clarification, because they clarify our cognition. — Every body is heavy, is a synthetic judgment, heaviness does not lie at all in the concept of body. Everything that happens has a cause. In the concept: it happens, the concept cause is not contained. To happen means to become what previously was not, here the concept of cause does not lie in it at all; this judgment is expanding. Everything that happens exists in time, is analytic. It happens, means: there was a time in which it arose, which another time preceded, in which it was not. The concept: everything happens, I need only dissect in order to bring out the judgment. The possibility of analytic judgments is easy, for I cognize only that which I already had in the concept of the *subject*, I need only dissect the concept. Whether synthetic judgments are possible is a question: synthetic judgments *a posteriori* are easily possible. E.g. every body is heavy, experience teaches that. Now there remains still to show the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*, and that is hard to answer, but makes up the spirit of the whole of transcendental philosophy. Had the philosophers investigated this sooner, they would already have got further in many respects. Mathematics has a multitude of synthetic judgments *a priori*. They must therefore be possible. Now we must see how they are possible, since reason makes a great multitude of such synthetic judgments beyond experience. I have a concept, of which I am not to say anything at all that lies in it, but outside it, and that indeed *a priori* without the assistance of experience. How is that possible? E.g. everything that happens has a cause, i.e.: Something must precede what happens, upon which this follows according to a rule, and what happens is considered as effect. How can I thus expand a cognition *a priori*? This *quaestio* has never been raised so generally, though it is so exceedingly necessary. Indeed, one can say that the whole of transcendental philosophy is an investigation of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*. Before we speak at length of the answer to this question, we go back once more to the analytic judgments.",["OCR slips silently corrected: V issenschaft, Eifaluung (Erfahrung), jjriori, Hie Mathe matic (Die Mathematic), W ißenschaft, Beyhiilfe.","Grund Begriffe / Grund Sätze rendered 'fundamental concepts' / 'fundamental principles'; Principien is also 'principles' (Satz and Grundsatz are both 'principle' in the glossary). Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'; Objecte occurs once ('The objects of pure reason').","'The objects of pure reason are such as can be given in experience': the German reads thus as it stands (no 'nicht'); left unemended, but it may be a transcription slip.","Kenntniß ('empirical knowledge') and Erkenntniß ('cognition') are distinguished; erkennen = cognize. 'Critic' = Kritik, rendered 'critique'. Eigenschaften = 'properties' (also Beschaffenheit in the glossary).","Zergliederungs Urtheile = 'judgments of dissection'; Erläuterungsurtheile = 'judgments of clarification' (Erläuterung = clarification); erweiternd = 'expanding' (erweitern/Erweiterung = expand/expansion, distinct from Ausdehnung, 'extension').","Wesen in 'vom Wesen der Vernunft' rendered 'essence' (glossary also allows 'being'). Hirngespinste = 'figments of the brain'; Grenzbestimmung = 'boundary-determination'; Gränzen = 'boundaries'.","Ambiguities kept: 'a priori' in 'cognizing a priori in general' and 'ein reine Vernunft, Erkenntniß durch pure Vernunft' (a stray comma and apposition kept as in the German); 'ehe das ist, müßen wir…' kept as a comma splice.","Latin kept in italics: *farrago*, *a priori*, *a posteriori*, *quaestio*; *subject* and *praedicat* are Latinate in the German and so italicized."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-006":["All judgments must have a principle, which is the criterion of truth. For without that there is no distinction at all between true and false judgments. (That synthetic judgments would have to have another principle, the metaphysicians surely saw — Leibniz made for this the *principium rationis sufficientis*: everyone believes the proposition, but how do we come to it? Wolff ventured to add a proof to it from the principle of contradiction, — but he saw indeed that it was not adequate, and so said in the remark on it that even if this proof were not stringent, one could appeal here to the common human understanding. — That is indeed true, but if it is to be a proposition *a priori*, it must also be proved *a priori* — and completely; this proposition also cannot be the principle, i.e. principle of judgment, because it itself needs a proof. Which is the principle of analytic judgments? For everyone easily sees that the synthetic ones must have an entirely different one. — For it is something other to say something that lies in the concept than to go beyond the concept. The principle of analytic judgments is the proposition of contradiction: *cuilibet subjecti non competit praedicato ipsi oppositum*. All analysis is nothing other than the consciousness that a certain mark is contained in the concept of the thing. The mark that is contained in the concept of the thing is in part one and the same with the thing. Every analytic mark is identical with the (not whole) concept, but rather with a part of the same, e.g. every body is extended is really judged identically. All analytically affirmative judgments rest on the proposition of identity: *Cuilibet subjecti competit praedicato ipsi identicum* (the predicate can be *ex parte identicum*, e.g. every body is composite — or totally identical, then it is an empty proposition). The human being is an animal, there I say nothing but what I already think in the concept of the human being. The proposition of identity indicates nothing but the analysis of the concept (all analytic judgments are easy — and propositions *a priori* — for we need only go through our concepts and see what lies in them; one falsely passes them off as propositions of experience). There are also analytic negative judgments. An analytically negative judgment is one where I find through the dissection that a certain mark conflicts with the thing, e.g. no body is simple. The simple conflicts with the composite. The *principium* is: all that must be denied of the thing which contradicts the concept of the thing — the *principium contradictionis* expresses the negation in which I may not go out of the concept of the thing. Are these the principles of all analytic judgments? Yes, all judgments, analytically affirming as well as negating, stand under the proposition of contradiction. For if a predicate is identical with the subject, then its opposite contradicts the subject, and I will recognize the falsity straightaway according to the *principium contradictionis*, if I have recognized the truth through the *principium identitatis*, and conversely, I will recognize the truth straightaway through the *principium identitatis*, as I have recognized the falsity through the *principium contradictionis*; since here I can infer from the one to the other, I will accept the *principium contradictionis* as the universal principle of all analytic judgments, e.g. a body is simple, i.e. the simple contradicts the concept of body, not the composite. The composite therefore belongs to it. It is the same whether I say: A concept contradicts the thing or its opposite is identical to it — and: a concept is identical to the thing or its opposite contradicts it. The *principium contradictionis* is therefore the principle of all analytic judgments. — Can we not also say, the principle of all human cognition? *Responsio*: Yes, but then it is not a principle from which all judgments can be derived, but a criterion of truth, to which no true cognition must be contrary, because then the thought is self-contradictory in itself, but what is not contrary to the *principium contradictionis* is not on that account true; it is therefore a negative criterion of truth. The thought of it can be possible, but the thing is not on that account possible: e.g. we can think a force of the mind through which we think others' thoughts; there is nothing contradictory in that, the thing is not yet on that account possible. Here the *principium contradictionis* can be no criterion of truth. But that is false according to the *principium contradictionis*, that I at the same time think and do not think — or stand in immediate combination with a distant object. The *principium contradictionis* is a universal principle of all human cognition, but only to derive falsity from it. It is indeed universal, but not sufficient. There can be no contradiction, and the thing can nevertheless be false. The *principium contradictionis* is the universal negative criterion of all truth; what conflicts with it is false (the *impoßibile* in the principle of the author can be nothing but the modality — it is meant merely to indicate the apodictic of the proposition — which it is not always necessary to express, as also here, because it is self-evident). Our author expresses it: *Impoßibile est aliquid simul eße et non eße*. How can we use this, since we have not yet spoken of time, and so many acute authors have erred (We must first, or rather can ask, to what extent is time possible?). The author believes this is at the same time the definition of the impossible. *Impoßibile quod est et non est* or what contradicts itself. (As we expressed it: *nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*, the *simul* drops out and yet holds universally, for to a subject a predicate opposed to it can never belong — but to a subject a predicate can well belong which is opposed to another predicate of the same subject, only not at the same time, e.g. a moved body that is simple is a contradiction, but a body that is in rest and motion is no contradiction, namely successively; if a predicate can belong to the one subject that is *oppositum* to another predicate of the same subject, one must and can cognize only through experience, or the possibility that a thing can be altered, experience teaches.)",["OCR 'ltespensio' read as *Responsio*; 'miißen' as 'müssen', 'wieder -spricht' as 'widerspricht', 'Gemüt-hs' as 'Gemüths' (silent corrections). The 'simul' clause after 'fälschlich' etc. is unaffected.","Satz (in 'Satz des Wiederspruchs', 'Satz der Identitaet', 'Erfahrungssätze') = 'proposition'; princip/*principium* = 'principle'; the two are kept distinct, and 'Urteilsprincip' = 'principle of judgment'.","Zergliederung = 'dissection', distinct from Analyse/analytisch = 'analysis'/'analytic'. Merkmal = 'mark'; Begriff = 'concept'; Bewußtseyn = 'consciousness'; Erkenntniß = 'cognition'; erkennen = 'recognize'/'cognize' (the German is the same verb).","'a priori' in 'ein Satz a priori seyn soll, muß er auch a priori erwiesen werden' is left as unattached as the German; 'und völlig' is kept in its place, its reference being unclear.","'wenn dem einen subject ein praedicat zukommen kann...' (final sentence): 'wenn' rendered literally as 'if'; the construction is irregular and left as it stands, as is 'oder die Möglichkeit... lehrt die Erfahrung'.","'andrer Gedanken denken': the genitive could mean 'others' thoughts' or be read otherwise; rendered 'others' thoughts'. 'Wie ich durchs principium contradictionis die Falschheit erkannt' is rendered with 'as', keeping the German's loose connective.","'zulänglich' = 'adequate', distinct from 'zureichend' = 'sufficient'. 'allgemein' = 'universal'; 'gemein' = 'common'. 'Impoßibile' and 'eße' keep the transcript's spelling; 'Autor' = 'author'; 'definition' stands for the German 'definition' (cf. Erklärung)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-007":["(The *principium contradictionis* is the critique of possibility and one can also say of truth, only not the sufficient one, but *conditio sine qua non*.) If that is so, I can convert it: what does not contradict itself is not impossible (*cui competit definitio competit definitum*). That is false, for otherwise all fantasies that do not contradict themselves would be possibilities. — The *principium contradictionis* is supposed to precede all cognition, thus cannot depend on the concept of time. Some have expressed it thus: *ut aliquid sit et non sit*, that is also false, for it is possible that a thing is and is not, but successively. All things can, as we know, be altered, and alteration is precisely the *successio praedicatorum contradictorie oppositorum*. He has expressed the proposition of identity thus: *quicquid est illud est* (the proposition of identity enunciates the positive, what the proposition of contradiction enunciates negatively; for if identical predicates belonged to the subject, the opposite would have to belong to it, that would be a *praedicatum oppositum*, but this is contrary to the *praedicatum contradictum*. — This proposition of contradiction is the highest logical negative principle, and that of identity is the highest logical positive principle) (from the combination of both principles there arises a third, namely the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*, i.e., to a subject, of two predicates that are opposed, one must always belong, for if I attribute both to it at once, the one cancels the other, and I think nothing; if I negate both, I also think nothing (logically), that is, where the one is A, the other is non-A. In the case of the *principium exclusi medii* we do not compare two contradictorily opposed judgments, but a thing with all possible predicates of things in general. The understanding sets out to provide a given concept with predicates from among all possibilities.) That is an empty proposition, all empty propositions are not means for arriving at distinctness, because *idem per idem* is explained, so no distinct concept arises, e.g., a body is, is a true proposition, but empty, and likewise the proposition *quicquid est aliud est*. If I explain the concept through itself, then I have thought nothing else. In our explanation we did not say: *praedicatum subjecto per tale identicum*. That would also be empty, e.g., body is body, but *ex parte identicum*. The predicate is a *criterium falsitatis* of all human cognition, but a positive actual predicate of all analytic judgments that can be derived and proved from it. (An analytic judgment is one in which the subject and predicate are identical; if I now attribute to the subject an identical predicate, I attribute to it an *oppositum* and therefore contradict myself — but not in the synthetic, because there subject and predicate are not identical.) Now the truth of analytic judgments cannot be cognized otherwise than that either the predicate is identical with the subject, or its opposite contradicts the subject. In synthetic judgments, however, there is no contradiction, therefore I cannot judge them according to the *principium contradictionis*, e.g., that something happens without anything going before does not contradict itself, but it is nevertheless false. A contradiction arises only when the same thing is cancelled that is posited in the concept of the subject. The truth of synthetic judgments does not rest on the proposition of identity. For the synthetic judgment consists in this, that I posit something other than what is identical to the thing, e.g., everything that happens has its cause. Cause is not identical with what happens, but is something entirely other.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Begriff = concept; Möglichkeit = possible/possibility; Satz = proposition (Satz der Identitaet, Satz des Widerspruchs); Urtheil = judgment; setzen = posit; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Wiederspruch/Widerspruch = contradiction (orthography varies in the source).","'Critic der Möglichkeit': 'Critic' rendered 'critique'; kept literal.","'Wenn das ist, kann ich umkehren': 'das' has no clear antecedent; kept as 'that'. 'Einige haben es so ausgedrückt' and 'er' (the author, i.e. Baumgarten) are left unresolved as in the German.","'Das ist ein leerer Satz' follows a long parenthesis; the referent is left as in the German. Likewise 'Das praedicat ist ein criterium falsitatis': 'praedicat' (not 'principium') is as printed and may be a transcript slip; kept as it stands.","'Ursache' = cause; 'Etwas geschieht' = something happens. 'ist … gesetzt' = is posited. 'erklären/Erklärung' rendered 'explain/explanation' (glossary also allows 'definition').","'Mittel' in 'nicht Mittel, zur Deutlichkeit zu gelangen' = means; the plural/singular agreement ('alle leere Sätze sind nicht Mittel') is kept awkward.","'e. gr.', 'z.E.' both rendered 'e.g.'; 'u.v.v.' not occurring here. Latin kept in italics as the author's own technical terms."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-008":["The author says *nihil negativum* is as much as *impossibile*, that is so usual, and quite good. It is distinguished from *nihil privativum*, which signifies a lack, e.g., light is something positive, darkness something negative. Bright darkness is a *nihil negativum*. *Nihil negativum* is that of which no thought at all is possible. I can think something affirming and negating, but I think nothing at all if I think affirming and negating at the same time, e.g., bright darkness. Here I cannot think darkness, because I think brightness, and not brightness, because I think darkness — thus nothing at all. If I think opposed things, then I have 2 thoughts, if I put them together, then I think nothing at all. One must guard in the progress of cognition against pairing real thoughts together in such a way that in the end nothing at all is thought.",["Fortgang (Fortgänge) = progress (glossary: progression; progress, *progressus*); Erkenntniß = cognition; reelle = real.","'Mangel bedeutet': bedeuten = signify.","'e.gr.' and 'z.E.' both rendered 'e.g.'; 'affirmirend/negirend' rendered 'affirming/negating'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-009":["Contradiction is divided into *veram et apparentem*, e.g., *pia fraus* seems to be none and is yet an actual contradiction, for one who is pious cannot deceive, and vice versa. That the earth attracts the moon immediately, without touching it, is an illusory contradiction. Further, they are more latent, where the *contradictio* can be cognized only through analysis, e.g., a permitted white lie is a hidden contradiction; one must first show the concept, for necessity can give no permission to lie — More patent, where no dissection is necessary. One must not at once accuse someone of manifest contradiction, for if it were known to him, he would not contradict himself. The *patentes* are thus only relatively *patentes*. If one wants to accuse someone of *absurditas*, i.e., of the contradictory *patentis*, then he must transform the *latentem* into *patentem*, and if the opponent nevertheless persists in it, then he assumes an *absurditas*.",["Schein Wiederspruch rendered 'illusory contradiction' (Schein = illusion); Wiederspruch/Widerspruch = contradiction.","'Nothlüge' rendered 'white lie' (literally an emergency or necessity lie); 'Noth' = necessity/need, rendered 'necessity' in the clause that follows, so the link is lost in English.","'u.v.v.' = 'und vice versa', rendered 'and vice versa'. 'Zergliederung' = dissection; 'Analysis' = analysis. 'Patenter' and 'latenter' are comparatives kept as 'more patent/more latent'.","Latin case forms ('patentis', 'latentem', 'patentem') are as printed in the transcript and left unemended, though 'contradictorischen patentis' is syntactically irregular; the Latin 'veram et apparentem' likewise kept."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-010":["In passing we can also cite the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*. 2 *opposita praedicata*, A and *non* A, one cannot posit at the same time, that is a contradiction. To cancel two opposed predicates at the same time is also not feasible, rather one must belong to the thing, that is what the *principium exclusi medii* etc. says, e.g. round or not round, both cannot belong to the *subject* according to the *principium contradictionis*, but both also cannot be denied of the *subject* according to the *principium exclusi medii* etc. This cannot be derived from the *principium contradictionis*: for if 2 opposed predicates cannot belong to One thing, but one must be denied, then the other is at the same time posited in its place. Hence the *principium contradictionis* is nevertheless the first. According to this principle we can make many analytic judgments *a priori*, we may dissect the concept and see what is contained in it, and affirm the one of these, or deny its opposite, e.g. body, there I think to myself something extended. I say, therefore, a body is extended, or no body is not extended. — That is an analytic judgment *a priori*. From all concepts that can be dissected one can pass analytic judgments *a priori*; if they are simple, they cannot be dissected, e.g. being, something. — We now come to synthetic judgments. — Experience is something other than a chain of synthetic judgments *a posteriori*. But we do not want to ask: How are synthetic judgments *a posteriori* possible, that is distinct, e.g. I have gold, I want to know more of it than lies in my concept of gold, so I take experience to help, posit it in various circumstances, through which I become aware of and notice more and more of it, e.g. malleable, fire-resistant etc., then I have a synthetic judgment *a posteriori*. The whole of experience is nothing but the *synthesis* of perceptions. Consciousness of sensation is perception. Of pure sensations one can make no concepts for oneself and cannot communicate them to others, for it is the way one finds oneself with regard to something. Another finds himself again differently. But of the *synthesis* of perceptions one can make concepts for oneself. Because we are not passive there, everything that we might do can therefore be conscious to us.",["*Wiederspruch* (Widerspruch) = contradiction; *Gewahr werden*/*bemerken* rendered 'become aware of'/'notice'.","*deutlich* ('distinct') is the glossary term; the sentence 'that is distinct' is left as in the German.","*Erfahrung ... nicht ... als eine Kette*: the German 'etwas anders als' is rendered literally 'something other than'; the following clause is kept as printed.","*Umstände* = circumstances; *Ausgedehntes* = 'something extended'; *Seyn* = 'being' (not glossary *Wesen*).","*sezze* (setze) = posit; 'it' is left unresolved as in the German.","*mittheilen* = communicate; *paßiv* = passive; *bewußt seyn* = 'be conscious' (cf. *Bewußtsein* = consciousness).","The last sentence ('Weil wir da nicht paßiv sind ... was wir thun möchten') is preserved as obscure; the mood *möchten* is rendered 'might'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-011":["Experience gives synthetic judgments *a posteriori*. Can we not have synthetic judgments *a priori*? On the answer to this question rests the possibility of the whole of metaphysics. (many deny synthetic judgments *a priori* and just thereby the whole of metaphysics — if metaphysics is possible, then such synthetic judgments *a priori* are also possible, and whether they are is the first cardinal question in metaphysics. We find equally great difficulties here. — We attach to a *subject* a predicate that did not lie in its concept, e.g. all substances are persistent, all accidents changeable, that is a synthetic proposition. In the concept of substance lies nothing except that it is no property of another thing, but is a thing itself. — Is it *a priori* or *a posteriori*? To distinguish that, one must see whether the proposition contains a necessity or not. For experience teaches merely that things are, not that they ought to be. — Here with this proposition there is necessity, therefore *a priori*. If something arises that was not before; then we say, it must have a cause. — That is a synthetic proposition, for happening is something other than having a cause. — It is therefore a synthetic proposition and indeed *a priori*.) First we ask: are certain synthetic judgments *a priori* not necessary for the possibility of experience or of synthetic cognitions *a posteriori*? Must there not be certain synthetic judgments *a priori* through which the synthetic judgments *a posteriori* are possible? and these were certainly true, because they lie at the basis of experience, and that is true. — We see first, therefore: whether an experience is possible without laying synthetic judgments *a priori* at the basis?",["*Möglichkeit* = possibility; *Metaphysic* rendered 'metaphysics'; *Cardinal Frage* = 'cardinal question'.","*Satz* (synthetischer Satz) = 'proposition'; *Urtheil* = 'judgment'.","*Begriff* = concept; *Eigenschaft* = 'property' (glossary has *Beschaffenheit* = property; different German word, same English).","*accidencen* = accidents; *beharrlich* = 'persistent'; *Nothwendigkeit*/*nöthig* = necessity/necessary.","*zu Grunde liegen* = 'lie at the basis' (*Grund* = ground/basis); *zum Grunde zu legen* = 'laying ... at the basis'.","*Erkenntniße* = cognitions. 'were' (*waren*) in 'die waren gewiß wahr' is retained as printed, tense unaltered; possibly a slip for *wären* or *währen*; doubtful.","The opening parenthesis is not closed until after 'and indeed *a priori*', as in the source; the 'many deny...' clause is lowercase in the source and so left."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-012":["Experience has matter, i.e. *data*, and form, i.e. the combination of the *data*. The matter is constituted by the perceptions. Experience is the unity of manifold perceptions. The unity is the form of the perceptions. Here there must surely be a rule of the connection of the perceptions, through which all experience is possible. Now this rule cannot in turn be a posteriori, for it must go before all experience, hence a priori. There will therefore have to be a rule a priori of the unity of the perceptions, which makes experience possible. There will be synthetic propositions a priori that contain the principles of the possibility of experience, presupposing that experience is not an aggregate of the perceptions, but a unity of the perceptions ordered according to certain rules, which we will still illustrate in what follows. Now we have come so far as to see from afar, as in twilight, that in a certain way synthetic judgments a priori must be possible; because some must themselves lie at the ground of experience. Now we must inquire which synthetic propositions may well go before all experience a priori. — I notice two kinds of things in my experience: intuition, which rests on the senses and is called empirical intuition, and concept, which does not rest on the senses but comes about through categories, and so rests on the understanding. I have, then, intuition, which belongs to the senses, and concepts, which belong to the understanding. Now the question is: whether there are intuitions and concepts a priori, or whether everything is a posteriori. The empirical intuitions are representations of an object, as our senses are affected by it. The empirical intuition has two pieces: matter and form, and the empirical concepts too. — The matter of everything empirical — of the empirical intuition, is sensation, the form is the shape. The concept has matter, i.e. content, representations, *data*, which are given — the form is reflection of the understanding, whereby it brings the sensations together in such a way that it thereby thinks something general. The concept is a sum total of the sensations worked over by the understanding. The empirical constitutes the matter in the perceptions and intuitions and concepts. — The empirical rests on sensations, which are a posteriori. The matter of all representations is sensation and is given to us a posteriori. — If I leave everything out of the intuition, I still retain the form, i.e. the shape. Before all empirical intuition there is something empirical, i.e. sensation, and something that can be represented a priori. In every empirical concept there is matter, *i. e.* sensation, and the form, which belongs to the understanding, for it is logical. Now I can retain the intellectual, the form, if I leave out everything empirical, e.g. with chalk I leave out the intuition, so that only the form remains, magnitude, shape, that is a priori. The cognitions a priori of the form of intuition and the form of concepts are the ground of synthetic judgments a priori. We must therefore have concepts that are possible before all experience, lie at its ground and are synthetic. Sensation constitutes the matter of all experience. If we leave all that out, the shape still remains. In empty space I can feign a thousand kinds of shape. In geometry real shapes are thought a priori, e.g. cones etc.: I will therefore call that of extended beings which remains when I leave out all matter of perception the form of intuition. With a body I think nothing but space and shape, i.e. the form of intuition. Everyone has the representation of space a priori, that it is extended in length, breadth and height, that between 2 points a straight line is possible — hence they are absolutely necessary. We have, then, an intuition of space. — If we leave out everything empirical, such as gravity, density and color, I retain form and shape. Now I ask, can I leave that out too? Yes, but then no body remains for me. Through body I think to myself a substance, so the concept still remains for me. Through substance I think a subject that is the predicate of no other. There I already arrive at concepts. It is a concept that remains if I leave out everything else from the object. Every body has a force in itself, i.e. a ground of action, that is again a concept. Form it has, a multitude of parts — or it is a whole, there I may not think space either. Finally there still remains the concept of a thing that is substance, has force, parts, is a whole, which presupposes no shape and figure. In the end, then, pure intuition remains, and if that too is left out, the pure concept: it is pure because it contains nothing empirical and also has no intuition, is therefore transcendental. All pure concepts belong to the understanding. The intuitions lie at their ground, they deliver the object — our understanding reflects, but does not intuit. What are intuitions? they are nothing but ways in which our senses are affected by the object. We have no *intellectus archetypus*, which would be the producing cause of things, so that the object would arise together with the representation. Since this is therefore not so — how can we represent to ourselves the objects that the understanding does not produce? Every representation must agree with the object, otherwise it is no cognition. The agreement is possible in 2 ways, either if my representation produces the object, or if the object produces my representation. Since now our cognition is not of the kind that it itself produces objects; there remains only this, that the things themselves produce the cognition, these are therefore cognitions that rest on the way in which we are affected by objects. On that all our cognition rests. Supposing we were affected by no thing, we could have a concept of no thing. The property of the power of representation that indicates to us how we are affected by things is sensibility. Sensibility is therefore a receptivity, susceptibility, according to which we are affected by things. Accordingly all intuitions are sensible. For if nothing affects us, we also have no representation, for if we are to cognize objects that are given to us, we can never have the slightest representation of them if they do not affect us. Empirical intuitions therefore belong to sensibility and even pure intuitions will belong to it, e.g. the empirical intuition of body contains warmth, cold, but if we take only space, also the extension of the body, this as pure intuition will be nothing other than the form of our sensibility, of being affected by things through outer circumstances. Space is therefore nothing but the form of outer intuition. It is the same with time. We find in our soul alterations and inclinations of the will, if we take everything together, there is still always a connection there, in that either something is simultaneous, or one follows upon the other. Time is therefore the being simultaneous and in succession, it is therefore nothing but the form of inner intuition, or of inner sense. Before all experience, then, there goes the pure form of sensibility, a priori we can know nothing of sensations, e.g. who can see of the aloe that it is bitter? but that we can know a priori, how we will be affected by things that are mere shapes, e.g. from the cube I leave out everything empirical, so I still retain the shape, if I see it from the corner, I catch sight of 3 squares, that I can know a priori, for that is pure intuition. There are, then, cognitions a priori, which are possible with regard to pure intuition. We find, then, in our sensibility a rule, whereby we are capable of intuitions a priori, but of course no more than the form of the intuitions. On the other side we also have pure concepts of reason. Every object of experience must be a subject of a substance. We can therefore think to ourselves various concepts a priori through the mere understanding, for mere thinking can be considered separated from all pure intuition, separated from all intuition and sensible sensation. The properties of things, insofar as they are objects of pure thinking, I can know a priori just as well as the objects of pure intuition. Here it is clear that we are not capable of any cognition a priori of things of experience, for everything rests on the form of sensible intuition, which I can know a priori, for of space I can say something a priori, without an object being there. I can say much a priori without experience, insofar as the objects of experience are thought by me, and if I did not think what I intuit, I could not say at all that I had experience. Now, then, I have intuition a priori. The intuitions are forms of sensibility, which I can know beforehand; for before I am affected, the mind must still represent to itself a form, how it will be affected. But we would also get no concept of things that are intuited, if we could not think any intuition. We can make concepts of things in general only through the understanding, even if no object is given, in that we only represent to ourselves the way in which we can think the object. We see, then, that at the ground of all experience there lies a priori an intuition of the understanding, which is nothing but the form of our sensibility, through which it is possible that we can perceive something and that all empirical intuitions are finite, 2 pure concepts a priori, for since experience is possible not through perception alone, but concepts must also come to it, concepts a priori must lie at the ground, whereby I can bring perceptions under concepts: they lie at the ground of experience as substance, and if we did not have concepts a priori, we would get none. — The sensations make no concept. This cognition a priori will therefore have 2 parts, the first contains the form of sensibility a priori, the other the form of the understanding or of thinking a priori. If we want, then, to consider the first source of experience, we have two, the one is aesthetic, which shows what belongs to the senses, and how we are affected by the objects, the other is logical and considers the form of thinking. We have, then, a transcendental aesthetic, which considers the intuition a priori and the conditions of sensibility a priori, and through it we will cognize the possibility of the aesthetic judgments, and when we have gone through this synthetic cognition a priori in its whole domain, we can say we have a priori principles on which the possibility of experience, which goes to all objects, rests; we will show that they are certain, because experience is certain and rests on them. We will be able to determine the boundaries of cognition a priori. Since all cognitions a priori have no meaning except that they are conditions of the possibility of experience, they also cannot be valid further than in the field of experience. The endeavor of transcendental philosophy will therefore be to show that all our cognitions a priori can go no further than to objects of experience, and thus to hinder our reason from overstepping the boundaries of experience and venturing attempts that aim at possible objects of experience. —",["OCR corrections made silently: 'lOOOley' read as 'tausenderley' (a thousand kinds), 'Empfindüngen' as Empfindungen, 'wrenn' as wenn, 'weide' as werde, 'dei Aloe' as 'der Aloe', and the stray mark ■ and quotation mark before 'Übereinstimmung' omitted.","'daß alle empirische Anschauungen endlich sind, 2 reine Begriffe a priori' is syntactically broken in the transcript (possibly 'zweitens'); rendered as it stands, with '2' kept.","'Verstandes Anschauung' is rendered 'an intuition of the understanding'; the German compound is left uninterpreted.","'Eigenschaft' (property) and 'Beschaffenheit' (glossary: property) would both be 'property'; here only Eigenschaft occurs. 'Principien' and Grundsatz/Grundsätze are both 'principle(s)'; 'Gränze' = boundary.","'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'wissen/wißen' = know (not *scientia*); 'Gegenstand' and 'obiect/object' both 'object'.","'Reflexion/reflectirt' rendered 'reflection/reflects'; 'schaut ... an' rendered 'intuit' (anschauen) to preserve the link with Anschauung.","'Gestalt' (not in glossary) = shape, and 'Figur' = figure; 'Empfänglichkeit' = susceptibility, beside 'Receptivitaet' = receptivity.","'da komme ich nun schon auf Begriffe' and 'da darf ich auch den Raum nicht denken' keep the German 'da' as 'there'; the latter is obscure and left so. 'Latin and Latinate terms (*data*, *datorum*, *intellectus archetypus*) are italicized; a priori/a posteriori are left unitalicized here, as in the source's German text."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-013":["The way in which we are affected by things makes the sensible representation possible. — All sensible intuition has a certain form, which is peculiar to human nature. Outer sensible intuition has the form that all outer things appear to us in space. That is a particular way in which we intuit things. Not a thing in itself, but a form of sensibility, that from the representations there arise relations, which are relations of space. Time too is the form of pure intuition; it is nevertheless not something that we cognize immediately, and in accordance with this double form of sensibility all representations are possible. These we must cognize beforehand, before we have impressions. We will therefore have intuitions a priori, without objects being given to us. We need have nothing but the form of intuition, which does not rest on the things, but on us. According to the difference of the *subiecta* one will be affected in different ways by the same things, e.g. the raven is pleasantly affected by a rotten carcass and we run away from it. Every *subiectum* has its own way of being affected. Its representation therefore rests not on the *obiectum*, but on the particular way of intuition. Our human nature is of such a kind that, when we are affected by outer things, it represents them to us in space; this form of intuition can alone be considered a priori, because it lies at the basis of every representation, and therefore precedes them. Likewise time can be weighed a priori, i.e. the form through which we cognize our inner state. — That is all possible because our intuition is sensible. — That rests on receptivity, receptiveness, to be affected by things. We can think a being that intuits through *spontaneitas*, from its own power, through itself without being affected by objects; so one represents God; how this is possible we cannot see, except perhaps through this, that the cognition by power brings forth things, for then it would affect them, not they it; of this, however, we also have no concepts: our intuition is *receptivitas*. By intuition we understand the sensible intuition through all *organa*, not merely through sight. In every way in which we are affected there are two pieces: matter, i.e. sensation's impression, and form, i.e. the way in which the impressions are united in my mind. Otherwise I would have millions of impressions, but no intuition of a whole *obiectum*. Intuition rests just as much on nature as does sensation, which presumably everyone has differently, although we have the same words for it. All *obiecta* of our intuition are appearances. We never see things as they are, but as they come to our senses — if a being had intellectual intuition, as we think of God, it would intuit the beings as they are, not as they appear. Our intuition-representations are only representations about the appearances of things. We therefore know only the appearances of things. — To our experience there therefore belongs 1st intuition, 2nd thinking, which does not belong to the senses. To intuit does not mean to have experience. Experience is a cognition that we have of the object of intuition. To it there belongs therefore thinking, which can be considered specially. Thinking makes up a part of experience, insofar as the understanding has a share in it. Intuiting is a part of experience, insofar as sensibility has a share in it. Everything empirical we can leave out, and then pure intuition remains, i.e. space and time. If we leave this out too, then pure thinking remains — if we think objects in general and the conditions under which they are, we call that pure concepts of the understanding or *categories*. They come from Aristotle, but since he had no principle for counting them off with certainty and could not deduce from where we have them, they finally fell into decay, because here one had no certainty. He also called them *praedicamenta*. We want to sketch in advance a system of the categories, which exhaust all concepts that the understanding makes use of in experience, (the investigation, how are synthetic cognitions a priori possible, is called critique of reason.) Every system of cognition is called, if no critique precedes it, dogmatic. We can therefore think a dogmatic metaphysics. And so all metaphysics has been until now. — All dogmatic procedure without critique — which one could also call anticritical procedure — is, however, a venture of reason. — This question divides into 2. 1st, How are speculative concepts, 2nd judgments possible a priori? The concept of ground is a synthetic concept, for I think under it something upon which something else follows according to a rule. Before we can answer this question (namely how can our reason connect a manifold, without the rule of identity) we must set out the table of the concepts a priori, which lie everywhere at the basis. First of the judgments, with this is connected all division of concepts",["Passage ends mid-sentence in the source; left unfinished.","'Ding' and 'Sache' are both rendered 'thing' ('Keine Sache an sich selbst'); 'Gegenstand' and 'obiect' both 'object'. Latin 'obiect/subiect' kept as *obiectum*/*subiectum* in italics (source forms obiect, subiect, obiecte).","'erkennen' = cognize, 'kennen' = know, 'einsehen' = see; 'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Verstandes Begriffe' = concepts of the understanding.","'erwogen/erwägen' rendered 'weighed' / 'considered' (not in glossary); 'betrachten' rendered 'consider' (Betrachtung).","'Wesen' = being (in 'ein Wesen', 'die Wesen'); 'Stücke' = pieces; 'Gemüth' = mind.","OCR: 'Vorkommen' (capital) read as 'vorkommen'; 'ltens'/'2tens' read as 1st/2nd; 'Untersucliung' corrected to 'Untersuchung'; 'ltensj' to '1tens'. 'Anschauungs Vorstellungen' rendered 'intuition-representations'.","'dürfen nichts als ... haben' rendered literally ('need have nothing but'); 'denn afficirte er sie, nicht sie ihn' is obscure and kept as is ('for then it would affect them, not they it', 'er' referring to the cognition/God ambiguously)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-015":["4)",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-016":["Modality problematic assertoric apodictic There are therefore as many categories as moments of the understanding in judging. Through this table we have the advantage that we see the origin of them. — Logic deals with connections of concepts, metaphysics with the origin of them. We see from this that the understanding exercises the same action when it makes concepts for itself as when it connects them.",["'Modalitaet' left as 'Modality'; 'aßertorische' (sic) read as 'assertorische'.","'Verknüpfungen' = connections; 'verknüpft' = connects (Verknüpfung)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-017":["All actions of the understanding reduce to judgments. As many moments as each judgment has, so many moments with regard to the thinking of objects in general must there be, i.e. as many moments of judgments as there are, so many categories must we have.",["'Urtheile' = judgments; 'Handlungen' = actions."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-019":["*Omnitudo* Limitation *actio mutua* or *commercium*",["Latin terms kept in italics as in the source; 'Limitation' is already the German/Latin-derived form given."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-020":["4)",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-021":["Modality *poßibilitas* *existentia* *neceßitas*",["Spellings 'poßibilitas', 'neceßitas' follow the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-022":["These categories can be called *praedicamenta*. Under these basic concepts stand derived ones, which are called *praedicabilia*: Under *Omnitudo* lie whole, perfection, under *Multitudo*: *numerus*, *Infinitudo*, under *ordo* etc., and so we get a complete system of pure thinking without regard to the intuition that is to be thought. — Space, time and sensation belong to sensibility, therefore not to the categories — We therefore divide transcendental philosophy into 2 parts: into transcendental aesthetic, i.e. *scientia sensuum*, the science of sensibility and its representations a priori. — The science of the sensible cognitions a priori therefore deals with nothing but space and time, for other sensible cognitions there are not — It is therefore an entirely separated part — and transcendental logic, which contains pure thinking a priori or the pure form of the understanding. — This transcendental logic differs from the common one in this: the latter concerns cognition without troubling itself whether the objects are a priori or a posteriori, the former considers the cognitions of the understanding insofar as they cannot be a posteriori or the possibility of pure cognition of the understanding a priori, here 35 belong the categories. Aristotle had 10 (many are ours) 1. Substance and accident, 2. *Qualitas*, 3. *Quantitas*, 4. Relation, 5. *Actio passio*, 6. *Quando* (belongs to time), 7. *Ubi*, 8. *Situs* (both belong to space), 9. *Habitus* (that is skill for something, that a thing is receptive to a form. Habilitaet).",["'35' is a stray line number in the OCR text and is left as it stands; the apparatus marks are otherwise omitted per instructions.","OCR corrections: 'einganz' read as 'ein ganz'; '(i. Quando' read as '6. Quando'; 'paß io' read as 'passio'.","'Philosophie' (transcendentale Philosophie) is rendered 'philosophy'; the glossary's 'Weltweisheit' is not at issue here.","'gemeinen' = common; 'iene' / 'diese' rendered 'the former' / 'the latter' ('this' in the first clause preserved as 'the latter').","'Möglichkeit der Reinen Verstandes Erkenntniß a priori' keeps the ambiguity of 'a priori' attaching to 'possibility' or to 'cognition'.","'Ganzes' = whole; 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-023":["In attempts he found that something was still lacking to him, so he assumed 4 more *postpraedicamenta*: *oppositum*, *prius et posterius*, *simul*, *motus* (is quite empirical), *habere* (that also cannot be a category). Through this table we have the advantage that we do not search together concepts at random, but here we can count off the elements of our pure understanding, so that no gap remains. (We also have the proper order and will rather begin with quantity, because those are the clearest, than with modality. as the author, those are already far more difficult, yet we must follow him here.) The predicables are also pure concepts of the understanding, but derived from the categories; the concept of the whole is a predicable, which stands under the category of *omnitudo*, and so we can have many predicates under this category. When one now speaks of categories, predicaments and predicables, one seems to be warming up the old scholastic philosophy. — But in fact nothing is left but the names from Aristotle, whose view, of enumerating the pure concepts of reason and bringing them under a register, was very good and worthy of a philosopher, but did not succeed. — In every system, at the slightest deficiency a gap shows itself, because an idea of the whole lies there at the basis. With a mountain of sand we do not see that if a few grains are lacking, but with a pyramid we do at once. Just this also takes place when we draft a system of the categories. — We can then know which are the concepts that make up the whole domain of pure reason. — What are categories? They are pure concepts of reason, i.e. such as are entirely a priori. A concept of experience, however, has its seat in the understanding as much as the category does, e.g.: the concept of body, where, however, there is still much that is empirical, i.e. what contains nothing further than sensation — and also pure intuition as space, which, as said, can be represented a priori. If we leave all that out, substance, force etc. remain. The pure concept of reason lies in experience, where I must leave everything else that belongs to sensibility. That is 1st sensation, 2 intuition, through which the object appears. Here, then, nothing remains but the pure thinking of the appearance, that is the categories. They are therefore the pure thinking about the objects, insofar as they are given through intuition. Our experience consists in intuiting and thinking. The empirical intuition is the way in which we are affected by things. Through it nothing is given but appearance. That can be considered according to matter and form. Space and time belong in the aesthetic. Thinking also belongs to experience; the concept a priori, through which thinking alone is possible in experience, is category. Here we abstract from the difference of all objects of experience, both with regard to sensation and to intuition. The categories are preconditions of the appearances, through which concepts of experience arise, although they are not concepts of experience. — Our common language already contains all that which transcendental philosophy extracts with effort. — These categories are all already contained in us, for without them no experience would be possible, e.g.: 'Snow has fallen.' In this lies that snow is, substance; 'fallen' signifies an accident, 'onto the earth' signifies an influence, that is *actio*, belongs therefore to the *causa*. 'Today' refers to time, 'fallen' to space. If we leave out all sensations, also space and time, substance remains, which acts in a certain way, they must therefore be combined, so that the concept of experience arises. Let us posit that we did not have such pure pure concepts of the understanding, then we could neither think nor speak at all. — Experience is the arrangement of the appearances and of the concepts, the concepts are categories, they are therefore the conditions of the possibility of experience, insofar as thinking belongs to it — just as space and time are the conditions of the possibility of experience, insofar as intuition belongs to it. Categories are pure concepts of the understanding, without which there would be no concepts of experience, hence no experience. Through them the intuitions are brought into a concept of experience — and now impressions of sensibility must still come to it. The transcendental aesthetic contains the elements of our cognitions that lie in sensibility. The transcendental logic the elements of our cognition that lie in the understanding. It is divided into the transcendental analytic, transcendental dialectic, as in general logic. The former contains all pure cognitions of the understanding, insofar as they hold only for objects of experience - — and is called the logic of the truth of the pure concepts of the understanding; it has to do with pure cognitions of reason that go only to objects of some possible experience. The transcendental dialectic contains pure concepts of reason, insofar as they are supposed to go further than to objects of all possible experience, thus beyond the boundaries of experience, and that is logic of illusion; for beyond experience they cannot go, because they have no other meaning than that through them experiences arise from given appearances. They have truth insofar as they go to objects of possible experience, for they are nothing but the thinking which must be added to the appearance, so that experience comes to be. — They are nothing but concepts through which given appearances are exposited, i.e. make a concept that is a concept of experience. Insofar as a part of transcendental philosophy contains nothing but the principles of possible experience, it is logic of truth. The proposition a priori, which precedes all experience, is certain, for what is more certain than experience, and only for the sake of so far is it certain. It is here quite particular with the understanding: with the concepts of which it avails itself to make experience possible, it attempts to outstrip experience. What arises from that? They must all lose meaning, since beyond the boundaries of experience the categories have no object, so all cognition through categories, if it goes beyond experience, is illusion, and the dialectic is logic of illusion. We will, as is easy to see, not show to contrive an illusion, but through which one can discover the illusion — and the dialectic is the greatest end of transcendental philosophy. But the analytic must go ahead. We will analyze the concepts and proceed dogmatically here. When we come to the synthetic propositions, we will show that the one that was to be proved only from pure concepts can do so only with regard to possible experience. — The dialectic we want to leave out here and to reserve what belongs to it, where it can be well inserted, and here we will be practiced in the critique.",["OCR corrections made silently: 'wrie' → 'wie' (twice); 'ltens' read as '1tens' ('1st'); 'Obiecte' = Objecte, rendered 'objects' like Gegenstände (one English word for both Gegenstand and Object).","'Reine Verstandesbegriffe' / 'Reine Vernunft Begriffe' rendered 'pure concepts of the understanding' / 'pure concepts of reason'; 'Erfahrungsbegriff' and 'Erfahrungs Begriff' both 'concept of experience'.","'wißen' (wissen, verb) rendered 'know'; 'Ansicht' rendered 'view'; 'Anordnung' 'arrangement'; 'Vorbedingungen' 'preconditions'; 'exponirt' 'exposited' (cf. Erörterung, expositio).","'Praedicabilien'/'Praedicamenten' rendered 'predicables'/'predicaments'; 'accidenz' rendered 'accident'; 'caußa' = *causa*; 'Omnitudo' = *omnitudo*.","Ambiguity kept: in the parenthesis, 'wie der Autor, die sind schon weit schwerer' (the 'as the author' clause has no verb; the full stop before 'wie' is as printed).","Ambiguity kept: 'daß der, der nur aus puren Begriffen sollte bewiesen werden, es nur in Rücksicht auf mögliche Erfahrung kann' — the antecedent of 'der' (presumably masculine, e.g. Satz) is not given; 'es' is likewise left unresolved.","Doubtful sense kept as printed: 'nur um so weit willen ist sie gewiß' (possibly corrupt or elliptical); 'Es ist hier ganz besonders mit dem Verstand' also kept literal.","'Aesthetic', 'Analytic', 'Dialectic', 'Critic' rendered 'aesthetic', 'analytic', 'dialectic', 'critique'; 'Schein' 'illusion'; 'Grenzen' 'boundaries'; 'Sätze' 'propositions'; 'principien' 'principles'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-024":["We now want to return to our author. But we cannot possibly proceed according to a system without laying a foundation beneath it. Our author had, like all the others, the intention of sketching a system, but freedom was entirely lacking, or the principle for ordering the manifold. In general the idea of metaphysics was lacking: it was no system, but an aggregate. Hence the author also cannot give an account of whether these were all the pure concepts. - — If we therefore have no system, then we will analyze all the concepts that he presents, even if they do not have the order of the categories. Meanwhile we will appeal to the categories and remark under which of them this or that concept belongs. In the Critique of Pure Reason, which Professor Kant has published, the domain of pure reason is dissected, the idea established from which all concepts of the understanding can be divided. The concepts themselves are not all analyzed, for it was not necessary, because what mattered to us was only to establish the domain and the boundaries of pure reason, so the parts could be left out. They are dissected insofar as it was necessary to grasp them in the system of pure reason. Now we must undertake this work of dissection, to be sure not according to the order of the categories. But we want to refer to it. What we have before us is analytic; here something essential is at once lacking to us, namely a principle of all synthetic propositions a priori. One can say that the sum total of metaphysics comes down to showing the possibility and the criterion of truth of all synthetic propositions a priori. A principle of all synthetic judgments a posteriori is experience; but that of synthetic judgments a priori we do not yet have. All concepts that we have contain a synthesis, and if we have seen into how it is possible, then we will soon also have a criterion of truth. Our author, who presents synthetic propositions a priori, we want to criticize, but really not him, for that would produce little benefit, but the whole of human reason, e.g. the proposition of sufficient ground. We will show that it is impossible to prove such propositions a priori through pure concepts, and to assign to each of them a place in the system according to the categories. We take first the concept. The author has here followed Wolff, for he saw well that the *principium contradictionis* is not sufficient, so he took the *principium rationis sufficientis* in addition. Hence the author here treats straightaway of the ground. We take first the concep",["'Begrif' at the end is cut off in the passage (the word breaks off, evidently 'Begriff'); translated as 'concep' to mark the break, not completed.","'Vorgehen' (OCR 'Vorgehen' with capital) read as 'vorgehen'; 'ohnmöglich' = 'unmöglich' (impossible), rendered 'cannot possibly'; 'Hie Begriffe' read as 'Die Begriffe'; 'festzusezzen' read as 'festzusetzen'.","Rein = pure; Umfang = domain; Grenzen = boundaries; Grund = ground; zureichend = sufficient (here 'sufficient' also renders Latin *sufficiens*/'sufficient' in the Latin phrase's gloss); Satz = proposition throughout; Urtheil = judgment.","'Summe' rendered 'sum total' (Inbegriff is not the German word here; 'Summe' is the word actually used, so 'sum total' is a choice, 'sum' being the plain cognate).","'zergliedern' (dissect) and 'analysiren' (analyze) are kept distinct; 'Mannigfaltige' = manifold; 'Wesentliches' = something essential.","'Zu thun war' in 'weil uns nur darum zu thun war' is idiomatic ('what mattered to us'); the German 'so konnten die Theile ausgelassen werden' is kept as a main clause after 'denn ... weil ...' clause.","The ambiguous 'darauf' in 'Wir wollen uns aber darauf beziehen' (to the order of the categories, or to the categories) is left as in the German; English 'to it' is likewise ambiguous.","The dash sequence '- —' after 'wären' is carried over as printed (OCR)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-025":["2nd Chapter",["'2tes Capitel' = 'zweites Kapitel'; rendered as an ordinal heading."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-026":["On Ground and Sequel",["Folge = sequel (glossary); 'Grund' = ground. 'Vom Grund und Folge' is kept in the German's uninflected form."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-027":["*Ratio*, *respectus*, is a manifold, insofar as through the one of it another is posited or canceled. Thus every relation is *relatio vel nexus, vel oppositionis*, e.g. the citizens of a republic are *in relatione nexus*, through the one a benefit is posited in the other, or *in relatione oppositionis*, insofar as the interest of one conflicts with that of the other. (The relation of the ground and the sequel and v.v. is *nexus*. — If a thing were not a ground of another, and this its sequel, the things would be entirely separated. The thing relates as ground to its sequel, i.e. *nexus a posteriori* and conversely, that is *nexus a priori*. — Something that was not in such a *nexu*, hence not ground and sequel, would be isolated, that is an Italian word and means Isola, an island; for then we would not know at all how we had come to it, would also have no mark whether it belongs to our cognition, hence all our cognitions are *in nexu*.) (*Nexus* as well as *oppositio* are either analytic, if the connection is in accordance with the proposition of identity, or synthetic, if it is not in accordance with this proposition — *oppositio* is analytic — in accordance with the *principium contradictionis*, is synthetic, if it is not in accordance with the *principium contradictionis* — that one can call *nexus* and *oppositio realis* — the analytic logical *nexus*, connection and contradiction rest on grounds, are logical or real. — They can be *rationis ponendi*, where something is posited in accordance with rules of identity, or not in accordance with the rules of identity, and *tollendi*, where something is canceled in accordance with the principle of identity or not, e.g. extension is a ground of divisibility, this is posited through that in accordance with the rules of identity, — but: every body has attractive force, here this is posited through that (body), but not in accordance with the rules of identity, and the *nexus* is real, that one logical. The real *nexus* is only possibility, to cognize *a posteriori*. The conflict is logical, if it takes place, *ob principium contradictionis* — real even without that — logical *opposita*, *simul sumta dant nihil negativum*, i.e. the impossible, *realiter opposita simul sumta dant nihil privativum*, i.e. lack, and that can certainly be thought; if there are 2 opposing grounds in the same subject, then the result is zero, therefore if there are 2 logically opposing grounds, then the result is the impossible. Logically opposed is that *quo posito tollitur aliud* — really opposed is that *quo posito tollitur ratio realem*, not the *aliud*). The opposition we can regard as the connection with the opposite, e.g. something is opposed to motion, so it is connected with the rest of the bodies. — Under *connexum* we want to understand every relation, as much *nexus* as *oppositum*, and say: *connexa sunt, quorum uno posito ponitur aliud*, e.g. when the horses move, then the wagon is also moved; in the case of other connection there is a *respectus* of two, namely *unum, quod ponitur, et aliud, quod uno posito ponitur*. *A ponitur, B aliud quod posito A ponitur*, thus in every *nexus* there are 2 *correlata*; the *unum quo posito ponitur aliud*, we call ground, the *aliud* sequel. *A est ratio, B rationatum*. In the relation of the ground and sequel we distinguish the ground, — namely that through which I posit something — and sequel, which is that which is posited. By the definition I cannot distinguish ground and sequel. For I can say just as well: *posito B, ponitur A* (namely in the case of hypothetical judgments, there we say: *posito antecedens, ponitur consequens*, but not v.v., rather: *posito consequens ponitur quod* the *antecedens*). Now we must return to logic, which contains the forms of our understanding without distinction of the objects. In it we see that we can infer from the ground to the sequel, but not conversely from the sequel to a determinate ground. The *nexus* of the sequel with the ground is certain, but not with the said ground. Now our definition is at once put in order: *Ratio est id, quo posito aliud determinate ponitur, rationatum quod non ponitur nisi posito alia*. — One can also say: *rationatum est quo posito ponitur aliud* — *sed indeterminate*; for if there is a sequel, then there must always also be a ground, and if something is a ground, then there must always also be a sequel, only in the first case it is *indeterminate*, in the other *determinate*.) In the case of many a thing one can see that it is a sequel, but the ground we cannot know. Now we have a criterion of the ground, namely: *quo posito determinate, ponitur aliud*. *Determinate* signifies *secundum regulam generalem*. Every ground gives a rule, therefore the *nexus* of the ground and the sequel is necessary. (Logical ground is a cognition, upon which another follows in accordance with a rule. E.g. necessity is the ground of unchangeability. The one is not the cause of the existence of the other. Real ground is the thing upon which another follows.) If something is posited, then thereby another is posited in accordance with a general rule. The expression sequel is indeterminate, often one uses it of time, we will therefore often avail ourselves of the expression *rationatum*. Thus it signifies the interconnection with the preceding. (Ground contains that upon which it follows in accordance with a general rule,) e.g. he always had the fever, but when he used China, it disappeared, so China is the ground of the disappearing of the fever. The criterion of the ground is not that something follows upon it, but that something follows in accordance with general rules. — Where does the concept belong? In the category of relation. — But to presuppose the concept of ground to the concept of cause, that would be the *praedicamentum* of the latter, and we have not brought it into the table of categories? *Responsio*: The two concepts of ground and sequel are logical, but not transcendental. Cause and effect are things. Cause is that from which the existence of another follows. In logic existence is not spoken of at all — not how a thing is the ground of another thing, but how a concept is the ground of other concepts.",["Folge = 'sequel' throughout (glossary); Grund = 'ground'; Wirkung = 'effect'; Ursache = 'cause'. 'Folge' is not the same word as Folgerung ('consequence'), which does not occur here.","aufgehoben / aufgehoben wird / Latin tollere: rendered 'canceled' (glossary gives no entry for aufheben); 'tollendi' and 'tollitur' left in Latin.","'v.v.' is the manuscript's abbreviation (presumably 'vice versa'); kept as it stands. 'umgekehrt' = 'conversely'.","Satz der identitaet = 'proposition of identity'; 'Satz' is glossed 'proposition'. 'Regel' = 'rule'; 'Erkenntniß' = 'cognition'; 'schließen' = 'infer'; 'wißen' = 'know'.","'Der reale nexus ist nur Möglichkeit, a posteriori zu erkennen' is kept as awkwardly as the German; a word may have dropped out (e.g. 'nur [in der] Möglichkeit').","'ob principium contradictionis' is left unchanged: 'ob' may be the Latin preposition ('on account of') or an OCR/transcript slip for another word; the reading is doubtful.","OCR: 'anteceden«' read as *antecedens*; 'ponitur quod dem anteceden«' is left as a mixed Latin-German fragment and may be corrupt; 'posito alia' is kept as printed (one expects *aliud*). 'LTnterschied' read as 'Unterschied'; 'Grandes' as 'Grundes'; the stray 'I' after 'Die Opposition' omitted. The parenthesis closing after 'determinate' has no opening one in the passage; kept.","'China' (cinchona bark) is kept as in the German; 'Isola' is the transcript's Italian word, kept; 'praedicamentum' and 'Responsio' kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-028":["We have here spoken of such a relation of concepts, where one can infer determinately from one to the other; but that there are such things, from one of which I can infer to the other, belongs to metaphysics. Now we want to consider the possibility of the real ground: that if one thing is posited, the other lets itself be posited thereby as determined, — not the logical, since I can infer from one to the other. — If the thing, which is to be inferred to, is *realiter* distinguished from the other, then no human reason can see the possibility that one thing could be the ground of another thing; experience teaches it, but reason cannot make it comprehensible to us. (All grounds are either *rationes cognoscendi*, when a cognition is a ground of others, e.g. composite is a cognitive ground of divisible — or when one sees a man's footprints, one says, people have been there, the former is thus a cognitive ground, not *ratio fiendi*, otherwise the footprints would have to be the ground of the existence of the man; *Ratio essendi* is the ground of that which belongs to the thing considered according to its possibility, e.g. the 3 sides in the A are the ground of the 3 angles. Here I speak merely of the possible A, considered in actuality, e.g. ink and pen is *ratio fiendi* of the A, and *ratio fiendi* is cause). But they must be really, not concealedly, distinguished, otherwise they are after all one and the same, e.g. if I posit A and B is nevertheless posited, it is not to be seen at all. I can nevertheless infer in this way, otherwise logic would be wrong. But I cannot do so with things *realiter* distinguished. Things are logically distinguished when they are actually one and the same, but concealed, and the *nexus* of the ground and the sequel is analytic according to the rule of identity: (What exists as *rationatum* is *dependens*) e.g. because something is a body, it is divisible. The inference that one has in general logic from ground to sequels, which is *nexus logicus*, is easily comprehended, but the real *nexus*, which is synthetic, not at all, where the sequel is really distinguished from the ground, e.g. snow with salt causes frost. Here there is no *nexus* according to the rule of identity. To the ground there comes a sequel that did not lie at all in the concept of the ground, and this synthetic *nexus* can be seen through by no human's reason. It is a remarkable property of the understanding: it infers from the ground to the sequel, and cannot see the possibility of this — and then more still occurs. — All relation is, as said, *nexus oppositorum*. Let us think to ourselves a logical *oppositio*, which is analytic. The real opposition is synthetic. Logical opposition is *contradictoria*. A square circle is a contradiction. Two logical *opposita* cancel each other entirely, and nothing remains (*nihil negativum*). Two real *opposita* do not cancel each other, but the sequels cancel each other, and what arises through their combination is zero, nought, *nihil privativum*, e.g. I get an inheritance, that causes pleasure, I must pay off just as much debt, that causes displeasure. The *opposita* can very well be together, only the sequels cancel each other, and I remain in the state of equality. Two real *opposita* can thus be in one thing at the same time, for the sequels are only canceled, but logical *opposita* cannot be in one thing at the same time (logical *opposita* are either *contradictorie opposita*, as e.g. A *et non* A, or *disparata*, e.g. every body is either red or green. The *disparata* contain, besides the *contradictio opposita*, still something that is added, e.g. something is either red — the contradictory opposition would be not red. In the disparate opposition there is still something over and above, namely green. The *principium exclusi medii* does not say that of 2 disparate predicates one belongs to the things, but *contra* opposition). One can say that the whole play of alteration of the *universum* comes from real oppositions. — Because only sequels are canceled through this, the same thing is not canceled, but another, namely the sequel. The real oppositions can thus be together. We come to the concept of",["Folge is rendered 'sequel' throughout (glossary); Folgerung ('consequence') does not occur. 'Grund und Folge' = 'ground and sequel'.","Erkenntniß Grund = 'cognitive ground' (Erkenntnis = cognition, glossary); *rationes cognoscendi* kept in Latin.","'Daseyn' = 'existence' (Dasein); 'Wirklichkeit' = 'actuality'; 'Möglichkeit' = 'possibility'.","'begreiflich' = 'comprehensible' (begreifen = comprehend); 'einsehen' = 'see' (not in glossary); 'Verstand' = 'understanding'.","OCR corrections: 'geredt-' read as 'geredt'; 'beiten' read as 'Seiten' ('sides'); 'e.gr.', 'z. B.' left as printed; 'Opposita' with stray hyphens and 'rnedii' read as 'medii'.","'3 beiten im A' read as '3 Seiten im A'; the OCR is uncertain, and 'Seiten' is a conjecture (the sense 'sides' fits 'Winkel').","'Verbindung' = 'combination'; 'Zustande der Gleichheit' = 'state of equality'.","'aufheben' rendered 'cancel' (sich aufheben = 'cancel each other'); 'aufgehoben' = 'canceled'.","The clause 'sondern contra Opposition' is obscure in the German as printed (possibly corrupt) and is rendered as it stands; 'eckigter Zirkel' = 'square circle' (literally 'angular circle')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-029":["Possibility and impossibility",["Heading; Möglichkeit = possibility, Unmöglichkeit = impossibility."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-030":["The author speaks first of impossibility, then of something and of the possible.",["'Etwas' = 'something'; 'möglichen' = 'the possible' (substantivized adjective)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-031":["Remark: If 2 concepts of, for example here: possible and impossible, are opposed, then they always stand under a higher concept — for opposition always represents a disjunctive proposition. Now there must surely be a divided concept, which has the opposed concepts as members of the division, and that is a higher concept. What is possible or impossible? The object (yet one can think object also with impossible predicates) is accordingly presumably the highest concept in ontology. The possible we call thing, something, and opposed to this is the impossible, nothing. But nothing is opposed to the object, hence it might well be a still higher concept than the something. E.g. by thing one understands 1. object in general, 2. the possible, 3. the positive or reality, 4. that which is actual.) One defines possibility falsely if one says, possible is that which contains no contradiction; what contradicts itself is impossible, but not v.v., the thought of it is possible, but whether the thing is objectively possible is not yet certain. Of what the thought does not contradict itself, that is not possible, also not impossible. It is difficult to define it so that it goes at the same time to things and thoughts. What agreed with all possible rules of thinking would be possible, but then one would have to know all possible rules. No possible is that of which the thought of a possible metaphysics is possible, that of which the thought is possible, and the thing in and for itself without regard to experience is not possible, e.g. a 4-cornered circle, is logically impossible. For the thought is impossible. Logical possibility is possibility of the concept, and the *principium contradictionis* is a sufficient criterion of it. Different from this is real possibility, there the *principium contradictionis* does not suffice. But what is logically impossible is also really impossible, but not what is logically possible is also really possible. (The impossible is 2-fold: I. If either the concept itself is nothing, e.g. 4-cornered circle, II. or if no possible object corresponds, e.g. the fairy tales.) Logical possibility is that in which there is no contradiction. Metaphysical possibility is when the thing is possible in and for itself without relation to my thoughts. No human being can have insight into that. How am I to judge of a thing what it may be in and for itself, without relation to experience? The possibility of the thought does not constitute the possibility of the thing. The thing itself we cognize purely through concepts, and then no mark of possibility remains left to us. Logic we can have insight into, but that makes nothing for the thing. What does not conflict with the laws of experience is physical possibility, this one can surely have insight into, e.g. that a large palace has been completed in 4 weeks, is physically impossible. Morally possible is that which is possible according to the rules of morals, and does not conflict with the universal law of freedom. It is necessary to note these differences. Many philosophers have confused logical with metaphysical possibility, e.g. the possibility or impossibility of ghosts no logical philosophy can demonstrate, but no reasonable person must believe them, because he has no concept of their possibility or impossibility. — One must not build on experience here, for commonly those who experience it are weak people, and they experience much that is not true. —",["\"Wenn 2 Begriffe von, zum Beispiel hier…\": the \"von\" has no complement in the text as printed; kept as \"of\" and left unresolved (possibly a transcription slip).","The closing parenthesis after \"wirklich ist.)\" has no opening parenthesis in the passage (it was presumably opened before the passage began); kept as it stands.","Ding and Sache are both rendered \"thing\"; Gegenstand is \"object\"; wirklich is \"actual\"; \"realitaet\" (Realität) is \"reality\", and real/reale is \"real(ly)\". \"ohiective\" is read as OCR for \"objective\".","\"Wovon der Gedanke sich nicht widerspricht, das ist nicht möglich, auch nicht unmöglich\" is kept literally; the sense is doubtful and the text may be defective or mis-transcribed.","\"Kein Mögliches ist das, wovon der Gedanke einer möglichen Metaphysic möglich ist, das, von dem…\" is syntactically obscure or corrupt in the transcript; translated as it stands without emendation.","\"v.v.\" kept as in the source (presumably *vice versa*). \"hinreichend\" = \"sufficient\"; \"criterium\" = \"criterion\"; \"einsehen\" = \"have insight into\"; \"pur\" = \"purely\" (rein).","\"kein Vernünftiger muß sie aber glauben\": \"muß\" is ambiguous between \"need not\" and \"must not\"; left as \"must\". \"sie\" (the ghosts) is plural.","\"die sie erfahren\": \"sie\" could be \"experience\" (Erfahrung, singular) or \"ghosts\"; rendered \"it\", ambiguity retained. \"es sei … fertig geworden\" is subjunctive; rendered as a plain \"that\"-clause."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-032":["If in the concept a synthesis is thought, not in accordance with the proposition of contradiction or of identity, then it cannot be cognized at all by the proposition of contradiction, for it is not logical but real, and here we have no logical mark of possibility, e.g. every body is extended can be cognized in accordance with the proposition of contradiction. A non-extended body is a non-thing or impossible. Here we cognize the possibility *a priori*, but analytically, and the possibility of an analytic ground can easily be seen *a priori*, e.g. every body has a changeability, analytic ground: it is a multitude of parts that can be separated, thus the concept of body contains the analytic ground of alteration. The possibility of an analytic ground and its sequel flows from the proposition of contradiction. But if I think a ground whose sequel is really different, then that cannot be cognized from the proposition of contradiction. — It does not contradict itself at all that because A is posited, therefore also B, but for that reason one cannot assume it as possible, because it is a synthetic judgment and we do not have the slightest concept of how that comes about. — Real possibility cannot be seen *a priori* without pure concepts. We will see in what follows that the criterion of the possibility of things is this: The synthesis that contains the possibility of the conditions of experience is possible, but that goes only to the objects of experience. (If something is considered apart from the *nexus*, one says: *spectatur per se* or *interne*; in the *nexus* one says: *interne spectatur*.) — Something is internally or in and for itself possible, and relatively in relation and connection with other things. (The *interne impossibile* is *nihil negativum*; nothing through a mere lack of something, one calls nothing. — *Interne* much is possible that is not possible *externe* in the connection, i.e. also conditionally possible, the condition here is as much as ground, e.g. it is in itself possible that a human being can become rich, but also conditionally, for his parents are rich, i.e. there is still besides a ground for it. — To take condition in this way is not in accordance with linguistic usage — properly it signifies a restriction, e.g. many a commander can give commands if the people agree to it.) The inner criterion of things is the proposition of contradiction, but far from sufficient, and relatively the possibility of a thing is in relation to its grounds or sequels. E.g. It is possible that the human being arrive at great riches, but because of laziness, unsuitability and lack of rich relatives it is impossible. — What contradicts the conditions under which something is possible is hypothetically impossible, what contradicts itself is absolutely impossible. Something can be possible in itself, but hypothetically, either under its logical or real hypothesis, it is impossible. The hypothetical impossibility tacitly presupposes the absolute possibility, for what in itself is nothing at all can be considered in logical relation. We now come to the famous proposition of sufficient ground (which is the first synthetic proposition *a priori*), The author speaks here not of existing but of possible things. Expressed in its whole generality it reads: *nihil est sine ratione*. Here we want to substitute other words: *id quod habet sme rationem aliquam est rationatum*, thus: *omne possibile est rationatum*, is entirely the same. (Thus the author expresses it, but he nevertheless afterwards asserts a being that is ground but no sequel. Now he helps himself out by saying that it has the ground in itself, that is absurd, the ground must always be something other, and if it is not, then there is no ground. It is as if I say, I want something, why? because I want it, i.e. there is still no ground at all, and the veile one must call *originarium*, (likewise the supreme being *ens originarium*). Now the proposition strikes the eye as very contradictory, we see nothing but sequels and no sufficient ground that would be merely a ground. The sum total of all things would be something, *quod non poneretur nisi posito nihil*. The ground of the possibility of all things would therefore be a non-thing. We will therefore have to require a restriction. Namely: everything contingent has a ground, contingent is that whose opposite is possible, that we cannot cognize from pure concepts. The logically possible, to be sure, but the real not. The logical contingency can be cognized from pure concepts, but the real possibility of the opposite can in no way be seen. Since the contingency of things cannot be cognized *a priori* through pure concepts, we must say, everything empirically contingent has its ground, i.e. the contingent in the appearance; empirically contingent is that whose opposite is empirically possible, as experience shows that something is which previously was not. Something happens, always signifies the empirical contingency. Thus the proposition also reads so: everything that happens has a ground. Happening contains within itself an arising or a passing away, those are the *species*, that the *genus*. This proposition of sufficient ground is supposed to be proved, but not from pure concepts. For it is a synthetic proposition, where I go out from my concept in order to add another that was not contained in my given one. But we do find in ourselves a certain partiality for this proposition, as the author presents it. For every assertion that is groundless is false. Here is precisely the delusion. If we must give a ground for everything that happens, it does not yet follow that things in themselves must have a ground, and in this one becomes confused, e.g. the highest being has no ground and yet we must adduce grounds that it is there. One can also express the proposition thus: Everything that follows in sensibility or sensible intuition, that follows in the concepts of the understanding. Or, what can be represented as sequel of sensibility, can be represented as sequel through the understanding. Through this this proposition recommends itself — since, as was just said, it cannot be proved from pure concepts, it will have to have another proof. — We will see in what follows, synthetic cognitions *a priori* have a validity insofar as they are principles of the possibility of experience (all our judgments have a ground why the *praedicat* belongs to the *subject*, in analytic judgments it is a ground of the analysis, in synthetic ones of the synthesis. — We also have judgments *a posteriori*; these are either judgments of perception or of experience, these always presuppose those, — the former have only subjective validity; I say e.g. I am cold. The latter objective or general validity. That has therefore merely subjective validity, if it is to have objective validity or be a judgment of experience, then the sequence of perceptions must be determined according to rules, i.e. be necessary — then, one says, there is also in the object a sequel, that one follows upon the other according to a rule, is ground, thus the *principium rationis sufficientis* is the ground of the possibility of experience, without that there would be no experience.) How is experience possible, or how does the understanding make out of perceptions cognitions of things? It must have principles, these are synthetic propositions and to these now the proposition also belongs (the perceptions can follow upon one another without the things therefore following. I can e.g. in a house perceive first the roof, then the foundation, then the windows etc., without the things following so, for they are simultaneous.) It (the proposition) holds of all objects of experience and we will see that it cannot be proved otherwise than as a proposition that holds of all objects of experience, but not beyond them, and so it goes with all synthetic propositions *a priori*, and the whole error of metaphysics consists in this, that propositions that go only to experience are used beyond the same, although they hold only of all possible objects of experience, not of things in themselves. They are rules of the synthesis of appearances, through which merely experience is distinguished from dreams, and so also *principium rationis sufficientis*. The author has proved it, but very strangely, namely: if a thing had no ground, then its ground would be nothing. For nothing would then be the ground of something, but that is a contradiction. He has here, which is hardly to be forgiven him, confused the logical and metaphysical nothing. One can easily refute this proof if one parodies it, that is proves something absurd from it, e.g. you have money in the chest for if you did not have that, then there would be nothing of money in the chest, then nothing would be money, therefore you must have money. The error is this, one means *nihil* once as a negation, the other time as concept. It is not the same whether I say: *nihil est sine ratione*, or *nihil est ratio*. (From a pure concept this cannot be proved, we would have to take a third, with which the concepts agree, namely the concept of a possible experience. The empirical cognition, insofar as it is considered as generally valid, i.e. necessary, is called experience, the empirical cognition insofar as it is subjectively valid, perception, and that is indisputable, but only for me, e.g. if the proposition: upon sunshine warmth follows, is to be a proposition of experience, — then it must read: upon sunshine warmth follows for everyone or necessarily. But what is that upon which something follows necessarily? Ground. If therefore the above perception is to become experience, then I must represent to myself that in the sunshine lies the ground of the warmth, then it is valid for everyone. These principles now of the necessary combination of perceptions are synthetic principles *a priori*, and to these our proposition also belongs — without it no experience can take place. If the above proposition is to become a proposition of experience, then it must stand under the principle of the necessary connection of perceptions. The chief connection is that of the ground and the sequel. — If connections of things are to be general, then there must be a constant rule, otherwise I cannot say that the sequence of things is to hold generally and then it is again no experience.",["Folge is rendered 'sequel' for the sequel of a ground, 'sequence' for the order of perceptions or things (Folge der Wahrnehmungen), and 'in what follows' for 'in der Folge'; one German word, three English renderings, as the glossary allows.","Grund = ground; Satz = proposition (Satz des Wiederspruchs, Satz des zureichenden Grundes); Grundsatz = principle; Gültigkeit = validity; eingesehen werden = 'be seen' (einsehen is not in the glossary and is kept distinct from erkennen = cognize).","OCR corrected silently: Pertheylichkeit → Parteilichkeit ('partiality'), I olge/un → Folge/nun, lenes → jenes, restiiction → restriction, iene → jene, beständigeRegel → beständige Regel; the stray ■ before 'die erstem' is omitted.","'sme rationem' (OCR) is doubtful, probably 'suam' or a similar word; left as printed. 'das veile' is too garbled to read and is left untranslated; 'originarium' follows it as in the text.","'denn was an sich selbst gar nichts ist, kann in logischer relation betrachtet werden' is translated as it stands; the sense is obscure and the transcript may be corrupt (a 'nicht' may be missing).","'Hiedurch empfiehlt sich dieser Satz' is rendered 'Through this this proposition recommends itself', preserving the doubling. 'Blendwerk' = 'delusion' (Schein is reserved for 'illusion'). Unding = 'non-thing'.","The author's 'Nichts durch einen bloßen Mangel woran, nennt man nichts' is awkward in the German and is kept awkward. The parenthesis opened at '(Aus purem Begriff' is never closed in the passage as given, and the passage breaks off there.","Latin kept in italics: *a priori*, *a posteriori*, *nexus*, *spectatur per se*, *interne*, *externe*, *nihil negativum*, *nihil est sine ratione*, *rationatum*, *ens originarium*, *species*, *genus*, *principium rationis sufficientis*, etc. 'Autor' = 'author' (Baumgarten, not named in the passage)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-033":["But we said above that empirical cognitions or experiences contain no necessity. It does not teach that something is necessary, abstracted from all perceptions. — They presuppose principles that are necessary and under which alone it is possible. It does not teach the necessity of things. — All principles of synthesis that I think a priori are principles of the possibility of experience and go merely to things of experience, but are nevertheless a priori, because they precede all experience, which is possible only through them. They are also synthetic, just like experience, for it is nothing but synthesis of perceptions, which holds necessarily, which I can cognize through concepts a priori. A synthesis that is given in no experience is here flatly impossible. But these synthetic principles a priori I can have insight into, and I do actually have insight into them, because otherwise there would be no experience. I can have insight into them, for they are merely cognitions that lie in my understanding. The author says, if everything has its ground and its sequel, then everything is connex a priori and a posteriori; that is correct, but does not fit all things, because otherwise there would be no highest ground.)",["Erkenntniße = cognitions; Nothwendigkeit = necessity; Wahrnehmungen = perceptions; Grundsätze and Principien both appear: Grundsätze = principles, Principien = principles (one English word for two German words; Latin-derived Principien marked here).","'Sie' in the second and third sentences shifts between singular ('Sie lehrt', Erfahrung) and plural ('Sie setzen ... voraus'); the antecedents are left as ambiguous as in the German. 'unter denen sie allein möglich ist' keeps the singular 'sie' after the plural 'denen'.","Folge rendered 'sequel' per glossary; 'connex' is left as in the text (German form of connexus/connexio, cf. Verknüpfung = connection); 'synthesis' kept as in the source. 'a priori' and 'a posteriori' are Latin in the source and are not italicized here, following the passage's own presentation of them as plain text.","einsehen = 'have insight into' (not in glossary). 'wirkleich' silently corrected to 'wirklich'; 'platt' = 'flatly'.","The closing parenthesis has no opening parenthesis in this passage; it is kept as printed. 'daß etwas nothwendig sey, abstrahirt von allen Wahrnehmungen': the participle could attach to 'etwas' or to the subject of 'lehrt'; left ambiguous."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-001":["On the sufficient and insufficient.",["Zureichend = sufficient (glossary); Unzureichend = insufficient. Heading rendered as nominalized adjectives, as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-002":["A ground is sufficient in a twofold way 1. We can represent the ground to ourselves as a whole like an aggregate, 2. Like a series. The completeness of a ground as aggregate is sufficient, as series it is also sufficient. The ground through which everything in the sequel is determined is sufficient as aggregate, hence everything must have a sufficient ground. An insufficient ground is also true, it is a part of the sufficient one. The ground in the series of grounds subordinated to one another is sufficient in no other way than if we trace everything back to the first ground. It is commonly called sufficient if it is capable of understanding the consequences from it immediately.",["Grund = ground; Folge = sequel (glossary), Folgen in the last sentence also 'sequel/consequences': 'die Folgen' there is rendered 'consequences' since the text uses Folge for both; one English word is not imposed here, a departure from strict consistency noted.","'wenn er fähig ist, die Folgen daraus unmittelbar zu verstehen': the subject of 'zu verstehen' is unexpressed; the German is kept as ambiguous (the ground is capable of understanding / one is capable of understanding through it); aggregat kept as in source.","'Vollständigkeit' = completeness; 'auf 2fache Art' = 'in a twofold way' (Art = way)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-003":["The author proves the proposition, everything has its sequel, also just like the one, everything has its ground; but it also cannot be proved from pure concepts. We consider grounds in a series if one is the *rationatum* of the other. In a series there is always a ground immediate, and each is a ground of the sequel in part. — We can therefore represent grounds to ourselves complete, coordinated and subordinated. All our grounds are combinations, are coordination, i.e. the combination of the parts in an aggregate — and subordination, i.e. the combination of the parts in a series. A ground is sufficient *simpliciter* or *secundum quid*. Mediately something is a ground of something else if it is the ground by means of another, — immediately, if it is the ground not by means of another, *remota posita* or *ratiocinium* of something through many *rationes intermedias*. In the coordination of grounds one is the *complementum* of the other toward the sufficient ground, in subordination a ground is the sequel of the other. In a series there are *rationes immediatae, mediatae et simpliciter talis*. The coordination of grounds is combination of the same into an immediate sufficient ground, the subordination of grounds is combination of the same into a mediate sufficient ground.) (A ground can be sufficient with regard to its sequels, and with regard to itself; with regard to its sequels, if it determines everything that is contained in them, with regard to itself, if it presupposes no other ground. If I take the sum total of all world-causes, then these contain the complete ground of the present state of the same, but it is not sufficient for itself, i.e. *simpliciter talis*, but only with regard to its sequels, i.e. *secundum quid* — in the world we do not find the *rationes simpliciter talis*. The relation of the ground and the sequel contains only logical consequence, i.e. if one is posited, so is the other, and indeed necessarily, and insofar it contains the ground of the possibility of the interconnected perceptions, i.e. experience. In the series of subordinated concepts that which is in a certain aim sufficient is *sufficient secundum quid*, in every aim *simpliciter*; the following *canones* we must cite:",["Inbegriff = sum total; Weltursachen = world-causes; Folgerung = consequence; Absicht = aim; Zustand = state; Verbindung = combination; Begriffe = concepts; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wahrnehmungen = perceptions.","Folge = 'sequel' throughout (glossary); 'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'. 'ein Grund unmittelbar' is kept elliptical ('a ground immediate'). 'zum Theil' = 'in part'.","Latin terms kept in italics: *rationatum*, *simpliciter*, *secundum quid*, *remota posita*, *ratiocinium*, *rationes intermedias*, *complementum*, *rationes immediatae, mediatae et simpliciter talis*, *canones*. 'sufficient secundum quid' (German 'sufficient') is Latinate in the source and kept as such; I italicized only 'secundum quid'/'simpliciter'… the word 'sufficient' as printed.","'Mittelbar ... unmittelbar': rendered 'Mediately ... immediately'. 'die Folgen' was not spelled out to differ from 'Folge'. 'enthalten die' = 'these contain' (die = demonstrative/article ambiguity: 'die' may be the relative or demonstrative pronoun referring to the Weltursachen; kept 'these').","'ist ... sufficient secundum quid' — the text is slightly irregular; 'in gewißer Absicht' = 'in a certain aim' (Absicht = aim). The opening parenthesis before 'Ein Grund kann' and closing parentheses at 'Grunde.)' are as printed; the second parenthesis remains unclosed at the passage's end. Passage breaks off with a colon.","'sich ... vorstellen' = 'represent to ourselves'. 'complet' rendered 'complete' (source's Latinate 'complet')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-004":["*posita ratione, ponitur rationatum*, is already evident from the definition of the ground, further *posito rationato, ponitur ratio quaedam non certa*. E.g. If one is strangled, he is dead, but if he is dead, he need not have been strangled.",["Latin kept in italics; Grund = ground; Erklärung/definition: the source has Latin-form 'definition', kept as 'definition'. 'Z.E.' = 'E.g.' ('zum Exempel').","'erwürgt' = 'strangled'; 'darf ... nicht gerade ... sein' = 'need not ... precisely/necessarily be', rendered 'need not have been'; 'sein' printed for 'seyn' as in source. The tense in the English ('have been') is an adjustment; literally 'need not be strangled'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-005":["To determine means to attribute to the thing one of 2 contradictorily opposed opposite predicates. I can compare everything with any possible opposite of a predicate and with the predicate itself. If I attribute the predicate to the thing, the opposite is excluded. Of 2 *praedicatis oppositis* one must belong to the thing, hence every thing must be determinable. (The author's inferences belong in logic, where they all occur as well, only there ground is called *antecedens* and sequel *consequens*. *Sublato rationato tollitur ratio*, for if the ground remained, the sequel would remain too, but not *sublata ratione tollitur rationatum*, for the sequel can have another ground.) (Since one of 2 contradictorily opposed predicates belongs to every sequel, each is thoroughly determined — but one still says that a thing is indeterminate with regard to certain *praedicata opposita* if, through the concept we have of it, neither of the two belongs to it, e.g. every human being that exists is either learned or unlearned, but through the concept that I have of the human being it is undetermined whether he is learned or unlearned. There is only one being that is thoroughly determined through its concept, since to it, namely, all reality belongs; otherwise things are, with regard to or through their concept, indeterminate in many respects.) A universal concept is not determined in itself, e.g. a human being is either learned or unlearned, is undetermined. But a learned human being is determined. One can determine only through synthesis, not through analysis, for with regard to what lies in it, it was not undetermined. Then I can determine the concept only if I add something. Every ground of the determination is called *ratio determinans*. Our cognition, if it is to be determined, must have a ground, so too with things. If 2 things are undetermined with regard to 2 predicates, they can be determined through a determining ground. Determination is distinguished from logical predicates. (The logical predicate can be analytic, but determination is always synthetic, e.g. one says, the concept of body is determined through extension — I determine the concept through a predicate if without the same it would have been undetermined, — but the concept of body is not undetermined without extension, for that already lies in its concept, but weight is a determination). (Can every ground be determined or not, or is it only the sufficient one? Most still make a distinction here, but that is false — ground consists precisely in this, that *quo posito, determinate ponitur aliud* — the insufficient ground determines Something in the sequel — the sufficient one Everything.) Determination is a predicate of a thing, through which the opposite is excluded. That is the usual way of thinking determination, namely one regards it as a predicate that is added to the thing, through which the opposite is excluded. That is not enough. Determination is a synthetic predicate, e.g. a body is extended. Through this it is not yet determined. A learned human being is determined, for learnedness does not lie in the concept of the human being. — A predicate belongs to the thing internally if it already lies in its concept, externally if it belongs to it in reference to others. (Internal predicates are those that belong to the thing in itself. — One otherwise also divides them into absolute and respective. The latter are respective (*ad interna* or *externa*.) The *respectus ad externa* is relation, e.g. that the human being is master over his passions is respective, but *ad interna*; here I consider the relation of a power toward the subjected. Here there is thus a *respectus* ; respective *ad externa* is when I consider him as master and subject. The author explains what *idem* and *diversum* are: A and B *sunt eadem*, insofar as the representation of B is nothing but a repetition of the representation of A — if I can also represent B through the representation of A. But we see that 2 things that we call one and the same can be *eadem* only in some consideration, because otherwise one could not say: the other. Below more of this will occur. The author speaks *de attributis, modis et affectionibus*, that all belongs in logic. We note the following: We find in most philosophies a wavering distinction between nature and essence. What is the distinction? One is otherwise not accustomed to attribute nature to a thing that does not subsist for itself, e.g. of an A one says, he has a nature; with things that subsist for themselves one makes no distinction. Above we distinguished the analytic relation between ground and sequel; here the ground is logical, — and the synthetic relation between ground and sequel; here the ground is a real ground. The relation of the ground and the sequel is analytic if the predicate can be cognized analytically, synthetic if it cannot be cognized so. The analytically first would be ground of all predicates of a thing and is called the logical essence — the synthetically first would be ground of all predicates of a thing, is called the real essence or nature (the first internal ground of all that belongs to the possibility of a thing is essence — and of that which belongs to the actuality of a thing, is nature, e.g. to the A in geometry one will not ascribe nature, in the concept of every thing there is always something of it separable and inseparable, the 1st is called *ad poßibilitatem rei seu ad eßentiam pertinens*, the 2nd *extra eßentiale*, which does not hang together necessarily with the concept or possibility of the thing. The Deren can again be, *ut rationis*, that is *eßentia*, or *ut rationata*, that is *attributa* — *complexus eßentialiam*, i.e. the first true higher ground of all that belongs to the existence of a thing, is nature) or the first logical internal ground of all determinations of a thing is essence, the first internal real ground of the determinations of a thing is nature. To find the complete internal real ground of all determinations is impossible. The logical one is easy, since I need only analyze my concept, until I arrive at such concepts as are no longer derived from others. (If we complain that we do not know the essences of things, that concerns only nature, the essences we can know very well — for if something is not actual, it lies merely in my concept. — If I therefore deal with that which belongs to the possibility of a thing, I deal with that which lies in my concept of the thing — and that I can surely know. If one divides essence, like H. P. Kant, into logical and real essence, then the latter is nature.) (The attribute that can flow only from the whole essence of the thing is *proprium* — for another thing would have another essence. That which is always a *consectarium* from a part of the essence is *commune*, for the part can also occur in other things, e.g. the human being is a rational animal. — On account of the first he has reason, through this he is not distinguished from spirit, on account of the latter he has senses, there he is again not distinguished from the animal.) But what human being will undertake to indicate the whole real essence of things that are given through experience? Comparatively we can call something the real essence, or in a certain aim we can stop at the investigation of the real essence, and be satisfied with that. We often hear the complaint, the essences of things are unknown, we know only the surface. That is quite correct, but holds only for the real essence. Much belonging there we can cognize, but still not everything. This real essence is nature.",["Wesen: 'essence' throughout, except 'Es ist nur ein Wesen, welches ...', where 'being' is the glossary's other rendering; one English word is not kept for both senses there.","bestimmt / unbestimmt / determinirt / indeterminirt / determinabel / determinate: all rendered 'determined', 'undetermined', 'indeterminate', 'determinable' as shown; the Latinate determinirt and indeterminirt are not distinguished from bestimmt and unbestimmt (except that indeterminirt appears as 'indeterminate'). Bestimmung = 'determination'.","Gegentheil and entgegengesetzt are both 'opposite'; opponirt = 'opposed'. Verhältniß/Verhältnis = 'relation', as is Latin/German 'relation'; Beziehung = 'reference'; Ansehung = 'regard'; Stück (in vielen Stücken) = 'respects'. Macht = 'power'. Folge = 'sequel' throughout, 'Folge' vs. Latin *consequens* as in the text.","OCR corrections made silently: 'Polge' to Folge; 'Avie' to wie; 'lte' to 1te; 'zureichen -de' to zureichende.","'Da einer ieden Folge von 2 contradictorisch opponirten Praedicaten eines zukommt' is kept as it stands; 'Folge' here may be a misreading (Ding?), the sense is doubtful.","'Die Deren können wieder seyn': 'Deren' is unclear (possibly garbled for 'Diese'); left as 'The Deren'. 'complexus eßentialiam' is kept as printed (presumably *essentialium*).","'ein *respectus* ;' is left with the dangling space and semicolon as printed; something may be missing after it.","'denn so etwas nicht wirklich ist': 'so etwas' could be 'so (wenn) etwas' or 'such a thing'; rendered as 'if something'. 'er' in 'von einem A sagt man, er habe eine Natur' kept as 'he'; the sentence may be corrupt (a 'nicht' may be missing)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-006":["We come now to the concept of",["*Begriff* = concept."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-007":["Existence.",["*Existenz* = existence (the glossary gives the same English for *Dasein*)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-008":["In the case of the categories we listed under the 4th title, namely under modality: possibility, actuality and necessity, then we found that they are not at all determinations of a thing or synthetic predicates; they are namely concepts through which a thing with all predicates is posited. Possibility is nothing but the agreement of the thing with conditions of thinking. The conditions are analytic or synthetic, if it does not agree with the former, then it is impossible because of the principle of contradiction. But it must also agree with the synthesis. The principle that contains the synthetic conditions of thinking is provisionally this: All synthesis must contain the conditions under which the manifold is brought into a unity - or: to no thing can a synthetic predicate be attributed, except insofar as it is a possible experience. All synthetic judgments a priori express nothing but the conditions of a possible experience. E.g. Everything that exists in the world of sense is in space, i.e. insofar as it is in experience. When I say, a thing is possible, I think nothing more in addition. It is not a predicate that belongs to the thing, but to the position of the thing, insofar as it agrees with the laws of thinking. Actuality is *positio absoluta*, necessity, if *positio absoluta* is so constituted that its cancellation contradicts the laws of thinking. Possibility, actuality, necessity are not concepts of things in themselves, but possibility already presupposes the thing with all its predicates, and is the comparison of the thing with the laws of thinking, whether it can be thought or not. (Actuality is that to which an object in experience corresponds; actuality is necessity that follows from possibility). (In the case of actuality the object is added to a concept, but nothing to the object). Likewise actuality; they are distinguished only in the way a thing is posited. Although we have said that they are not predicates of things in themselves, but *modi*, how it is posited, we must nevertheless concede that all concepts, also modal concepts, can be made into predicates, but not into real predicates or determinations, but logical ones, E.g. God is possible. Possibility, actuality and necessity presuppose the thing, but their determinations are not themselves determinations. Possibility is a *position* respective to our thinking, actuality is *positio absoluta*. In actuality more is contained than in possibility, i.e. not, through actuality more is represented in the thing than through possibility, but more is added to our thinking. First I posit a thing respective to my thinking, now I posit it simply, thereby I think evidently more. For illustration we want to note the following: — . (breaks off)",["*Modahtaet* (OCR/orthography) read as *Modalität* and rendered 'modality'; *Wiederspruch* = contradiction; *Möglichkeit* = possibility, *Wirklichkeit* = actuality, *Nothwendigkeit* = necessity.","*Wirklichkeit* is rendered 'actuality' throughout here (glossary: 'reality/actuality'); *Realität* would be 'reality'; 'real predicates' translates *realen Praedicaten*.","*Object* and *Ding* are both given: *Object* = 'object', *Ding* = 'thing'. *Bestimmung* = determination; *Aufheben* = 'cancellation' (not in glossary).","'It is not a predicate that belongs to the thing' etc.: German 'es' and 'sie' references kept as in the original; 'Ebenso die Wirklichkeit' is kept as it stands (the preceding clause may have read differently; possibly a slip in the transcript).","*position* (lower case, Latin form) kept as in the text; *respective auf* rendered 'respective to'. *offenbar* = 'evidently'. *rnodi* (OCR) read as *modi*.","'in demselben', 'nichts mehr hinzu' etc. rendered literally; the closing '(bricht ab )' is the editors' remark and is rendered '(breaks off)'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-009":[". . . because we cannot reduce everything to one, but the unity of each substance requires that there be only one fundamental force. The substance, insofar as it contains not merely the ground of the accidents, but also determines the existence of the accidents, acts; or the substance, insofar as accidents inhere in it, is in *action*, and insofar as it is the ground of the actuality of the accidents, it acts, the substance, in which accidents inhere through another force, suffers (passively). How is passion possible, since it was said before that it is active insofar as accidents inhere in it? Every substance is active insofar as accidents inhere in it, but also passive insofar as they inhere in it through a separating force, that does not contradict itself. E.g. a representation of a trumpet blast, it inheres in me through a foreign force, but not alone, for if I had no *vim repraesentativam*, then blowing could go on forever, and I could have no representation. From the union of one substance with another an effect arises, namely the representation of the trumpet blast. We can never be merely passive, but every passion is at the same time action. The possibility of acting is a *facultas*, and of suffering *receptivitas*. The latter always presupposes the former. Every substance is self-active, without that it could not be substance; in one respect it can be suffering, but in the very same also active. A merely suffering substance is *contradictio*, otherwise it could have no accidents. The *actiones immanentes*, which produce something in a substance itself, or *transientes*, act on another substance or *influxus*. *Substantia patiens* is *eo ipso agens*, for the *accidens* would not inhere if the substance did not have force through which it inhered in it, hence it also acts, *influxus* is therefore an unsuitable expression, it signifies as much as if the *accidens* migrated out of a substance. What then is *passio* properly? The *substantia agens* determines the force of the *substantiae patientis* for the production of this *accidens*, hence all *passio* is nothing but determination of the force of the suffering one through external force.",["The passage begins mid-sentence ('. . .'); 'we cannot reduce everything to one' is as it stands, antecedent not given.","*Kraft* = force; *Vermögen* = faculty; *Wirklichkeit* = actuality; *Bestimmung* = determination; *Vorstellung* = representation; *Ausdruck* = expression; *bedeuten* = signify.","*trennende Kraft* ('separating force') is the printed reading and may be an OCR/transcript slip (compare 'fremde Kraft' below); *mhaerirt* read as *inhaerirt*; *könte*, *handlen* left as the manuscript spellings.","*Handeln* ('acts') and *Handlung* are kept distinct from *action/actio*, left in Latin as in the text; *accidentien*/*accidens* rendered 'accidents' / *accidens*.","'durch eine andre Kraft inhaeriren, leidet' follows the German punctuation; the subject structure is ambiguous and left so.","'Die actiones immanentes ... oder transientes, handlen auf eine andre Substanz oder influxus' is syntactically loose in the German and is kept so."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-010":["*Actio transiens*, when I make something actually outside me, is 2-fold: *actio* (*actuare* means to make actual), *substantiae vel accidentiae extra se* — the first, when I make a substance actual outside me actually, is called *creatio* — when I make accidents actually outside me, were it determinate, that is called *influxus*. An action that consists of many actions is *composita*, that which is not composed of smaller actions is *Simplex*.",["*wirklich* rendered 'actual(ly)'; *wirklich machen* = 'make actual'.","'wäre es bestimmt' kept literally ('were it determinate'); the sense of *bestimmt* here is doubtful and the clause may be corrupt.","*Handlung* = 'action' (and *actio* left in Latin); *Simplex* capitalized as in the text, *einfach* in the glossary is 'simple'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-011":["Force is the faculty, insofar as it suffices for the actuality of an *accidens*. The difference between force and faculty is difficult to determine. The faculty, insofar as it is determined in regard to an effect, is force, and insofar as it is undetermin",["The passage breaks off mid-word ('unbestimm...'); the continuation is in the next passage ('ist, wird Vermögen').","*hmreicht* read as *hinreicht*, rendered 'suffices'; *Wirklic keit* read as *Wirklichkeit*; *m Ansehung* read as *in Ansehung* ('in regard to')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-012":["ed, it becomes faculty. Force contains the ground of the actuality of an action, faculty the ground of the possibility of an action.",["The OCR running head ('6 Kant's Schriften XXIX 1,2') is omitted; 'ist, wird Vermögen' continues the preceding word 'unbestimm-' and is rendered as 'ed, it becomes faculty' to mark the break.","*Grund* = ground; *Handlung* = action."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-013":["The force that contains the internally and externally sufficient ground of an action is living force — insofar as it contains merely the internally sufficient ground of an action, it is dead force; of dead forces in the world there are infinitely many in regard to living ones. The combination of 2 forces that resist one another is *confluens* — in itself every force is living — for if it exists, the effect must also exist.",["*Hie* (OCR) read as *Die* in both places; *zureichend* = sufficient; *Verbindung* = combination; *Ansehung* = 'regard'.","*Wirkung* = effect; *existiren* rendered 'exist'; *an sieh* read as *an sich* ('in itself').","'der todten Kräfte in der Welt ist ... unendlich viel' kept literally in construction."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-014":["*Reaction* is not counter-effect, but interaction. Counter-effect is the interaction that is opposed to the action of the other, or *actio opposita patientis in agentem*, that is also called *impedimentum*; that is 2-fold: 1. negative, where there are no effective causes, or the lack in the cause of that which would make up the *complement* of it: 2. positive, there are effective causes of the *patientis*, which are opposed to the actions of the *agentes*.",["*Gegenwirkung* = 'counter-effect'; *Wechselwirkung* = 'interaction'; *Wirkung* = effect; *wirkende Ursachen* = 'effective causes'.","*impediment* (OCR 'impediment') read as *impedimentum*; *eomplement* read as *complement*; *patient is in agentem* read as *patientis in agentem*.","'oder der Mangel von dem an der Ursache' is obscure and kept so; 'there are effective causes of the *patientis*' follows 'da sind wirkende Ursachen der patientis'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-015":["The author now speaks of presence, distance, absence and contact. The first ones could still belong here, but not the latter — that concerns extended beings that have a surface and are impenetrable, for that is *contactus*, how 2 bodies in space conflict with one another through their impenetrability. One substance, one can say, is present to another if it flows into it. That can happen *mediate*, also *immediate*. *Praesentia* is either *bilateralis*, if 2 substances flow into one another reciprocally, *vel unilateralis*, as God flows into all things, but they not into him — in *praesenz* all things are in the totality. The influence is *unilateralis*, where with regard to one substance another is *mere patiens*; *bilateralis*, if they can be regarded as *reciproce agentes*. In *influxu* the substance must be present. The internally sufficient ground of the action of a substance is force. It is distinguished from *patientia*, to which something must still be added if it is to become force. The force that suffices internally but not externally is dead force; that which suffices internally and externally is living force. Dead force is a ground through which an effect can exist, but does not exist because of external circumstances. If the ground suffices internally and the effect nevertheless is lacking, then there must be a real force, whose effect is an equally strong object, or a hindrance, i.e. something that is contrary to the effect of a given force. If the ground of the opposite of an effect is a force, then it is called *resistenz* (resistance); *vires mortuae* are those that have no actuality, because another force resists them. If one enlarges them internally, or takes away the hindrances, then it can indeed become externally sufficient. Between dead and living forces there is in itself no difference, but because of the hindrances. If we take away the hindrances, many effects in the world will occur without increase of the active forces.",["OCR repairs: Her/Hie = Der/Die; eontaetus = *contactus*; Hinge = Dinge; Einfluß, Kraft, hinzukommen restored; liiere = *mere* (conjectured).","'in der A\\ eit' is garbled; I read 'Allheit' (totality) conjecturally. 'Welt' is another possible reading.","'so muß eine reale Kraft , deren Wirkung…' has a space before the comma, possibly a dropped word; translated as it stands.","Gegenstand = 'object'; zureichend = 'sufficient'; Wirkung = 'effect'; Wirklichkeit = 'actuality'; Wiederstand = 'resistance'; Hinderniß = 'hindrance'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-016":["The author now speaks of *contactu*. 2 bodies touch one another if they have a common boundary. He believes it is the same as what *praesentia immediata* is. One substance is present to another if it flows into it. If this is immediate, then according to the author it is *contactus*. But that holds merely of space and of the extended, and is a physical concept, which presupposes impenetrability. *Praesentia* is *vel unilateralis vel bilateralis vel mutua*. In every *contactus* there is *influxus mutuus*, but conversely, where *influxus mutuus* is, there is not always *contactus*. In sensible beings indeed, but not in spiritual ones.",["'umlateralis' in the OCR is read as *unilateralis*; 'w'o' as 'wo'.","Gränze = 'boundary'; Begriff = 'concept'; sinnlich = 'sensible'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-017":["We now speak of",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-018":["the simple and the composite.",["Einfachen = 'simple'; Zusammengesetzten = 'composite' (from zusammensetzen, 'compose')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-019":["Of the *quantum* and *compositum* it has already been spoken above. In the *quantum* one must be taken several times, in the *compositum* not. We represent to ourselves that many make One; without *identitate plurium*, however, nothing can be regarded as *quantum*. The *compositum* will be divided into the real, i.e. *compositum ex substantiis*, and into the ideal, that is into the *compositum* that is composed of something that is not substance, or of accidents; the real is also called *strictus dictum*. If the parts exist for themselves, then the composition is an action through which the [lei e] are combined; if the parts can exist before the composition, then it is *compositum reale*; if the concept of the *compositum* precedes the concept of the parts, then it is *compositum ideale*, e.g. space, which I cannot think as composed of small spaces, but the small space I must think in the large. The *composition* is thought later than the *compositum*. We consider here the *compositum substantiale*, in which it is to be noted.",["OCR repairs: exiMiren = existiren; z = zusammengesetzt; 'mei 'en' = merken (conjectured); 'e.g.' for z.E.","'die lei e verbunden werden' is garbled; probably 'Theile', but left as printed in brackets.","Zusammensetzung = 'composition' (also Latin *composition*); Verbindung = 'combination'; Vorstellung (vorstellen) = 'representation' ('represent').","'Wesen' not present here; 'Begriff' = 'concept'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-020":["1) Matter, or the parts, of which the *compositum* consists,",["Materie = 'matter'; Theile = 'parts'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-021":["2) The way and manner in which the parts are composed, i.e. the form. The essence of each *compositum* consists in the form, i.e. in *modo compositionis*. The ancients said: *forma dat esse rei*. By that they understood, the matter in each thing is something real, but the form constitutes the difference of things; and we must think it sooner than the material.",["'clat eße rei' read as *dat esse rei*.","Materie = 'matter' but Stoff = 'material' (distinct English words kept); Wesen = 'essence'; Art und Weise = 'way and manner'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-022":["In the case of substance much was already represented, insofar as it is contained in one, but they were not parts, e.g. the soul does not consist of thoughts; these inhere in it. It is thus a *nexus* that is not *nexus* of composition but of inherence. We now take the former; the *compositum*, insofar as it is not regarded as part of something else, is *totum*. A *totum* and *compositum* both relate to parts. — Many, insofar as it becomes an aggregate in One — there the one is called *compositum* and, if the many is homogeneous, *quantum*. Now we speak only of the *compositum quatenus est compositum*.",["'da heißt das eine compositum' is ambiguous (the one / that one is called *compositum*); preserved as 'the one is called *compositum*'.","'e. gr.' rendered 'e.g.'; Seele = 'soul'; Theil = 'part'; Gedanken = 'thoughts'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-023":["*Compositum reale* is that whose parts can also exist for themselves outside the *nexus* with others — that whose parts can never exist outside the interconnection with others is ideal, e.g. inference is *compositum ideale*, its parts can exist only in the soul. Body is *compositum reale*.",["Zusammenhang = 'interconnection'; Schluß = 'inference'; 'vor sich' = 'for themselves'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-024":["The author still speaks of arising and passing away and says, the change from non-existence into existence is *ortus*, and conversely it is the *interitus*. *Ortus ex nihilo* is the origin of the substance, of accidents one cannot say that they have arisen from nothing — *ex nihilo ortum est* is that whose parts would have had *praeexistenz*. *Annihilatio* also concerns only the substance, the accidents are mere *modi existentiae* of the substance. That is however not change of a thing itself, for that can take place only with existing things. *Ortus* signifies the being which a non-being precedes. And *interitus*, which a non-being follows. In the appearance that is substance which stays, while the determinations alternate. That is the condition under which we call something the first *substantiale*, namely that it persists. For if there were nothing persistent, that did not arise and did not pass away, we could have no experience of change. There must be a *substantiale*, of which alone we can perceive the change. In what persists, the state was A, and now the state B, so it is changed through something or other, and this *perdurabile* we call substance. If one says, everything in the world is changeable, that means: nothing arises and passes away but accidents and combinations of bodies.",["'ex nihilo ortum est, ist das, dessen Theile praeexistenz hätten' is as printed and perhaps defective (a negation may be missing); not emended.","'nur wahrnehmen können' could attach 'only' to 'perceive' or to 'the change'; rendered 'of which alone we can perceive the change' — the German is less definite.","Existenz/Dasein not distinguished here: 'Nicht existenz' = 'non-existence'; Seyn = 'being' (also Wesen elsewhere rendered 'being'); beharren = 'persist'; Erscheinung = 'appearance'; Bestimmung = 'determination'; Zustand = 'state'; Erfahrung = 'experience'; wahrnehmen = 'perceive'; Verbindung = 'combination'; Welt = 'world'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-025":["The parts of a *compositum reale* are in *commercium*, and all substances, insofar as they stand in *commercium*, make up a *compositum reale*. *Commercium* is *influxus mutuus*, for how is the *commercium* of different substances possible, other than through one determining something in the other, for the substances then act in one another, e.g. in the body all parts are in *commercium*; what is not in *commercium* does not belong to the same. The connection of the highest cause with its *causata* ties nothing, is no *commercium*. The cause accordingly does not belong to the *causata*.",["'XHe Ursache' read as 'Die Ursache'.","Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Ursache = 'cause'; bestimmen = 'determine'; 'wirken in einander' = 'act in one another' (could also be 'on one another'; German 'in' kept)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-026":["Every whole consisting of substances consists of simple substances. We note further that the author asserts against Leibniz that one should not call a *compositum* a substance, although the parts are substances, for composition is only an *accidens*. That is true enough, but mere *subtilitas*. Leibnitz says, the bodies are substantial *phaenomena*, they seem to be so, but only the parts are substances. In this he presumably had a different sense than this one, which Wolf and the author impute to him, otherwise he could always have kept this to himself, anyone readily sees that. (A *monas* is as much as unity of an indivisible substance, i.e. that cannot be thought further than as an *aggregatum* of many substances. Now it comes to pass: can one say of every *compositum substantiale* that it consists of simple substances, i.e. is it *monadatum*? Yes, insofar as it is *noumenon*, for all interconnection is nothing but relation. Since the substances *ex definitione* know external existence for themselves, one can cancel all relation, and the substances remain and are simple. For if they were not simple, composition would not be cancelled. But how is it with a *composito reali Phaenomenon*? What is represented only through the senses as *compositum substantiale* and where the substances too are substances only for the senses, there that drops out, for the proof drops out. The appearance of a substance is not the substance itself, and it does not hold of it what holds of this. Suppose a body consisted of simple parts, then its shadow might not consist of simple parts. If we take away all composition, nothing remains.) If in the *compositum* of substances nothing is substance but the parts, from which we abstract all *compositio*, which is only an *accidens* of their reciprocal relation, then the substances remain, which are without composition, accordingly in every *compositum substantiale* the substances are the simple parts. If I set aside all relation, the simple remains. That is quite correct, if I speak of *composita quatenus sunt Noumena*, for through the understanding I can think no *compositum* without first thinking simple substances. The simple is the *substratum* of everything composite in the case of the *noumena*. *Compositio* is the relation of substances insofar as they are in community; but this does not take place in *compositio Phaenomenon*. *Compositum reale*, insofar as it is appearance, is considered in space. Space does not consist of simple parts, so neither does the *compositum*. Of a persistent appearance I cannot say that which holds of substances insofar as they are thought through the understanding. Bodies are appearances and in space, therefore do not consist of simple substances. If we were to cognize the unknown that lies at the ground of bodies and brings forth the corporeal appearances, we would cognize nothing but the simple, which would be in *commercio*. We make in the appearance the *phaenomena* into substances, but we must then stay with the concept of substance, for the persistent appearance suffers. To speak of simple beings, we must go out of the world of the senses, but then we have no proof for the objective reality of our concept, for we can give no example; but of all appearances that is feasible. *Composita*, of which I can give examples, are *phaenomena substantiata*. But that does not hold of them which holds of the *noumena*.",["wieder Leibniz: read as *wider* (\"against\"); 'wieder' is printed. 'Wolf' kept as printed.","accidenz / aggregat / subtilitaet / Phaenomenon / Nouinenon: Latinate forms normalized to *accidens*, *aggregatum*, *subtilitas*, *noumenon* (OCR 'Nouinenon'); 'cl.h.' read as 'd.h.' ('i.e.'). 'composito reali Phaenomenon' and 'compositio Phaenomenon' kept as printed.","Erscheinung = appearance; Phaenomen(e) = *phaenomena*, left in Latin form to keep the two German/Latin words apart. Zusammensetzung = composition; Zusammenhang = interconnection; compositio left in Latin.","wol wahr: 'wohl wahr' rendered 'true enough'; the sense could also be 'probably true'.","trägt es sich: idiom (probably 'trägt es sich zu'); rendered 'it comes to pass'. 'denn' in 'wir müßen denn' and 'aber denn haben wir' is probably 'dann' (\"then\"); so rendered.","vor sich äußere existenz kennen: 'kennen' as printed (possibly OCR or slip for 'können'); 'vor sich' = 'für sich'. Doubtful reading, translated as it stands.","dürfte nicht: 'might not' keeps the ambiguity of the German modal (could not / need not). 'leidet' ('suffers') and 'geht das an' ('that is feasible') are obscure in context and left so; 'welche' in 'welche nur ein accidenz...' could refer to composition or to composito.","fälltdas read as 'fällt das'; aufheben = cancel, wegnehmen = take away, wegfallen = drop out."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-027":["Have we then gained insight into anything new through this doctrine? No, for through the category of substance we know no things. Experience must give us examples — and these are appearances. We can just as little gain insight into how substances are supposed to make up a whole — well, of appearances that are in space — but not how substances in themselves, for here we must leave out space, because it is the form of sensible intuition. We are now speaking of",["Eorin read as 'Form' (OCR slip). einsehen = 'gain insight into' (not in glossary), used consistently.","'kennen' = know; 'an sich selbst' = 'in themselves'. The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Wir reden itzt von'); translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-028":["Space and Time",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-029":["Space and time are not determinations of things insofar as they are thought through the understanding, but insofar as they fall to us in the sense, therefore they are forms of the intuitions. (We now come to the important concepts, which are of such a kind that, if we have been able to develop their nature, they change the whole plan of metaphysics, and banish all contradictions that have brought metaphysics into discredit.) Space, the outer, time, the inner sense. The matter of all appearances is sensation, and what corresponds to that is real. Philosophy: the real we cognize only *a posteriori*, but the formal *a priori*. The representation of the impression of the object on us is sensation, is therefore something subjective, which we must all cognize *a posteriori*. In our cognition of the senses there is something of which we have a representation *a priori*, that is the form of appearance, the outer space, the inner time. They are the form in which objects will appear to us when they affect us, therefore we can represent to ourselves much that we have never seen, e.g. cones and pyramids as in geometry; accordingly one can cognize space and time *a priori*. Besides the appearance something more belongs to experience, for it is not merely perception, but the unity of perceptions connected with one another according to universal rules. The matter must be given, the form consists in the concepts of the understanding. These are the categories, which make up the form of all human experience, and the unity of appearances is *a priori*, those too are the categories, e.g. substance, they are namely the connection of the manifold of appearance according to rules. — We can therefore cognize *a priori*:",["in den Sinn fallen: rendered literally 'fall to us in the sense'; the idiom ('occur to mind') is not resolved. Sinn = sense.","'der äußren Raum, der innren Zeit': case as printed (masculine 'der' with Raum); kept in apposition without correction.","'sie' in 'denn sie ist nicht blos Wahrnehmung' is grammatically ambiguous (Erfahrung or Erscheinung); 'it' keeps this. Verknüpfung = connection; verknüpft = connected; allgemein = universal.","Philosophie: printed 'Philosophie' (not Weltweisheit), rendered 'Philosophy'. afficiren = 'affect'. Verstandes Begriffen = 'concepts of the understanding'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-030":["1.) Form of intuition, that is space and time and",["Passage breaks off; translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-031":["2.) Form of the understanding, insofar as it is applied to sensibility; or the form of the thinking of the objects that appear to us; that is a concept of the understanding and constitutes the form of experience. Space and time belonging to sensibility, and the categories belonging to the understanding, constitute the concepts that we can have *a priori* and to which we can give corresponding objects in experience. We also have still other transcendent concepts, to which, however, no object in experience can be given; they must be distinguished from transcendental ones. These are those through which I merely represent the possibility of synthetic cognition *a priori*. Transcendent can be expressed in German by overreaching. Concepts are immanent if their example can be given from experience or according to the analogy of an experience. — These concepts are the ground of the possibility of a cognition *a priori* — and of synthetic cognition *a priori*. They have always been a stumbling block. Of space one says that it would be something in itself, [but] then it is nothing at all without things, for in space there are places; the place, or the way in which things are next to one another, is not there if no things exist. Nevertheless our understanding represents: It goes before all things, it is regarded as an all-embracing *receptaculum* that would contain nothing but places of things. Relations it also cannot be, for one cannot represent to oneself that these should exist without things. Space is no thing, yet a something, in which I can represent to myself all relations. What then is space? It went just so with time. Time is that in which alterations subsist, it is not a thing, but in it are things; it does not presuppose the existence of things, rather I must have a time in which I posit the thing. If I take away all things, then there is a time in which nothing is, but in which something can come to be. What is it now? A flowing-off of moments. What are these again? Since they were compelled to regard space and time as something, but not as a thing, they did not know what to do with them. (The author says *Ordo successivorum est tempus — post se invicem posita coniuncta succedunt.* Time is therefore combination of things, insofar as they follow one another — but being at the same time he leaves out, and that is an important relation. When are things at the same time? when they are in the same time; and one after another, when they are things in different time. And no human being has comprehended sequence except when he presupposes time, hence the definition is false — Space is *ordo simultaneorum extra se positorum* — that things can be outside one another, to that space belongs. One says, things can be represented to oneself outside one another, without being in space, e.g. I say, the one substance is another than the other — yes, but the *extra* position also presupposes a space, that the author also says: *simultaneorum extra et intra se posita*, otherwise I cannot see what relations the different things have to one another.)",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'a piiori' to *a priori*; 'succeßivormn' to *successivorum*; stray ’ after 'erscheinen'. The Academy Edition's 'object' (lower case, Latin) and 'Gegenstände' are both rendered 'object'.","überschwenglich is rendered 'overreaching'; it is not in the glossary, so this is a choice.","'Ort' and 'Platz' (Plätze) are rendered 'place' and 'places'; 'Raum' is 'space' throughout.","'Er gehe vor allen Dingen vorher': 'Er' is grammatically masculine and could refer to 'Verstand' or to 'Raum'; the ambiguity is kept in 'it'. The subjunctive 'gehe' is rendered as an indicative.","'Vom Raum sagt man, daß er was an sich selbst wäre, so ist er…': the German has no 'but'; the bracketed 'but' is added only because English requires it. 'was' is rendered 'something'.","'Definition' (not Erklärung) is rendered 'definition'; 'Verhältniß' is rendered 'relation'; 'Veränderung' is rendered 'alteration'; 'Abfluß' is rendered 'flowing-off'.","'zugleich seyn' is rendered 'being at the same time'; 'Folge' is rendered 'sequence', as in the glossary, while 'folgen' is rendered 'follow'.","'ia' is the OCR/old spelling of 'ja', rendered 'yes'. The dash marks and the parenthesis are kept as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-032":["The author defines space through *Ordo extra se positorum*. *Extra se positorum* are things in different places. The concept of place presupposes the concept of space, and the concept is already assumed as known: *ordo plurium, quatenus post se existunt est tempus*; to be one after another is to be at different times, thus it is defined *idem per idem*.",["'erklärt' is rendered 'defines', since Erklärung covers explanation and definition in the glossary; the context is a definition.","Latin quotations are left in Latin and in italics as in the passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-033":["The main question is whether they are things subsisting in and for themselves, for then they are distinguished from all possible things that are in them. For the most part all the old philosophers have rejected this. Now some believed that space is a determination of things. Because space and time consist in and contain nothing but relations, one believed that this *relation* of things, insofar as they are combined, makes up space and, insofar as one the other is determined in a series, time, thus they would be true relations, which would fall away without the things that one can think to oneself as possible through the understanding. Others, that it would be nothing but a general concept of relations in general. We will see whether it is so. — Were they relations, we could have no concept of them a priori, which there nevertheless is in geometry, or we would have to be able to cognize the properties of space from the *relations* of things, and in the whole of geometry the synthetic propositions a priori would be impossible; a posteriori we can cognize the properties, but then there are no apodictic propositions. Space is no concept, but an intuition. The concept presupposes intuition. We can prove no \\ properties without drawing them. We have only one space, and by many spaces we mean parts of the one infinite space. But that is not so with a concept, e.g. virtue is not a part of general virtue, but it is not so with the all-encompassing space. To intuit a thing a priori means to intuit a thing that is not given at all, and so I can intuit nothing but the form of sensibility, how I intuit things, which is in me. Space must also be something of this sort, namely the form of outer appearance. It is not that which lies in the things, but in ourselves. Now I can explain it: it is the formal of outer appearances, and one can therefore cognize properties of space a priori. This condition, under which appearances are possible, is pure outer intuition. Now it is comprehensible how they can precede the things, or rather the appearances. They concern merely the nature of sensibility, in accordance with which alone it can be affected. (Space and time give us cognitions a priori before all experience, and consequently they cannot be borrowed from experience. — Propositions a priori we recognize by necessity; such necessary propositions we have in geometry, e.g. between 2 points only one straight line is possible — time too gives such necessary propositions, e.g. 2 times cannot be simultaneously, thus they cannot be properties of things in themselves. To have insight into the things in themselves a priori is flatly impossible for this. Since they are therefore not determinations of the object, they must be determinations of the subject — thus the forms of our sensibility. — Space is the form in which we intuit outer things, time, in which we intuit ourselves. What we cognize through outer and inner sense is merely appearance, not things in themselves. — One could say: could we not indeed cognize ourselves? Yes, where we are self-active, one can say that this does not belong to sensibility, but if we observe ourselves, that is nothing but a series of inner appearances. We can after all observe everything only as we are inwardly affected.) (We can now also see how we cognize time and space a priori. Things in themselves we cannot look at a priori — but how can we look at appearances a priori? Because I can know how they will affect me — for they are not things in themselves, e.g. I will see no body except when the rays of light from a point of the body come together in a point on my retina — that I can have insight into a priori.) (Space and time will therefore also not hold of things in themselves — but things as phenomena are in space and time, that is the foundation of the transcendental aesthetic. Aesthetic is the doctrine of sensibility. The ancients divided everything into *aistheta* and *noeta*; now one uses it for sensible liking and calls it doctrine of taste. — Since this cannot properly be raised to a science, it is better to stay with the meaning of the ancients. Transcendental aesthetic is the consideration of things as objects of the senses, insofar as we can cognize them a priori; it shows the grounds of the possibility of sensible intuitions a priori, which rests on space and time.) (When I say something is extended, that is appearance — for extended is a determination of space. If we speak of arising and passing away, alteration, etc., we speak merely of phenomena. This has no meaning if we speak merely of the *noumenon*.) The concept of time we cannot have a posteriori, for how would we go about drawing it a posteriori; we would have no experience at all that one follows upon another if the concept did not lie at the basis. It is not a concept of relations of things, otherwise we could have no properties a priori and no apodictic propositions about time, e.g. between two instants there is only one time — two times are not simultaneous — time has only one dimension; but were the properties concepts of *relation*, we could not have all these propositions — time is also intuition. All things are in the same time. — All times are parts of the same time. Intuition contains not the constitution of the thing, but the subjective form of sensibility, how I am affected. The form of inner sense is time. That I can have insight into a priori, how my inner sense intuits itself, and say much about it a priori. Do the concepts also have objective reality, or are they *entia rationis*? They certainly have objective reality, but are limited to mere objects of the senses; they hold of all appearances. Crusius says: all things are somewhere or sometime; that must be limited to appearances. The sum total of all possible objects of the senses is the world of sense; the concepts therefore hold only of the world of sense — world is the *aggregat* of appearances. I can well think to myself an intelligible world, just as we represent a sensual world, which can be cognized merely through the understanding; but the understanding does not have the faculty of intuiting, but of reflecting on appearances; it cannot therefore know what things in themselves are. It can make experiences, but thereby it has cognized only the connection of appearances, not the things in themselves. Appearance and illusion must be distinguished. Appearance can be true as appearance. Illusion is a perception through which I have wanted to make a concept, without taking in all other perceptions. Appearance does not judge. If we want to judge about appearances, we judge truly if it happens from all perceptions; if I want to judge from only one perception, that is illusion. God cognizes the things in themselves, for his cognitive power brings the things forth. We cognize only the appearances, i.e. the way in which we are affected, and things must affect us, otherwise we know nothing at all of them. — Space and time are nothing but representations of the things, and now we have a key to many problems. If appearances are nothing but representations, as all sensible things are appearances, then all sensible things are nothing but representations. Appearances therefore exist only in the representation itself. We can accordingly say: so much exists only in the world of sense as lies in our representation, e.g. suppose that in the midst of the music human beings lost their hearing, then for them too no music would exist, just so for those who have no musical hearing. The objects in themselves do not exist. Hereby we are relieved of many errors, e.g. is the world finite or infinite? The question, which has made so many difficulties, now falls away and means: Will I go on without end in the series of my perceptions or not? and to that the answer is determinate: without end.",["Verhältniß = 'relation'; the Latin *relation* (twice, in roman type in the source) is also rendered 'relation' and marked in italics. Eigenschaft = 'property'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution' (glossary offers both 'constitution; property').","Gegenstände / Objecte / Object / Objects: all 'object'; 'Gegenstände' occurs once (transcendental aesthetic definition), the rest are Objecte/Object.","'sensual Welt' (Latinate) rendered 'sensual world', 'Sinnen Welt' as 'world of sense'; 'sinnlich' elsewhere = 'sensible'.","'in so ferne eins das andre in einer Reihe bestimmt ist' is grammatically irregular in the transcript; kept as it stands ('one the other is determined'), either 'one determines the other' or 'one is determined by the other' being possible.","OCR corrections made silently: Wed→Weil, Iheil→Theil, Rischeinungen→Erscheinungen, mäßen→müßen, lealitaet→realitaet, Geschmackslehr 3→Geschmackslehre, Zusammenstößen→zusammenstoßen, Eigenschäften→Eigenschaften, Einigen→einigen.","'von keinem \\ Eigenschaften' is garbled (a word, probably a noun such as 'Begriff', seems lost at the stray mark); the backslash is kept and nothing supplied.","'Die Dinge an sich selbst a priori einzusehen, ist hierfür glatt unmöglich': 'hierfür' ('for this') kept literally; 'einsehen' = 'have insight into', 'ansehen' = 'look at', 'anschauen' = 'intuit'.","'wodurch ich habe wollen einen Begriff machen' is the transcript's wording (perfect with 'haben wollen'), rendered literally; 'vor die' (= für die) read as 'for those'. Greek *aistheta*, *noeta*, and *noumenon* kept in italics as in the source; 'phenomena' left as plain English cognate."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-001":["On the finite and the infinite.",["\"Vom Endlichen und Unendlichen\": substantivized adjectives (das Endliche, das Unendliche), rendered \"the finite\" and \"the infinite\"; the German heading is a genitive/dative construction with \"Von\" and is rendered literally as \"On\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-002":["The magnitude that cannot be intuited immediately as magnitude is estimated through the sequence. I represent it to myself like quality. *Quantitas qualitatis* is degree, is the author's definition, quite right, i.e. it is not represented immediately as quantity, but mediately, namely through the sequence. Likewise one can also say: *quantitas rationis* (of ground) is degree. The degrees are opposed extensive magnitudes, those are space and time and everything that is in them. Of inner magnitude one uses the expression degree, not magnitude, which holds only of extensive magnitudes. All reality has degrees. From sensation to the thought in which I think myself with regard to the understanding, i.e. to *apperceptio*, there are degrees. Something can have so few degrees that I can hardly notice it, but I am nevertheless still always conscious of it. There is strictly speaking no greatest and smallest in experience. We have 3 concepts: of the *maximum*, *illimitatum*, and *infinitum*, they are very closely related, but nevertheless different from one another. *Maximum* is that beyond which no greater is possible. The *illimitatum* is the negative representation of the greatest. In the *maximum* there is the concept of a totality to which nothing is lacking that is required for a certain kind of things. (What contains everything of a certain kind is *illimitatum*; what does not everything etc. etc., is restricted, the *limitatum* is therefore opposed to the *omnitudo*.) *Illimitatum* can therefore signify the *maximum*, insofar as it is represented through a negative concept. Comparatively something can be *illimitatum*, but not absolutely; *maximum* is a positive and *illimitatum* a negative concept of the totality. In the *infinitum* I represent to myself the relation of the magnitude of a thing to the being. The relation of the magnitude to the possibility of measuring it determines either the magnitude, or the infinity; I do not say how great the thing is — greater than all concepts with which I otherwise am accustomed to measure. One has confused it with the *illimitatum*: We estimate magnitudes successively *unum rei addendo*. All counting is a *progressus* in the construction of a magnitude. The magnitude whose construction is possible through *progressus finitum*, is *quantitas finita*, and *infinita*, whose *progressus* is *infinitus*. A thing in itself I cannot call *infinitum*. The usage of speech brings it with it that one often calls the *illimitatum* the infinite. In itself it is not the same. In the case of the *phaenomena* one can say, that is *illimitatum* and *infinitum*. The *ens summum* we represent to ourselves as *illimitatum*, can we now call that *infinitum*? That is not fitting, for I do not say thereby as much as through *ens summum*, i.e. it contains everything that a being can contain, or generally, that lacks nothing, but through *infinitum* I do not say what the being itself is, but only, its magnitude cannot be determined in relation to a measure, through this I do not know how great it itself is, whether it is great or not, but only that it is too great for each of my concepts, but for that reason it need not be the greatest among all things. (The infinite does not say how great a thing is in itself, but with respect to a measure it is greater than every number. All estimation of the greater in space and time is relative. e.g. the sun is far from the earth, relative to the fixed stars it is near to the earth, and the smaller the measure is, the greater is the number. — The absolute magnitude is totality, if we take something in reference to the totality, then that is restricted. The absolute metaphysical magnitude is that of the *ens realissimum*, through totality we cognize things in themselves also through restriction of the *omnitudo*.) (The concept of the infinite concerns only *phaenomena*, thus only space and time, and everything that is possible in them, and to these we also must apply it only. If I say, God is *ens infinitum*, then that means *illimitatum*.) (In the case of the *noumena* there is a *maximum*, but in the case of the *phaenomena* neither a *maximum* nor a *minimum* takes place.) The infinite signifies the progression into the infinite in the measurement of magnitude. The *infinitum*, in distinction from the *illimitatum*, is called *infinitum mathematicum* and insofar as it is the same as the *illimitatum*, it is called *infinitum reale*. *Quantum relative ad unitatem aliquant dabilem omni numero maius*, is infinite; if it is greater than every number, then it surpasses my concept, for I can make for myself a determinate concept of the magnitude only through number. A being that has a determinate concept of the magnitude without number can also comprehend the *infinitum*. In the case of the highest being we will not be able to make use of this word, for it presupposes that we have a measure. To that belongs homogeneity, but the highest being has no *homogeneum*. In the case of the *illimitatum* I compare it with no measure, and the concept of the infinite can be applied only to *phaenomena*; for it presupposes that the *progressus* from one to the other is greater than every number. The *progressus* occurs in time. Is it the *objectum* that is *infinitum* in the case of the appearances, or the *progressus*? The *phaenomenon* I cannot call infinite, for it is nothing outside me. If the world of the senses were a thing in itself, then one could ask whether it were *infinitum* or not. Thus I cannot ask: is the world infinite according to space, but rather: must I go on into the infinite in the world? Yes, the *pro-* and *regressus in infinitum* is possible, but from the other side one cannot represent to oneself that a *quantum* is infinite. A future eternity is infinite, it is not an *infinitum datum*, but an infinite possible progression of alterations that can follow upon one another, it is an infinite possibility, not actuality. (The infinity is *progressive*, that concerns potential magnitude, or *collective*, that concerns actual magnitude. *Infinitum actuale* does not let itself be thought, but certainly the *potentiale*, e.g. future eternity is not something actual, but the composition of several times, and that can be infinite. — If we wanted to think for ourselves a filled *collective tale*, then we would have to represent to ourselves as if the parts were all actual - — these we would have to run through and take together, but that is again the definition of the infinite, for that reason the infinite *collective tale* is not in itself impossible, for we say nothing further than that the magnitude cannot be cognized as determined without number — for because we go into the infinite, we cannot express it through any number — but in the case of an understanding that may not posit magnitude through numbers, it would be otherwise.) (The concept of the *infinitum* should at the same time be the concept of the *maximum*, beyond which no greater would be possible — but that is the *illimitatum*; whether the infinite thing contains everything is not yet evident from that. One has sought to prove various things from it. *Numerus infinitus*, one says, is *impossibilis*. That is apodictically true. — That would be a number that would be greater than all numbers, but for that reason, as one further concluded, *multitudo infinita* is not impossible, e.g. An infinite space. We say, it is a number of miles — here there is no number at all, one proved that thus: the *multitudo infinita* would be the greatest according to its definition; but to every magnitude a unity can still be thought, accordingly the greatest, hence infinite magnitude, is impossible — if one whittles definitions in this way, one can prove whatever one wants). Some authors say: because there is no infinite number, so too there is no infinite. *Summus infinitus est contradictio*, but not an infinite multitude. One must distinguish an *infinitum actuale* and *potentiale*. *Potentiale*, in which *progressus in infinitum* is possible, that is not given, *actuale*, that is given. The unrestricted belongs to the *noumena*; I represent to myself that no reality is lacking, bounds a concept that goes to the *phaenomenon*. Boundaries we think for ourselves in space and time, limitations in the case of *noumena*, *quod rationem in se continet limitis vel in tempore vel in spatio*, is boundary, which can therefore be only in the case of *phaenomena*. *Infinitum mathematicum non est dabile*, i.e. it cannot be represented to me as wholly existing, e.g. future eternity does not let itself be given wholly, otherwise it would be no eternity. Through what do we represent magnitudes to ourselves as wholly existing? Only through the *progressus* of the addition of one to one. An infinite magnitude cannot be given for the reason that the *progressus in infinitum* would also have to be given wholly, or the *progressus* would have to be completed, and that is impossible to represent to oneself. One can just as little an infinite space, but the *progressus* in space one can represent to oneself as infinite. If we wanted to represent to ourselves that it exists already before the *progressus*, then we have made for ourselves a concept with a property with which we cannot think it at all, for we can think no magnitude except through the *progressus*. (Measure is the magnitude that we can cognize intuitively.) *Progressus in infinitum* takes place, for I can continue a magnitude further *in infinitum* and in the addition go on ever further, it can also be thought in the coming eternity. *Progressus in indefinitum* is less than *in infinitum*. Here no determinate boundary is merely posited for me. We now speak",["Folge is rendered 'sequence' throughout; Größe 'magnitude'; Grad 'degree'; Vorstellung/vorstellen 'representation/represent'; Grenze 'boundary' and Schranke 'limitation'; eingeschränkt/Einschränkung 'restricted/restriction' (not in the glossary).","'Totalitaet' (maximum passage) and Allheit (later) are both rendered 'totality'; 'Verhältniß' is 'relation' and 'Beziehung' is 'reference'/'relation'; 'zum Wesen' (to the being) could also be 'to the essence' (Wesen = being; essence).","OCR: silently corrected Beyrn→Beym, giößer→größer, müßcn→müssen, findt→findet, Progeßus→Progreßus. 'des großem' (probably 'des Größern', rendered 'the greater') and 'collective tale' (perhaps 'collective totale') are doubtful and kept as read; 'unum rei addendo' and 'ad unitatem aliquant dabilem' are also kept as read and may be corrupt.","The sentence 'ich stelle mir vor, es fehle keine realitaet, begränzt einen Begriff, der aufs Phaenomen geht' looks defective in the German; translated as it stands without repair.","'Der' in 'der gilt nur von extensiven Größen' could refer to Ausdruck or Grad; 'which' preserves this. 'Heißt' in 'so heißt das illimitatum' could be 'means' or 'is called'; 'means' chosen.","'Man kann sich ebensowenig einen infiniten Raum' has no verb in the German; kept elliptical. 'Darf' in 'der Größe nicht durch Zahlen setzen darf' is rendered 'may not' (it could be read as 'need not').","Latin and Latinate terms (maximum, illimitatum, infinitum, omnitudo, progressus, phaenomena, noumena, etc.) kept in italics; 'apperception' and 'object' (objectum), 'qualitaet', 'Quantitaet', 'homogeneitaet' given in English or Latin form as spelled; 'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of the senses'.","'Apodictisch' rendered 'apodictically'; 'schließen' in 'wie man weiter schloß' rendered 'concluded' (cf. Schluß = inference; conclusion); 'Erscheinungen' = appearances, distinguished from Latin *phaenomena*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-003":["On agreement and difference",["Uebereinstimmung = agreement; Verschiedenheit = difference."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-004":["Now the author speaks *de praedicatis entium relativis*. He calls them predicates of connection (relation), which is not good. Predicates serve to cognize *identitas* and *diversitas*, but then there may be no relation, e.g. 2 humans can be very similar or agree in many marks, without having the slightest relation to one another. We note the following: the definition of quality is *similitudo*, of quantity *aequalitas* - — they are *vel partialiter vel totaliter similia*, *aequalitas totalis* is equality in regard to all predicates. Let us imagine 2 *totales aequalitates* are also *similia*, for if they are to be totally equal, then also totally similar. The *aequale* is identical quantity. *Homogenea* can, however, be compared only according to magnitude. E.g. I cannot compare a year and a mile; if they are completely equal, then they must also be completely homogeneous; for if they are to be equal in regard to all determinations, then they must also be homogeneous. Identity is threefold: a qualitative, b quantitative, both together make up inner identity, c *relationum*, i.e. outer identity, e.g. that a thing be at different times — *totaliter congruentia*, one should think, could be different *ratione relationis* — also *totaliter similia*, one should think, could be distinguished. Hence the author also says: *Quantitas est discrimen internum, mere similium totaliter eadem tanquam ad internos et externos determinationes*, says the author, *sunt numerice eadem* — or not *plure plura totaliter eadem sunt impoßibilia*, then there would be no inner difference of the things. The outer difference does not make a difference of the things, but of the outer relation — thus in respect of the kind they are not distinguished — for I have no ground to say that this is something else. Further: *perfecte aequalia sunt impoßibilia*, for according to the foregoing they would have to be completely similar, hence *totaliter congruentia*, which are impossible. Nor *mere similia sunt impoßibilia*, for if they were completely similar, they would differ according to magnitude, one is greater than the other. — The one is thus completely equal and similar to the part of the other, thus *totaliter congruentia*, hence impossible. These propositions Wolff, Baumgarten and before them Leibniz have asserted; he had the *principium indiscernibilium*.) (This *principium indiscernibilium* drops out in the world of the senses; it is also absurd to think that God should have created each little particle, which is supposed to do the same effect with others, etc., differently.)",["Latin passages are left as in the transcript, including apparently corrupt forms (*plure plura*, *numerice*, *impoßibilia*); the Latin sentence beginning *Quantitas est discrimen internum* is syntactically unclear and is left unresolved.","The closing parenthesis after *indiscernibilium* has no opening parenthesis in the passage; kept as printed. 'Totaliter congruent.ia' read as *totaliter congruentia* (OCR).","Wolf = Wolff (spelling normalized). 'Sinnen Welt' = 'world of the senses' (Sinne, Welt).","Gleichheit = equality; Größe = magnitude; Merkmal = mark; Bestimmung = determination; Art = kind (here 'der Art nach'); Grund = ground; Unterschied and Verschiedenheit both appear, rendered 'difference'; 'distinguished' for unterschieden.","'Das eine ist also dem Teil des andern' is as in the German; 'effect' (*effect*) is the German's Latin-derived word, kept as 'effect'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-005":["*Totaliter eadem* are those where there is no *diversitas*, *partialiter eadem* those that are different in regard to individual relations. Inwardly *eadem* are those that agree in inner determinations, outwardly *eadem* those that agree in relations. Things that are entirely and completely one and the same are called *numero eadem*, for it is one and the very same thing or *unum idemque*. Where is numerical unity or total *identitas* and *pluralitas numerica* or numerical difference? Leibniz wanted to bring into metaphysics a proposition, namely about things that agree in regard to quantity and quality and also in the inner determinations, then they are *eadem*, but if they are only in other places, then they are already different. Here it is to be noted: if one considers objects as *noumena*, then it is true, because we there consider two things merely through the understanding, and it does not distinguish them at all, even if one posits them in different relations, A with B and C with D, then it is all the same whether A is compared with D or C with B; 2 *entia Noumena* that are distinguished in nothing are the same. But if I consider things as *phaenomena*, then it is otherwise, they are not *eadem*, however much they are equal according to quantity and quality, and are in different places. For then they are already *plura* and *diversa*, for our representation of the space of things, which are outside one another and thus different, does not signify this. Leibnitz calls this *principium indiscernibilium* and wanted to use it to prove that in the world 2 things were not equal and similar, and could not be. The first is contingent and may well be true, but not absolutely, e.g. that God could not make at one place a similar thing located at another place. That is absurd. Accordingly we call things *numero diversa* that are in different places. But difference of time gives no numerical *diversitas*, e.g. I live at different times and am nonetheless the same; we speak of things that are next to one another.",["Object/objecte = object (Latinate form in the German, same English word); Gegenstand not occurring here.","'Denn sind sie schon plura und diversa, denn das bedeutet nicht unsre Vorstellung vom Raum der Dinge...' is obscure; 'das' could refer to 'plura und diversa' or to the preceding clause, and 'bedeutet nicht' may be a slip for a different reading. Kept as it stands, with 'then' for 'denn sind' (doubtful).","'Das erste ist zufällig' : 'the first' has no clear antecedent in the passage; left unresolved.","'Übereinkommen' = agree; 'Verschiedenheit' = difference; 'unterscheidt' = distinguishes; 'Ort' = place; 'Raum' = space; 'Zeit' = time; 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'zufällig' = contingent.","'Leibnitz' and 'Leibniz' both appear in the transcript; 'ungereimt' rendered 'absurd'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-006":["The *simultaneum* is a mathematical concept. Place is *determinatus positus*, i.e. relation toward other things in *spatio*; there is also a *positum* in time, i.e. *aetas*. *Positus* is nothing but the determinate relation of a thing with others, it is referred to space and time. The *positus* in time is determined 1. through that which is simultaneous, 2. precedes, 3. follows upon something. Place is no space, but in space there are places, and one place cannot have many places. That in space which is no space is a point, hence every place is a point. Since place must always be the simple in space, no space can consist of points. (Place is always something simple, if I want e.g. to determine the place of Paris, then it is not the same into which street I go — but it happens in the *Observatorio*, there is again a space. *Formaliter*, because it is relatively very small, one assumes it for a point.) For if space consisted of places, then the places would have to be able to be thought beforehand, which is not admissible. (A thing cannot be at different places simultaneously, for were that so, it would be as far removed from itself as the one place from the other, which is absurd.) Likewise time does not consist of moments, for these are *positus determinationis in tempore*. (Motion is continuous alteration of place, where I pass successively through all intermediate spaces — if I went from one place into the other without going through the intermediate places, but in the intermediate time I were nowhere, then that would be *mutatio interrupta*. Rest is the persistent presence of a thing at a place, i.e. which is throughout a time.) In the boundary there is something positive, in limitations something negative, for there a *realitaet* is lacking. The boundary of the line is a point, the boundary of the surface a line, of the body a surface (*limites sunt negationis entis alicuius quatenus non est. maximum*; boundary is a space and time, and the positive, which contains the ground of the limitations and through which they are determined.) (The space that is not the boundary of another space is called the bodily or the complete space; it can abut on the boundary of another, but is not it. A surface-space can be thought, which can only be the boundary of another (the bodily). — Line is the boundary of surface-space, and point boundary of the line. From this one also sees that space cannot consist of points, because points are boundaries. (What follows does not properly belong here. For the actuality of every *accidens* one must think a force. The moving force is *oppositum* of the force of inertia, that is *contradictio in adjecto*; a force that is the ground that nothing is done is no force, *vis resistens* one can say, but that is not *vis inertiae*: what resists is also material. From matter space is distinguished, which is nothing but the form. Matter is that which can be intuited in space. One distinguishes matter and body. By matter one always understood something passive, which lies at the ground of all appearances. — Body would be the active principle. That is false. No substance is merely passive — if matter is substance, and that lies in its concept, then it is active, otherwise no motion could inhere in it; one must distinguish them thus: *materia quo ad figuram et quantitatem determinata est corpus*.)",["OCR 'Kaum' read as 'Raum'; 'iecles' read as 'jedes'; 'dei' as 'der'; 'ent.ia' left as printed in the next passage's note; 'Gränze' = boundary, 'Schranke' = limitation, per glossary (Grenze/Schranke).","'Ort' = place; 'Oerter' = places; 'Raum' = space; 'Einfache' = the simple (einfach = simple).","'bestimmen/determiniert' = determine; 'bestimmte' = determinate; Latin *determinatus*, *determinationis* kept as in the source.","'beharrliche' = persistent (beharren); 'Wirklichkeit' = actuality; 'realitaet' left as Kant's Latinate spelling in italics.","'ungereimt' rendered 'absurd'; 'geht nicht an' rendered 'is not admissible' (both non-glossary choices).","Parentheses are unbalanced in the German (the one opened before 'Folgendes' is never closed); kept as printed. 'limites sunt negationis...maximum' is Latin of doubtful or corrupt text; left as printed, not emended.","'angeschaut' = intuited (Anschauung = intuition); 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Grund' = ground.","'Der Raum ... heißt der körperliche oder der vollständige Raum': 'körperlich' rendered 'bodily' (Körper = body)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-007":["The complete space is the bodily space. Motion is the continuous alteration of the place of things. The things that are outside one another cannot be in the same place, and a thing cannot be simultaneously at different places, for one would also have to see that at other places and call it another thing. The action by which I make the distinct concept of magnitude only through the successive addition of unity is measuring. The force that begins a motion, continues it and resists it is moving force. The force of resistance is called *vis inertiae*. The name, however, is unsuitable.",["'deutlichen Begriff' = distinct concept; 'Größe' = magnitude; 'Einheit' = unity; 'Kraft' = force (glossary).","'Veränderung' rendered 'alteration' (not in glossary), used consistently with the preceding passage.","'Handlung' rendered 'action'; 'meßen' = measuring.","'wiedersteht' (OCR/orthography for 'widersteht') read as 'resists'; 'Wiederstandes' as 'resistance'.","'ein ander Ding' kept literal as 'another thing'; the antecedent of 'das' in 'das ... sehen' is ambiguous and left so."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-008":["On the relations of time. They concern only appearances, because time is not the representation of the constitution of a thing in itself. The concept of time is not the concept of things in themselves, but order in the appearances. In time the present, past and future are to be distinguished. Properly we can represent to ourselves 2 relations of things in time, namely insofar as they are simultaneous, and insofar as they are one after another. Present is that which is together with the representation of time; past, what precedes the present; future, what follows upon the present. In themselves things are neither present, nor past, nor future, but these are relations, how the things have become in time. To the concept of time there also belongs the concept of *existenz*, namely in the present, past and future. Temporal existence can be considered as magnitude, and the magnitude of existence is duration; by that I do not understand the magnitude of things in themselves. On the existence of the thing in itself (*ent.ia in potentia* are those that can become in the future and whose ground of existence is already now encountered) (Ground of existence is duration. — The being-there of God cannot be measured in time, nor compared with it.)",["'an sich selbst' = in itself/in themselves; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Beschaffenheit' = constitution; 'Größe' = magnitude; 'Grund' = ground.","'Dasein' and 'existenz/Existenz' both rendered 'existence' in the glossary; here 'Daseyn' is rendered 'being-there' to mark it, 'existenz' kept in italics as printed.","'ent.ia in potentia' is as printed in the OCR (presumably *entia*); left unexpanded. 'itzt' = 'now'.","'wie die Dinge in der Zeit geworden sind' is literal ('how the things have become in time'); the sense of 'geworden' is left as in the German.","The sentence 'Von der existenz des Dinges an sich selbst (...)' is a fragment with no finite continuation outside the parentheses; kept as it stands.","'Dauer' = duration; 'betrachtet' = considered; 'zugleich' = simultaneous; 'nacheinander' = one after another."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-009":["How can we make for ourselves the concept of the magnitude of existence? If existence is considered in the case of the phenomena, then it is posited in time; if it is a long time, then there is great duration, if it is a short time, then there is short duration. Duration without time we cannot represent to ourselves; it is indeed no *contradictio* of the concepts, but has no objective reality, and is therefore problematic. — (*perdurabile* is that whose existence lasts — or whose existence is equal to a time - — *existentia non perdurabilis est instantanea* — the moment is that in time which is itself no time, thus that which the point is in space. What is a point? It is always the boundary of a line, where one line begins and the other ends, thus no part; just so the moment. The moment that concludes the one year begins the other at the same time.) The duration in time of a thing is the measure of the magnitude of the existence of the thing, insofar as it is a phenomenon. That whose existence has no duration is properly *instantaneum* — so one must not say: Something lasts, but exists for a moment. *Instantaneum* and *durabile* are therefore distinguished. A moment we can represent to ourselves as boundary of time, but not as part of time, for they presuppose time — just as little as points are parts of space. On beginning and end. Existence on which a whole duration follows is beginning, and the existence which a whole duration precedes is end.) (Eternity one can represent to oneself as time *in potentia* — if I represent it to myself as elapsed, then it is nothing. — Eternity, one says, is duration without limitations. Of that we have no concept. — Duration of a thing in time, insofar as time is without limitations, is existence at all times. That is *aeternitas phaenomenon* or *sempiternitas* and is to be distinguished from *aeternitas noumenon*; the latter I think as not in time and can ascribe it to God. *Sempiternitas* is the boundlessness of an existence in time. This can be thought *a parte priori* and *a parte posteriori*. (Both have no magnitude. The beginning is boundary *a parte priori* of the duration, and end boundary *a parte posteriori* of the duration, thus itself no duration. Duration without beginning and end is the negative concept of eternity, but also problematic, for I cannot exhibit it *in concreto*. *Aeternitas a parte ante* cannot be conceived in time, because the *processus infinitus* can be *ut absolutus*. Through *aeternitas*, as through duration without beginning and end, we gain nothing, because the concept is not valid at all of *noumena*. *Sempiternitas* is existence at all times. We now speak",["Existenz and Daseyn are both rendered 'existence' (glossary: Dasein, Existenz = existence); the German word is Existenz/existenz in the first sentences and Daseyn from 'Dauer ... Daseyns' onward.","Gränze/Grenze = 'boundary'; Schranken = 'limitations' (Schranke); Grenzenlosigkeit = 'boundlessness'.","Phaenomen/Phaenomenon rendered 'phenomenon/phenomena' (cf. Erscheinung = appearance, a different German word); *Noumenis* is an ablative/dative Latin plural, rendered 'noumena'.","Parentheses are unbalanced in the source as transcribed (several opened, fewer closed); preserved as printed. 'Beyde' (both) refers to the two directions a parte priori/posteriori; left as is.","'Wir reden ietzt' breaks off; the passage continues in the next id. 'Processus infinitus ut absolutus seyn kann' is obscure (the sense of 'ut absolutus' is not clear) and is kept literal.","'dessen existenz einer Zeit gleich ist' rendered 'is equal to a time' (einer Zeit could also be 'one time'); 'auf die eine ganze Dauer folgt' rendered literally, 'eine' could be 'one' or 'a'.","'Vorstellen' = 'represent'; 'Maaß' = 'measure'; 'Phaenomen' and 'Dauer' unglossed, rendered by cognates."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-010":["On cause and what is caused.",["Ursach = 'cause'; Verursachtes = 'what is caused' (the German nominalized participle; 'the caused' would be the more literal alternative). Reads as a heading continuing 'Wir reden jetzt' of the previous passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-011":["That which contains the ground of the existence of something is cause. (*Principium*, says the author, is the ground — properly it expresses a cognition, insofar as it [is] the general ground of other cognitions according to a general rule — but also sometimes the ground of a thing, for example thus the Manichaeans stipulated a *principium boni et mali*. — To the cause corresponds the *caußatum*. — What can exist only as *caußatum* is *ens ab alio sive dependens* — better *ens ab alio*, for sometimes a thing can be *rationis dependens* as to its state, but not in itself, e.g. the domestic animals from human beings — to it is opposed the *Ens a se*. — But we cannot have insight into how a thing is for itself, that is merely a negative concept.) *Eßentialia* contain the ground of the *attributa* and are *rationes logicae*, but are not causes, because I ask only after the possibility. *Ens ab alio est caußatum*, *ens a se* independent, *quod non existit ut causatam*. The concept of an independent being is not the concept of a necessary one, for I say, it exists *a se*, without making the distinction between independent and necessary. The author, however, says: *Ens a se* is alone necessary and *Ens contingens* is *caußatum alterius*. Necessary is a concept of the understanding, through reason I cannot cognize existence, except that it must be necessary absolutely or through something else. From the author's proposition it follows that everything that we cognize as contingent would have to have its existence from something else. By what do we cognize contingency? *Ens contingens* is that whose non-being is possible. By what then do I cognize the possibility of non-being? If the lack of contradiction were already enough, then everything is contingent. We have no concept of impossibility except through the proposition of contradiction, a non-being is a contradiction, thus nothing is necessary, and no supreme original cause and everything *caußatum*. We have no concept of absolute necessity, nor of absolute contingency. For we say: Contingent is everything that is not necessary in itself, or whose non-being is possible in itself — whose non-being can thus be thought without contradiction, is contingent; everything is therefore contingent. The author's proposition, taken generally, would have a meaning if I could cognize something as contingent in itself. We can have a concept of the contingent and the necessary, but only in the appearance. That is contingent which happens in time; what neither passes away nor arises is necessary in the appearance. Of the necessity and contingency of things we have no concept. Do substances arise or pass away? No, that was above a chief principle. We will say: all alterations are *caußata alterius*, but not substances, for in those I do not cognize contingency at all; they are persistent. That every state is *caußatum alterius* can well be comprehended. Because the author's proposition is correct, we cannot believe that it can be applied straightaway to things, but only to states, for we have no concept of the contingent in itself. — Principle is every cause, insofar as other *caußata* flow from it. — *Principium fiendi* is cause (*principium fiendi* is that which contains the ground of the actuality), *cognoscendi* judgment, *eßendi* the ground of the possibility, and concerns the essence of things. Many causes, insofar as they belong to one *caußatum*, are called *concaußae*, these are either *sibi subordinatae*, if one by means of the other is *caußa causati* — or *coordinatae*, if none is to be regarded as *caußa remota*, but all as *immediatae*. The *concaußae* are subordinated, either *eßentialiter*, if they are subordinated *quoad caußalitatem* or according to the determination through which it is the cause of the *caußati*, — or *accidentaliter*, if they are subordinated not *quoad caußalitatem*, but *quoad caeteras determinationes*. (*Correlata* signify the *terminus* and the subject of the relation — in the relation something is principle of the relation, i.e. *terminus*, what depends on it is subject, and both *correlata*, e.g. doctrine is *terminus* and apprentice subject. Every relation is one-sided — if one is ground of the other's sequel and not so much sequel as the ground is, or two-sided, if the sequel [is] ground and *v.v.* The first one can call *correlata heteronyma*, the other *homonyma*. If one cause is the supplement to the sufficiency of the other, then they concur — and their *nexus* is called *concursus*. The *minus principialis* is called the concurring — *caußa caußae est caußa caußatae*, insofar as it contains the cause of the causality of the *caußae subordinatae*, e.g. a father is *caußa* of the evil of his son, or if he does evil, not insofar as he has set him into the world, but if he has held him to evil, that is used in the *Theologia naturali*.) The causes are either *efficientes* or *finales*, *efficiens* is cause *per actionem*. What is the ground of the existence of another is cause, be it positive or negative, material or formal. All *Caußae efficientes* are therefore determinations of the forces; insofar as they are coordinated or *efficientes coordinatae*, they are called *sociae*. *Auxiliaris* is called the *minus principalis* (the efficiency becomes *oppositio caußa formalis*, e.g. a wedge is through its form the cause of the splitting of wood — or it contains the formal condition of the same — the mallet, with which it is driven in, and the solid matter of the wedge are *caußae efficientes*.) *Caußalitas caußa auxiliaris* is *auxilium*. *Eventus* is a single action with its outcome, occurrence considered in its connection, is called the connection or relation with other things in the same time (the outer connection in which the *eventus* happens is circumstance — *circumstantiae loci*, insofar as they concur to the occurrence, are called *opportunitates temporis* — *tempestivität*). Circumstance or *relatio eventus respectu simultaneorum est circumstantia*. In the *circumstantiis* the *concaußae coordinatae* are lodged. *Concursus est actio* (causality) *concaußarum coordinatarum*, not *subordinatarum*; all circumstances insofar as they concur to an occurrence, are called occasion; this is either occasion of time or of place, and much depends on both. The concurrence of the circumstances of time is *tempestivitas*, of place *opportunitas*. One says: *minima circumstantia variant rem*. For, if I want to have all *concaußas*, it depends on the slightest circumstance, even that can be *concaußa* (further *locus et tempus non variant rem* — for they are merely positions in which occurrences happen). *Effectus univocus* is that which is of one kind with the cause, *aequivocus* that which is not of one kind with it. In physics *effectus aequivocus* is denied, but generally that does not do, otherwise God would have to be homogeneous with the world (e.g. *effectus aequivocus* would be if insects might spring from carrion. Here cause and effect would be specifically distinguished. *Effectus aequivocus* has up to now not been completely refuted. The author, however, makes this general and says: *effectus aequivocus* is general, that does not do. What are God and world, watchmaker and the watch?) *Effectus testator de caußa*. The effect indicates the ground of the causality, i.e. the determination of the cause to act, through which the *caußatum* exists, for *effectus* shows only *vires vivas*, applied forces, not *mortuas*, which have no effect. (An effect can be *mediatus*, also *immediatus*, *plenus* and *minus plenus*. By *plenus* one understands the whole aggregate of the coordinated effects, i.e. the whole series of the subordinated effects, properly it is the former, yet the latter too one calls *effectus plenus*. — In the first one means merely all *effectus immediatos* — in the latter also all *mediatos* and hence one can say: A cannonball can bring forth an infinite series of alterations and we get an *effectus plenus*. *Effectus plenus* (or the whole aggregate of coordinated effects) *est aequalis viribus efficientibus*. The author says: *vivis*, that is to say the applied, that is to say the effects prove the degree of the force, but not the faculty, in the effects there can be no more worth than in the cause, i.e. no more reality. We consider here merely the metaphysical worth, which rests on the degree of reality. Both are called *effectus testatur de caußa*, i.e. the effect reveals the existence of the cause — also the quantity and quality of the same. The *nexus* between *caußa efficiens* and *effectus* is *nexus effectivus*.)",["Ursache = cause; Grund = ground; Daseyn = existence; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Realität/realitaet = reality; Zustand = state; Veränderung = alteration; Wirkung = effect; Kraft = force; Vermögen = faculty; Werth = worth.","Zufällig/Zufälligkeit and Latin *contingens*/contingenz are all rendered by 'contingent'/'contingency'; Nothwendig/Nothwendigkeit by 'necessary'/'necessity'. 'Selbständig' rendered 'independent'; 'beharrlich' 'persistent'; 'einsehen' 'have insight into'; 'statuirten' 'stipulated' (not 'posited', which renders setzen, as in 'gesetzt' = 'set').","'Folge' rendered 'sequel' per the glossary (in the passage on one-sided and two-sided relation). 'Satz' rendered 'proposition' in 'Satz des Wiederspruchs' and 'des Autors Satz'; 'Grundsatz' 'principle', here 'chief principle' (Haupt Grundsatz); 'Princip' 'principle'.","Silent OCR corrections: 'Ui Sachen' read as 'Ursachen'; 'che' as 'die'; 'dse' as 'des'; 'lorm' as 'Form'; 'coordmatarum' as 'coordinatarum'; 'wir' as 'wird' (effectus aequivocus geleugnet); 'deteiminatibus' read as 'determinationes' (doubtful); stray '_' and '-' dropped; 'Wiederspruch' and 'Widerspruch' both = 'contradiction'.","'vor sich selbst' translated literally as 'for itself'; possibly a slip for 'von sich selbst' (cf. *ens a se*). The Latin 'caußa', 'causati', 'causatam', 'testator' kept as printed (some are apparent errors in the original or the OCR).","The sentence 'Begebenheit in ihrer Verknüpfung betrachtet, heißt die Verknüpfung oder Relation…' is syntactically obscure ('heißt' could be 'is called' or 'means'); kept obscure. Likewise '(die efficienz wird oppositio caußa formalis…)' is obscure or corrupt and is translated as it stands.","'an beyden ist viel gelegen' = 'much depends on both' (play on 'Gelegenheit'). 'Stellen' rendered 'positions' (distinct from Ort = place). 'Ens ab alio … quod non existit ut causatam' and 'Effectus testator/testatur de caußa' left as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-012":["Of the useful, that does not belong here at all. For utility presupposes an object of willing. Willing, in turn, the concept of pleasure and displeasure. All of that does not belong in ontology. What is a means to the good is useful. The degree of usefulness is worth or *pretium*; the outer worth is price, the inner *dignitas*, dignity, and the human being only insofar as he is morally good has dignity. Misuse is a use that is against the utility. (The good is that which *consentirt* as ground to something else. Things are absolutely or respectively good, *bonum absolutum* is *dignitas* — *bonum respective utilitas*. The price of a thing is something other than worth — it signifies another thing that can be posited in the place of this one as *aequivalent* — - thus presupposes a standard. - — One calls a human being estimable, also inestimable — — estimable, whose worth can be estimated, that is has little or high worth, and then it comes close to the inestimable) We must still speak of",["Nutzen and Utilitaet are both rendered \"utility\"; nützlich/Nützlichkeit \"useful/usefulness\"; Gebrauch \"use\"; Mißbrauch \"misuse\".","Werth = worth; Preis = price; Würde = dignity (beside Latin *dignitas*); Mittel = means; Wollen = willing; Lust und Unlust = pleasure and displeasure.","\"consentirt\" is a Latinizing German verb (cf. *consentire*) and is kept as printed.","\"wieder die\" is read as \"wider die\" (against); \"mäßen\" as \"müssen\" (must). \"Noch mäßen wir reden von\" breaks off, continuing in the next passage.","\"schätzbar/unschätzbar\" = estimable/inestimable; the dashes and the closing parenthesis are as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-013":["Matter and form.",["Materie = matter; Form = form."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-014":["The Scholastics distinguished in all things matter, i.e. *genus*, and form, that is *differentia specifica*. In every judgment subject and predicate make up the matter, and the relation of the two the form. In the *ratiocinium* the premises the matter and the consequence the form. The form always makes up the essence of the thing. *Materia ex qua* is for example the wood, when a cabinet is made from it, *circa quam*, with which, one is occupied, *in qua*, the data, in which the determinations already inhere.",["Wesen = essence here; Urteil = judgment; Verhältnis = relation; Consequenz = consequence (the Latinate word, not Folgerung).","\"w'omit\" read as \"womit\". The clause \"circa quam, womit, man occupirt ist\" is kept in the German order; the Latin phrases are left as given.","\"die Scholasticer\" rendered \"the Scholastics\"; \"Beispiel\" = example."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-015":["Besides the concepts of the understanding we have still other concepts *a priori*, which are called concepts of reason or ideas, i.e. such necessary concepts of reason to which no corresponding objects can be given in experience. The meaning of the concept is determined through the naming of an example, and without that it has no meaning at all. Are there such ideas in us? Yes, e.g. the wise Stoic in morals is a mere idea. Wisdom is perfect morals without defects and exception, such I cannot find, for every human being has at least a propensity to vices, but that already does not agree with wisdom. One cannot say, such a concept is contradictory, but very well possible, indeed necessary. Above all ideas are the transcendental ones, with which we have to do. These are ideas of pure reason, and the one with which we are now occupied is the idea of the world, (besides the *nexus effectivus* there is also a *nexus utilitatis*, and everything is in *nexu utilitatis*. The *nexus usuum* comes down to the human being. The possibility of bringing forth utility is from providence. End, means, aim belongs in psychology. End is that which I represent to myself as good, for the sake of which something else is regarded as good as a means; in every end there is *caußa impulsiva*, i.e. the good in the end, on account of which something else pleases as a means. End and final end can be distinguished. Ends can be coordinated, there they are *cofines* — or subordinated, and that to which all ends are subordinated is called final end.) (Matter and form go through all parts of philosophy. Matter is the *determinabile*, — form the determination. — *Materia ex qua*, e.g. 3 sides, — that is the *determinabile*, *circa quam quidam occupatus est*, e.g. what does the preacher treat of? If the determination has already occurred, then that is *materia in qua*, e.g. the wooden clock.) *Id cuius simile intenditus est*, is *exemplar*, the copy is *exemplatum*. An *exemplar non exemplatum* is original — *de signo et de signato* does not belong here at all.)",["Begriff = concept; Idee = idea; Object = object (Gegenstand does not occur here); Bedeutung = meaning; Zweck = end, Endzweck = final end, Mittel = means, Absicht = aim.","\"Der nexus usuum kommt auf den Menschen an meiner\": \"meiner\" is unintelligible (OCR or transcript) and is left untranslated; \"kommt auf ... an\" is rendered \"comes down to\".","\"Seiten\" in \"3 Seiten\" could be \"sides\" or \"pages\"; \"sides\" is used, the German being equally open.","\"Ueber allen Ideen sind die transcendentale\": the singular is silently taken as \"die transcendentalen\"; \"deterrninabile\" read as *determinabile*. \"intenditus\" and \"exemplatum\" are left as printed.","Hang = propensity; Mängel = defects; Vorsehung = providence; Weisheit = wisdom; Möglichkeit = possibility.","The parentheses are unbalanced in the source (the final one closes a parenthesis opened before \"Materie und Form gehen\" and ends the passage); kept as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-016":["*De Cosmologia*",["Latin heading kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-017":["Introduction to Cosmology",["Cosmologie rendered \"Cosmology\" (American spelling \"Cosmology\" retained with the *c* of the source)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-018":["The concept of a world in general is one of the limitations of the world of the senses by reason. Now, however, we have 2 kinds of concepts in our soul: concepts of the understanding and concepts of reason. Concepts of reason arise when one enlarges a concept of the understanding into the infinite. Thus we have sensible concepts *a priori*, concepts of the understanding *a priori* and concepts of reason *a priori*. Concepts of reason are called ideas and are such representations whose object can be given adequately in no possible experience, but which are nevertheless extremely necessary for reason and yet do not contradict themselves at all. These transcendental ideas also have the utility that, since they lead us out beyond all possible experience, they make us believe that there can also be things outside the field of experience. All these ideas relate and consist in the absolute totality of the conditions in the world. The absolute totality of the series of conditions, which take place with the objects of experience, is the cosmological idea. This divides into 4 particular ones according to the categories",["\"Sinnen Welt\" = \"world of the senses\" (Sinn = sense); sinnlich = sensible.","Einschränkung = limitation (the glossary's Schranke is also \"limitation\"); Gränze not occurring here; Gegenstand = object; Totalität = totality; Bedingung = condition; besonder = particular.","\"beziehen sich\" is rendered \"relate\", the reflexive pronoun left unexpressed as in the English idiom; the subject of \"die ... statt finden\" could be the conditions or the series (\"which take place\" is kept ambiguous).","\"Nutzen\" = utility; \"glauben machen\" = \"make ... believe\" (Glaube)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-019":["1.) Into the cosmological idea of the quantity of the world. This we cannot know at all through experience. For were the world infinite, then that would not do so. But were it finite, then I cannot experience the boundary; for I can experience nothing negative.",["\"ginge das so nicht an\" (angehen) is obscure; rendered literally as \"that would not do so\".","kennen = know; Erfahrung = experience; Gränze = boundary; Quantitaet = quantity."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-020":["2.) Into the cosmological idea of quality or of decomposition, that the world consists of simple parts, which I also cannot cognize from experience; since experience happens through the senses and space and time, these forms of sensibility, do not consist of simple parts.",["erkennen = cognize; einfach = simple; Theil = part; Form = form; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Raum = space; Zeit = time.","The stray space before the semicolon is OCR and silently dropped."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-021":["3.) Into the cosmological idea of relation or the absolute totality of the order of nature. Since in the world the cause of the conditioned is always again a condition; so I seek the unconditioned, in order to arrive at the absolute totality, which experience cannot give me, since it is not encountered in the world, but is outside of it.",["\"Ra\" is an OCR misreading and is silently read as \"Da\" (\"since\"); the \"Da ... so\" construction is left as in the German, with the semicolon.","\"welches\" could refer to the unconditioned or to the totality; the English \"which\" keeps both open.","Ursache = cause; Bedingtes = conditioned; unbedingt = unconditioned; Relation = relation."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-022":["4.) The cosmological idea of the necessary, that for the sake of the absolute totality the highest being is necessary. — These ideas have the utility that they drive us, in the occurrences of the world, always to infer from one cause to another, in order to come to the absolute totality. 2. To teach us that besides the world of the senses something more must be present. Insofar they have immanent use. Since, however, they have no examples in experience, they are empty concepts and can teach us nothing of their objects.",["\"Noth wendigen\" is a broken word, read \"Nothwendigen\"; \"Ra sie\" is an OCR misreading of \"Da sie\" (\"since they\").","Begebenheit = occurrence; Wesen = being; Gebrauch = use; Obiecten = objects (Object).","\"2. Uns lehren\" is an infinitive fragment, as in the source; the numbering \"2.\" follows a first point (\"1.\") that is not marked."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-001":["First Section. On the concept of a world. First Chapter on the positive concept of the world.",["Heading; Abschnitt = section, Capitel = chapter, Begriff = concept."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-002":["The world is a *totum substantiale quod non est pars alterius*, i.e. *absolutum et non respectivum quod nullo respectu est pars alterius*. Mere connection of the coordination of things in *commercio* is combination of the parts into a whole; but connection of subordination, as effect and cause, is not that. Hence in the connection of the world with God the world is no part of the same. This explanation of the world is an explanation of the *mundi intelligibilis*, where we understand by substances the things as they are in themselves, and is well to be distinguished from the *mundus sensibilis*, which is a *complexus*, *datum* of all appearances, where we understand by substances the persisting in the appearances. Through the confusion of these two concepts there subsequently arise the antinomies in cosmology. If I say: the sensible world is a *totum* of substances, then this contradicts itself, since space and time are infinite. I can assert of the world of sense that it is finite, also that it is infinite; this comes about because I hold it to be one and the same with the world of understanding. So I also err with the second cosmological idea, if I assume the world of sense to consist of simple parts. I may well say that of the *mundus noumenon*, in that, if I remove the composition there, the composite substances remain to me, which, when they are no longer composite, must necessarily be simple. But if I remove the composition in the world of sense, then nothing remains to me. The world of sense is nothing other than synthesis or stringing together of my representations of objects according to the forms of space and time. If I therefore remove the latter, I remove at the same time the world of sense. Space and time do not consist of simple parts. These and other antinomies are the chief object and content of cosmology. Of the *mundus intelligibilis* little occurs, since through the understanding we can cognize little more of it than what flows from the definition. *Vide* Kant's *de mundi intelligibilis atque sensibilis Forma et Principiis*. Regiom. 1770. An outsider called it enthusiasm to speak of the *mundus intelligibilis*. But this is precisely the opposite, for one understands by it not another world, but this world, as I think it to myself through the understanding. —",["OCR 'altenus' read as 'alterius'; 'mundi intelligibilis' etc. restored from the garbled 'sensibihs'.","'a e Antinomien' is garbled; rendered 'the antinomies'.","'die binnen Veit' read as 'die Sinnen Welt' (sensible world); 'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of sense', 'Verstandes Welt' 'world of understanding'.","'weil un wenn ich' is garbled; translated as 'because I ... if'-less sense 'because I hold'; the 'un wenn' is not rendered.","'Haupt Gegenstand' = chief object (Gegenstand); 'Obiecten' = objects (Object); both 'object'.","'Verknüpfung' = connection; 'Verbindung' = combination; 'Erklärung' = explanation (could also be 'definition'); 'Definition' retained as 'definition'.","'Vorstellungen' = representations; 'Schwärmerei' = enthusiasm; 'beharrliche' = persisting; 'Verstand' = understanding."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-003":["In the given definition of the world every word is significant. Hence we want to develop it further. There are here nothing but ontological concepts. Hence it belongs to transcendental philosophy. The world is thus a *totum substantiale*, accordingly not merely ideal. We can think to ourselves various *tota idealia*, which however make up no world, e.g. I can represent to myself a *totum syllogismorum*, a *totum accidentale* or a whole in space etc.; but these are mere *tota idealia*, which consist of concepts. But the world is a *totum reale*, which consists of substances. The author mixes up these two kinds of *tota*. For he explains *series* through *multitudo*, and *totum*, in that he holds them to be synonyms.",["'bedeutend' rendered 'significant'; 'Art' rendered 'kind'.","'idealiadenken' in the OCR split as 'idealia denken'.","'Philosophie' rendered 'philosophy' (not Weltweisheit); 'Ganzes' = whole."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-004":["In every whole matter and form are to be found. Matter as the *data* and the form as the kind of combination of the same.",["'Art' = kind; 'Verbindung' = combination."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-005":["In a *compositum* the matter consists of parts, and in metaphysics these are the substances, which can also be spirits. For we take matter here not corporeally, but *transcendentaliter* for the *quacumque ratione determinabile*. If, accordingly, substances are the matter of the world, the accidents are not, but belong merely to states of the same.",["OCR 'rat.ione' corrected to 'ratione'.","'Geister' = spirits; 'Zuständen' = states; 'Accidentien' = accidents."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-006":["The form of the world is now the *nexus* of substances.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-007":["If the world is a *nexus* of substances, then I cannot think the world egoistically, i.e. I cannot say: I am the world. Everything outside me are not things, but mere illusion. For since the world requires many substances and moreover a *nexus* of the same, I as a single substance can make up no world. —",["'Schein' = illusion; 'Dinge' = things."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-008":["The *nexus* is ideal if I think the substances merely together, and real if the substances actually stand in *commercio* with one another.",["'wirklich' rendered 'actually'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-009":["The form of the world is a *nexus realis*, because it is a *totum reale*. For if we have a multitude of substances, these must also stand in combination with one another, otherwise they would be isolated. But isolated substances never make up a *totum*. If the substances are together, thus a *totum*, they must also be a *totum reale*. For were they ideal, they could well be represented in thought as a *totum*, or the representations of them would make up a *totum*; but the things in themselves would nevertheless not on that account make up a *totum*.",["'Menge' = multitude; 'Verbindung' = combination; 'an sich selbst' = in themselves."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-010":["Accordingly the author's definition of the world through a series is false, because it carries with it the concept of the *totum ideale*. Finally there belongs to the definition of the world the *omnitudo*, *quae non est pars alterius*.",["'Toti idealis' (genitive) rendered *totum ideale*; 'Reihe' = series."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-011":["A *compositum* can be either a *respective* or an absolute *totum*. It is a *totum respective* insofar as it is not a part of the whole of the same kind, but an absolute *totum* insofar as it is a part neither of the same nor of another kind, e.g. a house is a respective *totum*, insofar as it is a whole of its kind; but it is not an absolute *totum*, for it is a part of another kind, namely of a street.",["'Art' = kind. The German sentence is kept as one; 'derselben' ('of the same') refers ambiguously, as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-012":["The world is no *respective totum*, but an absolute *totum* in the metaphysical sense. In the physical sense the world is called the earth *relative spectatum*.",["'Verstand' in 'im metaphysischen Verstand' rendered 'sense' (a non-technical use, = Sinn/Bedeutung)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-013":["The concept of the universe is a mere problem, a concept of reason, which I can well think *in abstracto*, but can never give *in concreto*. For if we are to give it *in concreto*, we must represent it to ourselves in the space that it occupies. Now were this space unbounded, we would not count it out, but something would always still be left over. Accordingly we cannot experience the totality of the world. But if the space were also bounded, we still could not experience the boundary of the same. For how can we experience that nothing is there any more? We can no longer perceive something, but from that it does not yet follow that it also actually is no more.",["'Problem' = Aufgabe/Problem in the text (German 'Problem' kept as 'problem'); 'Weltall' = universe; 'Totalitaet' = totality; 'Gränze' = boundary; 'unbegränzt' = unbounded; 'wahrnehmen' = perceive; 'erfahren' = experience."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-014":["Are, then, these transcendental or dialectical ideas (also rationalizing concepts) not mere figments of the brain (*Entia rationis ratiocinantis*)?",["'vernünftelnde' = rationalizing; 'Hirngespinste' = figments of the brain (literally 'brain-spinnings')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-015":["No. It is necessary to reason to bring all its concepts to completeness and hence also to make the *compositum absolute completum*, for that indeed brings nature with it, to draft general rules, hence it can tolerate nothing incomplete.",["'complet' rendered *completum* as the OCR shows 'complet'; 'das compositum absolute complet' is ambiguous in the German (adverbial 'absolute' with 'complet' or the *compositum absolutum*); kept.","'die Natur' is the subject of 'bringt ... mit sich'; the antecedent of 'sie' ('it', reason or nature) is ambiguous, as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-016":["The unity of the manifold is threefold: *Compositum*, *Quantum* and *Totum*. *Compositum* is unity insofar as it is composite, *Totum* when this composition is complete, and *Quantum* this unity is called in relation to still other unities.",["'Einheit' = unity; 'Mannigfaltigen' = manifold; 'relation' kept as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-017":["The world is *Compositum*, because it has a multitude of substances, and *Totum*, because all of these stand in a *commercium*. Finally also a *Quantum*, if we see whether it is finite or infinite. The whole of cosmology goes to this.",["OCR 'commereio' corrected to 'commercio'.","'Menge' = multitude."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-018":["In ontology we have the concept of a *monas*, of a part, which is no longer a whole, and in cosmology the concept of a world: of a whole, which is no longer a part.",["'Theil' = part; 'Ganzen' = whole."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-019":["These are the two extremes in composition and decomposition. For in the dissolution (decomposition) I come no further than to the monad and in the composition no further than to the world.",["'Auflösung' rendered 'dissolution'; 'Zusammensetzung' = composition."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-020":["The author says in his definition that the world is a series of finite substances. But it is unnecessary that he adds *finitorum* (or, as others, *contingentium*), for it already flows from the definition itself. For if the world is a *totum* of substances, but each one of them requires the other as its *complementum ad totum*, then it surely cannot be infinite. Hence the world consists of finite substances. Since further the substances in the world stand in a *commercium* and find themselves in *actione et reactione*, each one, or rather its state, is dependent on the working of another. Consequently changeable and contingent.",["Reading 'commereio' (OCR) in the German text silently corrected to *commercium*; 'Zustand' = state; 'zufällig' = contingent.","'Wirken' (noun, 'working') is not in the glossary; 'Wirkung' = effect. 'einer' left as in the German (a feminine/neuter referent 'of one', not resolved to 'substance'); I render 'of another' without adding a noun.","'Reihe' = series; 'Totum' kept in Latin in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-021":["Now the author comes to the proposition: *in mundo non datur insula*, i.e., in the world everything is in thoroughgoing *nexus*. This already lies in the concept of the whole. For a substance separated from all the rest, hence isolated, would not belong to the world at all, since it would then not be combined with the whole. Indeed, a great multitude of isolated substances would not make up a world (the isolated substances are only the material for a world), because they would not make up a whole, but each of them would be entirely alone and without any community with the other. Now we come to the important question: is there only one world, or are several worlds still possible? This question is, however, ambiguous, for there is such a disjunctive plurality of the world that in the place of the one another could be; or a collective *vel copulative* plurality of the world is possible, such that one could be next to the other.",["'Nexu' (Latin ablative) kept as *nexus* in italics; 'Stoff' = material; 'Gemeinschaft' = community; 'Verbunden' = combined; 'Menge' = multitude.","The last sentence is preserved as in the German, which is awkward ('es ist so eine disjunctive Vielheit ... daß'; 'oder ... möglich, daß').","'möchten' (subjunctive) rendered 'would'; OCR 'Verbindung'/'verbunden' read as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-022":["Is accordingly 1 a disjunctive plurality of the world possible? Yes, for the world is not necessary, consequently in its place another could also be. That the world is contingent flows from the *commercium* of the substances in it.",["OCR 'eommercio' corrected to *commercio*/*commercium*; 'zufällig' = contingent; 'nothwendig' = necessary.","'1' stands as in the text (presumably the numeral 'first')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-023":["But is a collective diversity of the world possible? The author says a plurality of the world is impossible, and certainly, if one takes the world according to his definition for a *complex* of all things, then as collective it can be only one. For two *universa* cannot exist. But that is the concept of a world that makes up an ideal whole, but not a real whole of substances. For to the real whole of the world not all things, but only the substances standing in *commercium* with one another may belong. The substances not standing in *commercium* are isolated and can again make up a particular *totum*, if they are connected with one another. This *totum* can be another world, which need not stand in any combination at all with this one, and might also perhaps have a particular creator.",["'Verschiedenheit' (diversity) as printed, though the preceding passage has 'Vielheit' (plurality); possibly an error in the source, kept.","'Verknüpft' = connected; 'Verbindung' = combination; 'besonder' = particular; 'Ganze' rendered 'whole'.","'Werden' capitalized in OCR, read as 'werden'; 'dürfen' rendered 'may' / 'need not' in the negated clause ('darf ... nicht')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-024":["We can in general consider the world as *noumenon* and *phaenomenon*.",["Noumenon and Phaenomenon kept in Latinized form, italicized."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-025":["The definition is a cognition of the *mundus noumenon*. For I abstract there from the way in which such a *totum* of substances should be able to be intuited, and find no contradiction in this idea of reason. I also find no contradiction then in that this absolute whole should not also find a place in the appearances or in the sensible world. But it is nevertheless impossible for me to experience it, because the connection of the substances in the sensible world consists in space and time, but these are *quanta continua*, whose totality one can never discover, for here there is a *progressus in infinitum*, and through the completed *progressus* totality is nevertheless alone possible. Hence the concept of totality is called in metaphysics only *mundus noumenon* and *phaenomenon*.",["OCR slips corrected: 'Definiton', 'Wiederspruch m dieser' (in dieser), 'm der Sinnen Welt' (in der), 'Progreßusist', 'Total itaetsbegriff', 'Progreßus' = *progressus*.","'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Anschauen' = intuit; 'Vernunft Idee' = idea of reason; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Sinnen Welt' = sensible world (Sinnen- as sensible); 'Totalitaet' = totality (Allheit in the glossary; here the Latinate Totalität, one English word for both).","'Wiederspruch' = contradiction. The ambiguity of 'dann, daß ... nicht auch' is kept. The final sentence ('nur mundus Noumenon und Phaenomenon') is as printed and may be corrupt."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-026":["Only one sensible world is possible, for since there is only one space and one time, and 2 times cannot find a place together, but both make up the ground of the *commercium* of all substances in the sensible world, only one sensible world is also possible.",["'Grund' = ground; 'statt finden' rendered 'find a place'; 'Sinnen Welt' = sensible world."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-001":["Chapter 2. On the negative concept of a world.",["'Capitel' = chapter."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-002":["Here we consider the world as a *quantum* and indeed according to the absolute totality of its composition and decomposition. In composition we consider the world as a *quantum* and go from the parts to the whole. In decomposition we consider the world as *compositum* and go from the whole to the parts.",["'Theile' = parts; 'Totalitaet' = totality; Latin *quantum*, *compositum* kept (Quantum in German text rendered *quantum*)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-003":["In regard to composition the question arises: is the world finite or infinite, and in regard to decomposition: is the world bounded or not? Does it have simple parts or not? The first question: is the world finite or infinite? There is a double *infinitum*: an *infinitum reale* or the so-called *illimitatum*, and the *infinitum mathematicum*, which goes only to the appearance of things in space and time and therefore holds merely of phenomena. (The *infinitum mathematicum* is taken here not *potentiale*, but *actuale*. For a potential or future infinity we can always think.) Since we speak here of the sensible world, we understand here by the infinite the *infinitum reale*, and ask accordingly whether the sensible world is infinite or not according to space or time. Neither is true. That here the two contradictory judgments are at once false comes from the concept laid at the basis being false. If I say, for example: a 4-cornered circle must either have corners or be round, then both predicates hold, because the subject is false. So it stands here too. For the question whether the world is infinite or not presupposes that the sensible world is something in itself. But now the sensible world is a mere sum of the appearances and no whole of things. If we consider it, then, in itself; so that is a *contradictio in adjecto*. Hence these contradictions of reason with itself, which in the Critique are called antinomies of pure reason, for one may assume that the world is finite or infinite, one finds oneself in both cases entangled in contradictions and absurdities.",["'In Absicht der' = 'in regard to' (Absicht is glossed 'aim; intention'; here an idiom, not the noun's sense).","'begränzt' = bounded (Grenze); 'Erscheinung' = appearance, 'Phaenomenen' = phenomena; 'Summe' = sum, not 'Inbegriff'; 'Widersprüche' = contradictions; 'Ungereimtheiten' = absurdities.","'Gontradictio' (OCR) corrected to *contradictio*; 'Wiedersprüche' corrected; 'a priori'-type ambiguity: 'beide Praedicate' left as printed.","'Betrachten wir sie also an sich selbst; so ist das ...' retains the German semicolon/'so' construction, which is irregular; 'Daher kommen diese Widersprüche ... denn man mag annehmen' is syntactically loose in the German and is kept loose."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-004":["If one assumes that the world is infinite in respect of time, then an eternity has already elapsed, and this is absurd to think. But if the world is supposed to have taken a beginning in respect of time, then a time would nevertheless have had to precede, for the beginning is the existence that follows upon non-being. If, then, a time has preceded, then it must also not have been an empty time, for one cannot represent a time in it. In time, then, a world must again have been. Consequently a world has always been and yet has also taken a beginning. What a contradiction! Beginning is always that which happens. Everything that happens has a cause. Therefore something must have preceded before the beginning. In God nothing can have occurred, therefore what occurred must belong to the world, so there would after all be no beginning of the world. — Wolf may well want to wind his way out when he says: Before the beginning there was no time at all. But then there was also no beginning at all, for this always presupposes a time. One can also say that in the empty time that preceded before the beginning the necessary highest being would have existed. But then it would have effected nothing in this empty time, and at the time of creation have effected something, consequently an alteration would have had to occur with it. And why did this author not create the world earlier? Sheer indispensable difficulties. Further, if I assume that the world is infinite with regard to space, then I thereby assert that the world is a whole which through all possible composition nevertheless could not be thought as a whole, and in which, accordingly, the composition is never completed. But to think totality and infinite composition at once is a contradiction. If I finally assert that the world is finite with regard to space, then I assume, outside the space that the world occupies, also an empty space (through experience I cannot establish at all that the world is finite. For if I came to the boundary of the world, experience would teach me nothing more than that I notice nothing more, but not that nothing at all is there any more. — )",["Dasein = existence (Existenz would also be 'existence'; the verb existiren is rendered 'existed'). Nichtsein = 'non-being'; Wesen = 'being'.","Totalitaet (OCR-corrected) rendered 'totality', the same English as Allheit in the glossary; the German word here is the Latinate Totalität.","Veränderung = 'alteration', verändert = 'altered'; vorfallen/vorgefallen = 'occur(red)', geschehen = 'happen'; 'Wolf' kept as spelled in the source.","'unerläßliche' (literally 'indispensable', 'unavoidable') kept literal; the sense is odd in context and I have not emended it.","'denn eine Zeit in derselben': the antecedent of 'derselben' is ambiguous (the empty time or the preceding time); kept as 'in it'.","OCR slips silently corrected: Sehöpfung, unendliehe, mehi; the parenthesis opened at 'durch Erfahrung' is closed only at the end, as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-005":["But why is it not also in the empty space? Is it in motion or at rest? It can move and alter in infinitum without anything being altered, either in the world or outside it in empty space. Accordingly an alteration would take place there in which nothing would nevertheless be altered. How absurd. From these antinomies we see distinctly that the world with regard to time and space can be neither *finitum* nor *infinitum*. In the *Mundo Noumeno* we can well think that the world is finite and consists of simple parts, but not in the world of the senses. What is it then? The world of the senses is no whole of things but merely a sum of appearances (the world of the senses is nothing other than the stringing together of my representations of things, which I get through sense according to the relations of space and time, consequently it lies merely in my head and is given not in itself but in the progress of my experience of things. E.g. a rose is not red until I look at it. For its redness rests on the constitution of the eyes. To be sure the cause of its redness is with it, but that it is actually red rests on my eye. One has, after all, human beings who see no color at all but look at everything as in a copperplate engraving, as light and shadow. — This progress can now go on with us to infinity. Things lie at the ground of the appearances, but these make up the *mundum Noumenon*, and that is of course finite according to its concept as a *totum*. The *progressus in infinitum* is an *infinitum potentiale* but not *actuale*, for the latter is absurd. Hence it does not yet follow from this that the world is infinite.)",["Sinnen Welt = 'world of the senses'; Sinn = 'sense' (durch den Sinn = 'through sense'); Summe von Erscheinungen = 'sum of appearances' (Summe, not Inbegriff).","Progreß/Progresse = 'progress' (German form); *progressus* in Latin is left in Latin. Latin forms (*finitum*, *infinitum*, *Mundo Noumeno*, *mundum Noumenon*, *totum*, *infinitum potentiale/actuale*) kept in italics.","Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Begriff = 'concept'; zum Grunde liegen = 'lie at the ground of'; wirklich = 'actually'.","The parenthesis opened at 'Summe von Erscheinungen (' is closed only at the very end of the passage, as printed; 'ia' read as 'ja'.","'bei ihr' in 'bei ihr die Ursache ihrer Röthe' is rendered 'with it' (the rose); the German is loose."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-006":["The sum of appearances I can now experience, and this experience can go on to infinity, or have a *progressus in infinitum*, without my thereby determining that the world itself is infinite. For the expressions: There is something infinite, and it goes on to infinity, are distinguished from one another. If I say that the world or the *progressus* goes on to infinity, then the infinity here pertains not to the object but to my concept of the same. For all estimation of magnitude I cognize through counting. This, however, shows us not the magnitude of the things themselves but the relation of the same to my faculty of estimation. It is merely a condition of our understanding.",["Gegenstand = 'object'; Schätzung = 'estimation'; Größe = 'magnitude'; Vermögen = 'faculty'; Verhältniß = 'relation'.","'Progreßus' (OCR/ms. spelling) rendered as Latin *progressus*; 'Begrif' read as 'Begriff'.","'dem Gegenstände ... meinem Begrif von demselben': 'the same' follows the German 'demselben' (the object or the world)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-007":["Hence I cannot say straightaway: the world is therefore infinite in itself, but only my *progressus* in its appearances is infinite, and this it is merely for our nature, for for a higher being the world would nevertheless be finite. But are this sum of appearances not things themselves?",["'Sind aber diese Summe': plural verb and plural 'diese' with singular 'Summe'; the grammatical disagreement is kept as 'are this sum'.","an sich selbst = 'in itself'; Wesen = 'being'; Natur = 'nature'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-008":["No. For the world of the senses lies merely in my senses. These, however, show us only the way in which they are affected by the things, but not these themselves. They show us merely the appearances of the things. These, however, are not the things themselves. They lie, to be sure, at the ground of the appearances, and I can therefore well infer from the appearances to the actuality of the things, but not from the properties of the appearances to the properties of the things themselves, e.g. the colors of the rainbow are the mere appearance of the same, merely refractions of light, but no actual things. The water drops, however, which are the cause of these refractions, and consequently the things in themselves, we do not see, but merely their appearance; and this is yet something quite different, and from this we also cannot infer in the least to the water drops. The present world of the senses rests, then, merely on our sense. Had we other senses, the world would appear to us quite differently; we would see a new world. And even if the whole world of the senses should some time pass away, the things in themselves can nevertheless remain, only the appearance would then be altered. Perhaps this may be the case with a future world, since there we will intuit the things as they are in themselves; that would be an intellectual world. Because God is the cause of the *mundus Noumenon* and of the possibility of the *commercium* in it, we would, if we could cognize its source intuitingly, thereby also cognize the things in themselves. But the possibility of this we cannot have insight into. Malebranche and others built upon this, but sought already here to have intuitions of God, which is a fanaticism, since God is no object of possible experience.",["Schlüßen (capitalized infinitive, twice) = 'infer'; Wirklichkeit = 'actuality', wirklich(en) = 'actual'; Eigenschaften = 'properties' (the glossary's Beschaffenheit is also 'property').","'anschauen' = 'intuit' and 'anschauend erkennen' = 'cognize intuitingly' (participle of anschauen; Anschauung = 'intuition'); the adverbial sense is left as open as the German.","'Sie zeigen ... Diese sind ... Sie liegen': the pronouns are kept; the second 'Sie' refers back to the things (Dinge).","'Schwärmerei' rendered 'fanaticism'; 'einsehen' = 'have insight into'; 'Sinnen Welt' = 'world of the senses'.","Latin *mundi Noumeni* and *Commercii* appear in the genitive in the German and are rendered in the nominative as *mundus Noumenon*, *commercium* with the English; 'itzige' = 'present'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-009":["There is also an *intelligibile* world, which exists merely in my understanding outside all possible experience; in it, however, there is no talk at all of space and time. —",["'intelligibile' kept as the Latin form in the source; Verstand = 'understanding'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-010":["The world of the senses exists only in the senses and in their composition of the appearances. This, however, is a *progressus*. Now the question arises: is the *progressus* of the composition of the appearances finite or infinite. This is infinite, i.e. it goes on in such a way that I cannot determine the end of it. —",["OCR 'fiägt' read as 'fragt'; 'Progreßus' rendered as Latin *progressus*.","'Zusammensetzung' = 'composition'; 'Dieser' (masculine) refers to the *progressus*, kept as 'This'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-011":["In the appearances there is no first, because space and time have no first parts. Supposing the *progressus* were finite, then it would also have to be bounded. The world of sense lies merely in experience, accordingly I would have to experience the boundary of the appearances too. Now, however, I cannot experience the end, because it is nothing. Accordingly the *progressus* is infinite.",["Progreßus is kept in Latin, in italics, as a technical term (progress, progressus, in the glossary).","\"Sinnen Welt\" (OCR \"Sinnen Welt\") rendered \"world of sense\"; \"Sinnen\" is the plural form, not \"sinnlich\".","OCR \"m der Erfahrung\" corrected to \"in der Erfahrung\".","\"Grenze\" = boundary; \"begrenzt\" = bounded."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-012":["So the *regressus* in time is also infinite. For if we wanted to know the beginning of time, we would have to have been earlier than the world and would nevertheless belong to the world as well. The beginning of time is the existence of the same, before which a time precedes in which it was not. A contradiction, for there time is presupposed. And so then all the contradictions are removed if we assume that in the world of sense a *progressus in infinitum* with regard to space, and with regard to time a *regressus in infinitum*, takes place, but that the world in itself is neither finite nor infinite, because we know nothing of things in themselves, and also cannot infer to them from their appearances. The error in the representation that the world in itself is finite or infinite lies in this, that reason took a regulative *principium* for a constitutive one. The world is therefore not an infinite *compositum*, but my composition of the same is infinite.",["\"wissen\" = know (Wissen, knowledge); \"Dasein\" = existence; \"Wiederspruch\" (sic) = contradiction (Widerspruch).","\"Ansehung\" in \"in Ansehung\" rendered \"with regard to\".","\"derselben\" / \"von derselben\" rendered \"of the same\", leaving the antecedent as ambiguous as the German (time / world).","OCR \"const.itutives\" corrected to \"constitutives\"; \"Sinnen Welt\" as in the preceding passage.","\"auch aus ihren Erscheinungen nicht auf sie schließen können\": \"also\" (auch) could modify \"we know nothing\" or \"cannot infer\"; kept in German order."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-013":["So it is also with the decomposition or with the question whether the world consists of infinite parts or does not consist. Both concepts are false. For if I assume, e.g., that a line consists of finite parts, then it consists of simple parts. These are now points, points are places, and the line would therefore consist of places. Accordingly the places would be thinkable earlier than space; but that is contradictory. (The simple parts in space are points. These, however, do not make space possible, which would have to be the case if space consisted of points, but rather space makes them possible. If I think to myself 2 points, I cannot think them to myself otherwise than that a space be between them. Thus space does not consist of points and of no simple parts. For if I cancel the composition, nothing remains to me. So it is with space and so with appearances.",["The opening parenthesis is never closed in the source text as given; kept unclosed.","\"Decomposition\", \"Compositum\", \"contradictorisch\" are Latinate in the German; kept as \"decomposition\", \"contradictory\".","\"Oerter\" = places (Ort); \"einfach\" = simple; \"Begriffe\" = concepts.","\"gedenkbar\" rendered \"thinkable\"; \"sey\" subjunctive rendered \"be\".","\"aus keinen einfachen Theilen\": the double negative sense kept as \"of no simple parts\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-014":["If, on the other hand, I again assume that the line consists of infinite parts, then its composition must be infinite. Since, however, I can really compose a line, my composition is completed, but finite. Accordingly here the composition is finite and infinite, which is a contradiction.",["\"Zusammensetzung\" and \"Composition\" both rendered \"composition\"; German word marked here.","\"wirklich\" rendered \"really\"; \"Wiederspruch\" = Widerspruch, contradiction."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-015":["Here, then, the *noumenon* is again confused with the *phaenomenon*. For the world, considered as *noumenon*, must consist of simple parts, because otherwise it cannot be composed. But as *phaenomenon* it is otherwise. Things in themselves have neither finite nor infinite parts (at least we cannot know this), but rather the *regressus* of my decomposition is infinite.",["\"Noumenon\" and \"Phaenomenon\" kept in Latin, in italics.","\"betrachtet\" (Betrachtung = consideration) rendered \"considered\".","\"ists anders\": \"it is otherwise\", the subject \"it\" referring back as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-016":["For in space and time there is no last. They are *quanta continua*. Consequently my decomposition of the same, thus also of the rest that are found in the same, is infinite. These questions of com- and decomposition in the world concern the totality of the synthesis of appearances, insofar as they make up a *compositum*, and this is mathematical synthesis.",["OCR \"Polglich\" corrected to \"Folglich\" (consequently).","\"Totalitaet\" = totality (Allheit in the glossary is the Kantian German term; here the Latinate Totalität).","\"derselben\" ambiguous between space and time and/or appearances; rendered \"of the same\".","\"Quanta Continua\" kept in Latin, in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-017":["Now we come to the dynamical synthesis in the appearances, insofar as the things hang together with one another as causes and sequels. This is deduction of causality, and the relation between effects and cause is therefore its object. The mathematical synthesis, however, is merely synthesis of aggregation.",["\"Folgen\" = sequels (Folge); \"Wirkungen\" = effects; \"Obiect\" (sic) = Object, rendered \"object\".","\"Zusammenhängen\" (verb, OCR capitalized) rendered \"hang together\"; the noun Zusammenhang = interconnection/nexus.","\"Deduction\" and \"Aggregation\" are Latinate German words; \"Caußalitaet\" = causality."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-018":["We are now speaking of nothing but series in the connection of our manifold representations. A series is then, when one representation is the condition of another. The progress in space and time is also a series, but a series of appearances. If in the series of causes I go from the conditions to the conditioned, that is *progressus*, from the conditioned to the conditions is *regressus*. The *progressus in infinitum* is an *infinitum potentiale* and may therefore not be thought as totality. With space, however, we represent the things to ourselves all at once, and there totality must be. The whole cosmology has to do with the *regressus* of time. For reason demands the totality of the conditions, not of the conditioned. In space *pro-* and *regressus* are one and the same, for there each part is at once condition and conditioned. The world is infinite with regard to the causes, is the constitutive *principium* of reason. That is, in the series of subordinated causes in the world there is no first. But if one asserts that there is a first, then one asserts a *regressus in indefinitum*. In the world neither a *regressus in infinitum* nor *in finitum* can be thought, neither a first cause nor a causality led into the infinite. These are again antinomies of reason, in which *noumenon* and *phaenomenon* are again confused.",["OCR \"Prmcipium\" corrected to \"principium\"; \"defimtum\" to \"indefinitum\" and \"fimtum\" to \"finitum\". The reading \"in defimtum\" (probably \"in indefinitum\") is uncertain.","\"mit einander Zusammenhängen\" and \"Zusammenhang\" distinguished: verb \"hang together\".","\"Bedingung\" = condition; \"Bedingtes\" = the conditioned; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Fortgang\" = progress; \"Behauptet\" = asserts.","\"Die Welt ist infinit ... ist das constitutive Principium der Vernunft\": the syntax is kept as in the source, which looks faulty or elliptical.","\"Reihe\" = series; \"einerlei\" rendered \"one and the same\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-019":["Things in themselves are possible only in a series, i.e., in the connection between cause and effect. The series, however, cannot be infinite, but must have a first cause, which is no longer an effect of another. Here accordingly is *regressus in finitum*, that is with the *mundus noumenon*. Here no infinite *regressus* is possible, for since beyond this infinite series of conditions no condition any longer took place, one of the conditions from this series must not again be a conditioned, but first condition: because if it were not unconditioned, it would be at once conditioned and yet without condition; which is a contradiction. The *regressus* is *rectilineus* if I always go on in this way, or *curvilineus* if the last sequel is first cause of the first cause, and it goes thus round as in a circle. But there is still always a first cause. That is valid just as little as a circle in proof. A commandant in a Flemish fortress had during a siege provisions indeed, but no money. He therefore sold the former to the sutlers, these gave him money, which he paid to the soldiers, these to the sutlers, who again to him, etc. Thus a quantity of money ran continually in a circle as long as the provisions lasted; these were nevertheless the supreme cause.",["\"Mundo Noumenon\" kept in Latin; \"mundus noumenon\" is the nominative form of the ablative \"Mundo\" in the source.","\"Folge\" = sequel; \"Beweis\" = proof; \"Wiederspruch\" = Widerspruch, contradiction; \"gilt\" rendered \"is valid\" (gültig).","\"statt fände\" rendered \"took place\" (subjunctive, past in form); \"weil, wenn sie nicht unbedingt wäre\" kept in its clause order.","\"Marquetender\" rendered \"sutlers\"; \"Vestung\" = fortress; \"Lebensmittel\" = provisions.","\"Wirkung von einer andern\": \"effect of another\" could be \"effect from another\"; kept ambiguous."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-020":["But in the appearance there is no *regressus in finitum*. For there is no first in time there, i.e., that before which no other precedes. But if time is infinite in the *regressus*, then the series of occurrences in time must also be infinite. Nature is the interconnection of appearances according to universal laws. Now this interconnection is manifold, and to it belongs also the series, e.g., the inherence of the subject is also connection, but not series; for the subject does not inhere again.",["\"Begebenheiten\" = occurrences (Begebenheit); \"Zusammenhang\" = interconnection; \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Reihe\" = series.","\"Inhaerenz\" and \"inhaerirt\" rendered \"inherence\" and \"inhere\".","\"mancherlei\" rendered \"manifold\" here; it is not the substantive Mannigfaltige of the glossary.","\"das, vor dem kein anderes vorhergeht\": \"that before which no other precedes\", the antecedent left as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-021":["In all appearances of the occurrence the causality of the cause of the occurrence is in turn an occurrence. If now all causes in turn have causes, then there is nothing in the world but nature. Since now in the world of sense there is nothing but occurrence, we can go on to infinity; everything that we will experience will still be occurrence or *effectus*. For without its being occurrence, it would be no object of experience at all. Experience indeed consists precisely in this, that my perceptions are connected with one another through the combination of *causa* and *causatum*. If this is not there, then my perception is not much more than a dream, which has validity merely privately for me, — but can never be called experience. In the world, therefore, we do not get to experience any occurrence that would be the first, for our *regressus* goes *in infinitum*. But there is no actually infinite series of causes, rather merely a *regressus* is infinite.",["Begebenheit = occurrence throughout; Object = object; Wahrnehmung = perception; Verbindung = combination; verknüpfen = connected (Verknüpfung = connection, in the next passage).","'Effect' in the German (Latin-derived) is rendered *effectus* in italics as a foreign word; 'Caußa' and 'caußato' are corrected for the OCR/spelling to *causa* and *causatum*. 'Caußalitaet' = causality.","Sinnen Welt = world of sense (Sinn = sense).","'regreßus' (Latin, nominative as printed) kept as *regressus*; 'ein regreßus ist infinit' is rendered literally ('a regressus is infinite'); the article before it is as printed.","'Privat Gültigkeit' = 'private validity'; rendered 'validity merely privately' only to keep the German adverbial sense; the German is compressed here ('bloß für mich Privat Gültigkeit hat')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-022":["If we therefore never come in the world of sense to the first cause, there must nevertheless be one, for it would contradict our reason to think no first cause. Since therefore the world of sense, i.e. all objects of possible experience, are and can be nothing but occurrences, and therefore always lay a cause at the ground; so the first cause cannot be in the world, but is an *ens extramundanum*. It is thus merely a regulative, not constitutive principle of reason to say: the appearances and therefore also the connection of them according to cause and effect go on to infinity. For they are not things in themselves, and also not actual before we have arrived there in the *regressus*.",["'Begebenheitem' in the OCR is corrected to 'Begebenheiten' (occurrences); 'in Regreßu' is read as the Latin *regressus* (ablative *in regressu*), rendered *regressus*.","Gegenstände = objects; Princip = principle (Latin-derived, here 'principle'; Grundsatz would also be 'principle'); Ding an sich selbst = 'thing in itself'.","'Da ... sind und sein können, und daher immer eine Ursache zum Grunde legen; so' keeps the German's irregular syntax (plural subject, singular-looking 'nichts als Begebenheiten'); 'legen' has no explicit subject in the German beyond 'Gegenstände'.","'wirklich' = actual."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-023":["If an occurrence ensues through a cause that is no occurrence, then it is said of it *sponte accidit*. It happens first, for no occurrence goes before it. Freedom is the faculty of beginning a series of states by itself. If something is an effect of nature, then it is already continuation of the series of states; if it is an effect of freedom, then it is a new state: that is the transcendental concept of freedom. E.g. if I am always inserting out of hypochondria, this comes from my nature. But if I do this merely because I will it, then this comes from my freedom. Now the question is: is everything in the world nature or freedom? If we look to the appearances, they all hang together according to the laws of nature. All appearances, however, still have a transcendental cause, which we do not know, e.g. the body is composed, i.e. an appearance. Now there must nevertheless be a transcendental cause, which contains the ground from which this appearance arises. This cause is unknown to us; but because it does not belong to the world of sense, it also cannot be determined by other causes in it, consequently it also does not stand under the laws of nature or of the world of sense and is thus *totaliter* free.",["'einsetzen' (\"immer einsetze\") is rendered 'inserting' literally; its sense here is obscure and may be an error in the transcript. No object is given.","wissen = know (the glossary gives Wissen = knowledge; the verb is rendered 'know'; kennen would be 'be acquainted with'). 'unbekannt' = unknown.","'zusammengesetzt' = composed; 'zusammenhängen' = hang together (cf. Zusammenhang = interconnection).","'Zustände' = states; 'Vermögen' = faculty; 'Grund' = ground; 'Gesetze' = laws; 'bestimmt' = determined.","'z. E..' as printed (OCR doubled period); read as 'e.g.'. 'Wenn ich ... Thue ich aber dies bloß, weil ichs will' — 'ich will' is 'I will it' (the verb wollen, not future)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-024":["Thus in the world on the one side is nature, on the other freedom. What happens can also be regarded as such an effect as does not have its ground in the series of appearances, which are connected according to universal laws of nature, but happens *sponte* according to laws of freedom, e.g. if I do something according to the guidance of my understanding, then this, insofar as it arises from the understanding, is freedom; but insofar as it happens in conformity with the laws of nature (that I namely can also accomplish what I will), then it is nature. For it also happens in the world and what happens in the world of sense is nature. —",["Verstand = understanding; Wirkung = effect; Erscheinungen = appearances; verknüpft = connected.","'Freyheit' (as printed) and 'Freiheit' both = freedom.","'Denn sie geschieht': the feminine 'sie' has no clear antecedent in the passage as it stands (it may refer to the action, 'Handlung', or to 'Wirkung'); left as 'it'.","'was ich will' = 'what I will'; 'ausrichten' = accomplish."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-025":["If I want to explain an occurrence in the world, and I derive this from the universal laws of nature, then that is a natural occurrence. In the world as a series of appearances we can and must explain no occurrence *ex spontaneitate*, merely the reason of the human being is excepted from this. It does not belong under the series of appearances. It is independent of the laws of nature, and in this freedom consists precisely. With regard to his powers of mind the human being belongs indeed to the *mundus noumenus*, for he can through the understanding cognize the things as they are, as e.g. his moral relations, truth etc., and in this aim his actions are free, as also to the *mundus phaenomenus*, insofar as through his actions he belongs to the chain of appearances.",["'V ill' in the OCR is corrected to 'Will'. 'mundo Noumeno' / 'mundo Phaenomeno' are in the dative in the German; given as nominative *mundus noumenus* / *mundus phaenomenus* in the English, with the Latin forms as printed in the German noted here.","Gemüths Kräfte = powers of mind (Gemüt = mind, Kraft = power); erkennen = cognize; Vernunft = reason; Absicht = aim.","'Verhältniße' = relations; 'Handlungen' = actions; 'Kette' = chain.","'und in dieser Absicht sind seine Handlungen frei, als auch zum mundo Phaenomeno': the 'als auch' construction is awkward in the German and kept awkward ('as also to'); the sentence has no verb for the second clause.","'darin besteht eben die Freiheit': 'freedom' is the subject, as in the German word order, preserved in the English."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-026":["We now come to the negative principles with regard to the world. They are the universal *principia* of formed nature. Of these negative principles there are 4 according to the number of the 4 genera of the categories: 1. goes to quality, 2 quantity, 3 to relation, the 4th to modality; thus: 1 *in mundo non datur saltus*, 2 *non datur hiatus*, 3 *non datur casus*, 4 *non datur fatum*. These all relate to the *nexus* in the world.",["Gattungen = genera (Gattung = genus); Zahl = number; Principien / Principia = principles (*principia*).","'non datui Casus' in the OCR is corrected to *non datur casus*; 'Nexum' (accusative as printed) is given as *nexus*.","'geformten Natur' = 'formed nature' (Form = form).","'Categorien' = categories; Qualitaet, Quantitaet, Relation, Modalitaet are retained as the cognate English words."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-027":["A *In mundo non datur saltus*. *Saltus* is in general *eventus*. (So in the advance *in argumentando*; where however one can understand the intermediate grounds under it, there it is *saltus legitimus*. Here *saltus* is the immediate transition from a *quantum* to what is not *quantum*. Space and time are *quanta continua*, the real in them is also *quantum*. For the real is inwardly the sensations, outwardly what corresponds to them. Sensation however has a degree and an infinite multiplicity of degrees. The simple is, with regard to the *quantum*, a zero. From that up to the *quantum* there are infinitely many parts. A thing therefore does not consist of finite degrees, also not of finite parts, for that would be an *infinitum datum*, but the *regressus* in dividing is infinite). The *saltus* is an immediate sequence of two states not at all combined with one another, without going through the states that may be between them. Every body has extensive magnitude, insofar as it is in space and in time, and also intensive magnitude or a degree of reality. No bodies are so closely related with one another that the difference of them could not be still smaller. There is no passing over from one appearance, quality, to another, without a transition through infinitely many intermediate degrees between the least of reality that we may ever assume, and nothing, are infinitely many intermediate degrees. In the world there is no smallest of appearance, because in space and time there is no smallest, but everything goes on in an infinite continuity. This proposition could also be expressed thus: Everything hangs together according to the *lex continui*, e.g. the transition from an appearance is only possible if I run through all the infinite intermediate degrees. No appearance can therefore consist of the simple, because from the simple up to matter there would be a sudden transition to something that is different from it *in genere*. This proposition one uses particularly in the proper sense of alterations of the states of things, for every alteration has its degree. No body can pass over from one state into another without running through all the intermediate states or intermediate degrees, e.g. if a body that is at rest moves all at once, then it must run through all the intermediate degrees from complete rest up to the degree of motion that it now has. This is now to be proved:",["'A' at the start is read as a section letter. 'Avancement' is kept as 'advance' (French loan in the German); 'bestellt' in the OCR read as 'besteht' (consists); 'infinituni' read as *infinitum*; 'lege continui' (as printed) given as *lex continui*; 'quanto' / 'Quanti' / 'Quantum' all given as *quantum*.","'Uebergang' = transition, 'Ueberschritt' = 'passing over' (two German words; the English differs only for clarity of the repeated 'übergehen' = 'pass over'); 'Folge' = sequence (here 'immediate sequence'); 'Verbindung'/'verbunden' = combined.","'Größe' = magnitude; 'Grad' = degree; 'Realitaet' = reality; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Raum' = space; 'Zeit' = time; 'Menge' = multiplicity ('eine unendliche Menge von Graden'); 'Theile' = parts; 'Einfache' = the simple (einfach = simple).","The sentence 'Es ist kein Ueberschritt ... zwischen dem geringsten der Realitaet ... und dem Nichts, sind unendlich viele Zwischen Grade' is syntactically garbled in the transcript (a repeated 'sind unendlich viele Zwischen Grade' and no clean break); kept as it stands, without emendation.","'Zero' = zero; 'im eigentlichen von Veränderungen': 'im eigentlichen' stands without a noun and is rendered 'in the proper sense'; the construction is awkward in the German.","'Veränderung' = alteration (the glossary has no entry; chosen and used consistently); 'Bewegung' = motion; 'Ruhe' = rest; 'Zustand' = state."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-028":["Suppose a thing is in the state A and wants to pass over into the state B. These are only 2 different states, hence they also cannot exist at the same time, but each in a particular time. The thing would therefore be in A in one moment and in B in the other. Between 2 moments there is a time (it may be as small as it will), for the moments themselves are only positions in time. In this time the thing will therefore be in neither of the two states, nor in any other state, because these are the ones immediately following one another. Thus nothing more remains of it than that it finds itself in the transition from one state to the other. But between 2 states there is a degree, and the transition to this degree will therefore happen without a series of intermediate degrees. Accordingly the thing will in its transition from one state to the other pass through the intermediate degrees of them. Since now the opposite of this would be a *saltus*, the proposition is demonstrated: *nihil fit in mundo per saltum*.",["Zustand = state; Augenblick = moment; Stelle = position (not Ort, which would be place); Grad = degree; Satz = proposition.","Text reads 'ohne eine Reihe von Zwischen Graden' ('without a series of intermediate degrees'); kept as it stands, though the sense of the next sentence may suggest another reading (e.g. 'durch'). Possible transcript slip; not emended.","'derselben' in the last sentence could refer back to the states or to the degrees; the English 'of them' preserves the ambiguity."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-029":["In the world everything thus alters according to degrees, for the causes always act little by little. Between the two moments there could be no empty time, but the cause had to act, otherwise no effect would have arisen. And there the cause must always have acted more strongly, until the effect attained the degree that it was to reach. E.g. If there are obscure representations in my soul, then this must seek to make the representations ever clearer to itself, until it finally obtains the sufficient degree of clarity.",["verändert sich = alters (Veränderung = alteration); Ursache = cause; Wirkung = effect; Vorstellung = representation; hinreichend rendered 'sufficient' (cf. zureichend).","'diese' (feminine) is left as 'this'; its antecedent (Seele, 'soul') is not replaced."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-030":["Since all alterations thus proceed without a leap, but *in continuo*, this is also called *lex continui*.",["Veränderung = alteration; Sprung = leap."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-031":["A triangle or other rectilinear figure I cannot describe through *motum continuum*. For at each corner I rest for some time; in the movement of a body from A to B I rest for a moment in B, and from B to C I rest for a moment in B. Between 2 moments there is a time in which the body ceases from its movement, thus here is *motus interruptus* and not *continuus*. The curved lines, on the other hand, have a *motum continuum*.",["The text reads 'in B' twice, also for the rest after B to C; kept as it stands (possibly a slip for C in the transcript).","Latin forms (*motum continuum*, *motus interruptus*) are kept as in the text."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-032":["With regard to the possible completeness and manifoldness I can represent the world to myself as infinite. But since this *materiale* does not yet constitute the perfection of the world, the connection of the substances among one another belongs to it. Finally the substances in this world become a complete whole, to which nothing is lacking for its perfection. According to this connection and arrangement it cannot be complete, although it could well be so according to another. This is the complete nature of the world : the formal perfection of it and certainly the foremost, that namely everything is in the most beautiful harmony and order. This nature-order is also so sacred and inviolable to us that we would certainly despise a world that *ratione materiae* were ever so much better than this world, but *ratione formae* again worse, and would put it far below our world. The perfection of the world one can also think in the teleological sense (i.e. according to ends or practically). If one can unite all possible ends in one chief end, then it is called *summum bonum*. — *Perfectum* and *optimum* are thus distinguished from one another. The entire nature one thinks of as a realm. Realm is the relation of many to One as *summum Imperantem* or lawgiver, and so it is also in nature. What happens once under certain circumstances, that happens under the same circumstances through eternity. *Summum bonum* is accordingly the most perfect realm of nature as the most complete system of all ends.",["Materiale is left in Latinate form (neuter noun; not Materie = matter, nor Stoff = material). Vollkommenheit = perfection; Verknüpfung = connection; Zweck = end.","'im Teleologischen Verstände': Verstand elsewhere = understanding; here rendered 'sense' as the idiomatic 'in the ... sense'.","OCR 'Hie gesamte Natur' read as 'Die gesamte Natur'. 'Natur Ordnung' (two words) rendered 'nature-order'. The space before the colon after 'Welt' is kept from the source.","'summum Imperantem' kept as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-033":["2nd Chapter",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-034":["On the reciprocal influence of the substances in the world.",["OCR 'W eit' read as 'Welt'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-035":["The possibility of the *nexus* of the substances is the possibility of the formal perfection in the world. This I can just as little have insight into as the possibility of the first fundamental force of things. For each substance exists through its concept for itself, therefore seems to be isolated, and to concern the other substance not at all. One says, to be sure, that it could not be otherwise, for if a body moves, it moves along with it the body that is in its way and thus acts on it. There, however, *nexus* in space and time is already laid down as the ground, and then one would have to ask, how are space and time possible? This question did not come into philosophy until the question was raised, how soul as a spiritual and body as an extended being could stand in *commercio* with one another. This one would have to ask in general respect: how are substances in general in *commercio* with one another possible? Leibnitz first brought this onto the track. After him this investigation was driven to the highest by Wolf (in whose philosophy the proposition first stands) and Baumgarten. Now, however, since one seeks merely popularity and gladly lets thoroughness go by the board, this proposition too has been left lying, although it is one of the most important in the whole philosophy. (*Influxus realis* presupposes a passion, which however is at the same time *actio*. How this is possible I cannot derive at all from the concept of substance. In the *mundo Phaenomenon* that is possible from the mere existence of the substance in space. — For space combines them all. In the *mundo Noumenon* it is not possible otherwise than if one assumes a common cause, i.e. God, who has already laid that in the nature of substance. That is Leibnitz's *harmonia praestabilita*. The *Influxus physicus* is *qualitas occulta*, for through the word nothing is cognized. The concept of space accomplishes in the *mundo sensibilis* what the divine omnipresence does in the *mundo Noumenon*, and one can therefore call it as it were a *Phaenomenon* of divine omnipresence. Perhaps God wanted thereby to give us to cognize his omnipresence sensibly. Newton called it the *sensorium* of divine omnipresence. Perhaps space is also the only sensibility that belongs to all rational beings besides God.) (NB. as Leibnitz's system is represented here, he presumably also understood it, only because he expresses himself obscurely, one took it in another sense, which is false). All connection of the substances among one another lets itself be regarded as *nexus realis* or *commercium substantiarum*, if substances act reciprocally in one another, or as *nexus idealis*, i.e. *harmonia substantiarum absque commercio*. The *nexus idealis* is not connection in the things themselves, but merely in the idea of the spectator who considers them, e.g. if I hear the most pleasant music, I sense in my hearing a harmony of all the instruments. This harmony is however merely in me and in my thoughts; but the instruments have not the slightest harmony with one another. According to this *nexu ideali* 2 systems have been invented, which are to explain the *commercium* of the substances among one another. To this belong 1.) the Cartesian *Systema harmoniae occasionaliter stabilitae* or *Systema occasionalismi vel Assistentiae*. 2) The Leibnitzian *Systema harmoniae praestabilitae*. Both are *Systemata externa stabilita*. For since the *commercium* can never be explained from the nature of the substance, they derive it from an *ente extramundo*.",["OCR corrections: Hie/Hiese/Hieses = Die/Diese/Dieses; Henn = Denn; Ha = Da; Präge = Frage; Hinge = Dinge; Hie Möglichkeit = Die Möglichkeit; ei'klärt = erklärt; Aßistentiae read as Assistentiae; Philosphie = Philosophie.","Grund Kraft = fundamental force (Grund = ground, Kraft = force); einsehen = have insight into; Wesen = being; Begriff = concept; Möglichkeit = possibility.","Verknüpfung = connection; Latin *nexus* and *commercium* left in Latin; erkennen = cognize; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Idee = idea.","Parentheses and the NB parenthesis are kept as in the source; the sentence 'wie hier Leibnitz' System vorgestellet ist' is left with its ambiguous 'es' (it/the system).","Spellings Leibnitz and Wolf kept as in the German; Latin case endings (*mundo Phaenomenon*, *nexu ideali*, *ente extramundo*) are kept exactly as printed, including apparent irregularities (*mundo sensibilis*, *harmonia praestabilita*)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-036":["1. The *Systema Aßistentiae* of Cartesius consists in this, that in every cause and effect or in every *influxus* of substances God would be *Caussa intermedia* and would arrange the effects harmoniously to the causes. E.g. with Leibnitz there is the habit that only one clock strikes of itself, the others are struck by certain people with a mallet. In such a way all clocks strike together. These now harmonize, each in my ear, but this harmony between the first clock as cause and the other as effect does not come from the first clock, but from *caussis intermediis*, the people who strike the clocks, and these make the harmony among the clocks. So it is also with the *nexus* of substances in the world. Cartes had devised this system first in order to derive from it the *commercium* of the soul with the body. For because he could not represent to himself how the soul could act immediately upon the body, he assumed thereby a *Caussam intermediam*, which on every occasion governed the body in accordance with the will of the soul.",["*Aßistentiae* is kept as printed (presumably *Assistentiae*). Latin spellings with ß are rendered with ss.","*wirken* is rendered 'act' (with *auf*) when intransitive and 'effect' when transitive, throughout these passages.","'Cartesius' and 'Cartes' are as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-037":["2 Leibnitz's *harmonia praestabilita*. Is almost one and the same with that. Both assume a *causam intermediam extramundanam*. But Cartes assumes it with every alteration of the substance. Leibnitz, on the other hand, stated that the highest being had from eternity already so arranged the things that they harmonized with one another, and one's alteration would thus seem to be the work of the other, e.g. if clocks, which are all driven of themselves by a clockwork, strike at the same time, then this harmony does not come from them, but is preestablished. The author of it has arranged them so. These two systems, however, rest on the *nexus idealis*. But if the substances stood in no other *nexus*, there would be no world, but merely an ideal whole in thoughts. There indeed every substance would be rational and isolated, if it itself should effect nothing. With right it has been objected against this that then in respect of the soul the bodies would be entirely superfluous. For God could also have let the representations of the body arise in the soul, either *occasionaliter* or *praestabiliter*, without actual bodies having been necessary. These representations could indeed always harmonize with the soul. All of this, as one sees, runs out into idealism. Afterwards one can also say that *ratione corporum* the things would be superfluous. For since the souls effect nothing, God could indeed also without them effect all alterations in the bodies. Further, one body would also be superfluous against the other or with regard to the other. Each could be isolated. One sees from all this that these systems are like siblings.",["*in Absicht* ('in respect of') is the idiom; *Absicht* elsewhere = 'aim'.","'m den Körpern' in the OCR is read as 'in den Körpern'.","'Is almost one and the same with that' follows the German's sentence fragment after the title-phrase."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-038":["But there is still a second *nexus* in the world, namely the *nexus realis* or the *influxus*, when substances flow into one another reciprocally *per actionem et reactionem*. This is now possible in a twofold way: either as *originarius* or as *Defectivus*. That is the *influxus* of those substances that are already capable merely through their existence of acting on one another. Now the question is: can substances be *originarie* in *commercio*? No, for *substantiae originariae* are *per se existentes*, thus independent or isolated. Since, then, they would exist so that no determination of their existence depended on anything else, they would also not be in *commercio* with one another. A *totum originarium* of substances is therefore impossible. 2 *Derivationes*, when substances exist so that the possibility of their *commercium* depends on a third substance. If substances are in *commercio*, then they are dependent on one another, hence contingent and *caussatum alterius*. The *causa*, however, must be an *ens originarium*. But many *entia originaria* cannot be the cause of the world and of its *commercium*. For since every *ens originarium* would be isolated, its work too would have to be completely isolated from the work of the other. But this we do not find in the world, for there everything is in thoroughgoing *nexus*. Accordingly the world also must have only one cause. The *nexus* of substances is therefore to be thought possible only as derivative, but thereby not as ideal, but at the same time as real. This proof, however, holds only of the *mundus noumenon*. In the *mundus phaenomenon* we do not need this, for it is nothing in itself. Here everything is in *commercio* by virtue of space. The systems of occasional and preestablished harmony take place only in the world of the senses. For here the question is whether God arranged the harmony so in the beginning or in the continuation, and this presupposes time. *Influxus physicus* is called *crassiori sensu originarie*, insofar as God now effects nothing thereby; *sensu subtiliori* or *derivatus*, in that from God as the *ante originario* the possibility of the *influxus* nevertheless comes. —",["'Defectivus' is printed so; the context ('Derivationes', 'derivativ', 'derivatus') suggests *derivativus*, but the reading is kept and flagged as doubtful.","'ante originario' is as printed; possibly *ente originario* (cf. *ens originarium*). Doubtful.","'au zwiefache' read as 'auf zwiefache'; 'craßiori' normalized to *crassiori*.","*Bestimmung* = 'determination'; *Dasein* = 'existence'; *Möglichkeit* = 'possibility'.","*Sinnen Welt* rendered 'world of the senses'; *Mundus Noumenon/Phaenomenon* lowercased in the English Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-039":["The *influxus physicus* occurs according to universal laws, but the two systems of the *nexus idealis* do not. —",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-040":["3rd Chapter. On the Natural.",["*Natuerlichen* is the transcript's spelling of *Natürlichen*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-041":["Nature can be taken in a twofold understanding: 1 *adjective* as the particular nature of each thing, 2 *substantive* of the world according to universal principles and laws, that is the entire nature or the world of the senses according to its combination (*natura universa*). In *sensu substantivo* there is only one nature, in *sensu adjectivo* as many things, so many natures. Natural is that which can be explained from the nature of a thing, or an occurrence is natural if the cause of it is encountered in the whole of the world. It can now be properly natural, as it can be explained from the nature of the thing with which it takes place, or it is also respectively natural, if the ground of it lies in another thing. Thus art too is natural, in that it springs from the nature of some thing. Absolutely natural is that whose cause is encountered in the entire nature. This is also called natural simply (course of nature is succession of the series of occurrences), or the course of nature is the interconnection of occurrences, insofar as it is connected with experience. According to the course of nature I experience what happens. The order of nature is the ground of this connecting-together of appearances. — Course of nature shows the regularity of experience, order of nature the necessity of it.",["*im doppelten Verstände*: *Verstand* kept as 'understanding' for consistency with the glossary, though here it is the older sense of 'meaning'.","*schlechthin* rendered 'simply'; *Zusammenknüpfung* 'connecting-together' (cf. *verknüpft* 'connected').","'succeßion' (Latinate) rendered 'succession'; 'natürlieh' read as 'natürlich'.","*Begebenheit* = 'occurrence'; *Erscheinung* = 'appearance'; *Zusammenhang* = 'interconnection'.","'respective' is the Latin-derived adverb in the German; rendered 'respectively' (sense: 'relatively' is possible)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-042":["An occurrence belongs to the course of nature insofar as it is a part of the alteration that arise in the world. The order in nature arouses in us always admiration and high regard. But whence does that come? From this, because without nature we would have no objects of the understanding. Without nature nothing is an object of the understanding, since the understanding cognizes nothing without rules. Now we could not, however, make rules for ourselves, if in the world everything first happened as without the slightest order. We would then have either no understanding at all or a useless one. Since, then, the worth of our understanding, this nobility of our soul that elevates us to humanity, rests on nature, we hold this order of nature as it were for something holy, and shy from violating it; even if it should work to our harm, e.g. if a wall falls over from age and strikes a human being dead, we will surely not rejoice over it. But because this happens according to the order of nature, we do indeed wish that the human being had not gone by at that time, but we find it absurd to wish that the wall had stood longer than it could stand according to the laws of nature. —",["'the alteration that arise' preserves the singular/plural mismatch of the German (*die Veränderung, die ... entspringen*).","*Gegenstände* and *Object* are both rendered 'object'.","'langer' read as 'länger'; 'Natur Ordnung' rendered 'order of nature'.","*Verstand* = 'understanding'; *Werth* = 'worth'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-043":["4th Chapter. On the Supernatural and the Possibility of Miracles.",["*Uebernatuerlichen* is the transcript's spelling of *Übernatürlichen*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-044":["Supernatural, *supernaturale*, is what cannot be explained from the entire nature. Counternatural, *praeternaturale*, is what is contrary to the particular nature of a thing, but can nevertheless be cognized from the nature of other things or from the entire nature, e.g., that a human being cannot create does indeed not come from his nature, but nevertheless from the nature of other things, which are the cause of it. Not every *supernaturale* is a miracle, but [only] what happens contrary to the order of nature in the world. What happens contrary to the order of nature outside the world are no miracles, e.g., creation is no miracle. The miracles are either *miracula rigorosa* or *comparativa*; the former are those that cannot be explained from the entire nature and have as author a *caussam extramundanam*, and because here the order of nature is interrupted, only the author, namely God, can do that. — The *miracula rigorosa* are in turn either *occasionalia* or *praestabilita*; the former is that which God works *immediate* on every occasion and thereby interrupts the order of nature. This is the proper miracle. A *miraculum praestabilitum* is what God works *mediate* through natural causes, which however are used by God for particular aims, or what God has already determined from the beginning and uses natural causes for its execution. This is also called an effect of nature, but it is just such a miracle as the occasional one. For it does indeed have a natural cause as its nearest cause, but this lies not in the laws of nature, but in the predetermination of God, who wanted it to work precisely at that time and under those circumstances. *Miraculum occasionale* is also called *materiale*, and *praestabilitum*, *formale*. (A *miraculum materiale* is that where the cause and causality of the miracle is outside the world, or where God brings forth something new through his mere might. *Formale* — where the immediate cause does indeed lie in nature, but the causality of it is in God. Both are equal miracles, for here it depends merely on the form; *forma dat esse rei*.) One explains the *miracula praestabilita* as miracles of nature, for one says: God works them after all according to nature, indeed not according to the general, but still particular laws. In these, however, one must again assume a particular divine direction, and thus this is an emanation of God. It is just as unnatural as the occasional one. Thus, e.g., Whiston believes he can explain the Flood naturally, if he assumes that a comet came, poured out its watery tail, and that thereby it happened. Thereupon it drew the water back to itself. But that is nevertheless a miracle. For it is nevertheless no law of nature that a comet should flood the earth merely then and because its inhabitants were godless. (Thus Teichmeyer too believed he had discovered something great, since he showed that, if an ice-cloud stepped in front of the sun, its shadow might then retreat by that much, and that thus the miracle with Ahaz's sundial would be easily explained.) The *miracula praestabilita* are still greater miracles, for they are devised still more artfully. But one gladly makes all miracles into preestablished ones, because one thereby pays one's own reason a compliment, that one has been able to contrive it so artfully.) Meanwhile one nevertheless believes to find more reason in the explanation of a miracle as a *miraculum praestabilitum*. It does not come from our not believing the possibility of a miracle at all, but from the fact that, in the acceptance of a miracle that is occasional, all our understanding ceases in the explanation of it. And if one means that God predetermined these miracles, that they should happen through ordinary natural causes, then one judges of God anthropopathically, for just as it costs human beings more trouble to do something little by little than all at once, so one believes it costs God also more trouble to do miracles on every occasion than if he had already arranged it so at the creation.",["Wiedernatürlich / wieder die Ordnung: 'wieder' is the printed spelling of 'wider'; rendered 'counternatural' / 'contrary to'. 'Uebernatürlich' = 'supernatural'; 'unnatürlich' = 'unnatural'.","erklären / Erklärung rendered 'explain / explanation' (glossary: Erklärung = explanation); erkannt = 'cognized'.","Absichten = 'aims'; Gesetze = 'laws'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Vernunft = 'reason'; Möglichkeit = 'possibility' (möglich).","'sondern das, was ...': the bracketed 'only' is not in the German; it is added for English syntax only.","OCR corrections: 'Wfiiston' = Whiston; 'Siindfluth' = Sündfluth; 'pi aestabilitum' = praestabilitum; 'Mira culum' = Miraculum; 'caußam' kept as printed (for causam); 'eße' kept as printed (esse); 'Teichmayer' (Teichmeyer) is the transcript's spelling, normalized to the usual form. The numeral '2' before 'Miraculum praestabilitum' is a stray mark and omitted.","'Natur Ordnung' / 'Natur Ursachen' / 'Natur Wirkung' / 'Natur Wunder' (separated in the text) rendered 'order of nature', 'natural causes', 'effect of nature', 'miracles of nature'.","Ambiguity kept: 'dessen Vollziehung' and 'sie' (natural cause / miracle) are left as in the German; 'Dieses heißt auch eine Natur Wirkung' — 'this' is not specified. 'denn' in 'bloß denn und darum' is temporal ('then'), as in the German; 'aussinnen' = 'contrive'.","The parentheses and the unclosed/misplaced bracketing around the Teichmeyer and 'compliment' sentences follow the OCR; the closing parenthesis after 'aussinnen können' has no matching opening and is kept as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-045":["Nevertheless it is always good if one seeks to explain miracles naturally, at least better than if one seeks to make it into a miracle. Now the question is: How are miracles possible at all? Yes, *effectus testatur de caußa*. Since now a *caußa extramundana* is the cause of the order of nature, it can also suspend the same. No world can be thought without deficiencies, without certain negations and restrictions, and miracles are therefore possible even in the best world, in order to supplement the defect of nature, and according to the concept of God's goodness and truth quite probable. Now the question still arises, however: How is reason authorized to assume miracles? For in general we must admit that miracles are possible, but in particular cases we must always resist assuming them, for they conflict against the subjective maxims of our reason, which command us to assume only that in which a use of reason takes place.) 1 Every miracle presupposes order of nature. For if this were not, there would be no miracle at all. Everything would be miracle. Since this is not so, however, we see that miracles are merely an exception from the order of nature. Exceptions, however, cannot be frequent; otherwise all rules and their certainty cease. Miracles, therefore, cannot be frequent either. Even the concept of the word distinguishes it from the everyday. 2. If we regard nature as a cause of things subsisting for itself, and God accordingly not as the creator, but as architect and ruler of the same, then we could assume more frequent miracles. For since matter would not come from God, it could also have many deficiencies, which could be improved only little by little. Since, however, God is creator of the world, he will surely have created it unimprovable. Therefore in such a world miracles can be extremely rare. It is therefore also false if one says that he who denies miracles cognizes nature as a cause subsisting in itself. Rather the opposite takes place here. For he does this merely because he represents God to himself as the creator of the world, and not merely as the architect of the same. Since, however, he represents God to himself as the cause of the order of nature, he therefore also believes that all occurrences in the world come from God. He assumes, then, merely God as *caußa mediata* of all occurrences; whereas he who establishes miracles regards God in them as *caußa immediata*. Rather, he who regards nature as a cause independent of God can for that very reason believe miracles all the less, in that he represents to himself, and can represent to himself, that nature sometimes comes into collision with God and God, because he is more powerful than it, makes exceptions to its rule. Yes, he can then wish for miracles, because he cannot expect it of a blindly running nature that it will properly attain perfection. He who again assumes miracles everywhere, in that he believes thereby to glorify God's power, does precisely the opposite. For he asserts thereby the incompleteness and inadequacy of the order of nature, whose deficiencies are supplemented through miracles. Since, however, the order of nature is God's work, he says thereby that God has made an imperfect work, and in this God's wisdom and omnipotence suffer very greatly. From all that has been adduced we see that miracles, if they are ever in the world, must occur only extremely rarely. Now we also want to see through which grounds our reason can be moved to assume miracles. (If the cause of an occurrence is not met with in nature, then it is a *hiatus*. The sequels of what springs from it have no interconnection with the previous; but in order to know that, I must know all causes in the world, in order to know that this one is not in the world. Every object that is connected with others is experience. Miracles are spoken of as objects of experience. The cognition of miracles does not rest on mere perception. Miracles presuppose nature and are mere exceptions from it, must therefore not be frequent, otherwise they suspend the order of nature and would be no miracles.) Historical testimonies cannot prove them, but moral ones must also be added. In order, therefore, to demonstrate the credibility of a miracle, one must presuppose a natural order of the human will, that it be impossible to lie. This is of no use, however. For here I must believe that something is against the order of nature on account of the testimony according to the order of nature. But can the moral order of nature not just as well be suspended? Can man not lie, and is *pia fraus* something unheard of? Further, it is also no miracle to deceive oneself. For almost again a miracle belongs to it, to distinguish occurrences of nature from miracles and always to know what a miracle is. Historical and moral testimonies can therefore not yet convince us of the existence of miracles. There must therefore still be added this, that reason has insight that it is an occurrence that is indispensable to the perfection of the world and yet impossible according to the order of nature. This occurrence, however, cannot contribute anything to the perfection of nature, for this is made unimprovable by God; but it can contribute merely to the furtherance of our moral world. This does not depend at all on the order of nature. If, therefore, miracles happen for the confirmation of a doctrine that conduces to our moral perfection, then they are credible. For we see at once that God could not lay them into nature at the beginning of the world and yet necessarily had to do them.",["The passage begins mid-sentence in the German as given, and has a closing parenthesis \")\" with no opening one before \"1\"; I kept both as they stand. The \"1\" and \"2.\" are the transcript's enumeration.","\"Wunder\" = miracle throughout; the first sentence's \"es zum Wunder zu machen\" has \"es\" with no clear antecedent (the plural \"Wunder\" precedes), kept as in the German.","\"Mängel\" = deficiencies; \"Defect\" (Latinate) = defect; \"Schranke\" would be limitation, whereas \"Einschränkungen\" is rendered \"restrictions\"; \"Unzulänglichkeit\" = inadequacy (not in glossary).","\"wieder\" in \"streiten wieder die ... Maximen\" and \"wieder die Ordnung\" is the transcript's spelling of \"wider\" (against); in \"fast wieder ein Wunder\" and \"Der nun wieder überall\" it could be \"again\" or \"against\"; rendered \"again\", the sense left open.","\"aufheben\" = suspend, throughout; \"statuirt\" = \"establishes\" (Latinate *statuere*); \"Natur Ordnung\" (written as two words) = \"order of nature\", but \"eine Natur Ordnung des menschlichen Willens\" = \"a natural order of the human will\".","\"Götter Macht\" is read as \"Gottes Macht\" (apparent slip in the OCR or transcript); \"mäßen\" read as \"müssen\".","\"vor sich selbst bestehende\" = \"subsisting for itself\" versus \"an sich bestehende\" = \"subsisting in itself\"; \"Folgen\" = \"sequels\" (Folge); \"Zusammenhang\" = interconnection; \"Zeugniß\" = testimony.","\"Erkenntniß\" = cognition; \"erkennt\" in \"für eine ... Ursache erkennt\" = \"cognizes\"; \"Begebenheit\" = occurrence; \"Gegenstand\" = object; \"Begriff\" = concept."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-046":["This is now the only case where we can assume miracles and which is not against our reason and the wisdom of God. For the small loss that is caused by the interruption of the order of nature is seen here to be abundantly replaced by the great advantage that humanity has from it. Miracles would also be possible then, if the hypothesis were true that the highest perfection would be attained when human beings were determined to a certain community with the highest being. —",["\"ersetz\" read as \"ersetzt\" (OCR or transcript slip).","\"nicht zuwieder\" = \"zuwider\" (against), rendered \"not against\".","\"bestimmt wären\" is subjunctive; \"determined\" (bestimmen), with \"vocation\" (Bestimmung) as the other glossary sense; \"Gemeinschaft\" = community; \"Wesen\" = being."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-047":["2 *Miracula comparativa*. These are occurrences that are supposed to happen from higher beings or unknown forces of nature. But that is nothing at all. For if I do not know the cause, then I also cannot conclude anything from it. But not merely the unknownness of the cause, but also that of its law of effect belongs to the *miraculum comparativum*. Natural causes whose law of effect is nevertheless known, even if not the causes, are not *miracula comparativa*, and that is the case with the *qualitatibus occultis*. They are indeed unknown, but their laws of effect nevertheless are not; thus their effects too are not *miracula comparativa*. With higher beings, on the other hand, to whom we ascribe *miracula comparativa*, e.g. with devils, we know neither them nor their laws of effect. Then, however, it is quite contrary to reason to assume them as cause of many occurrences. For since we know of no rules to give of their effects, we cannot after all determine what comes from them or not. Thus we assume them merely arbitrarily. To believe in *miracula comparativa* would be indeed in the mouth, but not in the heart. Many human beings believe everything that they do not have insight into; at once they know the supernatural, because they do not want to use their reason. But whoever believes in the natural can sooner expect the supernatural from God than the opponent. But the human beings who love the supernatural so very much have after all also a particular ground. They do it, namely, from pride. For there are two ways to arrogance: either to raise oneself above others, or to draw others down to oneself. The latter is the easiest. Since now these people do not want to apply so much reason, or do not even have any, to have insight into the natural causes of such occurrences, they envy these others nevertheless and want to make them also into such blockheads as they themselves are. Hence they seek to offer them, in a concealed pious language, the belief in the supernatural. Whence does it come, then, that everywhere one does not at once call present ones, but only old ones, miracles? Not from this, that now no miracles are needed any more, for that one will never be able to prove, but for this reason: the old miracles and their sequels are already brought under certain rules. With the new miracles, however, we do not know the sequels that can arise from them. They could overturn in our reason morality and everything, and hence we do not at once assume them.",["Wirkungs Gesetz: rendered \"law of effect\" (Wirkung = effect); plural \"laws of effect\". Natur Ursachen = \"natural causes\"; Ursache = cause throughout.","*qualitatibus occultis* and *miraculo Comparativo* stand as printed (case forms as in the transcript). \"Kall\" read as \"Fall\"; \"willkiihrlich\" as \"willkührlich\"; \"Wundem\" as \"Wundern\".","\"Denn ist es aber ganz vernunftwiedrig\": \"Denn\" is probably dialectal \"dann\"; rendered \"Then\". This is a reading, not certain.","Folge = sequel (in \"ihre Folgen\", \"die Folgen\"); folgern = conclude. kennen = know (acquainted with); einsehen = \"have insight into\" (not in glossary).","\"ia\" = \"ja\", rendered \"after all\"; \"besonderen Grund\" = \"particular ground\"; Hochmuth = arrogance; Stolz = pride; Dummköpfen = \"blockheads\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-001":["Third Part On Psychology Introduction",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-002":["All cognition of things is twofold. Either they can be given through experience or not. Both ways of cognition belong to pure metaphysics or transcendental philosophy. Transcendental philosophy was supposed to be merely ontology, but the cognitions are twofold in it, as we have already stated. The objects of ontology can be given in experience and are cognitions of the understanding. But there are also cognitions of reason that cannot be given in experience. These arise from the cognitions of the understanding, which reason, however, so extends that they can never be given in experience. It does this by giving them absolute totality. Of such ideas of reason there are two: 1. the absolute totality of many things, i.e. the absolute world-whole, 2. the absolute totality of one thing, i.e. *ens realissimum*. These are now treated in cosmology and theology, hence these should properly belong to ontology, and these 3, ontology, cosmology and theology, make up *metaphysica pura*. In *metaphysica pura* there occur the intuitions of sensibility, as space and time; the categories of the understanding and the ideas of reason. The second part of metaphysics is *metaphysica applicata*, whose objects are in experience, but which nevertheless rest on principles *a priori*. That is the metaphysics of nature, and indeed 1. of corporeal nature or objects of outer sense, that is *physica generalis*, and 2. thinking nature, or of the objects of inner sense, i.e. *psychologia rationalis*. Both make up *metaphysicam applicatam*. The *physica generalis* occurs in cosmology, but the *psychologia rationalis* will follow.",["Totalitaet = \"totality\" (glossary Allheit is also \"totality\"); Vernunft Ideen = \"ideas of reason\"; Welt Ganze = \"world-whole\".","\"principien\" (Latinate) rendered \"principles\"; the glossary gives the same word for Grundsatz and Satz.","OCR \"Psychologia rationale\" and \"rationahs\" read as *rationalis*; \"realißimum\" as *realissimum*; stray \". 1\" at the end omitted.","\"Arten\" = \"ways\"; \"Gegenstände\" = \"objects\"; \"Erkenntnisse\" = \"cognitions\"; *Metaphysicam applicatam* kept as printed (accusative)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-003":["Opposed to these two sciences are empirical physics and psychology, which draw their principles merely from experience, and not *a priori*. These belong, however, on that account not at all in metaphysics. Empirical physics also makes up a particular science; empirical psychology, since it has only recently begun to be worked on, is not yet so complete that it could furnish a particular science. Hence it is, since one knew how to pair it with no other science, attached to the *psychologia rationalis* as a stranger and guest, in that it is still most akin to this. We direct ourselves according to the author, and want therefore to take up empirical psychology first.",["OCR \"I hysic\" read as \"Physic\" (rendered \"physics\").","\"besondre/besondere Wissenschaft\" = \"particular science\"; \"verwandtesten\" rendered \"most akin\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-004":["First Section",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-005":["On empirical psychology.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-001":["First Chapter On the Existence of the Soul",["Dasein = existence."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-002":["The soul is the object of inner sense. — Whoever denies it therefore also denies inner sense. (One calls the soul πνεῦμα, but that means a quite free and pure spirit, properly more commonly it is called ψυχή, which signifies a papillon, butterfly, and is very fitting, in that it gives a glorious symbol of its discharge, passing away in dying, from the burden of the body and of the passage over into a better life. (The word soul signifies properly the inward of a thing, e.g. in a quill, a cannon.) My thoughts are not an object of the outer senses, for they have after all no figure. In myself I have the body as an object of outer senses. This, however, is distinguished from the soul. Its determinations are different, for the form of its intuition is different. The form of intuition in the body is space, in the soul time, for thoughts are not in space.",["The Greek is badly garbled in the OCR (\"TCveujaa\", \"^uxf)\"); read as πνεῦμα and ψυχή, a reconstruction.","\"bei einer Jeder, Canone\": \"Jeder\" read as \"Feder\" (quill/spring) and \"Canone\" as \"Kanone\"; the reading is doubtful. The first parenthesis is never closed in the text, kept as printed.","Gegenstand = \"object\"; Bestimmungen = \"determinations\"; Anschauung = \"intuition\"; Seele = \"soul\"; Geist = \"spirit\"; Sinn = \"sense\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-003":["Here we consider the soul merely as the object of inner sense, and that rests on our own experience, which no one can deny. Moreover we do not concern ourselves here with the question whether the soul is material or not, because we cannot prove this from experience, and it therefore belongs in *Psychologia rationalis*. The soul is merely our I, not the body, but body and soul together, as human consciousness, are also called I. In empirical psychology we consider our I as soul and as human being. The body, however, we consider on the one hand as organ of the soul, that depends on it, on the other hand however as dwelling, since the soul also often depends on it. In empirical psychology, therefore, a brief anthropology is presented. We assume it as an undoubted experience that the soul exists. Kartes, however, and in a similar way the author, seek to bring this out first through an inference. The former says: *Cogito ergo sum*. But that is unnecessary, for *cogito* or *sum cogitans* is the same. Kartes himself said, of an actuality as of an object of inner sense I am conscious *immediate*. Of the actuality, however, of objects of outer sense, e.g. of my body, I can become conscious only *mediate* through an inference, and yet he here infers the existence of the soul, thus sins again against his own rule. That I exist as human being is already an inference, and indeed an inference that gives no mathematical certainty, for the idealist denies the existence of bodies. Empirical psychology divides into 2 main parts; namely 1. into the consideration of the soul in itself — 2. into the consideration of the community of the soul with the body. The forces of the human soul divide into 3 main classes: 1. faculty of cognition, 2. pleasure or displeasure, 3. faculty of desire. Pleasure precedes the faculty of desire, and the faculty of cognition precedes pleasure. According as the faculty of cognition is different, so also are the two following different. There is an upper and a lower faculty of cognition. The former is understanding, the latter sensibility. So there is also a rational and a sensible feeling of pleasure or displeasure (and so it is also with the faculty of desire). Through the sensible faculty of cognition the subject itself is passive, and hence it is said, it has receptivity; through the upper it is itself active and hence it has spontaneity. These two faculties are thus different through the way in which the subject is constituted thereby.",["OCR: 'aut' read as 'auf'; 'Existenzder' as 'Existenz der'; 'tliatig' as 'thätig'; 'sin' as 'sind'.","The text repeats a stretch ('Nachdem das Erkenntniß Vermögen verschieden ist … vor der Lust vor') with a broken 'fol- / und' at the join; I translated the repeated stretch once.","*Wirklichkeit* = 'actuality'; *Existenz* and *Dasein* are both 'existence'; *Schluß* = 'inference'; *Gemeinschaft* = 'community'; *Kräfte* = 'forces'; *Erkenntniß Vermögen* = 'faculty of cognition'; *Begehrungs Vermögen* = 'faculty of desire'; *Lust oder Unlust* = 'pleasure or displeasure' (not in the glossary).","*Kartes* is the transcript's spelling of Descartes; 'the author' is *der Autor*. *Receptivitaet* and *Spontaneitaet* are rendered as 'receptivity' and 'spontaneity'; *leidend* = 'passive'.","Ambiguity kept: 'der von ihr abhängt' (the relative 'der' could refer to the body or the organ; 'that depends on it'). *immediate* and *mediate* are Latin adverbs in the German, kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-004":["We have pleasure or displeasure without desiring or abhorring, e.g. when we see a beautiful region, it delights us, but on that account we will not at once wish to possess it. Pleasure or displeasure is thus something entirely different from the faculty of desire. But on the other side we can desire or abhor nothing in which pleasure or displeasure does not lie at the ground. For to what I have no pleasure, I also do not will. Thus pleasure or displeasure precedes desiring or abhorring. But what I desire I must yet previously cognize, also that in which I have pleasure or displeasure; accordingly the faculty of cognition lies at the ground of both. So there are also many representations that are connected neither with pleasure nor with displeasure, and thus the faculty of cognition is entirely distinguished from the feeling of pleasure or displeasure. All these 3 main forces of the soul taken together make up its life.",["'auch' after 'an was ich Lust oder Unlust habe' stands as in the text, after a comma and a semicolon; the sentence is elliptical and kept as it is ('also that in which I have pleasure or displeasure').","*Grund* = 'ground'; *Vorstellungen* = 'representations'; *Gefühl* = 'feeling'; *Hauptkräfte* = 'main forces'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-005":["(Consciousness is principle of the possibility of the understanding, but not of sensibility. Consciousness according to choice is attention — the counterpart of the same, abstraction. The self lies at the ground of consciousness and is the peculiar of the mind. This self, however, we can consider 3-fold: I think as intelligence, i.e. the subject of thinking is intelligence. I think as subject that has sensibility, and am soul. I think as intelligence and soul, and am human being. A body that is animated only by a soul and not by intelligence is an animal. The faculty of grasping the thought: I am, belongs merely to intelligence. This I remains, even when everything has changed, when body and principles have changed. What now constitutes the identity of its self is difficult to know; to this everything is related, everything can change, only consciousness and the apperception or the faculty of referring the representations to its self remain. This apperception in a certain degree gives distinct, in a lesser degree obscure representations. These are thus distinguished not in kind but in degree. The clarity in intuition, however, is very different from the clarity in concept. The former is aesthetic, the latter logical clarity. The actualization of attention, whereby only a single representation is made clear and all the rest are obscured, is abstraction. In abstraction attention does not cease, but is only directed from one or several objects to one, and the remaining representations all obscured, and the one clear).",["*Princip* = 'principle' (as *Grundsatz* is also 'principle' in the glossary); *Willkühr* = 'choice'; *Geist* = 'mind'; *Mensch* = 'human being'.","'das Wiederspiel derselben Abstraction' is left as ambiguous as the German ('the counterpart of the same, abstraction'); 'derselben' could refer to attention or be read with 'Abstraction'.","'sein Selbst' / 'seines Selbst' rendered 'its self'; the German does not settle the possessor. 'der Art, sondern dem Grade nach': 'in kind but in degree'.","*Aufmerksamkeit* and *Attention* are both 'attention'. *Klarheit in der Anschauung* / *im Begriffe*: 'in intuition' / 'in concept'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-006":["The first and the main representation is that of I or the consciousness of myself, the apperception (as Herr Prof. Kant calls it in his Critic.)",["The closing parenthesis follows the full stop, as in the text."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-007":["This is now peculiar to the human soul alone and distinguishes it from all animals. The animal has no apperception, and hence it is also capable of no moral principles, no understanding and no use of reason, as will be shown further afterwards. The body of me is my body, because its changes are mine. For I can only thereby get representations of outer things and my body be affected through them. A being that is merely object of inner sense cannot know its relation to things that are objects of outer sense. Where my body is, there is also my thinking I, for only through it can I know the position of myself. I myself, however, take up no particular space in the body, since I cannot be intuited according to the form of space. The body determines only my relation of place with regard to other things (that I namely am in it), but not with regard to myself and where I am in it. Our representations are either obscure or clear etc. Obscure representations are those of which I am not immediately conscious, but can yet become mediately conscious through inferences.",["OCR: 'Kaum' read as 'Raum', 'Eorm' as 'Form', 'nehmlieh' as 'nehmlich'.","Ambiguity kept: 'dadurch' ('thereby') and 'durch sie' ('through them') have no stated antecedents; 'sie' could be the things or the representations.","*Verhältniß* = 'relation'; *Ort* = 'place'; *Raum* = 'space'; *besonder* = 'particular'; *Position* = 'position'; *Veränderungen* = 'changes' (not in the glossary).","*unmittelbar* / *mittelbar* = 'immediately' / 'mediately'; *Schlüsse* = 'inferences'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-008":["Locke on the contrary makes the objection: of obscure representations I am not conscious. Whence then does one know that I have obscure representations? To be not conscious of something and yet to know it is *contradictio in adjecto* — but that is mere chicane.",["OCR: '\\ orstellungen' read as 'Vorstellungen'; the '1' after 'habe' read as a question mark; 'Ghicane' as 'Chicane'; the text has 'Contradictio' capitalized.","*wißen* / *wissen* = 'know' (not *kennen*); *Einwurf* = 'objection'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-009":["We do not know it, to be sure, immediately, but yet through inferences, e.g. we are not conscious that the Milky Way, when we consider it with the naked eyes, consists of nothing but small stars, but through the telescope we see it. Now we infer: since we have seen the whole Milky Way, we must also have seen all the individual stars. For were that not so, we would have seen nothing. But what we have seen we must also have represented to ourselves. Since we know nothing of these representations, they must have been obscure. We thus have obscure representations, and these indeed in such great multitude that they far exceed the number of our clear representations. Our soul is as it were like a map, on which a few places are illuminated. If all our obscure representations were to become clear once, we would be astonished at the multitude of them. We would see ourselves as it were transposed into another world. The obscure representations make up the depth of the soul, and the multitude of them is the field of obscure representations. This term is taken from the simile (with the map).",["OCR \"Run\" read as \"Nun\", \"miißen\" as \"müssen\", \"wurden\" as \"würden\".","Vorstellung = representation; dunkel = obscure; klar = clear; Benennung = term; Menge = multitude; Anzahl = number (Zahl in the glossary); Gleichniß = simile.","\"immediate\" is rendered as the adverb \"immediately\"; \"z. E.\" as \"e.g.\""]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-001":["Second Chapter. On the lower faculty of cognition",["Erkenntniß Vermögen = faculty of cognition (Erkenntnis = cognition; Vermögen = faculty)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-002":["All our cognitions, pleasure etc. and desire are either sensual or intellectual. So there are also sensible and intellectual representations.",["sensuell = \"sensual\", sinnlich = \"sensible\": two German words, two English words, kept distinct.","Erkenntniße = cognitions; Lust = pleasure; Begierde = desire."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-003":["According to the author they are distinguished merely according to logical form, but this is false. In this the author, along with Wolff and Leibniz, differs from all philosophers and they make great confusion. They distinguish them merely with regard to distinctness and indistinctness. But that is no essential difference. For intellectual cognitions can also be obscure or indistinct and sensible cognitions can in turn be ever so distinct; the former nonetheless remain intellectual on that account and the latter sensual, e.g. the concept of right and wrong is obscure and yet intellectual, whereas the concept of tones is very distinct and yet sensible. (Distinct cognition is not a cognition different *in specie*, but rests merely on the relation of consciousness to the cognition.) Hence the cause of their difference must lie in their different origin. Sensible representations are representations according to the way in which I am affected by things; intellectual representations are those that are independent of this. Cognition that rests on receptivity is sensible, that which rests on spontaneity is intellectual. Through sensibility I cognize the things as they are for us; through the understanding, however, as they are in themselves. For all objects of our senses are merely appearances. Sensibility is in all our cognitions the material. (It is the faculty of intuition, and the science of it aesthetic.) Everything that we represent to ourselves comes from the senses. For through them we can only intuit the things. If we had intellectual intuitions, our understanding would have to be creative and bring forth the things themselves. Since that is not so, the things must bring forth the representations in us, and that through sensible intuition. The understanding then adds nothing to experience but the form. In cognition sensibility creates the sensation. In pleasure or displeasure the gratification. In the faculty of desire the incentives.",["OCR \"d< n\" read as \"den\"; \"Schaft\" read as \"schafft\" (\"creates\"), doubtful.","Deutlichkeit/deutlich = distinctness/distinct; Undeutlichkeit = indistinctness; dunkel = obscure; Ursprung = origin; Ursache = cause.","\"receptivitaet\" and \"spontaneitaet\" are Latinate in the source and are given as \"receptivity\" and \"spontaneity\"; *in specie* is Latin in the source.","\"das materiale\" = \"the material\", left as in the German (substantivized adjective).","Gegenstände = objects; Vergnügen = \"gratification\" and Triebfedern = \"incentives\" are not in the glossary.","\"Ansehung\" = \"regard\"; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Verstand\" = understanding."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-004":["The force to bring forth sensible representations is the sensible faculty of cognition, and the force to bring forth intellectual ones is the intellectual faculty of cognition. The former is called the lower, the latter the upper faculty of cognition.",["Kraft = force; Erkenntniß Vermögen = faculty of cognition."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-005":["The sensible faculty of cognition can be divided A with regard to time, there it is a the faculty of intuition of the present or the senses, *Sensatio*, B of the past or the reproduction, and C of the future or *Praevision* (the reproduction or reproductive imagination connected with consciousness is *memoria*). Time and space are conditions of sensible representations, all of which rest on the faculty of intuition. The faculty of intuition, insofar as it begins from the presence of the object, is sense, insofar as it is without object, but yet in respect of time, is imagination, and without any relation of the object to time faculty of poetic invention. These 3 faculties make up the intuition of the objects.",["OCR \"Gegen -wart\" read as \"Gegenwart\".","Gegenstand (\"object\") and Objecte (\"objects\") are both rendered \"object\"; the second occurs in the last sentence.","Sinn = sense; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Dichtungs Vermögen = faculty of poetic invention (not in the glossary).","The letters A, a, B, C are as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-006":["B with regard to the generation of representations the faculty of cognition is a *facultas comparandi*. To this belong wit as the faculty of encountering similarity, and power of judgment as the faculty of encountering difference among things, b *Facultas fingendi* or the faculty of generating new representations, i. e. productive imagination. c. *Facultas signandi*, the faculty of the connection of representations with a subject, *vel Facultas characteristica* or the faculty of bringing forth others through certain representations as means, or the faculty of bringing forth *repraesentationes vicarias* or *subsidiarias*, which in themselves have no validity, but yet serve to bring forth other representations in us. E.g. the word Rom brings forth the representation of a city in Italy. Language also rests on this. The sensible faculty of cognition rests on the way in which we are affected by things — hence receptivity or a passive property of being determined by other things — ; the intellectual faculty of cognition rests on spontaneity or the faculty of determining oneself, for it is independent of sensation. The understanding belongs to spontaneity, thus to the upper faculty of cognition. The feeling of pleasure or displeasure, however, is partly spontaneity, partly receptivity.",["OCR \"V itz\" read as \"Witz\"; \"30Z.E.\" read as \"Z.E.\" (30 a line number); \"Erkenntnil\" read as \"Erkenntniß\".","Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Verknüpfung = connection; Gültigkeit = validity; Empfindung = sensation; Gefühl = feeling.","Eigenschaft = \"property\" (the glossary gives property for Beschaffenheit).","\"anzutreffen\" = \"encountering\"; \"a\", \"b\", \"c\" kept as in the source; the Latin terms are as in the source, \"i. e.\" kept.","\"receptivitaet\", \"Spontaneitaet\", and \"Receptivitaet\" are all given as \"receptivity\" and \"spontaneity\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-007":["3rd Chapter on sense",["Sinn = sense (n.)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-008":["A The sense (*sensatio*) is twofold: 1. the inner and 2. the outer sense. The faculty of inner intuition is the inner sense. The inner sense is the consciousness of our representations themselves. (Apperception lies at the basis of the inner sense). It has the soul as object. If the soul is conscious of itself, without being conscious of its state, then it is apperception. If it is also conscious of its state, then it is sensation or perception. The outer sense is divided 1. into organ senses, which are restricted to certain parts, of which there are, as is well known, 5, and 2. into the vital sense or the sense of sensing something without a particular organ. This is everywhere where there is life, and since this is in the nervous system, especially in the outer ends of the nerves. This is found in a certain gentle well-being, in shuddering, horror etc. It is the 6th sense of the French, namely the sensation of voluptuousness in sexual community. The vital sense can also be called *sensus vagus* and the others *fixus*. — All organ senses have something subjective and objective, but some are more subjective than objective, and vice versa. That is: in some we represent to ourselves more alterations of the subject than of the object *et vice versa* (other creatures can have more or fewer organ senses, and of some animals it is known. Thus, e.g., the turtles, which at a certain time of year swim 300 miles from the island of Yucatan to the Cayman island, must have a particular sense peculiar to them for this; also more senses may be peculiar to us, which we however reckon to feeling). Smell and taste are more subjective. For if one merely smells or tastes, one cannot thereby yet distinguish the thing from another. I cannot know color, shape etc. These therefore also work most upon us. From strong smelling we can fall into a faint, and from bad taste disgust arises, and thereby convulsions are excited in the whole body. Feeling, seeing and hearing are more objective than subjective. With feeling one could not doubt, but then one unites it with the inner sensation, and that is the vital sense, which certainly goes more to the subjective than the objective. Here, however, the talk is of the outer feeling or of the sensation of things through touching. This consists chiefly in the fingers. Through this I can determine the figure, the hardness and softness of a thing. It lies at the basis of all the remaining senses. One born blind can even distinguish the colors through feeling, and when he becomes sighted, this seeing nevertheless is of no use to him at the beginning: seeing has still more objectivity in it. When I look around me, I do not feel what goes on in my eye, for it is so weak that one does not always notice it. Hearing is more objective than subjective. For when I hear talking, I feel no blows and impacts of the air on my eardrum. Unless someone screeches very much; yes, then the ears hurt one. It is now asked whether there can be still more senses. (Yes, for how many fine matters may there not be that we do not perceive on account of the lack of organs, e.g. the magnetic matter. One believes that the animals have certain senses of which we have no concept, and that is quite possible, e.g. when the wild geese travel in summer to cold countries, they take in the darkest night nevertheless the straightest line toward the north. Some senses have a large sphere, in which they can perceive things. Feeling and taste have the narrowest sphere, smell however already a wider one, e.g. the eagle smells carrion from as much as a mile. Hearing has a still wider one and sight the widest. — It is admirable that we can still see stars, the nearest of which is 200000 times farther than the sun. This comes in part also from the fact that the air is only a fine matter, through which light, as a still finer one, easily penetrates. Now we come to the fourth chapter or the",["Sinn is rendered 'sense' throughout; Gefühl 'feeling'; Empfindung 'sensation'; Wahrnehmung 'perception'; Gemeinschaft 'community' (Geschlechts Gemeinschaft = 'sexual community').","Object / objectiv / Objectivitaet all rendered 'object', 'objective', 'objectivity'; Subject likewise 'subject'.","'Organ Sinne' rendered 'organ senses'; 'Materie' = 'matter' (in 'fine matters', 'magnetic matter').","Original has the unbalanced parenthesis opening at 'andre Geschöpfe' and 'Ja, denn' closed only partly; the second parenthesis is left unclosed as in the text.","'Dieser ist überall, wo Leben ist, und da dies im Nerven System ist, besonders in äußern Enden der Nerven' is syntactically incomplete in the German; kept as it stands.","'Dieser besteht vornehmlich in den Fingern' / 'Er liegt allen übrigen Sinnen zum Grunde': the masculine pronouns refer back (presumably) to 'Gefühl'; left as 'This' / 'It'.","'Ich kann nicht Farbe, Gestalt etc. wissen': 'wissen' rendered 'know'.","Passage breaks off at 'oder der'; the following heading is in the next passage. 'Iucatan', 'Caiman' modernized spelling in English; 'subiectiv' and 'obiective' silently normalized."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-009":["B Reproductive power of the soul or of the imagination",["Kraft = 'power' here (glossary 'force; power'); Imagination left as 'imagination'; Einbildungskraft elsewhere 'imagination' too."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-010":["This is the faculty of representing to oneself the past states. We become conscious of our past representations, and indeed according to the laws of association or *Aßociation*, as *Ideae sociae sese invicem reproducunt*.",["'Vergesellschaftung' rendered 'association'; the Latin sentence is left unchanged and is itself grammatically as in the transcript.","'Zustände' = 'states'; 'Vorstellungen' = 'representations'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-011":["*Facultas imaginationis* is, with respect to past time, *reproductiva*, with respect to future *praevidendi*, and with respect to no time *productiva*. The first has the *lex aßociationis idearum sociarum*, through which the ideas that have been combined with one another also follow one another again, the 2nd the *lex expectationis casuum similium*, the 3rd the *lex sociabilium*, that one must rhyme what is grasped together.",["Absicht rendered 'respect' in 'in Absicht auf' (idiom 'with respect to'), not glossary 'aim; intention'.","'Ideen' in the German-Latin 'ideae' kept in Latin; 'Idee' glossary = 'idea'.","'daß man reimen müßte, was sich zusammen faßt': 'reimen' rendered literally 'rhyme'; the reading may be doubtful (possibly a transcription slip), left as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-012":["The *Facultas imaginandi* can be divided a. into *Facultas praevidendi* or *fingendi*, when my soul brings forth new representations through the imagination and b. into *Facultas reproducendi*, when it merely renews those it has had. The reproductive imagination relates either to the inner or outer sense. Combined with it is:",["Einbildungskraft = 'imagination'; Vorstellungen = 'representations'.","Passage breaks off at the colon; the continuation (C.) is in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-013":["C. *Facultas praevisionis*, when I infer to my future state (the *facultas praevisionis* or anticipating imagination rests on the reproductive. E.g. the bell is rung, and it occurs to me thereupon that then it is the meal; so that is reproductive imagination. But I now at the same time infer from this that when the bell is rung, a meal follows. The anticipating imagination with consciousness is *Praesagition*, without this it is *praesensio*), and I infer to the future, when a similar case has already been, upon which something has followed, and believe therefore that this case will also have a like sequel. It is thus merely an application of the law of the reproductive imagination to the future time. All these 3 faculties of cognition can be accompanied with apperception or not. If they are, then they belong merely to the human being, if not — then the animals also have them. We should therefore have 2 different names for each, but this is only so for one, namely the reproductive imagination; for this, accompanied with apperception, is called memory. Now we come to the 2nd difference of the sensible powers of cognition, namely",["'schließen' rendered 'infer'; 'Folge' = 'sequel' and 'folgt' = 'follows'; 'Erkenntniß Vermögen' = 'faculties of cognition'; 'Erkenntnißkräfte' = 'powers of cognition'.","OCR 'hall' read as 'Fall' ('case'); 'b* amen' read as 'Namen' ('names'); 'Facultas praevisionis' as in the transcript.","'Menschen' rendered 'the human being'; 'Anwendung' = 'application'; the parenthesis is closed after 'praesensio' as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-014":["II. With regard to their generation, and indeed A to the *Facultas comparandi*. This distinguishes itself entirely from the *Facultas coniungendi seu componendi*. For in comparison I do not put concepts together, but only hold them against one another, in order to bring out new representations. Here we look to *identitatem* and *Diversitatem*. *Facultas ad cognoscendum identitatem* is *ingenium*; *acumen* however *ad cognoscendum diversitatem*. The use of the *ingenium* is positive, but of the *acumen* negative. This preserves us from errors, for it shows us not to assume things as one and the same that are not. But it does not have the agreeable and entertaining of the *ingenium*.",["OCR 'I acultas' read as 'Facultas'; 'Ingenii' genitive kept as 'ingenium' in English.","'Ansehung' rendered 'regard'; 'Erzeugung' = 'generation'; 'Begriffe' = 'concepts'; 'Vorstellungen' = 'representations'.","'Irrthümer' rendered 'errors'; 'Gebrauch' would be 'use' but the text has 'Nutzen' = 'use' too (one English word for two German words).","'Dieses' / 'es' in 'Dieses bewahrt uns' refers to 'acumen' (grammatically neuter), left as 'This' and 'it'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-001":["Ch. 7. On the faculty of fiction",["OCR 'C ap.' read as 'Cap.'; 'Dichtungs Vermögen' rendered 'faculty of fiction' ('Vermögen' = 'faculty')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-002":["*Facultas fingendi* is the faculty of bringing forth representations of things that we have never seen. This is either imagination or fantasy. Imagination is when we play with the imagination, and fabricate something for certain aims and purposes. Fantasy is when the imagination plays with us. The former is voluntary, for we can suspend and direct it as it pleases us, but the latter is involuntary. Every fabrication must occur according to the analogy of experience, otherwise it is *effrenis*, ruleless fantasy. *Materialiter* we can therefore fabricate nothing, but merely *formaliter*. If the fabrication is according to the analogy of experience, it is *Phantasia subacta*. If it is involuntary, it is called in particular *Phantasia effrenis*. Hypochondriac people commonly have it; whoever has it is called a fantast. (The feeble imagination is like the donkeys in England, with which one still has fun at the end of the horse race. Namely, when the rider is quite near the goal, he gives the donkey the spurs, and since it cannot bear this, it stands still at once. So it goes also with the imagination that is spurred too much. If rulelessness and unbridledness are united in the imagination, then arises the so-called *Koller*. One sometimes ascribes this to too much studying, but it does not come from that, if he has not already had it before, or at least has come to studying with a predisposition to it. Madness consists in this, when we take the images of the imagination to be real, and insanity, when one passes quite perverse judgments. The criterion of a disturbed person is when he is incapable of comparing his judgment with that of others and does not ask about this at all. Another phenomenon of the imagination is the dream. It arises quite naturally. For since the imagination is constantly active, and in sleep the effects of the understanding cease, this remains over and thereby gets free course, and makes for us, in place of the understanding, representations of things — hence it also comes that these seem necessary to us. It is just as with the moon.) This productive imagination manifests itself chiefly in the dream. It is a series of fabrications, which however are involuntary. In waking we live in a common world, but in the dream in our own. — It is very similar to the power of fabrication in waking, yet with the difference: in the dream the productive imagination is involuntary, without order and aim. But in waking I can for the most part give my fantasy all sorts of directions and order, and always call myself back from it, if it pleases me. In waking the fantasy is indeed sometimes also involuntary, but the ideas are not yet as clear as in the dream, because here the impressions of the senses hinder us, but there all the senses rest, and merely the field of the productive imagination is astir. For the dream suspends all consciousness of our state. Hence also comes the singular thing, that we represent to ourselves the past time without knowing that it is past. Here, so to speak, a compartment of the reproductive imagination is merely opened up for us, in which we roam about without being conscious of our present state.",["Einbildungskraft = \"imagination\" (glossary); Imagination (Latinate) is also \"imagination\"; Phantasie = \"fantasy\"; Phantast = \"fantast\".","\"Die ist aut Imagination oder Phantasie\": OCR reading doubtful; \"aut\" is taken as a misreading of \"entweder\" (\"either\"), but may be otherwise.","willkührlich / unwillkührlich = \"voluntary\" / \"involuntary\" (literally of free choice, Willkür); erdichten / Erdichtung = \"fabricate\" / \"fabrication\"; Dichtungskraft = \"power of fabrication\".","\"me gesehen\" read as \"nie gesehen\"; \"auf heben\" as \"aufheben\"; \"nehmlieh\" as \"nehmlich\"; \"Regelund\" as \"Regel- und\"; \"Reuter\" = \"rider\".","\"Koller\" left in German (a kind of frenzy or staggers); Wahnsinn = \"madness\", Wahnwitz = \"insanity\" (no glossary entry for either).","\"andern ihrem\" (colloquial: with others' their own) rendered \"that of others\"; \"Zügellosigkeit\" = \"unbridledness\"; \"regellose\" = \"ruleless\".","Wirkungen des Verstandes = \"effects of the understanding\"; \"Eindrücke\" = \"impressions\"; \"Zustand\" = \"state\"; \"Bewußtsein\" = \"consciousness\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-003":["Meanwhile the dream, like everything in the world, has its use. For if the mind is not always in consciousness, the body seems not to be agitated enough. Now if our body were not affected internally and, because it is in sleep, also affected externally, that would harm it very much. In the dream the mind and thereby also the body is moved along with it, especially through the affects. Hereby the soul works on the springs of life, and that is very salutary and necessary for us, because through no bodily consciousness can we put the springs of our life into consciousness and activity, but can merely further the circulation of the blood. The soul works especially on the respiratory system, and without this we cannot subsist. E.g. if someone has given himself good exercise and goes to sleep afterwards, he commonly sleeps quite soundly. Since he then also dreams nothing, the drawing of breath is lost in him, and then he commonly gets anxious dreams, from which he wakes again. After the dream one is also in the morning much more awake than otherwise, because thereby life is as it were strengthened. What then is sleep? It is a phenomenon, which belongs to physiology or to the science of the human body and therefore cannot be determined metaphysically — Whoever is healthy also sleeps well.",["Gemüth = \"mind\"; Seele = \"soul\"; Wissenschaft = \"science\"; determiniren = \"determine\".","\"afficirt\" = \"affected\", \"Affecten\" = \"affects\" (no gloss on the sense).","The clause \"Würde nun unser Körper nicht innerlich afficirt und, weil er im Schlaf ist, auch äußerlich afficirt\" is kept as printed, with its construction unresolved.","\"Athem System\" = \"respiratory system\"; \"Quellen des Lebens\" = \"springs of life\"; \"Kreislauf des Geblüths\" = \"circulation of the blood\".","\"metaphysicsh\" read as \"metaphysisch\"; \"Gemeiniglich\" = \"commonly\"; \"aufgewekter\" read as \"aufgeweckter\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-004":["If one compares the productive imagination with the *vi signatrici*, there arises from it the art of dream-interpretation. One takes dreams to be signs of future occurrences, and that partly because they often agree little with experience, and because they represent the occurrences so vividly that one believes these had really happened. These *signa prognostica* can be 1. natural and 2. voluntary. *Signa naturalia* are the representations of their effects. These do certainly take place, and in this respect the dreams can always be *signa prognostica*, e.g. if in a human being the bile pours out at night, he gets frightful and vexing dreams; these are the cause that he gets up in the morning with whims, and because of his vexed and surly temperament finds vexation, annoyance and quarrels everywhere. Thus the bile is the proper cause, but the dreams only the mediate cause, of the vexation of the following day.",["\"vi signatrici\" kept in Latin as printed (ablative; the OCR form of the second word is taken as it stands).","Begebenheit = \"occurrence\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Ursache = \"cause\"; \"mittelbare\" = \"mediate\".","\"willkührlich\" = \"voluntary\", as in the preceding passage; the following passage has \"arbitraria\" for the same member.","\"Grillen\" = \"whims\"; \"Händel\" = \"quarrels\"; \"Gemiithsart\" read as \"Gemüthsart\" = \"temperament\".","\"Traum Deuter Kunst\" rendered \"art of dream-interpretation\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-005":["2. *Signa arbitraria*. That is merely superstition, for here I find not the slightest interconnection. I surely cannot know that at all, namely what they signify. The use of the lower powers of cognition is done by the higher, and indeed the higher have, by means of imagination, dominion over the lower. It is admirable when the lower powers of cognition let themselves be governed by the higher. In this state is he who can abstract nothing and attend to nothing. The imagination must rest entirely on our arbitrary choice and it is constituted in the same way as the function of the lung in drawing breath, which we have in our power, yet not so that we could entirely do without it. We must therefore indeed sometimes let our imagination run its course, yet so that we can always govern it and always put a stop to it. But if it respects the guidance of the understanding, then it is *effrenis*.",["\"Imagination\" here is the Latinate word, rendered \"imagination\" as is Einbildungskraft elsewhere.","\"Erkenntnißkräfte\" = \"powers of cognition\" (Erkenntnis = cognition, Kraft = power); \"Zusammenhang\" = \"interconnection\".","\"Willkühr\" = \"arbitrary choice\"; \"abstrahiren\", \"attendiren\" = \"abstract\", \"attend\".","\"respect-irt\" read as \"respectirt\" (\"respects\"). The last sentence is kept as printed; the sense may require a negation (\"does not respect\"), and the text may be corrupt or omit a word.","\"bewunderungs würdig\" read as \"bewunderungswürdig\"; \"derienige\" = \"he who\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s15-001":["Of the Faculty of Designation",["\"Bezeichnungs Vermögen\" = \"faculty of designation\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\" (glossary)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s15-002":["*Facultas characteristica* is the faculty of furnishing one's understanding with certain signs, or of so associating representations that the one becomes the means of reproduction of the other, and is also called *Facultas signandi*. It is mechanical, i.e. without striving of force and also involuntary, e.g. if I say Rome, there arises at once the representation of this city. I may want it or not. This comes from habit. I fabricate signs, in order to express with them what I think. These are words and either a. *demonstrativa* or *repraesentativa*, which concern what is at the same time, e.g. the pulse is a sign of health or illness; b. *rememorativa*, which relate to the past, e.g. Cicero; c, *prognostica*, which relate to the future, as e.g. the low flight of the swallows is a sign of impending rain.",["\"seinen Verstand mit gewissen Zeichen zu belegen\": \"belegen\" rendered \"furnishing ... with\"; it could also be read \"covering\" or \"assigning\".","\"Bestrebung der Kraft\" = \"striving of force\" (Kraft = force here); \"unwillkührlich\" = \"involuntary\".","\"erdichte\" = \"fabricate\"; \"Reproduction\" = \"reproduction\"; \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\".","Enumeration \"a.\", \"b.\", \"c,\" kept as printed (the comma after \"c\" is in the source)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s16-001":["Of Understanding, Power of Judgment and Reason",["Verstand = \"understanding\"; Urtheilskraft = \"power of judgment\"; Vernunft = \"reason\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s16-002":["The higher faculty has spontaneity in its representations. We therefore regard ourselves as the compelling cause of it. Thus the will too belongs to the higher faculty of cognition, so it itself is its master, and the inclination to the receptivity of the higher faculty of cognition is called understanding in general. The intellectual faculty of cognition is the faculty to think or to make concepts for oneself. It represents to itself only the object in general, without looking to the way of its appearance. This is discursive, that intuitive. *Intuitus* is an immediate representation, concept, *conceptus*, a mediate one through a mark (the faculty of consciousness of the manifold through concepts is after all an imperfect faculty, and the greater and more perfect the faculty, the more it approaches intuition. The most perfect understanding would accordingly be the one that cognizes merely through intuition, to be sure, however, not through sensible intuition. Thus we represent to ourselves the divine understanding. The question arises here: whether, namely, we can come to general representations only through comparison? But it is rather correct conversely, that with comparison general concepts arise for us, Thus we come, e.g., to the concept of a triangle not through comparison, but when we see it for the first time, we become aware at once that we are not at all restricted by its magnitude to give the name triangle to all triangular figures that we will see in the future). The concept is the consciousness that in one representation of the same the same is contained as in another, or that in manifold representations one and the same marks are contained. This therefore presupposes consciousness or apperception. Animals indeed compare representations with one another, but they are not conscious of wherein they harmonize or disharmonize with one another. Hence they also have no concepts, and because the higher faculty of cognition consists of these, also no higher faculty of cognition. This is therefore distinguished by apperception from the lower faculty of cognition. The latter we have in common as animals with the latter, the former however elevates us as thinking beings above the animals. Whoever can make concepts for himself thinks. All thinking is threefold: 1. through concepts, and the faculty of these is called understanding. 2. through composition of two concepts, i.e. through judgments, i.e. power of judgment. 3. through derivation of one concept from the other through inferences, i.e. reason. The understanding is the faculty of bringing different representations under a rule. It rests on apperception. (It is the faculty of determining the particular through the general. With the higher power of cognition one considers the faculty of cognition not in relation to intuition, but to the unity of consciousness. This is the representation of its representations and is also called apperception for that reason. Without the consciousness of the sameness of a representation in many representations no general rule would be possible. For rule is necessary unity of the consciousness of a manifoldness of representations, relation of the manifoldness of representations to one consciousness.) But how are concepts possible through apperception? Thereby, that I represent to myself the identity of my apperception in many representations. The concept is *perceptio communis*, e.g. the concept of body. This holds of metal, gold, stone, etc. In it I represent to myself one in the manifold. The logical function of the same consists in generality. This is the analytic unity of apperception, and much in one is the synthetic unity of the same. The analytic unity of apperception represents nothing new to us, but is conscious only of the manifold in one representation. The synthetic goes to much, insofar as it is contained in one. Insofar as the understanding judges according to this, it is a pure understanding. The understanding makes rules. It draws out of manifold representations the general, which is encountered in all. It is therefore also called the faculty of rules. Experience presupposes understanding, because it is a connection of perceptions according to rules. It has *a posteriori* and *a priori* rules.",["Erkenntniß Vermögen = faculty of cognition; Erkenntniß Kraft = power of cognition (Kraft rendered 'power' since it is paired with Vermögen); Allgemeine/allgemein = general throughout, Besondere = particular; Mannigfaltige = manifold, Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness.","'so er selbst sein Herr ist': the German is obscure (so = 'insofar as'/'so'); 'er' could be Wille or another masculine antecedent, so 'it' is left unresolved. The sentence may be corrupt in the transcript.","'Intuitus ist eine unmittelbare Vorstellung, Begriff, conceptus, eine mittelbare...': the elliptical construction is kept as in the German.","'dieses ... ienes' = 'this ... that', antecedents left unresolved; likewise 'Dieser' (masc.), 'desselben', 'derselben', and 'den letztem' in 'mit den letztem' (probably 'letztern', animals; left as 'the latter').","'einerlei Merkmale' rendered 'one and the same marks'; Einerleiheit = sameness; Identitaet = identity; Verhältniß and Relation are both 'relation'.","'Es beruht auf Apperception': 'Es' is neuter and its antecedent is unclear (Vermögen?); left as 'It'.","Parenthesis opening at 'das Vermögen des Bewußtseins...' closes only after 'Triangel zu belegen'; 'Thiere' = animals; 'Wesen' = beings.","OCR corrections: 'a posterioii' read as 'a posteriori', 'w'ir' as 'wir'; 'Gegenstand/Object': here only Object, = object."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s16-003":["(Without power of judgment we would not cognize the *differentia specifica*, and without perception not the *genera*.) (Every estate has need of power of judgment, but yet one more than the other, e.g. physician more than jurist. Here there are many instances, if one does not decide rightly, then it will be the other all the same, but there it is not so.) Power of judgment brings the objects under the rules. Reason applies the rules to them, is therefore the faculty of acting according to principles. Reason determines the particular from a general rule. Principles are the determinations of the particular from the general. The power of judgment sees under which rule each thing stands, and whether this thing is contained under this rule or not. There are persons who think much *in abstracto* and little *in concreto*, i.e. they have understanding and no power of judgment, for that must be innate and cannot be learned. For then one would have to give new rules for how the rules of the understanding are to be applied, but one would have to teach again how to apply these new rules, consequently there would again have to be new rules, and that would then go on thus without end. Prudence consists in power of judgment. Hence many a one is very learned and yet not prudent. Reason infers that what the rule says generally also holds particularly of this or that thing. The understanding draws the general from the particular, namely from the various things in experience, reason draws the particular from the general. These 3 powers of cognition one finds in the syllogism. The understanding gives the general rule, *propositio major*. The power of judgment is the subsumption of one concept under the other. Reason finally makes conclusion, for it infers that what holds generally also holds of the subsumed thing. What the cognition of reason cognizes is necessary; it is however very limited, because it fetches nothing from experience. One must seek to cultivate (*excoliren*) and improve the power of judgment through *praxin*. Now we come to the 2nd chapter of this psychology, namely to the feeling of pleasure and displeasure.",["Urtheilskraft = power of judgment; Urtheil = judgment; schließen = infer; Schluß = inference; Syllogismus = syllogism; Conclusion kept as 'conclusion'.","'Stand' = 'estate' (also 'station'); 'Instanzen' = 'instances' (here probably legal instances); 'so wirds doch der andre' is elliptical and left so ('it will be the other'); 'hier ... dort' unresolved.","'Principien' = principles (Grundsatz would also be 'principle'); Klugheit = prudence; Hauptstück = 'chapter'.","The transcript prints 'Besonderen,' twice ('aus dem besonderen. Besonderen, nehmlich'); the doubled word is translated once, as a probable dittography.","'nothwendie sie ist aber...': 'nothwendie' read as 'nothwendig', with punctuation missing in the OCR; 'Vernunft Erkenntniß' (two words) rendered 'cognition of reason', which is a choice; may be a compound 'Vernunfterkenntniß'.","'excoliren' is a Latinate German verb (excolere), rendered 'cultivate' with the German kept in italics; 'Praxin' is Latin/Greek accusative, kept in italics, 'through practice' not added.","Latin terms kept: differentia specifica, genera, in abstracto, in concreto, propositio major."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s17-001":["Pleasure or displeasure",["Heading only; Lust = pleasure, Unlust = displeasure."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s17-002":["Pleasure or displeasure cannot be defined if one does not presuppose the faculty of desire. Through the feeling of pleasure or displeasure the cognitive faculty is combined with the faculty of desire. The author calls it *voluptas* and *taedium*. That is false, for this holds only of sensible liking. — For often the understanding can have a disliking of something that pleases the senses best. One must call it *facultas complacentiae* and *displicentiae*. One could really call this *sensus internus*, since it goes to our state itself; meanwhile no one has yet properly developed the concept of it. The feeling of pleasure is the capacity of my power of representation to be determined, through a given representation, to the preservation and promotion, or to the avoidance, of the same. In displeasure we summon our whole faculty to prevent the representation from penetrating further into the mind. Everything that arouses the feeling of the promotion of life awakens pleasure. (The faculty of a subject, insofar as it is determined through pleasure in an object to the production of this object, is called the faculty of desire.) It is particular that our representations can become the cause of the actuality of objects, e.g., the musician is the cause of the tones that he produces through his instrument. Pleasure is therefore the consciousness of the agreement of an object with the productive imagination of our soul. In connection with the cognizability of the will this definition will be made still more distinct. — Our liking or disliking is either mediate or immediate; the former is intellectual, the latter sensible. What pleases me mediately is what pleases me not in itself, but merely as a means to an immediate liking. It is no particular object of pleasure, but an object of reason, which cognizes that I thereby become a participant in a pleasure, e.g., in money I have a mediate liking. For in itself it does not please me, but merely because by means of it I can procure for myself a thousand kinds of enjoyments. Since I need reason in order to cognize whether something is good as a means to its end, this too is an object of reason. We must therefore have a means to liking merely for the sake of the end, and if we do not do so, we act foolishly, e.g., whoever has a liking for money in itself, without regarding it as a means to an end, and therefore always locks it up in his chests, is a miser. This, in which I have a liking merely mediately, is also called useful or beneficial. This is not universal, but according to the aim of each. To one this is useful, and to another the very same is harmful. But this cannot be spoken of. The other way of liking is the immediate liking, which pleases us in itself. This now is twofold: either subjective or objective. Subjective, if it rests on the alteration of our state, and thus lies merely in the subject. This is called the agreeable; it is also called the private liking, because it takes place only in relation to the senses of this or that subject and is therefore not universal, e.g., warmth, for one can find a certain degree of warmth quite agreeable that is unbearable to another, which comes from his constitution of life and in general from his state. Objective is the liking that rests not at all on the subject, but merely on the object; hence it is a universal liking, because it must please all who sense the object. This now pleases universally either according to laws of sensibility, and then it is beautiful. The beautiful pleases in the imagination; or according to laws of the understanding and of reason, and then it is good. The good is immediate and mediate. Mediately good is the useful, e.g., the punishment of the evil does not please in itself, but as a means to the attainment of a good end. Hence it is useful and good to us. Much is mediately good, but immediately good only morality. The human being is not immediately good — the cannibals and slave traders according to their custom; immediately, if he is moral. For then he is himself an end.",["OCR corrections: 'Lhilust' read as 'Unlust'; 'aul' as 'auf'; 'Gas' as 'Das' (the opening of the parenthesis); 'ihiem' is garbled and taken as 'ihrem' ('according to their custom'); 'woran' in 'woran ein Mißfallen haben' read as 'daran'. The latter is uncertain.","'facultas complacentia und displicentia' is printed thus in the source; rendered with Latin genitive endings, an assumed correction.","Wohlgefallen = liking; Mißfallen = disliking; gefallen = please. Vorstellungskraft = power of representation; Begehrungsvermögen = faculty of desire; Erkenntniß Vermögen = cognitive faculty.","Wirklichkeit = actuality; Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'. Gemüth = mind; Seele = soul. Erkennen/Erkennbarkeit = cognize/cognizability. 'deutlicher' = more distinct.","'Es ist besonders' is rendered 'It is particular' (besonder = particular; special); the sense is obscure and left so.","'Davon kann aber nicht geredt werden' is rendered literally; the antecedent of 'Davon' is not clear.","'Das Vermögen eines Subjects … Hervorbringung' etc.: the parenthesis is carried over as printed. 'Lebens Constitution' = 'constitution of life'. 'Mittelbar gut ist es das Nützliche' is kept as awkward as the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s17-003":["The beautiful pleases universally, it therefore has universal rules, e.g., a person is beautiful if she pleases everyone. But if she has a charm merely for this or that subject, then she is merely agreeable. Hence it comes that we dispute only about the beautiful, but not about the agreeable, because the former rests on universal rules, and it could therefore rather be said: *de gusto est disputandum*. The faculty of judging the beautiful is taste. The faculty of judging the agreeable has no name, and also needs none. Taste judges according to universal rules; the agreeable one judges according to its source, sensation. The power of judgment is therefore an objective faculty. The universal rules of taste belong only to the sensibility of the human being and to beings who have the same sensibility as he. The universal rules of the good extend to all rational beings, even to God, for they go to cognition. In taste, sensibility and understanding are combined in judging. For since it rests on universal rules, the understanding must give these; but through the senses it must sense the object. What pleases in sensation or in the agreeable, gratifies; what pleases the sensible power of judgment, namely the beautiful, pleases in the stricter sense. Something pleases me if its existence is indifferent to me, e.g., a beautiful plan can please me, although the thing itself does not yet exist, but the existence of the agreeable is not indifferent to me. If a beautiful region pleases me, it pleases me for this reason, because it would please me thus even if it were not there, but I merely represented it to myself in thought. — What gratifies me, I either possess, or its existence is not indifferent to me. Every mediate gratification presupposes an immediate one. Beauty is the universality of sensible liking, or the judgment of the object according to universal liking. Feeling goes to the matter, but taste to the form of the composition of the manifold in sensation. Talent belongs to taste; not to sensation. The agreeable has more degrees or intensive magnitude than the beautiful, and this again more extensive magnitude than the preceding. The good has still more extensive magnitude. Because in taste some exceptions still always occur, some fall into the extreme of denying its universality. Beauty is the object of liking in sensible intuition, or also according to the author *perfectio phaenomenon*, if by *perfectio* one understands the object of sensible liking. Goodness or good constitution of a thing is the object of liking according to concepts of the understanding. The agreeable gratifies, the disagreeable pains, the beautiful pleases, the bad displeases; the good is approved, the evil is disapproved. To the agreeable belongs sensation (the agreeable pleases because of the matter); to the beautiful belongs taste — (the beautiful pleases because of the form) and to the good, way of thinking (sentiment). Virtue is a true inner good, but nothing agreeable. In it there are intellectual and not sensible judgments. But it nevertheless has something that comes close to the beautiful. For if one represents to oneself in thought a whole realm of the virtuous, then the order and regularity of their conduct, which would necessarily have to arise from this, awakens in us a sensible liking.",["OCR corrections: 'nehmlicli' as 'nehmlich'; 'V erstände' as 'Verstande'; 'Warme' as 'Wärme' not needed here. 'Beurtheilungs Kraft' = 'power of judgment' (Urteilskraft in the glossary); 'beurtheilen' = judge/assess, rendered 'judge'.","'im strictern V erstände' read as 'im strengeren Verstande' ('in the stricter sense'); here Verstand is not the faculty.","'perfectio phaenomenon' is printed thus (nominative) and left in Latin as it stands.","Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'. Existenz and Dasein would both be 'existence'; here Existenz. Vergnügen/vergnügen = gratification/gratify; gefallen = please; Wohlgefallen = liking.","'Denkungs Art (Sentiment)' = 'way of thinking (sentiment)'; Art = way.","'und es könnte also lieber heißen' is literal; 'es' has no clear referent in the German. 'Die Beurtheilungs Kraft ist also ein obiectives Vermögen' is kept as it stands.","'Gefühl geht auf die Materie': Gefühl = feeling; Mannigfaltige = manifold."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-001":["On the faculty of desire",["Heading, translated as it stands. Begehrungs Vermögen = faculty of desire."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-002":["*Causalitas* is the determination of a cause, whereby it becomes cause, or the determination of the relation of a thing as cause to a determinate effect. The cause is therefore always distinguished from the *causalitas*, e.g. the human being is the cause of the letters but not by means of his head or his feet etc., but by means of his fingers. — The faculty of bringing forth objects through its representations is the faculty of desire. The faculty of desire rests on the principle: *Nihil appeto nisi quod placet, nihil averto nisi quod displicet*. Representations, however, cannot become cause of the object unless we have pleasure or displeasure in it. Hence this is the subjective condition thereof, under which the representation alone can become cause of the object. Hence desire too is a *complexus* of the reality of the object and of the faculty of desire. The faculty of determining oneself to act conformably to the object of liking. The objective of the desires is the physical faculty thereof. This, however, belongs to the mechanism of nature. We presuppose it, however, otherwise our desires would be empty. Here, however, we consider desires in general (besides the things that are moved by external causes, there are living ones, which are moved by internal ones. A being is living if its representing power can be the ground of the reality of its objects. The *causalitas* of the representation with regard to the reality of its objects is therefore life. This *causalitas* of representations is now, with regard to the subject, the feeling of pleasure or displeasure. With regard to the object, however, the faculty of desire).",["Wirklichkeit rendered \"reality\" here (glossary: Realität / Wirklichkeit = reality / actuality; reality); Realität is not in the passage, so no doubling arises. Obiect/Gegenstand both \"object\" (Objecte, Obiects, Gegenstand, Gegenstände).","Begehrungsvermögen = \"faculty of desire\"; Begierde = \"desire\"; Vorstellungs Kraft = \"representing power\" (Kraft = power, as glossary allows \"force; power\").","\"Das obiective der Begierden\" and the sentence \"Das Vermögen, sich dem Obiect des Wohlgefallens conform zum Handeln zu bestimmen\" are kept as they stand (the latter is a sentence fragment in the German; Wohlgefallen = \"liking\"; Wohlgefallens is the genitive).","\"Hier aber betrachten wir\": Betrachtung = consideration, so betrachten = \"consider\". \"Hence\" renders both Daher occurrences.","OCR \"durci\" corrected to \"durch\". The parenthesis opened at \"(außer den Dingen\" is closed only at the end, as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-003":["Pleasure is the representation of the agreement of an object with the productive power of the soul, and displeasure the opposite. The faculty of desire is the *causalitas* of the object that is brought forth. Accordingly pleasure is the agreement, and displeasure the conflict with our faculty of desire. Pleasure, however, is now also the feeling of the promotion of life, and whence that? — Hence. A thing lives if it has a faculty of moving itself according to choice. Life is therefore the faculty of acting according to choice or according to its desires. This, however, is now the faculty of desire, taken practically. Since pleasure is therefore agreement with the faculty of desire, it is also agreement with life, and displeasure conflict with life. Pleasure and displeasure, however, presuppose sensation. Accordingly I can also say: pleasure and displeasure is a feeling of the agreement and the conflict or, which is the same, of the promotion or the hindrance of life. All desires always go to something future. All desires are, like pleasure and displeasure, intellectual or sensitive. —",["Willkühr = \"choice\" (not in glossary); Empfindung = \"sensation\"; Gefühl = \"feeling\"; Seele = \"soul\".","\"Daher\" in \"und woher das? — Daher.\" rendered \"whence that? — Hence.\" to keep the play; \"Uebereinstimmung\" = \"agreement\", \"Wiederstreit\" = \"conflict\".","\"intellectuell oder sensitiv\" rendered \"intellectual or sensitive\"; the German sensitiv is a Latinate word distinct from sinnlich (\"sensible\")."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-004":["That which is the cause of the desire is *causa impulsiva* or *elater animi*. If they have arisen from sensibility, they are called *stimuli*, and their effect *appetitio per stimulos* or sensible desire. But if they spring from the understanding, they are called *motiva*, their effect *appetitio per motiva* or intellectual desires.",["Sinnlichkeit = \"sensibility\"; sinnliche = \"sensible\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Wirkung = \"effect\".","Original has the singular \"Begierde\" then plural \"sie\" / \"Begierden\"; kept as is."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-005":["From the agreeable spring the *stimuli*, because this is for the senses and it is merely a private judgment. The beautiful and good is intellectual, because their judgment is logical, hence also the source of the *motiva* or motives. The desires are furthermore idle (*otiosae*) if the objects are not in our power. They are also called fantastic and contemplative desires, e.g. if we wish to see a comet this year, then we have idle desires, as also do readers of novels (in the case of affect one feeling is greater than all the rest, and so also in the case of passion one inclination. The consciousness of the agreement of all our inclinations is contentment. Affects destroy the agreeableness. Passions the ends.). We often desire something before we yet see whether it is possible for us to attain it. But why has nature given us this? In order to encourage us all the more to activity. For thereby we are driven to investigate properly whether they do not stand in our power; but if we find that they do not stand in our power, and we nevertheless still harbor the desires, then we act unnaturally and absurdly. We also have a contemplative liking, for many things happen in which we take pleasure without their standing in our power. This always takes place — but idle desires not. Animals have no idle desires, for even if they apply their powers in vain, they still do not know that the thing is not in their power. Opposed to idle desires are the practical ones. These are desires for such things as stand in our power. If the *stimuli* have become habitual, they are inclinations, and the source of the same instinct or habit; habitual sensible desires are passions. They concern, as desires and affects, sensation. There the subject is not capable of comparing the inclination to an object with the sum total of all inclinations; here, of comparing a sensation with the sum total of all. The author explains affect indeterminately through a higher degree of pleasure or displeasure. The harmony of the lower and upper faculty of desire must be negative, namely so that the lower lays no hindrance in the way of the upper. Therefore the *causae impulsivae* to virtue must not be taken from sensible advantages.",["Urtheil = \"judgment\"; Bewegungsgründe = \"motives\" (glossary: Bewegungsgrund = motive); Summe = \"sum total\" (Inbegriff is not in the text; Summe is rendered \"sum total\" for consistency with the glossary's sense, but the German word is Summe).","Gewalt = \"power\" (\"in unserer Gewalt\"), distinct from Kraft = \"power\" (\"ihre Kräfte\"): one English word for two German words.","\"Affekten\"/\"Affect\" = \"affect(s)\"; \"Zufriedenheit\" = \"contentment\"; \"Annehmlichkeit\" = \"agreeableness\"; \"Wohlgefallen\" = \"liking\".","Ambiguities kept: \"die Quelle derselben Instinct oder Gewohnheit\" (no verb or case marking; \"instinct or habit\" is left as it stands); \"Dort ... hier\" clauses are elliptical; \"Harum\" corrected to \"Darum\" (OCR); \"Llnlust\" to \"Unlust\", \"Emphndung\" to \"Empfindung\".","\"Den müßigen Begierden sind die practischen entgegengesetzt\" rendered literally; the closing parenthesis after \"Zwecke\" is placed as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-006":["On choice",["Willkühr = \"choice\" (not in glossary)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-007":["The faculty of desiring practically or *Facultas appetitionum practicarum* is *arbitrium*. The *arbitrium* or choice is either *sensitivum*, which presents to us things that are agreeable to the senses, or *intellectuale* — things that the understanding approves. Better, however, the *arbitrium* is divided into *brutum* and *liberum*. *Brutum* is that which is determined or necessitated by *stimuli*, and *liberum* that which is determined by *motiva*; the animals have the former, human beings the latter, hence it is also called *humanum*. The human being can indeed be affected by *stimuli*, but not necessitated, for he is independent of the *stimuli*.",["OCR \"Has\" corrected to \"Das\"; \"prac tisch\" joined. \"eingetheilt\" = \"divided\" (Eintheilung = division).","\"ienes ... dieses\" rendered \"the former ... the latter\"; \"neceßitirt\" = \"necessitated\", \"determinirt\" = \"determined\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-001":["On freedom",["Freiheit = \"freedom\" (not in glossary)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-002":["The *arbitrium liberum* is determined *per motiva*. Since these come merely from the understanding, they are *caussae impulsivae intellectuales*. These are the concepts of the good. *Arbitrium liberum* is *voluntas*. Freedom is therefore a faculty of acting according to concepts of the good and evil (in a pure intelligence the objective necessity is at the same time the subjective. So it is with God). That is the positive concept of it in the practical sense. Negatively it is the faculty of choosing independently of the sensible incitements in just the same sense; we are therefore indeed free from *stimulis*, but not from *motivis*. This can be called the higher faculty of desire, because the rules of it (the concepts of the good) are universal. (What is determined through motives is end, what precedes the resolution, the reflection. *Nihil appeto nisi sub ratione boni — et nihil nisi sub ratione mali* is as a principle indeed good, but as a proposition of experience false, for under the representation of the agreeable I often designate the evil, and under the representation of the disagreeable I reject the good). Whence do we know that the will is free? — Freedom is not an attribute that we learn from experience; for we can experience nothing negative. We do indeed perform many actions in which we seem against all *stimulos* to act, but we cannot yet conclude from that — for a secret *stimulus* can nonetheless have driven us to it, e.g. if a criminal despite the torture and all torments does not confess, he acts indeed against all *stimulos*, but nevertheless in favor of a *stimulus*, namely the fear of death. The inference therefore, because I notice no *stimulos*, so there are also none, can accordingly be very uncertain.",["*caussae*: spelled thus in the source (*causae*); kept as printed. *Neceßitaet* is rendered \"necessity\" (Latinate form; same English word as *Nothwendigkeit* elsewhere).","*Verstände* in \"bloß aus dem Verstände\" = \"understanding\"; but in \"im practischen Verstände\" and \"in eben dem Verstände\" (idiomatic, = sense, respect) I use \"sense\", since \"understanding\" would misleadingly point to the faculty. Same German word in both places.","*Eigenschaft* = \"attribute\" (not \"property\", which the glossary reserves for *Beschaffenheit*). *Anreitzungen* = \"incitements\"; *Entschluß* = \"resolution\"; *Ueberlegung* = \"reflection\" (glossary).","\"bei denen wir wieder alle stimulos zu handlen scheinen\": *wieder* is probably OCR/transcript for *wider* (\"against\"), as the later \"allen stimulos entgegen\" suggests; so rendered. The word order is kept, which leaves the sense of \"seem\" rather open.","\"Diese kann das obere Begehrungs Vermögen heißen\": the antecedent of *Diese* is not stated; left as \"This\". *Begehrungs Vermögen* = \"faculty of desire\".","The Latin is left as the student wrote it, including the case forms *stimulis*, *motivis* after \"free from\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-003":["We ourselves are also not immediately conscious of freedom. We are conscious only of the incentives or *stimulorum*, which are clear representations. But we can indeed also have obscure representations and stimuli to something, of which we are therefore not conscious (all our actions are *actiones voluntariae*, but these are again either *libentes* (actions that happen gladly), if we are conscious of no desire to the contrary, or *invitae*, insofar as we are conscious in them of one or more grounds to the contrary. These are composed of *appetitio* and *aversatio*, only that the former is stronger. Coercion, *coactio*, is *necessitatio actionis invitae*. It is either *in se ipsum* or *ab alio*. It can be physical or moral. Physical it is either through *illecebras* or *minas*. Through *illecebras* there is properly no coercion, but necessitation, e.g. hunger is the *illecebra* of the thief to stealing. Physical coercion without *illecebras* and *minas* is *extorsio*. The coercion is not *necessitatio arbitrii sensitivi*, but *intellectualis*; for were it *necessitatio per stimulos*, the human being would be an animal. But thus the human being can through reason always, even with the strongest physical necessitation, give himself a preponderance over the sensitive representations. That is: *Voluntas non potest cogi physice, sed semet ipsam cogere potest volendi*. Thus we can coerce others, if we represent to them motives that move them to coerce themselves. *Necessitatio actionis mvicem moraliter bonae est obligatio*, that is the practical coercion. Were the practical or also objective coercion in the human being at the same time always subjective, he would be perfectly free. The moral obligation can only be thought in relation to a sensible power of choice that is affected through *stimulos*. In animals there is therefore no moral obligation, but much necessity. Every created being, be it ever so perfect, has moral obligation. For they all have needs, which give certain stimuli, with which they have to struggle. The human being therefore has not holiness, but virtue. We can indeed have satisfaction in the object, only an *elater animi* is lacking to us, these are therefore not the causes of pleasure or displeasure. If the satisfaction hangs together with my state, then the existence of the object will not be indifferent to me, i.e. I will have interest in it. In virtue we must therefore also have interest. The moralists, however, are disunited about the incentives of it).",["The parenthesis opened at \"(alle unsre Handlungen\" is never closed before the final \")\"; the punctuation is carried over as it stands.","\"*mvicem*\" in \"*actionis mvicem moraliter bonae*\" is doubtful (OCR; perhaps *invitae* or *invicem*); kept as read, not emended. The Latin sentence is also left as it stands, including the genitive *volendi*.","*neceßiatatio* in the OCR is read as *necessitatio*. *Zwang* = \"coercion\", *Nöthigung* = \"necessitation\" (and *Nothwendigkeit* = \"necessity\"); *Neceßitatio* is left in Latin.","*Willkühr* = \"power of choice\" (not in the glossary). *Verbindlichkeit* = \"obligation\" (distinct from *Verbindung* = combination). *Triebfedern* = \"incentives\". *Wohlgefallen* = \"satisfaction\". *Intereße* = \"interest\".","*Mensch* = \"human being\". *Grund* = \"ground\" and *Bewußtsein* = \"consciousness\" (\"conscious\" for *bewußt*), as in the glossary. *zusammenhängt* is rendered \"hangs together\" (cf. *Zusammenhang*, interconnection).","\"So aber kann\" is rendered \"But thus can\"; \"diese\" in \"diese sind also nicht die Ursachen\" has no clear antecedent in the transcript and is left as \"these\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-004":["Freedom is a mere idea, and to act in accordance with this idea is called being free in the practical sense. The practical necessity is objective and not subjective. Here I must do something; but the natural necessity through *stimulos* says only: it will happen. Practically necessary is that without which our actions do not take place, namely without laws of the understanding and of reason. Freedom is therefore practically necessary — the human being must therefore act according to an idea of freedom, and otherwise he cannot. But that does not yet prove freedom in the theoretical sense. Hereby all difficulties and contradictions fall away that the concept of freedom has caused. One may prove or also refute freedom in the theoretical sense as one will, enough, one will nevertheless always act according to ideas of freedom. Many people do not admit certain propositions in speculation, but they nevertheless act according to them.",["*Verstände* in \"im practischen Verstände\" / \"im theoretischen Verstand\" = \"sense\" (idiom), whereas \"Gesetze des Verstandes\" = \"laws of the understanding\"; the same German word, two English renderings.","*Natur Nothwendigkeit* = \"natural necessity\". *Wiedersprüche* = \"contradictions\" (*Widerspruch*), *wiederlegen* = \"refute\" (spelling as in source).","\"Hier muß ich etwas thun; aber ... sagt nur; es wird geschehen\": the colon/semicolon articulation and the unmarked quotations are kept as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-005":["Freedom is merely the faculty of acting according to the *arbitrio intellectuali*, and not the *arbitrium* itself. The *caußae impulsivae intellectuales* are either *secundum quid* or *simpliciter talis*. *Caußa impulsiva secundum quid* is when I merely choose a good means in order to attain my end, which derives from a *Stimulo*, e.g., when someone writes a book, he commonly pretends to do it from love of truth, although he often does it for the sake of earning money. The means that he uses, namely to write a good book, is good. He must overcome many *Stimulos*, namely the love of laziness, he can also benefit the world, but his aim is nevertheless not intellectual, but sensible. Future well-being was his end; this was therefore *caußa impulsiva secundum quid*. But if someone writes a book from mere love of truth and has it made known only after his death, when he has no more benefit to hope for from the world, then his end is good and the *caußa impulsiva intellectualis*. This depends on no other *stimulo*. Morals has such laws *simpliciter intellectuale*, and yet with morals freedom must be presupposed. All human beings choose good means, the aim may be whichever it will. They proceed prudently, in that their desires always go to something good, but merely as a means, the aim may be whichever it will, to a sensible end. The end he sees as agreeable, the means through understanding and reason as good. Here he always acts *secundum quid*, the *arbitrium* is merely intellectual if it is *simpliciter tale*. Not the means, but the ends determine the desires. If reason settles that which is actually good, or specifies the ends, then it looks after its interest and is *Domina*. If it merely invents a good means for the best of the end, which arises from inclination, then it merely looks after the interest of inclination and is the *Serua*. It is then not incentive, but the inclination. The *arbitrium simul intellectuale* is also *purum*, for there merely reason takes place. But where reason and sensibility governs, and the latter is the ruler, there is *arbitrium sensitivum* or *impurum*, for the *caußa impulsiva* indeed lies in sensibility. And since end is more than means, *a potiori denominatio* occurs. Happiness has two kinds of meanings: 1. the sum of all agreeable sensations. But this we cannot determine. 2. the satisfaction of all present inclinations. If the aim of our actions is merely happiness and satisfaction of all our sensible inclinations, then our *arbitrium* is merely *sensitivum*. This is therefore not virtue, if we exercise good actions on account of certain advantages, e.g., if a citizen does his duty because he fears punishment, but rather if we do the good for the reason that we see that it is good and our duty. Here we act intellectually, but there sensibly. *Arbitrium intellectuale* or *strictius sic dictum* or *arbitrium purum*. Thus freedom is the faculty of choosing that which is good in itself and not merely as a means. We are therefore free if we arrange our actions entirely according to the laws of understanding and of reason, and the more we do this, the freer we are. For although the will is free of the *Stimulis*, it nevertheless cannot be entirely free. For since we desire merely that which pleases us, the pleasing is the cause of our desiring. But the cause of the pleasing is either sensibility or understanding. No third is possible. If I therefore do not desire something from sensibility, then I must desire it for the reason that it pleases my understanding. But if I were to desire neither according to understanding nor sensibility, I would will that which displeases me, I would act without incentive and cause, and that is impossible. Understanding and reason give the will laws, by which it must direct itself if it is to be free. But through mere representations of reason we cannot be determined; it must also give us incentives, and these it also gives us, in that our conscience approves or disapproves. For if we follow it, we can attain a tranquil conscience and all spiritual happiness. It exercises here not only *vim legislatoriam* but also *executivam*. According to it it is therefore not only objectively necessary but also subjectively possible. The human being is nevertheless free on that account, for he can choose between sensibility and understanding; but if he were to act according to sensibility, he would become like the animals. But reason raises him above the animals, and the more he acts according to it, the more moral and at the same time the freer he becomes. For morals is science of that which is good, otherwise the concept of freedom would become superfluous, if there were no morals; and then reason would also be superfluous. For it is there merely for the sake of the morality of the laws. The field of morality is either affected by inclinations or not. Both is *arbitrium liberum*. For we have even there the freedom to act according to the understanding. The *arbitrium intellectuale* is when we act independently of inclinations, although we have them. And now we can reflect whether we ought to follow them or not. We are free only in order to follow the laws of ethicality. For otherwise we would be permitted to have no reason. So far we have spoken of freedom in the practical understanding. But freedom in the transcendental understanding is a faculty of determining itself independently of all remote or natural causes. Here there is again an autonomy of reason. For whether I assume this or the opposite, I lose myself in contradictions. Sensibility is determined through objects; these through laws of nature and these through God. Hence God determines our sensibility and we are not free *transcendentaliter*. But if we assume that everything happens merely according to necessity, we never arrive at the first, and then we can also ask, by what is God determined. But all these speculative considerations of the contradictions do not harm the practical concept of freedom. For this sees not how something happens, but that it ought to happen, and ought presupposes freedom. If in the use of our will we merely take regard of empirical happiness, then reason determines not through the concept of the highest good, but through the concept of the highest agreeableness. There inclination governs, determines the end, and reason is the slave, who must procure the means. But in morals it is said, the senses are not to *raisonniren*, merely reason must be master. For the desires I cannot entirely fulfill, ever new ones arise. — But the highest good I can attain. Hence the concept of an *arbitrii intellectualis* must necessarily lie at the ground, and it itself must be important for every human being.",["OCR slips silently corrected: iemancl, Glrikseeligkeit, sirnul, le mehr, wrir, kieiheit (read as iemand, Glückseeligkeit, simul, je mehr, wir, Freiheit). *Serua*, *caußa*, *Obiecte* kept in the transcript's own orthography.","Zweck/Zwek = end; Absicht = aim (kept distinct throughout). Mittel = means. Triebfeder (not in the glossary) = incentive; Begierde = desire; Neigung = inclination; Summe = sum (not Inbegriff).","sinnlich = sensible (adverbially 'sensibly'); Sinnlichkeit = sensibility. Moral = morals; Moralitaet = morality; Sittlichkeit = ethicality (one English word is not available for it that does not collide with the others).","einsehen is rendered 'see' (in 'sieht ... ein' and 'einsehen, daß'); in 'dieser sieht nicht, wie' the verb is sehen, also 'see'. Gefallen (noun) = 'the pleasing'; geistig = 'spiritual'.","'Ihr zu Folge ists': 'it' (ihr) has no clear antecedent (Vernunft is the nearest candidate); the impersonal subject of 'ists' is likewise left open. Likewise 'sie selbst' in the last sentence is feminine and its antecedent is not fixed; both left ambiguous.","'dürften wir keine Vernunft haben': dürfen may carry the older sense 'need' (we would need to have no reason) or 'be permitted'; rendered 'would be permitted', ambiguity noted.","'im practischen / transcendentalen Verstande': Verstande rendered literally as 'understanding'; the idiomatic sense 'in the practical / transcendental sense' is a possible reading.","*raisonniren* kept in the original; it is not vernünfteln. 'Beides ist arbitrium liberum' and 'a potiori denominatio' kept literally."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-001":["Of indifference and equilibrium.",["Gleichgültigkeit = indifference; Gleichgewicht = equilibrium."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-002":["Indifferent to us is that in which we have neither agreeable nor disagreeable sensations, thus as much as insensibility. We are also indifferent when we neither desire nor abhor something. But equal is that which we equally desire and abhor, or for the exercise of which we have equal grounds and counter-grounds.",["gleich gültig / gleichgültig = 'indifferent'; gleich = 'equal' (the verbal play between gleich-gültig and gleich is not reproducible; 'gleich begehren und verabscheuen' = 'equally desire and abhor').","Unempfindlichkeit = 'insensibility'; Ausübung = 'exercise'; Gründe und Gegengründe = 'grounds and counter-grounds'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-003":["One wants to prove, through the faculty of choosing indifferent things, that freedom is a lawlessness from all incentives. But *libertas indifferentiae* is a non-thing. For what does not please me I also cannot will, I therefore remain in the same state. The *aequilibrium* or the equality is when we desire and abhor something equally: but this seldom takes place in the human being. We do not find a complete *aequilibrium* even in the scale-pans. The *aequilibrium* can be either 1. *aequilibrium stimulorum*: when the incentives of sensibility are equal on both sides. Here, one says, the human being will nevertheless be able to do and choose one of the two, and thus act freely. But no, he will then rather do neither of the two and omit the action. This omitting is also equilibrium, or there still always remains to him, to alter and determine himself through reason, e.g. if someone is left the choice, to do for something determinate, of taking either 5 rthl. or a Friedrichsd'or, then he is, with regard to what he is to take, *in statu aequilibri*. But he may take neither of them, if he wants to act honestly.",["Triebfeder = incentive (not in the glossary); Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Zustand = state; Unding = \"non-thing\" (Ding = thing).","Willkühr = \"choice\" (not in the glossary).","\"Friednchsd'or\" is read as OCR for Friedrichsd'or; \"rthl.\" (Reichsthaler) left as printed.","\"gleich begehren und verabscheuen\": \"equally\" may attach to both verbs; kept as in German.","\"für etwas Bestimmtes zu thun\" is obscure and kept literal; \"sich durch die Vernunft zu ändern und zu bestimmen\" is also kept literal."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-004":["2. *Aequilibrium motivorum*. But here complete equality can never take place. If there were full equality, he could not choose; for if there is no ground, then there is also no choice, or one can also omit the action. There is no complete equality, for even if the objective grounds are equally strong on both sides, particular subjective grounds can still be added on the one side, which give it a preponderance, e.g. if the freedom is given to me of choosing one of 2 ducats completely equal to one another, then the objective incentives on both sides would be equally strong therein. I would therefore deliberate for a while which I should take, until at last a subjective incentive, avarice or also impatience at delaying so long, would so stimulate me that I would take the first best that then fell into my eyes, or I would also take, from comfort, the one that was nearest to me. Thus one sees, then, that a subjective cause, of which kind there can be countless according to the constitution of our mind at any given time, which often remain obscure to us and may work in secret, must give the object the turn of the scale. Otherwise the human being will not be able to choose anything in complete *aequilibrium*. *Libertas aequilibrium* is therefore nothing. For here we nevertheless always act according to the greater multitude of incentives and thus not according to our own conceit. Finally the concept of true freedom has been posited therein, that one could commonly choose this to which *caussae impulsivae* drive us. This is called *libertas contrarietatis*. But that comes from the fact that one has thought the concept of freedom merely negatively. But we must nevertheless have a positive concept of it, otherwise we have no concept at all. The positive concept is the faculty of acting according to concepts of the good and the evil. — Because freedom is not subject to the physical laws or the laws of sensibility, one believes it is subject to no law. But that is concluded too far. That we are free from the laws of sensibility is our prerogative. The animals are not, therefore they are merely passive. For we are not passive when we act according to laws of the understanding, since these indeed come from ourselves. We are therefore determined by no one, but determine ourselves and thus we have spontaneity. Since here our own reason is the incentive of the laws, we are indeed free, since we govern ourselves. Freedom is therefore no faculty at all of choosing the evil, but the good, because our reason commands us merely the good. If we are free from the *stimulis*, we must nevertheless have a motive according to which we can direct ourselves. For to act without motive would be for us no prerogative at all, but a disgrace. For what would one think of the human being who, in order to show his freedom, wallowed in the gutter in fine clothes. The *libertas contrarietatis* is merely an imagined dream, for in all our actions we will nevertheless find that they occur from causes, and it would also be *contradictorisch* not to will what pleases me in the highest degree, but the opposite. The good is always what every human being wills, and he would also always do it, if only it were not difficult for him to carry it out, and if our nature were so constituted that we always acted according to the concept of the good, then we would be truly free. But because reason comes from ever other causes, the human being is merely free *secundum quid*. One also makes the objection, when one says: A being created thus, that according to its nature can do nothing but good, is not free. For it then could not lie and would then have to speak the truth, would accordingly be coerced and not free. One can answer to this: It stands in its power, or it is physically possible, that he could lie, but he never wills to lie. Yet a spirit created thus is limited by the highest cause and acts like a clock. All these disputes about the transcendental concept of freedom have no influence on the practical. For there I look not to the highest cause, but to the final end.",["\"Liberias aequilibrium\" is read as OCR for *Libertas aequilibrium* (possibly *aequilibrii* in the original); \"caußae\" given as *caussae*; \"I reiheit\" read as Freiheit.","\"contrarietalis\" (first occurrence) corrected to *contrarietatis*, as in the second occurrence.","\"Gemüth\" = mind (as Gemüt); Geist is also \"mind; spirit\" in the glossary, here rendered \"spirit\". Wesen = being; Grund = ground; Bewegungsgrund = motive; Triebfeder = incentive; Zweck = end.","\"Willkühr\" and \"Ausschlag geben\" are not in the glossary; the latter is rendered \"give the turn of the scale\".","\"Denn sind wir nicht paßiv\" has inverted word order as printed; rendered \"For we are not passive\" because the construction is taken as a statement, not a question.","\"sondern das Gute\" is elliptical (\"but the good\"), kept as is; \"es stehe in seiner Kraft ... er\": the pronouns (es/seiner/er) refer back to Wesen/Geist and are kept as in German. \"zu weit geschloffen\" = \"concluded too far\" (schließen).","\"contradictorisch\" is left as \"*contradictorisch*\" (Latinate German adjective); \"praerogativ\" = prerogative; \"dies\" in \"dies wählen\" is rendered \"this\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s21-001":["Second Section Rational Psychology Division",["Einteilung = division."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s21-002":["This is to be derived entirely independently of empirical principles merely *a priori*. Here we have 3 sorts of things to observe: 1. the transcendental predicates of the soul, whether it is substance and persistent, finite or infinite etc., 2. the soul in comparison with other beings, and indeed a in the comparison with matter, b in comparison with other thinking beings, 3. the connection of the soul with other beings, a with the body, b with the world by means of the body. This connection is called *commercium animae et corporis* and constitutes life. Thus a possibility of life, b the beginning of it, c the end or death, d accordingly the state after death, immortality.",["\"beharrlieh\" read as OCR for beharrlich (persistent).","\"bloß a priori\": \"merely\" may attach to \"derived\" or to \"*a priori*\"; the German order is kept.","\"3erlei\" rendered \"3 sorts of things\"; Leib and Körper would both be \"body\" (Leib here); Verknüpfung = connection.","The enumerators a, b, c, d are printed as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-001":["On the Nature of the Human Soul",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-002":["Here we deal with the transcendental predicates of the human soul. Here it is asked 1. Is it material or immaterial? The I is already a mark of the immaterial of the human soul. The I is throughout an object of inner sense, and everything of which I am conscious of myself is merely an object of inner sense. All efforts are therefore in vain to want to make the faculties of the soul distinct through corporeal intuition. We do not find the slightest analogy between thinking and matter.",["\"selbet\" read as OCR for selbst.","Merkmal = mark; Gegenstand = object; Sinn = sense; Anschauung = intuition; Vermögen = faculty; deutlich = distinct; Bewußtsein = consciousness.","\"frägts sich\" rendered \"it is asked\"; \"das Immateriellen\" rendered \"the immaterial\" as printed (\"des immateriellen\")."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-003":["2. The soul is substance, and not only that, but I am also conscious of the substantial of the soul. For of matter only the accidents are known to me, but not the substantial. I am the last subject and cognize myself without accidents. Of the substantial, however, both in the body and in me, I properly have no concept; I know nothing of it except that it is a something. Now it comes only to this, to derive the properties of the soul from this sterile concept of something. Upon the same the whole rational theology is supposed to be built; we easily see that this is impossible, for from the concept of something nothing can be comprehended. That the soul is a substance we cannot prove from its persistence. For whence do we know this? That is substance which remains nevertheless under all alterations. Of the soul we have experience of itself, experience beforehand up to death. Whether it will still remain after death, I do not know. If we therefore call the soul a substance, we can say nothing more than that substance is here a logical function — namely the subject and not the predicate. If I consider this subject alone without all predicates, then I consider the substantial or the something. To pick out all properties from it is not possible. But if we cannot discover any new properties, we can nevertheless avoid false inferences and errors. Thus I can, e.g., determine from the narrow concept of a subject, from the I, that the soul is no *compositum*. — The I is the last subject. But the last subject is never composite. The soul we cognize, further, through nothing but such predicates, of which not a single one can be cognized through sensible intuition. They are merely objects of inner intuition. Matter, however, as object of outer intuition, we cognize by its extension and by its figure. Thinking makes no extension, no figure. — The soul must therefore also be something other than matter. If this investigation has no positive use, it nevertheless has a negative one, which consists in this, that we do not fall into the errors of the materialists and explain the actions of the soul physico-mechanically. The materialist here really commits a μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος. That the soul is not matter can be made out distinctly; but it can still be that the *substratum* of matter is the same as the *substratum* of the soul. Yet the *phaenomena* are different. — One proves the simplicity of the soul and thus its immateriality in the following form. Namely, if the soul were composite, then its representations would have to be so divided that in each part of the soul there would be a representation, which taken together make up the whole representation (With every thought there is a unity of consciousness, always the same I, which therefore also presupposes unity of the subject). That would be as if, in a company, of a thought of mine a word were said by each member of the company. But the whole thought cannot possibly arise in this way, and I be conscious of it, but there must then again be a subject that gathers together all the parts of the thought and so forms for itself the whole thought. Now, can the persistence of the soul not be inferred from the simplicity of the human soul, as Mendelssohn thinks? If we could think of no other perishing than by division into parts, then this proof of the persistence and immortality of the soul would be irrefutable. But another perishing of the soul is still possible, namely through *evanescence*. — Its reality can become ever smaller until it vanishes. The soul is not matter, yet for that reason it can be material, i.e. the *substratum* of the soul: the I, the last subject, can be of the same content as the *substratum* of matter and could have become the *substratum* of matter. So thought Leibniz, and the Monadology brought him to this thought. Namely, because he believed that all monads had *vires repraesentativas* and the soul also had a *vim repraesentativam* and was a monad, he held both to be one and the same. But we do not have the slightest cause to assume that the soul is material, since thinking is entirely different from all accidents of matter, and we do not see why we should assume something for which we have no ground. Hence we say the soul is immaterial or pneumatic, although we cannot prove this. From this, however, that does not follow which was a necessary consequence of the Leibnizian Monadology, namely that through composition of many souls a *compositum*, an appearance, might finally arise. The author explains *animam* through *vim repraesentativam*. But that is false; for from this it follows that the soul could be only one fundamental force. Several fundamental forces would also make up several souls. Accordingly it is better: *anima est vis repraesentationis*. For *anima* is not one and the same with *vis*, for *vis* is the *effectus* of the soul upon a causality. —",["OCR garbled Greek 'peTocßamv eiq aXXo yivoc,' silently read as μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος; 'kömnits' read as 'kömmt's'; 'Reahtaet' as 'Realitaet'; 'n* Materie' as 'Materie'; 'Thei!' as 'Theil'.","'Erfahrung über sich, Erfahrung voraus bis in den Tod' is obscure in the German and is kept literal; 'voraus' may be doubtful.","'Object' and 'Gegenstand' both 'object'; here only 'Object(e)' occurs. 'Subiect/Subject' = subject. 'Beharrlichkeit' = persistence; 'Erkenntniß'-forms 'erkennen' = cognize; 'begreifen' = comprehend.","'Accidentien' = accidents; 'Schlüsse' = inferences; 'Realitaet' = reality; 'Kraft/Grund Kraft' = force/fundamental force; 'Inhalt' = content; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Ausdehnung' = extension; 'Erscheinung' = appearance.","'Vergehen' rendered 'perishing'; 'Zertheilung' 'division into parts'; 'Evanescenz' kept as Latinate in italics.","'Einheit des Bewußtseins' = unity of consciousness. 'Der Autor' = Baumgarten (not named in the passage). 'ienein' / 'ienem' not present; 'Das ist Substanz, was...' kept in construction.","'denn vis ist der effectus der Seele auf eine Caußalitaet' is obscure and kept literal.","'Etwas' rendered 'something'; 'Eigenschaften' rendered 'properties' (Eigenschaft is not in the glossary; Beschaffenheit is also 'property')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-004":["Of the Souls of Animals",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-005":["We now come to the second piece of rational psychology, namely to the comparison of the soul with other beings. — We can think beings that have only the lower faculty of cognition and in which the higher is wholly lacking. These we call *substantias brutas*. Do animals not have the higher faculty of cognition, or is it only buried in them? That they do not have it, we cannot demonstrate. But why should we assume more for the explanation of certain appearances than is necessary. — Hence we assume that animals can continue on in eternity and grow in their forces and yet will not get understanding, because then an essential piece must be added to their sensibility, through which alone understanding is possible, namely apperception. Perhaps animal souls continue in eternity and, as in this life, so also in the future stand at the service of man. But they will nevertheless never become human souls, because they are, not as Meier believes merely in degree, but in kind different from man. But it is easy to think that, since animals are now a part of the needs of men, in that life too, when man will be perfect, there will be more perfect animals that will serve him. Meanwhile these always remain mere fantasies. As do the various opinions and enthusiasms about higher beings than man is. Even if there are such, man is nothing less than they, for the rational being is itself end and capable of happiness, if it makes itself worthy of it.",["'Erkenntniß Vermögen' = faculty of cognition; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Kräfte' = forces; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Wesen' = beings (being); 'Zweck' = end; 'Art' = kind (as against 'Grad' = degree).","'Ewigkeit' rendered 'eternity'; 'fortdauren' 'continue on'; 'Schwärmereyen' 'enthusiasms'.","'der Mensch ist' at the end of the 'higher beings' sentence is kept as in the German; 'nichts weniger als sie' is ambiguous in German (could also read 'anything but they'); the literal reading is kept.","'ihrer' in the last clause could refer to 'Glückseligkeit' only; kept as 'it'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-006":["Psychological Systems of the *Commercium* of the Soul with the Body",["'Commerciums' kept as Latin *commercium* in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-007":["We come now to the 3rd part of rational psychology, namely to the connection of the soul with other beings and indeed with α matter. This connection is α immediately with its body, β mediately by means of the body with the remaining matter. I say: that is my body, its alterations are my alterations. Why that? The soul can through the mere consciousness of its self never designate its position in the world — rather the body determines its position. The soul is no object of outer sense, and one can therefore also not determine where it has its seat; hence it is also not possible to say where it actually is in the body. To that end soul and body are in *commercium*, and this *commercium* is so strong that mere thinking already has influence on the body. On the nervous system the soul acts immediately, the remaining parts of the body are mere instruments, through which it acts by means of the nervous system. To determine this *commercium* more closely is the business of anthropology. We consider now α. the possibility of the *commercium*. We already have difficulties to deal with merely in explaining the *commercium* of the body with itself, how much harder will it therefore be to have insight into the possibility of the *commercium* of the soul with the body. This *commercium* manifests itself in this, that the soul through the mere will can cause movements in the body. To this the following serves: movements are no effects, but are phenomena of the effects. Movement is successive alteration of the place that a body occupies, but no effect, but a phenomenon of an effect, which is unknown to us. Whether that which has influence and acts is a Leibnizian monad, we do not know. But this we do know, that between cause and effect homogeneity is not necessary, but merely heterogeneity can take place. (One stumbles in the explanation of the *commercium* with the body chiefly on this, that movement and thinking are so different that one cannot have insight into how one is supposed to bring about the other; but the body is a phenomenon and consequently also its properties. The substrate of it we do not know. How now this could be in *commercium* with the soul amounts to how substances in general can be in *commercium*, and that difficulty on account of the heterogeneity now falls away. That bodies are mere appearances follows quite clearly from this, because all properties and forces of the same come down to the moving force. So also impenetrability. Movement is successive presence in different places. But what brings it about that a thing can be in its own place, and successively in different places, we do not know.) Therefore the force to think can be wholly different from the body, and the heterogeneity makes no difficulty at all. We are thus therefore not yet at all compelled to think to ourselves a *harmoniam praestabilitam*; here merely the *systema causarum efficientium* is to be assumed. The connection *per harmoniam praestabilitam* is merely ideal. According to it the body would have to be able to do its functions even if it had no soul, even if the soul were already separated from it. Thus here the soul is superfluous. The best system is that of the *causarum efficientium* or, more determinately, of the *influxus physici* — through this, that the soul is combined with the body, it is called soul. The soul makes use of the nerves as instruments and through these influences immediately the remaining parts of the body; the nerves are on the other side again also the instruments through which the body influences the soul. When a nerve is tied off, I see nothing. Cartes said: there were *ideae materiales*, imprints in the brain, which the soul produces as on a tablet. When we think, we imprint this in the brain, and this leaves traces behind, which are struck again and refreshed through some related brain nerves. Thus memory is to be explained. Bonnet is also of the same opinion. But materialism, as we have already seen, is nothing. His thought-imprints can remain over, but it is still useless artifice, e.g. when one wants to retain something, one's head aches, but these are no ideas, but serve only to awaken ideas. So also, when she makes intuitions for herself through the imagination.",["OCR: 'a' and 'ß' before 'der Materie', 'unmittelbar', 'mittelbar' read as α and β; 'a.' before 'die Möglichkeit' read as α.; 'durchweiche' read as 'durch welche'; 'einei' as 'einer'; 'iene' as 'jene'; 'druken' left as 'drucken'.","Stelle = 'position' (Ort = 'place'); Veränderung = 'alteration'; Wirkung = 'effect', wirken = 'act', bewirken = 'bring about', verursachen = 'cause'; einsehen = 'have insight into'.","Verbindung/verbunden = 'combination/combined'; Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Eigenschaft and Beschaffenheit would both be 'property' (only Eigenschaft occurs here); Erscheinung = 'appearance', while Phaenomen(on) is rendered 'phenomenon'.","'Commercium', 'harmoniam praestabilitam', 'systema causarum efficientium', 'influxus physici', 'ideae materiales' are left in Latin; the transcript gives 'ideas materiales' and 'caußarum', normalized. 'per harmoniam praestabilitam' is as in the text.","'seine Denk Abdrücke' is doubtful: 'seine' (his/its) may be an OCR or transcript slip; rendered 'His' as the grammar allows, with the referent left open (Cartes? Bonnet?). 'Cartes' is kept as spelled.","The last sentence ('So auch, wenn sie sich ... Anschauungen macht') is a fragment; the antecedent of 'sie' is left unresolved (the soul, die Seele, is feminine). 'das sind aber keine Ideen' has no clear antecedent for 'das'; likewise 'dient'.","'Werkzeug' = 'instrument'; 'Gedächtnis' = 'memory'; 'Künstelei' = 'artifice'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-008":["Where does the soul have its seat? In general it has it in the body, but where in particular? There is great dispute about this. The ancients said: in the diaphragm, because this is moved in certain affects. Cartes said it is in the pineal gland. But this is often found petrified, and people have nonetheless lived (the physicians call it *commune sensorium*, from where the soul first begins its effects; people have now sought to find this out in all kinds of ways). The ancients also said: *anima est tota in corpore, sed totum tamen in parte ejus*. This is nothing. Many have assumed that in each part of the body there is a proper degree of movement, and that consequently many souls would also be combined with a single body. Bonnet puts it in the *corpus callosum*, callosity of the brain, but has no other ground for this than the conjecture that it must be in that part of the body in which no two are different. But are all these opinions at all reasonable? The soul is an object of inner sense and therefore takes up no space. But if I give it the same, I make it an object of outer sense and matter. Hence its presence in the body can be determined not *localiter* but *virtualiter*, through the influence that it has on the body. This influence is only immediate on the nerves (i.e. the soul is immediately present to each nerve) and strongest in the brain, because there the nerves come together. With matter, to be sure, presence in the place precedes the influence, but with the soul not. — So also God's omnipresence is not to be explained otherwise than *virtualiter*. — Since the soul cannot become conscious of its place in the body, because it cannot be intuited with any outer sense, it is also not capable of going out of the same at will. Does the soul go away from the body immediately after its death? If it is an atom, it must probably still remain in it, as long as until the body is destroyed.",["OCR: 'tarnen' read as 'tamen'; 'iniedem' as 'in jedem'; 'demienigen' as 'demjenigen'; 'iedem' as 'jedem'; 'unmittel -bar' joined.","'Zwirbeldrüse' (the pineal gland) and 'Hirnschwiele' ('callosity of the brain', glossing *corpus callosum*) are rendered as given; 'Zwerchfell' = 'diaphragm'.","'in dem nicht zwei verschieden sind': 'verschieden' rendered 'different'; the sense of the clause is left as obscure as in the German. 'vernünftig' = 'reasonable'.","'einen eigner Grad' = 'a proper degree' ('eigen' = own/proper); 'Grad' = 'degree'; 'Gegenstand' = 'object'; 'bewußt werden' = 'become conscious'; 'wahrscheinlicherweise' = 'probably' (cf. Wahrscheinlichkeit).","'Meinung' = 'opinion'; 'Einfluß' = 'influence'; 'Gegenwart' = 'presence'; 'angeschaut' = 'intuited' (Anschauung = intuition).","'commune sensorium', 'localiter', 'virtualiter', 'corpus callosum' and the Latin sentence of the ancients are kept in Latin. 'Aerzte' may be read as 'physicians'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-009":["Chap. 3. Origin of the human soul",["'Cap.' (Latin *caput*) rendered 'Chap.'; this is a heading only."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-010":["As regards the birth of the human being, he arises through procreation. If the soul were material, it would also arise in the way that the remaining matter of the body does. All parts of this matter were there beforehand, and through procreation they receive only a new form. A *compositum* can arise without creation, because the substances are already there beforehand. But a simple thing must arise through creation, hence the soul too, since it is simple. Creation is *actuatio substantiae*. The author's definition: *actuatio ex nihilo*, is not right. For it already follows from the concept of a substance that it must arise from nothing. For if the parts were there beforehand, then now merely the form of them would spring forth, which is an accident, but not substance. Now, however, the question arises: Did God or the parents create the soul? Substances that are in the world cannot possibly create others. For all substances stand in *commercium*. Now if one brings forth the other, it creates something in respect of which it afterwards is itself passive. It is thus passive before itself, which cannot be thought at all. — Those who assume that the parents have created the souls of the children are called Traducianists. The *propagatio per traducem*, through a soul, the others begetting, is a common concept. It rests on materialism. Parents cannot communicate the soul to the children through division, otherwise their own would be composite. God has thus created the soul. But when? At birth, or at the beginning of the world? The first is asserted by Inducianists (Concreatianists), the last by Preexistentianists. These are right. For all substances must have been created at the creation of the world. If souls were still being created now, then these would always be miracles, for every creation is a miracle. But if these were to become ordinary, they are contradictory.",["Silently corrected OCR 'Eie Schöpfung' to 'Die Schöpfung'.","'Substanz' rendered 'substance'; 'Wesen' not occurring here. 'Begriff' = concept; 'Theil' = part; 'Form' = form; 'Accidens' rendered 'accident'.","'Commercio' (Latin ablative) kept in Latin as *commercium* in the nominative form; the German text has 'im Commercio'.","'Die propagatio per traducem, durch eine Seele, die andere erzeugten' is syntactically obscure in the German; kept as it stands, literally.","'Würden aber die gewöhnlich, sind sie contradictorisch' is obscure (the referent of 'die' and of 'sie' is unclear); kept literal, and 'contradictorisch' rendered 'contradictory'. 'Praeexistentianer' and 'Concreatianer' rendered with English cognates.","'actuatio' is Latin in the source; 'Erklärung' here = definition."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-001":["Immortality of the human soul",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-002":["Here we have to answer the following questions: Will the soul continue to live? 1. as human being or as intelligence? 2. Does this continued existence flow from the constitution of its nature (i.e., it is actually immortal) or from a particular divine decree? 3. Will this continued existence be general or particular? With regard to the first question the materialist has to concern himself only with whether the human being will also continue to exist, but the pneumatist with whether he will also continue to exist as intelligence. The first question we can set aside, for it can be indifferent to us, and the human body also seems from its first determination to incline toward the earth (Priestley is a materialist and assumes that the body will be awakened again after death, but that is then no immortality. For it is no continuing life and it is also not I who am awakened, but a similar being. For it is all one whether God composes such a body from the parts of my body or from other matter, for as matter or substances they are entirely one and the same. Priestley says the doctrine that the human being has a soul is a senseless doctrine and entirely contrary to Holy Scripture. For πνεῦμα is a breath and indicates something external. But whether the human being will continue to exist as intelligence is more important for us to know, and there we must again ask whether his continued existence after his death is necessary as a general law of nature, or is grounded on an extraordinary divine decree. The former is more important to us, for with the latter the continued existence could be particular. The question here is not about a future life in general, for that we can prove teleologically; rather we want to be convinced of the immortality of the soul or of its impossibility of dying. For then we are certain of the continued existence of all souls after death. (To immortality belongs the proof of 1. the natural impossibility of dying, 2. the continued existence of the soul with all its powers and of the same, for otherwise life would be nothing, 3. the continued existence of its personality, that it remains conscious of its previous life. For otherwise I do not continue to exist, but another spiritual being.) Of the immortality of the soul a natural and a moral proof will be possible. For all philosophy goes either to nature or to morals. Morality is the determination of action according to laws of freedom. Philosophy, of morals as well as of nature, is grounded on principles *a priori*. The natural proofs are of 3 kinds: 1. from experience or from the nature of the human being known through experience, 2. from rational psychology, 3. from the analogy of the nature of the soul with the whole order of nature. As for the empirical proof, it has very uncertain arguments. Since we do not have the slightest experience of immortality, all inferences from the experiences of this life are mere conjectures. The experiences occur only during animal life, I can therefore know the powers of the soul only when it is in *commercium* with the body. Hence they cannot give us the slightest light on how the soul will be constituted after the *commercium* with the body.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'nveu^oc' read as πνεῦμα (Greek, doubtful reading); 'Kathschluß' as 'Rathschluß'; 'Eifahrung' as 'Erfahrung'; 'lolgeiungen' as 'Folgerungen' (rendered 'inferences'); '3erlei' as 'dreierlei'; 'hortdauer' as 'Fortdauer'; 'noth wendig' as 'nothwendig'; 'dur ch' as 'durch'; 'zuwieder' as 'zuwider'.","'Fortdauer/fortdauern' rendered 'continued existence / continue to exist'; 'Fortfahren zu leben' as 'continue to live'. 'Dasein' does not occur.","'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Beschaffenheit' = constitution; 'besonder' = particular; 'Erfahrung' = experience; 'Kräfte' = powers (Kraft glossed 'force; power').","'particulair/particulaer' = particular; 'Folgerungen' (garbled) rendered 'inferences' though glossary gives 'consequence' for Folgerung; reading doubtful.","'Intelligenz', 'Materialist', 'Pneumatist' kept as English cognates. 'Rathschluß' = decree. 'Principien a priori' retained with *a priori*.","'der sehr unsichre Argumente' in 'so hat der sehr unsichre Argumente' is as printed (likely 'er'); rendered 'it has'. The sentence 'Die Seele ... Ich der ich aufgeweckt werde' is kept literal. The opening parenthesis after 'neigen' is closed only after 'ähnliches' — see next passage; here it is left unclosed as in the source."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-003":["(In order to conduct a proof from experience, we would have to be able to place ourselves outside the *commercium* with our body, or be able to ask other souls placed outside it about it, but neither of which is feasible. One also compares the soul with a caterpillar, which after its apparent death becomes a beautiful *papillon*, and wants to find therein an analogy in nature. But it is entirely false, for if one kills the caterpillar, a *papillon* never comes of it. Altogether there is nothing similar to it in nature and experience). One says: in old age the body becomes ever weaker, but the mind ever stronger. People who rave in a feverish illness become rational shortly before death, and from this one concludes that the soul, if it were freed from the body, would be able to think better and more freely than now. But against this one can on the one hand object that those appearances can be so explained that, when the human being dies, the vital fluid in the brain becomes the more lively the more the lower part of the body dies off; on the other hand one also has experiences that attest the contrary: namely that the body has much influence on the soul, that e.g. a blow on the head can produce madness and stupidity, that the soul alternates with the body, etc. In old age memory, wit and easy overview of the whole decline, but a power of judgment practiced through long experience arises. Hence the old are the best counselors, and one has always chosen them for it).",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'iene' = 'jene', 'ieher' = 'jeher', 'iemehr' = 'jemehr', 'W itz' = 'Witz', 'Liebersicht' = 'Uebersicht', 'Urtheilskraft' = power of judgment.","The parentheses are as in the source: the first closes after 'Erfahrung nichts ähnliches', and the final ')' has no matching opening in this passage (the opening parenthesis is probably missing or misplaced in the OCR/edition); kept as printed.","'Erscheinungen' here rendered 'appearances' (Erscheinung = appearance); 'Papillon' kept as a French loanword in italics.","'daß die Seele mit dem Körper wechsele' rendered literally; the sense of 'wechsele' ('alternates') is doubtful.","'Gemüt' and 'Geist': 'Geist' rendered 'mind' per glossary (also 'spirit'); 'geistiges Wesen' in the preceding passage rendered 'spiritual being'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-004":["In rational psychology we have 3 pieces to prove: 1. the persistence of the soul as substance, 2. the continued duration of this after death, as intelligence, 3. its continued duration as person. One believes oneself finished with the first, since substance is persistent. But since the soul is cognized as substance only through the I, we do not even know whether it is substance in the sense that as such it could not pass away. Mendelssohn first attempted to prove the immortality of the soul as substance, but he did not succeed. He says: It is simple, and could therefore not be dissolved through division. But it can expire and its reality gradually vanish. He says that would be a leap; but it could indeed still happen in accordance with the *lex continui*. But if its continued duration as substance is certain, that still does not prove its continued duration as intelligence. The previous proof proves the *incorruptibilitas* of the soul. On that account, however, it can after death fall into an eternal dreaming or perpetual sleep, which is called the sleep of souls. But if it continues to endure as intelligence, then this is not to be feared at all. Finally one must prove the continued duration of its personality. Immortality is the impossibility of dying. Dying is the end of life. Life is animal in *commercium* and spiritual without *commercium* with the body. The animal life can therefore well cease, but the spiritual cannot. The life of the thinking being consists in personality, since it is conscious of itself. Immortality will be the necessary continued duration for this personality, not *vita bruta*. If the soul loses its personality or became another person, then it would no longer be the same and one could not say that it continued its life. Thus, e.g., metempsychosis is such an interruption, since there the personality is altered. Immortality will therefore be continuous life. It must connect the following state with the previous one and know that it is the same one that it was before. If the soul in the other world were conscious of nothing of what had gone on with it here, then its substance would indeed, but not its person, continue to endure.",["OCR 'Lege continui' read as *lege continui* (lex continui); 'ia' read as 'ja' ('indeed'); '1 .' read as '1.'; 'mit. der' read as 'mit der'.","'Beharrlichkeit' = persistence; 'Fortdauer' = continued duration; 'Dauert … fort' rendered 'continues to endure'.","'Substanz' = substance; 'Intelligenz' = intelligence; 'Persönlichkeit' = personality; 'Person' = person.","'erkannt wird' = is cognized; 'wissen' = know (Wissen).","'Realitaet' = reality; 'Theilung' = division; 'Zustand' = state; 'Verknüpfen' = connect; 'bewußt' = conscious.","'Commercium', 'Incorruptibilitaet' and 'vita bruta' are Latin/Latinate in the source and are kept in italics (the latter in Latin spelling 'incorruptibilitas' regularized from the OCR/transcript form).","'Wenn die Seele … verliert oder eine andre Person würde, so wäre': the tense shift (present, then subjunctive) is kept as in the German; 'vor diese Persönlichkeit' (for 'für') is rendered 'for this personality'.","'der Seelen Schlaf' rendered 'the sleep of souls' (genitive plural/singular of 'Seele' is ambiguous in form)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-005":["2. The rational proof. All matter is lifeless, has no faculty to determine itself, and the principle of life is something other than matter. For every matter remains in motion or rest until it is changed by something else. Matter has, therefore, merely receptivity or passivity. But the principle of life is spontaneity or the faculty to determine itself from inner principles (the body is an obstacle to life, because it contributes nothing to the promotion of life and yet flows very strongly into the soul. It is just as with someone who is shackled to a cart and must always drag it with him. Now if the cart is well greased up to death, then he can draw it more easily and go faster, if not, then he must go more slowly. Should one now think that, free from the cart, he would not be able to go at all? No, he will rather go more easily, better.) Matter has no principle of life in itself, and therefore also contributes nothing to the promotion of life. Because it is wholly lifeless, it also provides no condition of life. In the human being, therefore, it is not the body that is the principle of life, but the soul, and it also does not serve for the promotion of life. On that account the separation of the body from the soul must also be no alteration of life. It must signify just as much as, e.g., the separation of the horse from the wagon it has hitherto drawn. But if in spite of all this we nevertheless see that the body flows into the soul, then it must be an obstacle to life. For other kinds of influences cannot be thought. Death is therefore a promotion of the life of the soul, and its future life will first be its true life. The animal is almost half of lifelessness. This proof is also not wholly strict. For the lifelessness of matter is merely a property of the appearance, namely of the body. But whether the substance lying at the ground of the body does not also have life, we do not know, yet the proof is good against the materialists.). Now, however, there is still against this the difficulty that the body, even if it contributes nothing to life, can nevertheless be the sole condition on which life depends. Had the soul no senses and sensations, it would also have no material for thinking. If the soul were therefore separated from the body, then indeed not its vital force, but still its life, would cease. Through illness one can lose one's memory. Hence the soul may perhaps need the body for memory. Perhaps it will in the future be able to be conscious of itself without a body, for I must become conscious to myself through clear representations. These, however, rest on the body, since they are sensations. For all that, this proof has much in its favor and shows that it is not necessary that the soul must cease with the body. Besides, it also serves to renew the difficulties that are opposed to the assumption of immortality. This was, then, a proof from the particular nature of the soul. From this we shall presumably never be able to prove it with certainty, but perhaps we can prove it from the entire nature of all things according to analogy. That we now want to carry out, and so β. the proof from the analogy of the entire nature with the nature of our soul.",["The closing parenthesis after 'Materialisten.)' has no matching opening in the passage beyond the one at 'der Körper ist Hinderniß'; the German punctuation '.).' is kept as printed.","'ß.' in the OCR is read as 'β.' (the second item after an implied 'α.'); doubtful reading.","'V ermögen' read as 'Vermögen' (faculty); 'Schwürigkeiten' (sic) as 'Schwierigkeiten' (difficulties); 'wiedei' as 'wieder' ('against'); 'itzt' = 'jetzt'; 'des Vermögen' kept as 'the faculty' (sic, for 'das').","'Bewegung oder Ruhe' = motion or rest; 'Receptivitaet', 'Paßivitaet', 'Spontaneitaet' rendered receptivity, passivity, spontaneity.","'Hinderniß' = obstacle; 'Beförderung' = promotion; 'Bedingung' = condition; 'Stoff' = material; 'Empfindungen' = sensations (sensatio); 'Vorstellungen' = representations; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Eigenschaft' = property.","'Lebens Kraft' = vital force (Kraft = force); 'Seele' = soul; 'bewußt' = conscious.","'Das Thierische ist beinahe die Hälfte der Leblosigkeit' is kept literally; the sense of 'die Hälfte' is obscure.","'Beweis' = proof; 'Wesen' not occurring here; 'die dem Körper zu Grunde liegende Substanz' = 'the substance lying at the ground of the body' (Grund = ground)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-006":["We cannot do without analogy at all. Most of our inferences are analogical; namely we infer: because 2 things have in common with one another those properties that we know, they will also agree in those properties that we do not know. Admittedly deception is also possible here, and in many cases actually. Here we prove from the nature of ends, and for that reason this proof is a teleological one. For nature is physically a realm of efficient causes and teleologically a realm of ends. The principle in the realm of ends is: that everything has its end and nothing is in vain. That is a necessary postulate of reason. For otherwise it cannot explain anything to itself. But it is also confirmed by experience. (Even the atheist must assume ends, otherwise he cannot at all explain to himself the structure and organization of bodies; through the principle those learned in nature have been guided to many a useful experience. People have been able to see without the crystalline lens, and one therefore did not know what it was supposed to signify. The crystalline lens is a thicker fluid than the glassy fluid lying beneath it. Euler therefore came upon the thought that it would hinder the shadings of colors in the eye, and that if one also produced for telescopes a darker glass than the color glass, it would go thus too. Dolland contradicted him, but finally tried it, and brought forth the flint glass and the Dollandian telescopes.) We find, however, in nature that everything not only has its end, but is also determined to develop itself entirely and to reach its entire end, because it actually reaches it. The animals have instincts that are precisely appropriate to their desires. All members have their determinate use. Now there are in the human soul predispositions that in this life do not at all reach their vocation. Therefore we infer that a time would have to come in which they will reach their vocation. Now everything rests on the proof of the *minoris*, and that is the following: The human soul has 3 capacities: faculty of cognition, feeling of pleasure-displeasure and faculty of desire. As regards the first, it is not at all satisfied here. Here we find in particular that our desire for knowledge extends to things whose cognition can be of no use to us in our life, yes that we find all the greater pleasure in the cognition of things and find this cognition all the more sublime, the more distant the things are from us, e.g. stars. A human being who thinks merely of what can serve him for the preservation of his life we despise. What indeed was the use to Newton of the discovery of the laws of attraction and other philosophical investigations? all this is not at all necessary for this life. It would thus be superfluous if there were no future life, and further we also do not satisfy our desire for knowledge in this life. The striving for cognition seems, driven to a certain degree, even to be contrary to our vocation on earth. One thereby weakens one's mind, cannot provide so for one's physical happiness, not fulfill so one's animal vocation. It is just so with the feeling of pleasure or displeasure. What does it help us that we have liking in the morally beautiful, and with regard to the faculty of desire the human being feels laws in himself that drive him to the good, and which he esteems higher than his life; and we cannot even practice them properly here, for in order to practice them we would often have to risk and forfeit our life itself, for which the human being can expect no reward here. Even if he knows that no human being knows his good deeds and he is only inwardly convinced of it, this already makes him the most estimable pleasure. That would however be fantasy and chimera if he were determined only for this life. Thus all talents in this life are disproportioned. Newton, a man of such great talents, becomes hardly 60 years old, has hardly begun to discover something new, when he already dies. Had the man now lived only once as long, he would always have been able to build further on his experiences, and in time discover as much as the whole human race will not discover in 1000 years. But as it is, when a human being first properly has insight into how things are, when he properly comes to his reason, he also dies right away. To this comes still this, that it is not even advisable to develop all one's predispositions, since this often runs contrary to our physical vocation. Thus one can draw an early death upon oneself through intense studying. One harms oneself in household management. Many are thereby prevented from even marrying. This proof was invented by Fordyce, who uses in it the simile: If a higher being were to perceive in the uterus of a mother a creature that has eye, ears and other members, it would infer thus: this creature is determined for a life where these members could be applied. — This proof is especially excellent because we here infer from a general law of nature. For according to it all human beings are immortal. The previous proof went still further, for it also proved the immortality of the souls of animals. This one, however, does not prove that. But those also have not so much interest for us. But whether the soul of the human being will live eternally, that I do not yet know from this proof. Who knows, do I not die just when all these predispositions have developed? Supposing that I will also live ever so long, if I nevertheless cease once, I would rather wish to have ceased sooner. Now comes the moral proof. All moral arguments abstract from the nature of the soul, but they do not deny immateriality. Thereby, however, that they do not build the proofs on immateriality, they are more understandable to the common man than the others. This proof is either a moral theological or a theologico-moral one, in this one we lay the moral properties of the highest being at the basis. It rests on God's goodness and justice, but is very weak. One says: virtue is so little rewarded here in the world, and vice so little punished. If God is just, then a future life is to be hoped for, where this disproportion is canceled. But if we could only assess the virtuous one quite sharply, his vices will certainly amount to more than his virtue. (Who knows the innermost of every human being, in order to know whether his vicious actions are not already punished here and the virtuous ones rewarded? And then it is presumption to want to judge about God's justice and goodness) If we however also admit a future retribution, it does not follow at all that it will be eternal. Can we now demand an eternal reward for our few virtues? and if we then also wished to have some myriads of years, which then passed — what would that help us? Finally this proof also shows only that the future life will be particular. For children, imbeciles and quite stupid ones, — peoples whose life has neither moral worth nor unworth, are according to this proof excluded from it, at least not comprehended therein along with it. One has still a proof from the wisdom of God, that this namely would suffer great offense if rational beings were not to continue after the alteration of the world, so that they regard the proof of the divine goodness as an entire source. There would otherwise be nothing but fragments; the universe would be in vain if rational beings were not to intuit the whole. But there can indeed be other rational beings whom God has determined for that, for it does not follow at all from all. Now there remains to us still the moral theological proof. This is grounded on the moral laws, which are as it were geometrically necessary. Morality would be without incentives if there were no immortality of the soul. Only in the idea would it have a force without belief in immortality, but not in reality. Since therefore reality is lacking to morality, the hope of immortality cannot be separated from it. Accordingly human beings cannot without it take any resolution to good actions, and the hope and the belief in an immortality is a practical postulate of reason. (One makes an objection against the continuation of the soul, that with birth it is such a contingent something. How many millions of germs are not suffocated or die in the earliest age? A regent can multiply his subjects like a farmer his hens, if he promotes all branches of sustenance. How should now so contingent a thing as the origin of the human being be determined for immortality? Answer: The soul must already have existed beforehand, although birth is the first beginning of its life in the world of the senses. The metempsychosis of the Indians is yet a special idea, namely that the soul was beforehand a pure spirit (Dewa), which for testing is combined with the human body, afterwards passes into other animals, until it becomes a human being again, where it is then entirely holy, which is the Lamaic rebirth, and then it is swallowed up in the abyss of all spirits, i.e. God). Morality rests on the concept of freedom and is a necessary hypothesis. Although we cannot comprehend it, we must nevertheless necessarily assume it. If the actions of human beings seem to flow only from the mechanism of nature, this holds only of the appearances of them. What lies at the ground of the actions, namely the purer way of thinking of the human being, is unknown to us. Freedom in the speculative understanding we cannot comprehend. It is a practical postulate. This is distinguished from hypotheses. A hypothesis is a proposition that one assumes for the explanation of certain phenomena, which one could however well still explain through another hypothesis. A practical postulate, however, is only the single possible one that can explain certain appearances. That human beings are obligated to act honestly and to be good is an apodictic proposition. Incentives for this only immortality can give. Therefore it is a practical postulate that the soul is immortal. The analytic and moral proof for immortality are very strong, especially the latter. Although one cannot count this one among the scientific proofs, it nevertheless has strength. For I thereby bring the opponent *ad absurdum praticum*.",["OCR corrections made silently: 'Dri' read as 'Der' (the genitive with *entbehren*); 'l nsterblichkeit', 'kreiheit', 'Untertlianen', 'Menschliehen' and similar slips corrected. 'Natur Kündiger' read as *Naturkundiger* (rendered 'those learned in nature').","Fähigkeiten = 'capacities' and Vermögen = 'faculty' (Erkenntniß Vermögen, Begehrungs Vermögen); Erkenntniß = 'cognition'; Wißbegierde = 'desire for knowledge'; Begierden = 'desires'.","Bestimmung is rendered 'vocation' throughout (glossary: determination; vocation); bestimmt (ist/sind) = 'determined'. Zweke = 'ends'; Beweis = 'proof'; Triebfedern (not in glossary) = 'incentives'; Anlagen = 'predispositions'; Eigenschaften = 'properties' (the same word as for Beschaffenheit in the glossary, but a different German word).","'Saft' (in 'dickerer Saft', 'gläserne Saft') rendered 'fluid'; 'Dollandsche Fernröhre' rendered 'Dollandian telescopes' (spelling of the name as in the text). 'Haus Wesen' rendered literally as 'household management'; 'Sinnen Welt' = 'world of the senses'.","'Aber iene haben auch für uns nicht so viel Intereße': 'iene' = 'jene', rendered 'those'; the referent is left open as in the German. Likewise 'ohne sie' (after 'Unsterblichkeit' and 'Hoffnung') is rendered 'without it', and 'in diesem' ('in this one') is left open between the two named kinds of proof.","'nicht mit drin begriffen' is rendered 'not comprehended therein along with it' (begreifen = comprehend); 'verbunden sind' = 'are obligated'; 'anschauen' = 'intuit'; 'Moral ... ist eine nothwendige Hypothese' and 'Sie ist ein practisches Postulat' keep the ambiguity of their subjects (morality or freedom).","'im speculativen Verstande' rendered literally 'in the speculative understanding' (Verstand = understanding); it may also be read idiomatically as 'in the speculative respect/sense'.","Doubtful readings kept as printed: 'Der analytische und moralische Beweis' (possibly a different adjective in the original, since the earlier proof is called *analogisch*); '*ad Absurdum praticum*' (sic, for *practicum*). 'Dewa' and the names Euler, Dolland, Fordyce are kept as printed. 'minoris' is Latin and is kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-007":["How can we think the state after death? Either so that the soul will be entirely free of my body, or so that a new body will be handed over to the soul. If it is the latter, then a palingenesis takes place. Priestley and other materialists have had to assume that the present body of the soul would surround it also in my new life. It is a bold thought to compare the resurrection with the transformation of a butterfly. For if the caterpillar is burned, no chrysalis arises from it. Entire nations have differed in regard to the way in which they thought the immortality of the soul — the Romans, e.g., regarded the body as a prison of the soul and burned it to that end. The Egyptians, in turn, believed that it is a necessary *vehiculum* of the soul and sought to preserve it and to protect it from decay. — Whether the soul will fall into a sleep for a while right after death, for that we have nothing to say for it and also nothing against it. Supposing too that it is so, no human being needs to be disturbed on that account, because the brevity of this sleep is lost against an eternity. But what are we to represent to ourselves under the separation of the soul from the body? Nothing further than the beginning of the intellectual and the end of the sensible life. It is a materialistic representation if one thinks that the soul, as it were, betakes itself out of the room when the human being dies; for it has no local presence. The soul then begins to intuit the things differently from how it has been accustomed to in the connection with the body. Even now we find ourselves in the intelligible world, and every human being can, according to the constitution of his way of thinking, count himself either in the society of the blessed or of the damned. Only he is not now conscious of it, and after death he will become conscious of this society. The human being thus does not first come into heaven or hell, but only sees himself in it. This is a splendid representation. Where are heaven and hell? If we ask about the place, then that is sensible, and the soul made into a body. Heaven is the realm of the one who rewards and hell is the realm of the punishing judge. Through reason we are even now conscious of ourselves as being in an intelligible realm; after death we will intuit and cognize that, and then we are in an entirely different world, which, however, is altered only according to the form, where, namely, we cognize the things as they are in themselves. The opinion of Leibniz, that the soul already here has a *vehiculum* of matter, which is indestructible, and also will have it in the future, is sensible and explains nothing. —",["OCR: 'hndt' read as 'findet'; 'deslntellectuellen' as 'des Intellectuellen'.","'meinem Körper' and 'meinem neuen Leben' kept as printed (my); the first-person possessive is odd in context and the reading may be doubtful.","'sie' in 'umgeben würde' could refer to Seele or to Körper; 'it' keeps this open.","'Die Römer sahen ... an' and 'Denkungs Art': Art rendered 'way' in 'way of thinking' and 'the way in which'.","Ob ... da haben wir nichts dafür: the construction is loose in the German and is kept as it stands.","Vorstellung = representation; vorzustellen rendered 'represent to ourselves'; anschauen = intuit; erkennen = cognize; erklären = explain; sinnlich = sensible."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-008":["End of psychology",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-009":["From another version",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-010":[". . Kaestner gave this up and said: Whoever wants to prove the *lex continui* must prove this proposition. Otherwise it would be, if one came immediately from one direction to the other, a leap. Only in a curved line can it change its direction continuously. Hence Newton also says that the rays of light fall on the mirror in a curved line and are also repelled by it in this way, although the angles x = y are equal).",["OCR: 'eontinui' read as 'continui'. The closing parenthesis has no opening one in the passage (the text begins mid-sentence).","'gab dies auf' could be 'gave this up' or 'set this as a problem' (aufgeben); the English keeps the first, ambiguity remains.","'sie' (in 'kann sie ihre Richtung') has no clear antecedent in the passage; rendered 'it'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-011":["This law of nature one can also apply to the species and genera of things (but how far this application of the proposition can go is unknown), namely when one says: no species or genus is so closely related to one another that there should not still take place between it an intermediate species or intermediate genus (The *lex continui* serves to refute the monadology; the world cannot consist of monads, for the simple is, with regard to the *Quanti*, a zero. But from zero to the *Quanto* the transition is a leap. However much of the simple I may put together, it never becomes a *Quantum*. With regard to quality the simple is indeed something positive, but with regard to quantity not. One cannot get a complete concept of the *lege continui* if one does not take the first state in which a thing is for the infinitely smallest part of the state into which a thing is still to come. So one must hold the *Zero* for the infinitely smallest part of the following state. Rest, e.g., for the infinitely smallest part of motion. One must not hold the first state, with regard to the following, for a mere 0, because 0 is entirely different from every state. We must arrive at this *lex*; for two states are never the nearest to each other, but there is still a moment between them. In the world everything is according to the *lege continui*, hence also all motion according to curved lines). This is also called the law of nature, and indeed the mechanical law of nature, since that one is called the cosmological. We find it, to be sure, because it is so constituted; but we cannot prove through reason that it must be so. Hence it does not belong at all in metaphysics. This is also called the ladder of steps of things, to which one then also counted God, so that he would thus have a merely infinitely higher degree of perfection than the creatures. Voltaire mocks at this, in that he represents it as a great procession, where God, the Pope, the angels, the cardinals etc. would be. It is also void to include God in this ladder of steps, for he is indeed entirely specifically distinguished from the creatures. He is *causa* of everything, the creature is *causatum*; that would be just as if I, in order to describe a surface, continued a straight line unceasingly. It would nevertheless in eternity not become a surface.",["OCR: 'eontinui' read as 'continui'; 'caußa/caußatum' as *causa*/*causatum*; 'Qualitaet/Quantitaet' as quality/quantity.","*Quanti*, *Quanto*, *Quantum*: Latin case forms kept as in the German; 'Zero' and 'lex'/'lege' likewise kept (the German writes 'das lex').","Art/Gattung = species/genus; 'Zwischen Art' rendered 'intermediate species'. Stufenleiter rendered 'ladder of steps' (not in the glossary).","'es' in 'weil es so beschaffen ist' has no explicit antecedent; kept as 'it'.","'Dieses' in 'Dieses heißt auch das Gesetz der Natur' and 'ienes' (that one): antecedents left unresolved.","Fläche = surface; z. E. = 'e.g.' (zum Exempel)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-012":["II.) *In mundo non datur hiatus*: There is in the world no empty space and no empty time. This proposition belongs under the category of magnitude. Two kinds of empty spaces are possible",["The passage breaks off without punctuation; it continues in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-013":["1 An empty space outside the world or *vacuum extra mundanum*, which encloses it. And that is nothing. For the world of sense has no boundaries, at least we cannot determine them through any possible experience. Hence we also cannot have insight into the empty space outside the world at all, because it is no object of our experience at all and nothing real. Of this empty space, however, there is also no talk here, but rather",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence ('sondern'); not completed.","'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of sense'; 'einsehen' rendered 'have insight into' (not in the glossary); 'reelles' rendered 'real' (Realität = reality)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-014":["2 On the empty space in the world or the *vacuum intermissum vel concretum*. Of this too no experience is possible for us. Through a *hiatus* furthermore two things in the world would also be separated from their *nexus*, and that is not permissible, since the world stands in thoroughgoing *nexus*. Besides, a *hiatus* would at the same time also be a *saltus*; and that this latter does not take place has already been proved before. An empty time, in which nothing elapsed, would be a *saltus*. An empty space is also a *saltus*. For if a body only moved in an empty space, then neither in it itself nor outside it would anything be altered. Thus no alteration at all would happen, [boiglich] on the preceding alteration another would follow immediately, all at once, without this one attaining the degree through a gradual increase in the intervening time. That would however be a *saltus*, e.g. if a body falls through a hollow sphere, and this has an empty space, then the time that it spent in falling through would be an empty time. For neither would it be altered in itself, nor anything outside it, since then nothing would be there outside it. Through its motion however it would not be altered at all. The empty time however between 2 states is a *saltus*.",["OCR 'boiglich' is too garbled to read with certainty (perhaps 'folglich', 'consequently'); left in brackets untranslated.","Latin kept in italics: *vacuum intermissum vel concretum*, *hiatus*, *nexus*, *saltus*; the German 'Saltus' at the end is rendered likewise.","'Veränderung' = alteration; 'Zustand' = state; 'Grad' = degree; 'Zeit' = time; 'Raum' = space; 'Erfahrung' = experience.","'in ihm selbst' / 'an sich verändert': 'in it itself' and 'in itself' follow the German; 'an sich' is glossed 'in itself'.","'Es würde denn also' kept close to the German ('Thus ... would'); the 'denn' is ambiguous between 'then' and 'thus'.","'auf einmal' rendered 'all at once'; 'allmähliges Zunehmen' = 'gradual increase'. 'Zwischen Zeit' read as 'intervening time'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-015":["III. *In mundo non datur casus.* *Casus est eventus in mundo non determinatus secundum leges naturales.* A contingency is the arising of an occurrence without cause and sufficient ground. This runs again against the *principium rationis sufficientis*, without which no experience is possible. The world is a sum total of appearances. In the world everything happens according to laws of nature. The actions of human beings too happen according to laws of nature, although the will of them does not stand under laws of nature. Between natural necessity and contingency there is a middle, namely: freedom. This can indeed also be contrary to causes of what happens, but contingency not at all. For from contingency I cannot explain the least thing. The proposition: *In mundo non datur casus*, holds only of occurrences — thus merely of the world of the senses.",["'Zufall' is rendered 'contingency' here, following the glossary's 'zufällig' = contingent; the Latin gloss is *casus*.","'Begebenheit' = occurrence; Latin *eventus* is rendered 'event' in the Latin sentence only by the Latin itself, which is left untranslated (cf. German 'Ereignis' = event).","'Summe' rendered 'sum total' (glossary: Inbegriff); the German word here is 'Summe'.","'Sinnen Welt' = 'world of the senses'; 'Natur Gesetzen' = 'laws of nature'.","'Diese kann wohl Ursachen ... zuwieder sein' is syntactically ambiguous: 'This' (freedom) 'can indeed also be contrary to causes of what happens', or 'be contrary to causes'; preserved as the German stands.","'der Wille derselben': 'the will of them' preserves the ambiguity of 'derselben' (the actions, or human beings)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-016":["One also calls chance a blind happenstance, and that for this reason: A thing through which one cannot see one sometimes calls blind, e.g., blind glass. If we now assume a chance as the cause of the occurrences, we cannot at all see why and to what end the occurrence happened, since a sufficient ground is lacking to us (In the world everything happens according to universal constant rules. That holds only of the world of sense. For happening presupposes a time, consequently nothing happens in the *mundo noumeno*, hence the *principium rationis sufficientis* holds only of *Phaenomenis*. — For world of sense is objectively the sum total of all possible experiences, and through *ratione sufficientis* experience is first possible. Cause is that upon which an occurrence follows according to a necessary rule. In the world of sense the causality of an occurrence is again an occurrence, for in the world of sense everything happens. Hence here in the series of conditions there is no totality. The causality of an occurrence is also again an occurrence. For if it had been causality at all times, the occurrence would have been at all times. That contradicts, however, the concept of occurrence, which designates the arising of a thing, thus presupposes a time when the thing was not. The causality has therefore also arisen, hence is also an occurrence. A whole series of such occurrences must also contain an unconditioned: For if the series is whole, it must be unconditioned. Therefore there must be an unconditioned first cause. But if I say: God gave the world a beginning, then he was the causality of the same, and since I thus connect him with time, his causality was again an occurrence. In order to get out of the difficulty, I must think that God is considered as a thing in itself. In such a one the causality is no occurrence. The arrangement that everything in the world happens according to constant rules, one can call the mechanism of nature. Mechanism of a thing otherwise means the arrangement of a thing according to laws of motion, but more generally it can signify the arrangement according to all sorts of kinds of laws. In the world of sense, therefore, everything goes according to the mechanism of nature, according to natural necessity. Opposed to this is happenstance, *Casus*. Between the two now is the middle — freedom. Now one should think that in the world of sense there is not freedom; but it is nevertheless. — If I namely think to myself a thing in itself, then this is indeed a *substratum* of appearances and insofar, as appearance, it belongs under the mechanism of nature, but as thing in itself not. Such a noumenon therefore acts indeed as appearance according to the mechanism of nature, its actions do not happen so, but according to its will and not through the mechanism of nature. Its causality of an occurrence is not again an occurrence, for it is no appearance. My reason is a faculty to cognize the things as they are, hence I must surely also be a thing in itself. All my actions happen according to the mechanism of nature etc., insofar as they have their ground in the other, but not through the mechanism of nature, but I have the faculty to determine myself through reason according to objective grounds, independently of all external circumstances, *stimulis* etc. Insofar I have freedom. The difficulty with freedom lies therefore merely in this, that one takes appearances for things in themselves. For those cannot help us out, since with appearances everything happens according to the mechanism of nature. Since the soul is also a thing in itself, it is free. For there its causality is not again occurrence, but it acts there according to its own motives, which it takes from itself. As thing in itself it brings forth its actions not at all in the time-sequence, but so they only appear to us. Thus the antinomy, that in the world freedom and necessity be, is removed, in that it is shown that these are not actual *opposita*, in that they go to different objects, namely the former to the *mundus Noumenon* and the other to the *mundus Phaenomenon*, and hence both could take place at the same time. So it is also with the following antinomy: Whether in the world everything, both conditioned and unconditioned, is contingent, or whether something necessary is therein. The former holds of the *mundo Phaenomenon*. For in space and time there is nothing necessary, because everything that has a cause is occurrence. But in the *mundo Noumenon* the cause can be necessary, and of that the latter therefore holds. — If a world is, then something necessary must exist, neither as cause nor as part. Both propositions are true, however contradictory they may seem to be. If we consider the world as the content of the appearance, then nothing is necessary, because in the world of sense there is no totality. But if we regard the world as sum total of the things themselves, then something must be necessary. With appearances nothing is necessary. For if there were a being that had brought forth the appearance, it would have had to create this also in time. Accordingly there would have to be a time when God had not yet created any appearance. He would accordingly be himself an occurrence, himself an appearance, and would accordingly have his ground in another occurrence. We escape this contradiction if we consider God as thing in itself, whose effects in the world of sense happen indeed according to the mechanism, but not through the same, but through freedom. *In mundo non datur casus*, this proposition therefore signifies *positive* so much as that everything in the world happens necessarily according to certain rules. In the world there is therefore in itself no chance, but we can call the one a comparative chance, which indeed has its ground, which we however cannot see, and of such chances there are enough. Such a comparative chance is called a good fortune or misfortune. Then the cause of our well-being rests not on our actions, but on other circumstances.",["Zufall = \"chance\" (not 'contingency'), to keep it apart from zufällig = \"contingent\"; Ohngefähr = \"happenstance\"; the *Casus* is left in Latin.","Begebenheit = \"occurrence\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Sinnen Welt = \"world of sense\"; Bewegungs Gründen = \"motives\" (Bewegungsgrund); Wesen = \"being\"; Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Zeitfolge = \"time-sequence\". einsehen is rendered \"see\".","\"Escheinung\" read as Erscheinung and \"Gliik/Unglük\" as Glück/Unglück (OCR/transcript slips). The sentence \"Ein solches Noumenon handelt also zwar … seine Handlungen geschehen nicht so, sondern …\" has a zwar with no aber and is left as it stands.","\"in der andern ihren Grund haben\": the referent of \"der andern\" (feminine) is unclear and is left as \"the other\". \"weder als Ursache noch als Theil\" is kept as in the German.","\"wir können den einen comparativen Zufall nennen\": \"den einen\" is ambiguous (\"the one\" / possibly \"den, einen\", i.e. \"that which … a comparative chance\"); the ambiguity is kept.","\"wirkliche Opposita\": wirklich is rendered \"actual\"; \"gehen auf\" is rendered \"go to\"; \"positive\" is printed in Latin form and italicized.","The long parenthesis opened after \"fehlt (\" is never closed in the text as given; it is left unclosed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-017":["If I assume a blind happenstance, then I have no trouble spying after the grounds of the occurrences, and this proposition is, like the following, an emergency aid of lazy reason. IV. *In mundo non datur fatum*. Fates are deviations from maxims. These are principles that are taken from the subject, and *Principien* principles, that are taken from the object. They are speculative or practical. Those demand that which furthers the interest of my reason. Fates conflict with the interest of reason. For if I assume them, I must renounce the use of reason; also against the practical maxims, which are in combination with the will, is fate, e.g., Mohammedanism, that life depends on blind fate, that for each human being, even before his birth, it has been determined how long he is to live.",["\"Schicksahle\" read as Schicksale; \"jiractischen\" read as practischen (OCR slips). \"wieder\" in \"auch wieder die … Maximen\" taken as wider (\"against\"); this is a silent normalization.","Grundsatz = \"principle\"; \"Principien Grundsätze\" is kept awkwardly, with *Principien* left in the original. \"Jene\" (\"Those\") has no clear antecedent and is left so.","Gebrauch = \"use\"; Verbindung = \"combination\"; Nothhilfe rendered \"emergency aid\"; Intereße = \"interest\"; Mohomedanismus rendered \"Mohammedanism\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-018":["*Fatum* signifies something once pronounced. Fate is a blind necessity without law. Natural necessity, however, is according to laws; according to this the occurrences in the world happen. Blind necessity is the *contrarium* of contingency. But of the fact that something should nevertheless be necessary without any grounds and causes, we have not the slightest concept. For we find nothing at all contradictory in it, that something would also not have happened. To want to explain something from fate is contrary to sense, for that I appeal to fate means precisely that I cannot explain something. If we do something, insofar as it stems from physical causes, then we must explain that from the laws of nature and not from spontaneity, otherwise we would arrive at intelligible grounds, which belong to the *mundus noumenon*; and that would be *metabasis eis allo genos*, etc.; that takes place only in moral relations. To the *mundus noumenon* belong the *intelligentiae* (beings of understanding and free beings, and so also the human being.) The author begins here to speak of the *ens extramundanum*. If this world is a contingent being, then it is *caußatum alterius*. But that it is contingent comes from this: *mundus noumenon* is a *totum* of substances, which stand in *commercio* with one another. By means of this *commercium* the substances depend reciprocally on one another. The substances therefore have no necessary existence. For absolute necessary existence presupposes an independent existence. But the world does not have that. Accordingly it is dependent on another being. This being does not belong to the world, for otherwise it would be at once cause, in that it would have produced the world, but also at once effect, in that it at once belonged to the world; but that is not possible at all. For cause and effect must be distinguished. Accordingly the world too, as effect, must be distinguished from its cause. Therefore the *caußa mundi* is an *ens extra mundanum*.",["OCR \"peraßaaK ; etc ; aXAo yevoc;\" is garbled Greek; read as μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος and given in transliteration. \"V irkung\" read as Wirkung.","Zufall = contingency, zufällig = contingent; Begebenheit = occurrence; Dasein = existence; Gesetz = law; Grund = ground; Ursache = cause.","\"wiedersinnisch\" is rendered \"contrary to sense\"; \"das geht gar nicht an\" is rendered \"that is not possible at all\" (literal sense uncertain).","\"Spontaneitaet\" and \"caußatum\" are printed in the German-Latin spelling of the transcript; \"Totum\" is lower-cased in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-019":["*Caußa mundi est ens super mundanum*, since the *caußa* is nobler than the *effectus*, in that in it lies causality of still more and other *effectuum*.",["\"caußalitaet\" (German text) is rendered \"causality\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-020":["What is necessary can subsist for itself or is isolated; the *ens extra mundanum* is therefore also isolated. It can indeed act into other substances, but other substances cannot act back. The world of sense is certainly contingent, for the appearances are indeed in themselves nothing. The nearest cause of the world of sense is *mundus noumenon*, which we now do not know, but into which we can come after our death as into an entirely different world. —",["\"Sinnen Welt\" = world of sense (Sinn = sense); \"kennen\" = know; Erscheinungen = appearances."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-021":["Second Section.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-001":["On the general parts or Monadology. First Chapter On the general simple parts",["Theile = parts; einfach = simple."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-002":["Egoism is when someone asserts that there is nothing present outside him, but everything that we see is mere illusion; and he who asserts this is an egoist. Egoism can be dogmatic, also skeptical. Skepticism some have asserted in earnest, and that is also feasible, namely if one asserts that all grounds against it are not yet sufficient. The egoist says: In a dream I also represent a world to myself, and am in it, and nevertheless it is not so. Can it now not also be so with me when awake? But against this is that dreams do not hang together with one another, but I dream now this, now that, whereas the appearances when awake hang together according to general rules. Egoism is a mere problem, which has no ground before it at all, — but nevertheless is also very hard to prove and to refute. Through experience I cannot refute the egoist, for this teaches us *immediate* only our own existence. *Mediate* we indeed experience through the senses that other things are there; but the egoist says that in these senses lies merely the ground that we become aware of the appearances. But in themselves they would be nothing. Since one can therefore accomplish nothing on this side, one needs moral proofs, and indeed the following: The human being is limited, thus contingent. Accordingly he must have a cause. This highest cause must be unlimited, because it depends on nothing, and thus also can be limited by nothing. Therefore one cannot presume that one should be there entirely alone.",["Schein = illusion; Erscheinung = appearance; Beweis = proof; eingeschränkt/uneingeschränkt = limited/unlimited (Schranke = limitation).","\"dawieder\" rendered \"against it\"; \"hinreichend\" rendered \"sufficient\" (the glossary's zureichend is not the word used).","\"immediate\"/\"mediate\" are Latin adverbs in the German text and are italicized.","\"Zusammenhängen\" (verb) rendered \"hang together\"; the noun Zusammenhang would be \"interconnection\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-003":["Opposed to egoism is dualism (pluralism). The dualist believes in thinking beings and bodies outside himself. Altogether it is so absurd that surely no one will ever let it occur to him to maintain this error in earnest, even if it were irrefutable.",["Ambiguity: \"er\" in \"ist er so ungereimt\" could refer to the dualist or to egoism (der Egoismus); rendered \"it\", which leaves the German's masculine pronoun partly open but cannot preserve both.","\"statuiren\" (Latin statuere) rendered \"maintain\"; \"ungereimt\" rendered \"absurd\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-004":["Idealism is when one represents to oneself that outside oneself there would indeed be thinking beings, but no bodies present. This error one can refute neither from experience nor *a priori*. For it is not necessary that bodies are. Now one has here also a moral ground. Namely one says, God is the most perfect being and author of me. Consequently he also created my senses, through which I constantly see bodies. The illusion too, whereby we are persuaded to hold what is merely a sham of our senses for something, would have to come from God. But that he would deceive us is not to be presumed. This ground is however very lame, for one can rightly object against it: that we deceive ourselves if we believe that which our senses teach us. It stands indeed rather in our power to believe this or not.",["\"Illusion\" (German word, distinct from Schein) = illusion; \"beredet\" relates to Überredung = persuasion; \"Blendwerk\" rendered \"sham\".","\"hintergehen\" and \"betrügen\" are both rendered \"deceive\"; \"Gewalt\" = power (not Kraft or Vermögen).","\"vollkommenste\" = most perfect (Vollkommenheit = perfection); \"glauben\" = believe (Glaube = belief)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-005":["Idealism also has actual grounds before it and is therefore also more probable than egoism. Bishop Cloyd in Ireland namely said: If spiritual beings were in such *commercio* that their bodies were merely effects of their imagination, the world would lose nothing in its worth. For this rests properly on the thinking beings. Hence it would be useless to assume a world of bodies according to the principle: *Entia non sunt sine neceßitate multiplicanda*. Bishop Berkeley in Ireland went still further, for he asserted that bodies are even impossible, because if one assumed them, one would always contradict oneself. This is dogmatic or crude idealism, that outside us no bodies existed, but that the appearances were nothing and lay merely in our senses and our imagination. But there is also a critical or transcendental idealism, if one assumes that the appearances in themselves are indeed nothing, but yet something unknown really lies at the ground. That is correct.",["\"Cloyd\" is the transcript's spelling (Cloyne); kept as printed. \"neceßitate\" kept as printed. OCR \"Körpeiwelt\" read as Körperwelt.","wirklich = actual/actually (Wirklichkeit); Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Einbildungskraft = imagination; Grundsatz = principle.","\"zum Grunde liegt\" rendered \"lies at the ground\"; \"Richtigkeit\" rendered \"correct\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-006":["Materialism is when one assumes that everything is material. Many assume this hypothesis in complete earnest. Between matter and material there is a difference. Material is that which is or can be a *pars constitutiva* of a matter, and matter is the *extensum* that consists of such *partibus*, or an impenetrable whole. He who therefore asserts: matter consists of simple parts or monads, asserts also that these parts are, to be sure, not matter, but *Materialia*. If I therefore say: the soul is simple, I cannot yet prove from this that it is immaterial, and distinguish material from immaterial monads. No, if the soul is simple, then it can also be material, but for that reason it does not yet need to be matter. If the monads of bodies are also actual, then they must, as well as the soul, have a representation, which Leibnitz also asserts. The soul would then also be such a monad, hence material. If bodies therefore consisted of simple parts, the materialist would not be wrong. The grounds from which Leibnitz wanted to derive the existence of the monads and their force of representation are these: World, he says, is a whole of simple parts or *Monadatum*, for what is composite presupposes something simple from which it is composed. There are therefore monads. But what forces do these now have? All the forces that we know in things go merely to outer relations, e.g. moving force is merely alteration of place, impenetrability aims at persistence in place. These inner realities must nevertheless be, even if we do not know them. They must be the causes of the outer ones. But we can become aware of these inner forces in no thing other than in our soul. For we cannot perceive them through the outer sense, but merely through the inner. And there we find that their inner force is thinking, from which willing, pleasure and displeasure arise. Since now the monads are also simple like the soul, they can also have *vires repraesentatives*, and their influence can be merely *modificatio virium repraesentativarum*. (They can also have a *facultatem appetitivam* that is in accordance with the *repraesentativae*.) Accordingly the monads have force of representation of all parts of the world by which they are affected. But because they are not alone, but are always combined with other monads, they are thereby hindered in their force of representation and therefore have obscure representations, are *sopitae*, slumbering monads. Thereby they are distinguished from the soul. Leibnitz here actually understood merely *mundum Noumenon*, only he did not express himself quite distinctly.",["Kraft is rendered \"force\" throughout (Vorstellungskraft = \"force of representation\"; bewegende Kraft = \"moving force\"); Vorstellung = representation; Theil = part; einfach = simple; Dasein = existence.","\"Monadatum\" is printed thus in the transcript (apparently a genitive plural); left as it stands. \"vires repraesentatives\" is the transcript's spelling.","\"ihre innere Kraft\" (\"their inner force\"): the antecedent of \"ihre\" is not made explicit in the German; left as ambiguous as there.","\"ob wir sie gleich nicht kennen\": kennen = know (acquaintance); \"wirklich\" in \"auch wirklich sind\" and \"auch wirklich bloß\" rendered \"actual\" and \"actually\".","Spelling \"Leibnitz\" kept as in the source. \"material\" (adjective) and \"Materie\" (matter) are kept distinct as the text itself distinguishes them."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-007":["That is now the famous monad doctrine of Leibnitz. But it is a mere figment of the brain. For Leibnitz has here committed the error of confusing *Mundum Noumenon* and *Phaenomenon* with one another, for it holds only of the *Mundo Noumeno* that bodies consist of monads. But of these we know nothing but the mere name. The nature of them, however, we do not know. (With regard to the *Mundi Noumenon* bodies consist of simple parts. For if I remove the composition of the *compositi substantiale*, the parts nevertheless remain. Since these are then not composite, they must be simple. With the *Mundus Phaenomenon*, however, it is otherwise. If I here remove the composition, nothing remains to me. For space and time are here the essential of the composition; without these no thing can appear to me.)",["Hirngespinst rendered \"figment of the brain\" (literally brain-spinning). Lehre = doctrine.","wissen = know (knowledge); kennen = know (acquaintance); both appear in \"wir wissen nichts\" / \"kennen wir nicht\".","The Latin case forms (*Mundum*, *Mundo*, *Mundi* Noumenon/Noumeno, *compositi substantiale*) are kept as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-008":["With regard to the *Mundi Phaenomenon*, however, it is obviously false. For all these inferences, those of Leibnitz as well as those of the materialists from this proposition of Leibnitz, fall over in a heap through the following proposition: matter, or rather the appearances of it in the world of sense, do not consist of simple parts, for 1stly no part of space is simple, 2ndly, of as many parts as the space consists which the body occupies, of so many parts the body too must consist. Hence the body is divisible into the infinite, because space is divisible into the infinite. The bodies themselves can perhaps consist of simple parts, but not the appearances, for they are not the bodies themselves. Now I can say: thinking beings are simple. But matter is not simple, nor is the material. Accordingly the souls are immaterial. The *Impenetrabilitaet* belongs to physics.",["\"übern Haufen fallen\" rendered literally as \"fall over in a heap\" (an idiom for being overthrown).","OCR \"lstens\" read as \"1stens\" (\"1stly\"); \"Sinnen Welt\" = \"world of sense\" (Sinn = sense).","\"Physic\" in the source rendered \"physics\"; \"Impenetrabilitaet\" left in the transcript's form, italicized.","\"ins Unendliche\" rendered literally \"into the infinite\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-009":["Leibnitz and the author distinguish mathematical and physical points. These are the monads, those the places in space. They say: all monads are simple, hence not in space, otherwise they would be divisible. Hence they are points that are indivisible. But space now does not consist of points. Hence that which is in space also does not consist of points.",["OCR \"I unkte\" read as \"Punkte\"; \"dei\" as \"der\"; \"iene\" as \"jene\".","Oerter = places (Ort = place)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-001":["2nd chapter. On the first origin of bodies.",["\"2tes Capitel\" rendered \"2nd chapter\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-002":["The author now speaks of the first possibility of the generation of bodies and here proves various propositions *a priori*. (Here what is understood is not the physical but the metaphysical possibility. The author explains the possibility of a body as of a *monadati* — the alteration of space is always reciprocal. Of a ship that goes to Pillau, I say: it moves there. But I can just as well say: Pillau moves toward the ship always nearer than the ship to it. That can be strictly proved, and from this I can also prove the possibility of the *commercium* *a priori*. The author seeks to explain the possibility of motion from the *vi repraesentativa*. But it is absolutely, impossible to prove that that which is an object of inner sense can be a cause of that which is an object of outer sense.) (No concept of motion can be given *a priori*, because something empirical necessarily lies at the basis of it. Material is either that which is matter or that which is at least *pars constitutiva materiae*. Those who make the human soul into a monad cannot have the difficulty that follows from many souls making up a body; our elements are the basic parts of the body. *Elementa qualitativa* are those basic parts that can no longer be resolved into elements of a different kind. *Elementa quantitativa* are those basic parts that cannot be divided at all any more; they are called *Atomi*.)",["\"reciproque\" in the source (Latin/French form) rendered \"reciprocal\"; \"Commercii\" in the German is genitive of *commercium*, rendered here as *commercium* in the English.","\"schlechterdings, unmöglich\": the comma is in the source and is kept.","OCR \"hegt\" read as \"liegt\"; \"Dieienigen\" as \"Diejenigen\". \"Grundtheile\" rendered \"basic parts\"; Art = kind.","\"monadati\" is as printed (apparently a Latin genitive/dative form); left as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-003":["The proposition that substances flow into one another reciprocally through *actio* and *reactio* is, indeed, right. But it can be proved only in physics from experiences, by no means however *a priori* in metaphysics. The expressions *bellum omnium contra omnia* etc. are unsuitable. It signifies only harmony, e.g. the coachman acts on the horses, and the horses act on the wagon and pull it along. But *reactio* is an opposed effect, a resistance. The author wants to prove this here *a priori* from the *nexus* of substances, but from this only the *commercium* flows. But the author errs in taking *reactio* and *Impediment* for receptivity.",["\"wol recht\": wol = wohl, which may be \"indeed\" or \"probably\"; I give \"indeed\" and the other reading is possible.","\"Actio, und Reactio\": comma kept in the source form; \"Nexu\" is ablative/dative of *nexus*, shown as *nexus*; \"reciptivitaet\" (sic) read as \"receptivity\".","\"einfließen\" rendered \"flow into\"; \"Impediment\" kept in the source's form, italicized."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-004":["Now he wants to prove also *a priori* that the monads hang together. But how can I have insight *a priori* that substance has a force? Contact is only a kind of immediate effect and counter-effect by virtue of *Impenentrabilitaet*. But this is a physical concept.",["\"Zusammenhängen\" is the verb (\"hang together\"), related to Zusammenhang (interconnection).","\"Impenentrabilitaet\" (sic) is the transcript's spelling of *impenetrabilitas*; Art = kind; Kraft = force."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-005":["Moreover attraction is entirely different, since one body acts on the other in such a way that the other wants to penetrate into the space in which the acting one is. The [?] in contact does not belong here, but pertains to matter and rests on impenetrability. This we can prove only *a posteriori*. Immediate effect is a concept of the understanding. The author has therefore proved it quite badly.",["\"Der bei der Berührung gehört nicht hierher\" is garbled or incomplete in the OCR/source (\"Der\" has no clear referent); I mark the gap with [?] rather than supply a word.","OCR \"V erstandes\" read as \"Verstandes\"; Verstand = understanding; Undurchdringlichkeit = impenetrability."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-006":["An *atomus* (*indivisibile*) is either *absolute* or *mathematice talis*, which no longer consists of any parts, but is simple; or *physice talis*, which can be divided further by no chemistry, although it still consists of parts nonetheless.",["The German has 'welches' after the masculine 'Atomus' and then 'welcher'; the gender shift is kept. 'einfach' = simple."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-007":["*Atomus absolute talis* would be a monad, and that is not to be assumed *Atomus physicus* is however assumed by all physicists. The figure, they say, makes up the *differentia specifica* and can never be dissolved. For if they were dissolved into monads, then their figure too would have to cease; consequently there would no longer be any difference among them and all manifoldness in the things in the world would cease. But now we see that gold remains gold, even if it is dissolved into the most subtle parts.",["No stop after 'anzunehmen' in the German; kept as printed.","'Mannigfaltigkeit' is rendered 'manifoldness' (cf. 'Mannigfaltige' = manifold).","'Differentiam specificam' is an accusative in the German; given in the nominative in italics.","'Physicern' = physicists (OCR reading taken as it stands)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-008":["The principles, that assumes the *atomos physicos* to be the first ground-parts, is *philosophia pigrorum*.",["'Prmcipien' read as 'Principien' (OCR). Plural noun with singular verb ('annimmt', 'ist') and 'die' as possible relative or demonstrative; the grammar is left as it stands and may reflect a transcription slip.","'Grund Theilen' = 'ground-parts'; 'Theil' = part."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-009":["The one that explains the *phaenomena* from *corpuscula* (invisible parts) is *philosophia corpuscularis* (*Corpuscula* are *atomi physici*. According to Cartesius's hypothesis they are indivisible and make up the difference of matter) (With him the *atomi* are also the ground-material of body. Body is that whose limitations of the space of the matter are determined.). Gassendi, Cartesius etc. etc. assume these. The *atomistica* of Epicurus is the same as this, except that he assumed in addition a *vacuum* or *inane*.",["'Die die' = 'the one that' (the subject is not specified in the German).","'Grund Stoff' = 'ground-material' (Grund = ground; Stoff = material).","'Schranken des Raums der Materie bestimmt sind' kept in its German order; 'Schranke' = limitation.","'erklart' read as 'erklärt' (OCR)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-010":["The one who explains the appearances from monads is *atomista metaphysicus*, and the one who explains them from the *corpuscula* or *atomis physicis* is *atomista physicus*.",["'Erscheinungen' = appearances. 'atomis physicis' is the ablative as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-011":["The ancients called *ignava ratio* that, when one goes straightaway from the *ratio proxima* *ad remotissimam*, without touching the *rationes intermediae*. When one refers the explanation of all occurrences straightaway to God, without tracking down the nearest natural causes.",["The second sentence is a fragment in the German, with no main clause; kept so.","'remotißimam' read as 'remotissimam'; 'ratione', 'rationes' put into the nominative for the italicized terms.","'Begebenheit' = occurrence; 'Erklärung' = explanation."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-001":["Third Chapter. On the Nature of Bodies",["'Koerper' read as 'Körper'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-002":["The objective principles are the general grounds of reason why the things should happen (nature is the existence of the thing, insofar as it is inwardly determined according to general laws.) We cannot have insight into how a single created being can cognize the things in themselves. We would not cognize the things if we did not cognize them through the action. Those are the dynamical principles of nature. The ground of the determinability of the appearance must lie in the understanding, also the ground of the determinability of the essence. According to their essence they are determined in space and time. The understanding is the faculty of rules; without this there is no experience. We must therefore send ahead laws of nature before experience, which the understanding gives. Space and time make the appearance and so also the laws of nature of experience possible. — Our understanding therefore prescribes laws to nature. But one says: but where will the things conform to my understanding? The appearances cannot become experiences if they are not in accord with the understanding. That signifies this merely. These are therefore called the highest dynamical principles. — The understanding does not prescribe all laws of nature, or have insight into them, but merely those that belong to the possibility of experience. 1. Only substance persists, and the states change. 2. What happens has a cause. 3. In all appearances there is no effect without counter-effect). Nature is now the first general inner objective principle of everything that belongs to the existence of the causality of a thing. Thereby it is distinguished from essence, which is the inner principle of the possibility of a thing. Nature is the first principle, and thus subalternated to no other, e.g. the ground of inventions is reflection. This comes again from the understanding; this again from consciousness, and this is nature.",["'Wesen' is 'being' in 'erschaffenes Wesen' and 'essence' in the other occurrences (Wesen = being; essence).","'Principien' (here 'principles') and 'Grundsätze' (also 'principles') are both rendered 'principles'; 'Grundsätze' occurs in 'dynamischen Grundsätze'.","'Gesetze' = laws; 'allgemein' = general; 'Vernunft Gründe' = grounds of reason.","'einsehen' = have insight into (both occurrences). 'Nachdenken' is rendered 'reflection' (not 'Überlegung').","The closing parenthesis after '3.' has no opening parenthesis in the German; kept. The opening parenthesis after 'Gesetzen' is closed after the full stop, as printed.","'die der Verstand giebt' is ambiguous in its antecedent; left so. 'Das bedeutet dieses bloß' kept in German order.","'Caußalitaet' = causality; 'subalternirt' = subalternated."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-003":["Nature is further a general principle, from which I can explain all things. Finally it is also an inner principle, which is encountered in the things themselves. Thereby it is distinguished from a miracle. This is also a principle, but an outer one, e.g. if a sick person becomes healthy through his nature, then that comes from himself, from the structure of his body. But if he is cured through a miracle, then that comes from another cause outside him.",["'Wunder' = miracle; 'Bau' = structure."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-004":["Nature is finally the principle of the existence of things. Thereby it is precisely distinguished from possibility. Even acute men have left this distinction out of account, as e.g. Reimarus explains nature through essential force, which is false. For forces belong to nature, because actuality belongs to nature. But if a substance is actual, then it has, insofar as it can be the ground of something else, force.",["'Wirklichkeit' = actuality; 'wirklich' = actual; 'Dasein' = existence.","'welches falsch ist' could refer to the explanation or to 'essential force'; left as in German. 'wesentliche' = essential."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-005":["But formal conditions of outer forces also belong to nature. E.g. a wedge must, because it is to split wood, be pointed. This is the formal in the force and belongs as well to the nature of the wedge. What is only possible has no nature, e.g. a triangle. The definition of Reimarus of nature is therefore 1. incomplete, for not merely forces belong to nature, but also the combination of them with one another. Further it is 2. confused with essence.",["'Erklärung' is rendered 'definition' here (glossary: explanation; definition; declaration), whereas 'erklären' is 'explain' elsewhere.","'Verbindung' = combination; 'Wesen' = essence here."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-006":["All objects of experience have their nature, for without this no experience is possible. Experience is not an aggregate of perceptions, but a whole of perceptions connected according to a principle. Consequently there must be in every thing a principle according to which the perceptions are connected and this is — -nature.",["'Obiecte' = objects (Object); 'Wahrnehmungen' = perceptions; 'verknüpft' = connected (Verknüpfung = connection).","The dash and hyphen before 'Natur' are kept as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-007":["Nature can be taken in 2 kinds of meaning. First *substantive* or *materialiter*. This is called simply the nature of all things. It is the whole of all natures, the unity of all connected nature, in short, the general nature of all things, or that which all things have in common with one another.",["'2erlei Bedeutung' = '2 kinds of meaning'; 'Bedeutung' = meaning."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-008":["2 nature is also used as the particular nature of each thing, or *adjective vel formaliter*. Each thing has its own nature, according to which it is distinguished from others, e.g. the nature of body is extension and force of motion. The nature of the soul is thinking and willing etc.",["'2 wird Natur auch gebraucht' is kept in German order (the numeral 2 stands as printed). 'Bewegungs Kraft' = 'force of motion'; 'besondere' = particular."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-009":["Finally one also calls nature, and indeed for the most part, the order of things according to certain general laws.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-010":["It is reasonable to explain everything that happens in the world according to these laws; but one also must not do that everywhere, where it is not at all feasible. This is just as great an error as if we assume unknown forces as the ground of cognition. For then I have properly explained nothing. One often uses nature as a person or as a form that is no substance, and yet is held for one. Aristotle called that *forma substantialis*, and that was with him also the soul.",["'nannte des Forma substantialis' is doubtful; 'des' read as 'das' (OCR or transcript slip) and rendered 'that'.","'Erkenntniß Grund' = 'ground of cognition'; 'vernünftig' = reasonable (Vernunft = reason).","'dafür gehalten wird' = 'is held for one' ('one' = a substance); 'angeht' rendered 'is feasible'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-011":["Opposed to nature are 1. chance. But because all things have nature, nothing is by chance. Everything is according to laws. But *fatum* necessity without laws, without ground.",["'Ohngefähr' and 'Ungefähr' are both rendered 'chance'. The last sentence has no verb in the German; kept."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-012":["Freedom is also a nature, insofar as it is determined not by others, but by itself. The human being has this. The animals indeed determine their instinct, but this is nevertheless already determined beforehand by God to it. To call the beginning of nature (as the author wants) life, and the end death, belongs only for living, but not for lifeless creatures. That would be too symbolic. *Machina* is not *corpus secundum leges universales motus mobile*, as the author explains it, but *secundum leges particulares*. Otherwise every body would be a machine. Mechanism is *modus compositionis* of a machine. It is the formal, just as the moving force the material law in nature.",["'parti culares' read as 'particulares' (OCR). 'Mensch' = human being; 'dazu' = 'to it', i.e. 'for this'.","The last sentence ('sowie die bewegende Kraft des materiale Gesetz') is grammatically doubtful as printed ('materiale' is uninflected, 'des' with 'Gesetz'); left as it stands, with no verb supplied.","'Der Autor' (Baumgarten) is as in the German; 'erklärt' = explains."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-013":["To explain something from the nature of bodies is called physical, and to explain from the nature of thinking beings, pneumatic. If I explain something not from nature but from a cause that goes beyond nature, then this is explained hyperphysically. But if this happens from *qualitatibus occultis*, then it is hypophysical. The body is *in nexu mechanico*, which can be explained from the moving forces. There are still 2 kinds of ways of physical explanation: 1. *Philosophia mechanica*, which explains all phenomena from the shape and the general moving force of bodies. This is also explained *mechanice* or *physico mechanice*. Here I explain not the origin but the alteration of motion from motions already present. E.g. one explains mechanically how vinegar dissolves chalk, thereby: vinegar has in its *Atomis* nothing but pointed little parts that look like spears. These now pierce the chalk and so it is dissolved. This force, which drives the vinegar-spears into the chalk, comes from the warmth of the outer air. But where does that come from? There we sit. Mechanically, then, one cannot explain the phenomena after all.",["Erklärung/erklären = explanation/explain throughout; Kraft = force; Theilchen = little parts (Theil = part); Veränderung = alteration (not in glossary).","\"Erklärungs Arten\": Art rendered \"way\"; \"2erlei physische Erklärungs Arten\" is awkward in the German and kept so.","Phaenomene rendered \"phenomena\" (not Erscheinung)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-014":["2. The dynamical way of explanation, when one assumes certain fundamental forces from which one derives the phenomena. This was first invented by Newton and is more satisfying and more complete than the previous one. Thus to explain something mechanically means to explain something according to the laws of motion, dynamically, from the forces of bodies. With every explanation one never comes to an end. The right way of explanation is the physico-dynamical, which includes both in itself. That is the way of explanation of the present time. The first is the way of explanation of Cartesius, the 2nd that of the chemists. Thus, e.g., Cartesius explains the attractive force of bodies in such a way that he assumed a matter that flowed around the bodies and held them together. But if one asks where this matter comes from, one can derive it from nothing other than God. Newton explained this appearance dynamically, so that God had laid into the substance a force to draw bodies to themselves.",["Erscheinung (\"appearance\") here, versus Phaenomene (\"phenomena\") earlier in the passage.","\"an sich zu ziehen\": \"to draw ... to themselves\"; the reflexive could also be read as \"to it\" (the substance); the German is ambiguous.","\"flöße\" is subjunctive (reported assumption), rendered \"flowed\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-015":["Third Section",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-016":["On the Representation of the Whole",["Vorstellung = representation."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-017":["1st Chapter",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-018":["On the Best World",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-019":["(In the transcendental understanding all things are heterogeneous and consequently one cannot say in this understanding that one thing is more perfect than another. One must therefore look to a certain similarity of things, and then one can make the distinction between the good, the best and the most perfect. Good is that which is the objective ground of our choice, for that which is the subjective ground of our choice is agreeable. The greatest objective ground of our choice is the best. Perfection is the ground of the greatest objective unity of cognition. Thus the good is for the will, the perfect for the understanding. To the greatest perfection of the world belong: 1. The greatest multitude of substances according to their extensive, intensive and protensive magnitude. 2. A complete connection of the same according to the rules of the greatest unity. This latter belongs to it especially and is the foremost.) One can think *Summum bonum originarium* and *derivativum*. The world is the latter. For a *Totum* is always contingent and therefore *caußatum*. Here what is properly at issue is the perfection of the world. The author has confused this with *Optimum*, although it is different from it. Perfection, insofar as it signifies a completeness of the whole and reciprocal harmony and connection of the whole, is transcendental perfection. Here, then, the perfection of the world also signifies the perfection of the whole, to which no part is lacking that could still be added, and which stands in the best order and combination.",["\"im transcendentalen Verstände\" / \"in diesem Verstände\": Verstände (dative, old -e) could be \"understanding\" or \"sense\" (meaning); rendered \"understanding\" per glossary, ambiguity left.","Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung = combination; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Erkenntniß = cognition; Menge = multitude; Größe = magnitude.","\"caußatum\" is the spelling of the transcript (*causatum*); \"Der Autor\" presumably the textbook author; not glossed.","\"bedeutet\" = signifies."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-020":["In the perfection of the world, then, we have two things to remark: 1. a perfection with regard to the parts or extensive magnitude 2. the perfection in the connection or intensive magnitude. No world is absolutely complete with regard to its parts. More substances can still always be added; just as something can still be added to every multitude. The multitude of the parts, however, does not constitute the perfection, but only the manifoldness of the same. Meanwhile a greatest is nevertheless possible, e.g. from the multitude of subjects in a land I cannot immediately infer the perfection of the state. (breaks off).",["\"Mannigfaltigkeit\" rendered \"manifoldness\" (glossary has Mannigfaltige = manifold, n.).","\"Untherthanen\" read as Unterthanen (subjects); \"Größestes\" rendered \"a greatest\".","\"( bricht ab)\" is the editor's remark, carried over as \"(breaks off)\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-021":["I Were there no hope for a future life, then the vicious man, who sought by all kinds of means and intrigues to put himself in possession of earthly happiness, would be the happiest.",["\"I\" at the start left as printed (apparently an enumeration mark)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-022":["From this one sees that metaphysics first arose from this practical interest of our reason.",["Vernunft = reason."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-023":["The use of metaphysics is",["Nutzen rendered \"use\" (Gebrauch in the glossary is a different word)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-024":["2. Logical, in that it extends our understanding, for in that we analyze the concepts of our understanding, we thereby clarify it. But this use is too insignificant for it to have been able to give occasion to the invention of metaphysics, and were it alone, the whole of metaphysics would be dispensable.",["\"aufklären\" rendered \"clarify\"; \"zergliedern\" \"analyze\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-025":["For the sake of physics and mathematics one would not need metaphysics. For the principles of the possibility of experience one sees daily from experience itself, and the error in experience I can soon discover if I repeat it. Besides, metaphysics does not go to natural science, but beyond the same. The use of metaphysics considered in general is either positive or negative. Namely, the positive use of a thing always consists in how we can attain through it some more perfection; the positive use of metaphysics, then, is the attainment of an acquaintance with God and of the hope of a future life. The negative use, to preserve us from errors.",["Kenntniß rendered \"acquaintance\" per glossary; \"Physic\" and \"Mathematic\" rendered \"physics\" and \"mathematics\".","\"geht ... auf die Natur Wissenschaft, sondern über dieselbe\": \"goes to ... but beyond the same\"; \"auf\" could also be \"is directed at\".","\"wie wir durch sie\": \"how\" kept as in German (possibly \"that\")."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-026":["13»",["Only a number and mark; probably a page or line number from the OCR, translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-027":["The negative utility of metaphysics is the greater, in that it consists in the warding off of imperfection. It is surely, however, more important to remedy pain than to procure oneself a pleasure. The negative utility of metaphysics is also almost the only one, for the existence of God and the hope of a future life can be cognized by every human being, with some consideration of nature and of his state, through healthy human understanding, and he therefore has no need of speculative inquiry, which can in general be done by the fewest human beings. But this is merely a practical belief, in which the human being can be led astray by every speculative doubt. In order now to resolve these speculative doubts and investigations, which cannot be refuted by practical belief, and are nevertheless indispensable to our reason, speculative principles must also be opposed to them, and this metaphysics does. Its chief utility is therefore to purify our cognition of errors and to preserve it from them. The chief ends of the whole of metaphysics are the cognition of God and the hope of a future life. If metaphysics wanted merely to expound this question: Is it true that there is a God and a future life? it would not be necessary at all, but one can indeed see this, according to the rules of a rational practical belief, with the greatest probability. But it investigates rather whether we can arrive, through mere speculation, at the cognition of God and of a future life, and how far we are in a position to do that. There are 2 kinds of way to treat a science, dogmatic and critical. 1. The dogmatic method is that one in which I lay down some cognition as indemonstrable propositions as the ground and build the others upon it. 2. The critical method, when I investigate the principles themselves according to their way of arising, from which power of mind they have sprung, and according to their possibility, how they could have arisen a priori. In geometry one proceeds dogmatically, as also in other sciences, e.g. the proposition: between 2 points only one straight line can be. It is not at all necessary to investigate this, for one can confirm it at once from experience. Hence indemonstrable propositions also hold in mathematics; but in philosophy, especially metaphysics, one cannot proceed dogmatically. E.g. if I accept the principle: Everything that arises is in time, I can conclude and derive from it many false and unprovable propositions and inferences. I can say, if all things are in time, then the first cause of them must also be in time. It must therefore also first have begun in time to act. But why then could it not have acted earlier? This is insoluble for the understanding, and if it inquires further into the matter, it finally falls into errors. Hence in philosophy it will not do to proceed according to principles, because they are not certain and sufficient to explain all the consequences drawn from them. The critical method goes not to the cognition itself or to the object, but to the understanding. Hence it is not objective but subjective. If I have therefore accepted a principle in philosophy, I must first have proved it. But if I cannot prove it, it is suspect and not to be used. For if the cognitions concluded from it comprise a contradiction, I cannot remove it, because metaphysics goes beyond experience and I therefore cannot test the principles according to experience. In metaphysics critique is most indispensable, because otherwise one can very easily err, since it has no guiding thread, in that it ascends beyond the boundary of experience; hence the utility of metaphysics is also negative, and indeed this utility is therefore the greatest, because critique is most necessary to metaphysics. In that it is therefore built on critique and preserves us from errors, it is a bulwark of religion against all speculative doubts. In our lecture we will combine both methods, first dogmatically, and afterwards critique it step by step. For if critique does not accompany the dogmatic, we have no touchstone of truth. If I speak, e.g., of the beginning of the world, I can impossibly have this from experience, I can also never confirm it through experience, but this goes beyond all experience and is therefore a mere idea in my reason. — But how does my understanding come to this, and how far does it reach? I must then ask. It is peculiar that these questions have still almost not at all been investigated. One has indeed posed the questions and investigated whence our concepts come; but that does not belong here.",["Nutzen = utility; Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkennen = cognize; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; Grundsatz = principle; Satz = proposition; Glaube = belief; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Zweck = end; Gemüthskraft = power of mind; Grenze = boundary; Betrachtung = consideration; Zustand = state; Idee = idea.","'Zweck' in 'Hauptzwecke' = 'chief ends'; 'Zweifel' = doubt; 'Schluß' = inference (twice, 'Schlüße'), 'schließen' rendered 'conclude', 'folgern' also 'conclude' ('gefolgerte').","'Art' occurs in 'Es giebt 2erlei Art' and 'Entstehungsart': rendered 'kind of way' and 'way of arising'; the German 'Art' is thus kept literal.","'Gegenstand/Obiect': only 'Obiect' occurs here, rendered 'object'.","'gesunden Menschen Verstand' = 'healthy human understanding' (literal, for gesunder Menschenverstand).","'hinreichend' (no glossary entry beside 'zureichend') rendered 'sufficient'; 'gar nicht nöthig' kept as 'not necessary at all'.","Ambiguity kept: 'sie' in 'es hernach Schritt vor Schritt critisiren' — 'es' left as 'it' (the dogmatic, or the matter). 'Obiectiv/subiectiv' rendered 'objective/subjective'.","OCR corrections: 'Irrthiimern' → Irrthümern; 'Metyphysik' → Metaphysik; 'wiederlegt' etc. left as in the source spelling. 'Hauptnutzen' = 'chief utility'; 'a priori' left in roman as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-028":["In every science that contains concepts of reason in itself, one represents to oneself a metaphysics of the science, which one must well distinguish from this metaphysics. If in a science of the understanding I separate the pure cognition of reason from the empirical, then this collection — - is a metaphysics of the same science. It is therefore the pure product of reason in the sciences. This is very useful in a science, in order to separate the cognition of reason from the empirical, in order to see the errors the more distinctly. Metaphysics is the spirit of philosophy. It relates to philosophy as the *spiritus vini* to wine. It purifies our elementary concepts and thereby makes us skillful at being able to see into all sciences. In short, it is the greatest culture of the human understanding.",["Begriff = concept; Wissenschaft = science; Vernunft = reason; Verstand = understanding; Erkenntniß = cognition; deutlich = distinct; Geist = spirit (in 'Geist der Philosophie'), with *spiritus vini* kept in Latin as in the source.","'Verstandes Wissenschaft' kept literal as 'science of the understanding'; 'Vernunft Begriffe' = 'concepts of reason'; 'Vernunft Erkenntniß' = 'cognition of reason'.","The dash after 'Sammlung' ('— -') is carried over as it stands; it may be an OCR artifact or a gap in the transcript.","'einsehen' appears twice, rendered 'see' / 'see into'; 'geschickt' = 'skillful'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-029":["History of Metaphysics",["Heading only."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-030":["(breaks off)",["Editorial remark ('bricht ab') translated as it stands."]]};
